HT_DATA.load("detail", {"AnrlSqtpOkw":{"wy":"HN returns to it again and again as a demonstration of how programmable and composable 1970s systems were compared with today's.","q":{"x":"a great example of what's not-even-close to possible on today's systems","i":15321922,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":34991036,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2023-03","story":"50 Years Later, We’re Still Living in the Xerox Alto’s World","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Alto demo shows user-programmable system beyond modern OSes","quote":"Look how programmable this is by the user!","text":"The Alto did things that even modern computers don't do as well. There's a demo I like to point at for this: [VIDEO] Look how programmable this is by the user! Today's OSes and their division of software into \"applications\" erect barriers to software composition that don't need to be there."},{"id":15321922,"author":"kennethfriedman","month":"2017-09","story":"Then and Now: How Little Things Have Changed (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows what is not-even-close to possible on today's systems","quote":"a great example of what's not-even-close to possible on today's systems","text":"OP here. Definitely some improvements... but (at least in my eyes) incremental. Notebooks are pretty sweet, but in the hands of very few. They're definitely growing in popularity, which is great. But even the \"notebook\" concept is a very limited \"low pass filter\" version of the visions of Engelbart, AKay, TN, etc. The video that AKay has been sharing around in the post than @dang linked to[0] is a great example of what's not-even-close to possible on today's systems. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44242581,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2025-06","story":"Malleable software: Restoring user agency in a world of locked-down apps","stance":"rec","why":"Alan Kay's talk on Smalltalk","quote":"Another reference I usually bring up is Alan Kay's talk on smalltalk","text":"In the 'Tools, not Apps' part of the article they reference Michel Beaudouin-Lafon's talk 'A World Without Apps' which goes back in time and shows the Xerox Star operating system: [LINK] Another reference I usually bring up is Alan Kay's talk on smalltalk: [VIDEO] My related comments on this, just to show other stories along this theme: - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":35782669,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2023-05","story":"Mercury OS","stance":"rec","why":"Alto composing data live across windows like a shell pipeline","quote":"The Alto let you compose data live from multiple windows kind of like a shell pipeline does for command-line apps","text":"I love that this concept tries to separate content from apps. Ever since I saw some of the ways early GUIs differed from today's, I've had a feeling that we've lost something with the way apps in today's desktops are completely isolated from each other. The Alto let you compose data live from multiple windows kind of like a shell pipeline does for command-line apps: [VIDEO] The Locus and Flows in this concept seem like they could enable a similar workflow, as data isn't confined to \"apps\" the way it usually would be."},{"id":34376376,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2023-01","story":"Emacs Is Not Enough","stance":"rec","why":"Shows 1970s desktop computing directions that were more composable","quote":"I'm always struck by the useful directions that desktop computers were going in the 70s","text":"Every time I see something like this, I'm also reminded of this demo of the Xerox Alto from a few years back. I'll link to the start of the most relevant piece: [VIDEO] I'm always struck by the useful directions that desktop computers were going in the 70s, and how computing could be so much better than it is right now. But we've veered so off-course from all of this, away from composability and towards independent boxes (\"applications\") that can't talk to each other at all. Emacs is in some ways the best thing we have today in the other direction, but it pales in comparison to what's possible."},{"id":29725734,"author":"infinite8s","month":"2021-12","story":"PineNote Developer Edition – E-Paper Tablet","stance":"rec","why":"Smalltalk-78 as ideal starting interface for e-ink tablets","quote":"would be an ideal starting interface for e-ink based tablet","text":"Well, something like the smalltalk-78 that Alan Kay demos here ( [VIDEO] ) would be an ideal starting interface for e-ink based tablet."},{"id":27470826,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-06","story":"Try APL","stance":"rec","why":"Time travel to an alternate computing world with Smalltalk","quote":"Easy, here is some time travel","text":"> Thanks for sharing, I love these types of stories. Really makes me pine for the \"old\" days, and wonder if there's a parallel universe where technology took a very different route such that languages like APL, Lisp, and Smalltalk are used instead of JavaScript, Java, and C#, and what that world looks like. Easy, here is some time travel, \"Alan Kay's tribute to Ted Nelson at \"Intertwingled\" Festival\" [VIDEO] \"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\" [LINK] \"Yesterday's Computer of Tomorrow: The Xerox Alto │Smalltalk-76 Demo\" [LINK] However to be fair, Java, C# and Swift alongside their IDEs are the closest in mainstream languages to that experience, unless you get to use Allegro, LispWorks or Pharo."},{"id":19722205,"author":"alankay","month":"2019-04","story":"Why OO Sucks by Joe Armstrong (2000)","stance":"rec","why":"NoteTaker Smalltalk-78 revival demo, about 10,000 lines of code","quote":"I used it to make my visual material for a tribute to Ted Nelson. See what you think.","text":"It's out of the context of this thread, but we were quite sure that \"simulation-style\" systems design would be a much more powerful and comprehensive way to create most things on a computer, and most especially for personal computers. At Parc, I think we were able to make our point. Around 2014 or so we brought back to life the NoteTaker Smalltalk from 1978, and I used it to make my visual material for a tribute to Ted Nelson. See what you think. [VIDEO] This system --including everything -- \"OS\", SDK, Media, GUI, Tools, and the content -- is about 10,000 lines of Smalltalk-78 code sitting on top of about 6K bytes of machine code (the latter was emulated to get the whole system going). I think what happened is that the early styles of programming, especially \"data structures, procedures, imperative munging, etc.\" were clung to, in part because this was what was taught, and the more design-intensive but also more compact styles developed at Parc seemed very foreign. So when C++, Java, etc. came along the old styles were retained, and classes were relegated to creating abstract data types with getters and setters that could be munged from the outside. Note that this is also \"simulation style programming\" but simulating data structures is a very weak approach to design for power and scaling. I think the idea that all entities could be protected processes (and protected in both directions) that could be used as communicating modules for building systems got both missed and reject"},{"id":17915331,"author":"shalabhc","month":"2018-09","story":"The Early History Of Smalltalk (1993)","stance":"rec","why":"Revived Smalltalk-78 system shows what was possible in the 70s","quote":"To get another taste of the kinds of systems possible in the 70s, in spite of the constraints, see Kay's demo","text":"To get another taste of the kinds of systems possible in the 70s, in spite of the constraints, see Kay's demo of a revived Smalltalk-78 system: [VIDEO] (I believe this system is about 10-20K LOC software in total) Notes about the the revival are here: [LINK]"},{"id":16841190,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favorite tech talks","quote":"alan kay - tribute to ted nelson at intertwingled fest","text":"#Softwarish - I'm biased a bit more towards interface development: greg wilson - What We Actually Know About Software Development, and Why We Believe It’s True - [LINK] steve wittens - making webgl dance - the title is deceptive, it's in some ways a visual crash course in linear algebra - [LINK] glenn vanderburg - software engineering doesn't work - [LINK] chris granger - in search of tomorrow - [LINK] alan kay - tribute to ted nelson at intertwingled fest - [VIDEO] bret victor - this is already mentioned but if I had to pick one it'd be 'the humane representation of thought' - [LINK] #Hardwarish: saul griffith - soft, not solid: beyond traditional hardware engineering - [LINK] deb chachra - Architectural Biology and Biological Architectures - [LINK] #Getting more meta in technology and history: James Burke - Connections"},{"id":15269014,"author":"alankay","month":"2017-09","story":"Alan Kay is still waiting for his dream to come true","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of revived 1978 Xerox PARC software","quote":"If people are interested to see part of what we had in mind","text":"People often ask me \"Is this a Dynabook, is that a Dynabook?\". Only about 5% of the idea was in packaging (and there were 2 other different packages contemplated in 1968 besides the tablet idea -- the HMD idea from Ivan, and the ubiquitous idea from Nicholas). Almost all the thought I did was based on what had already been done in the ARPA community -- rendered as \"services\" -- and resculpted for the world of the child. It was all quite straightforwardly about what Steve later called \"Wheels for the Mind\". If people are interested to see part of what we had in mind, a few of us including Dan Ingalls a few years ago revived a version of the Xerox Parc software from 1978 that Xerox had thrown away (it was rescued from a trash heap). This system was the vintage that Steve Jobs saw the next year when he visited in 1979. I used this system to make a presentation for a Ted Nelson tribute. It should start at 2:15. See what you think about what happens around 9:05. [VIDEO] Next year will be the 50 anniversary of this idea, and many things have happened since then, so it would be crazy to hark back to a set of ideas that were in the context of being able to be built over 10 years, and would be ridiculous if we didn't have in 30 (that would be 1998, almost 20 years ago). The large idea of ARPA/PARC was that desirable futures for humanity will require many difficult things to be learned beyond reading and writing and a few sums. If \"equal rights\" is to mean something over the entire pla"},{"id":34308843,"author":"morphle","month":"2023-01","story":"Alan Kay on “Should web browsers have stuck to being document viewers?” and ...","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Alan Kay made scathing comments on the World Wide Web browser (reinventing a broken wheel) at length in a lecture \"Normal Considered Harmful\" for computer science students at University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign. For context: Alan himself invented WYSIWYG at Xerox PARC. He was present at \"the mother of all demo's\" by Douglas Englebart. Ted Nelson who invented hyperlinks and Xanadu got Alan in touch with his future wife [2] and Alan was there at Apple when Bill Atkinson made Hypercard. He mentions that when he told Tim Berners Lee about the much better work of Douglas Englebart, Tim was chagrined (that he had not read any of the papers about earlier and much better designs of 28, 25 and 20 years before the WWW browser). Of course Alan also discusses how the web should have been done. In short, it should be that when you click a hyperlink you load a virtual machine in a secure sandbox of a Operating System Kernel. The virtual machine would then render the (bitmap or vector) pages. Operating System 101, well known in 1965. Of course, Alan's team did implement this (several times). I can demo this if you contact me. Email me at morphle [at] ziggo [dot] nl for a video where Alan Kay, Douglas Englebart and Tim Berners Lee meet and talk about this at a forum discussion. You can ask me about anything related to Alan, Ted or Douglas, I maintain an extensive archive with all scientific papers, emails, talks, interviews and lecture video's. Alan, Ted and Douglas worked closely togeth"},{"id":25216839,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2020-11","story":"Technology as It Should Be","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"In Imagine a world without apps Shira Ovide asks “a wild question: What if we played games, shopped, watched Netflix and read news on our smartphones — without using apps? Our smartphones, like our computers, would instead mostly be gateways to go online through a web browser.” This question can be extrapolated into a larger question: “What do we want from our technology?\"\" To quote Bret Victor, \"Toolkits, not Apps\" : [LINK] [VIDEO] Jumping straight into talk about providers, personal data, security, etc. assumes cultural scaffolds of 'personal' around the device. That's fine, but the assumption needs to be made aware."},{"id":22524066,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-03","story":"The Call of the Ocean: Hypertext, Universal and Open (1988)","stance":"reference","why":"Alan Kay's Ted Nelson tribute, on Tron's script editing","quote":"Alan Kay's tribute to Ted Nelson at \"Intertwingled\" Fest","text":"Ted Nelson: Computer Lib / Dream Machines (1975) [pdf] (worrydream.com) [LINK] [LINK] For what it's worth, YC is helping Ted Nelson sell his \"Computer Lib / Dream Machines\" book: [LINK] This book is a truly unique and is worth owning in hardcopy format. [LINK] Ted versus The Media Lab [video] (youtube.com) [LINK] [LINK] Interview with Ted Nelson (notion.so) [LINK] [LINK] Ted Nelson on What Modern Programmers Can Learn from the Past [video] (ieee.org) [LINK] [LINK] Ted Nelson struggles with uncomprehending radio interviewer (1979) [audio] (youtube.com) [LINK] [LINK] Ted Nelson’s published papers on computers and interaction, 1965 to 1977 (archive.org) [LINK] [LINK] Ask HN: What is the best resource for understanding Ted Nelson's ZigZag? [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Alan Kay's tribute to Ted Nelson at \"Intertwingled\" Fest (how the script of Tron was the first movie script to ever be edited by a word processing program, on the Alto computer) [VIDEO] \"Silicon Valley Story\" — a Very Short Romantic Comedy by Ted Nelson A playful story about the microcircuitry of love, with Ted Nelson as an absentminded genius, featuring Doug Engelbart as Ted's father and Stewart Brand as the villainous CEO. Closing song: \"Information Flow\", sung by Donna Spitzer and the auteur. With Timothy Leary as the Good Venture Capitalist! [LINK] Ted Nelson's Channel [LINK]"},{"id":21653923,"author":"grblovrflowerrr","month":"2019-11","story":"How to annotate everything","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes, this siloing stops me from using annotation features \"native\" to each app and format as such as well. Instead, I prefer to exfiltrate information from the silos(apps, formats, etc) and put them into my note taking system. Then I can do highlights, annotations, etc. on my own terms and also get the benefits of centralization such as searching and linking(the OP has another post describing their own system, which is pretty cool[1]). Currently I'm using Notion, which is also a silo of its own, but it's one that gives me a lot of control over how I lay my information out(and an escape plan from). There are a lot of perspectives on this issue with data silos and walled gardens. But I'm of the opinion that it's a fairly bad state for all of us \"end users\". Computers to me are about infinite flexibility and malleability, but ironically the tools we have for annotation and remixing are in practice worse than what we have in the physical world. Reading a book in the physical world, I can converse with the author simply by jotting marginalia with my pencil. It's fluid, intuitive, and the medium of paper encourages it(in fact it can't help but be mutated by my use!: pages get bended, stained, torn, etc.). If I want to go further I can add post-it notes to mark interesting passages, I can xerox some pages and create subsections, if it's a magazine I can just tear them all out! That kind of flexibility just isn't available on a computer. I think it's worth thinking really hard why we"},{"id":21656627,"author":"jiriro","month":"2019-11","story":"How to annotate everything","stance":"reference","why":"Demo magic the commenter wants to reproduce today","quote":"Can I do similar magic as shown in the demo [0] today?","text":"Can I do similar magic as shown in the demo [0] today? I mean .. is there a system/tool/platform which allows that kind of interaction? I know about Smalltalk instances like Squeak[1], Etoys[2], Pharo[3] .. Are these capable of what is shown in the demo? [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":18257038,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2018-10","story":"Systems, not Programs","stance":"reference","why":"Excerpt from the Smalltalk era related to programmable substrate","quote":"an excerpt from the smalltalk era","text":"“The real goal is to design the form and nature of the software ‘entities’ — the ‘programmable substrate’ that we manipulate and compose when we program and use the system” Reminded me of bret victors “toolkits, not apps” comment ( [LINK] ) and an excerpt from the smalltalk era : [VIDEO]"},{"id":18317445,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2018-10","story":"Duckspeak vs. Smalltalk: Decline of the Xerox PARC Philosophy at Apple (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Having everything in the same space, and being able to inspect and modify anything in real time by the seat of your pants, was exactly how Dan Ingalls impressed Steve Jobs on his fateful visit to Xerox PARC! It's unfortunate the iPad never did (and never will) achieve that level of power and flexibility. [LINK] >Q: What was it like to be at Xerox PARC when Steve Jobs visited? >A: Alan Kay, Agitator at Viewpoints Research Institute >[...] The demo itself was fun to watch — basically a tag team of Dan Ingalls and Larry Tesler showing many kinds of things to Steve and the several Apple people he brought with him. One of Steve’s ways to feel in control was to object to things that were actually OK, and he did this a few times — but in each case Dan and Larry were able to make the changes to meet the objections on the fly because Smalltalk was not only the most advanced programming language of its time, it was also live at every level, and no change required more than 1/4 second to take effect. >One objection was that the text scrolling was line by line and Steve said “Can’t this be smooth?”. In a few seconds Dan made the change. Another more interesting objection was to the complementation of the text that was used (as today) to indicate a selection. Steve said “Can’t that be an outline?”. Standing in the back of the room, I held my breath a bit (this seemed hard to fix on the fly). But again, Dan Ingalls instantly saw a very clever way to do this (by selecting the text as usual,"},{"id":15528887,"author":"shalabhc","month":"2017-10","story":"The Xerox Alto, Smalltalk, and Rewriting a Running GUI","stance":"reference","why":"Fun live demo using Smalltalk emulators","quote":"(see for a fun one)","text":"An easy way to try ST-72 is in your browser here: [LINK] and ST-78 here: [LINK] The above links run emulators in Javascript and have been used for live demos as well (see [VIDEO] for a fun one) Related, for a Javascript based live system check out [LINK] (also created by Dan Ingalls)."},{"id":11953910,"author":"alankay1","month":"2016-06","story":"Alan Kay has agreed to do an AMA today","stance":"reference","why":"Alan Kay's presentation made in revived Smalltalk-78","quote":"to create a visual presentation for a tribute to Ted Nelson on his 70th birthday","text":"When I write code it is usually either \"kiddicode\" for future \"kiddilanguages\" or \"metacode\" (for future languages \".\") I did have a lot of fun last year writing code in a resurrected version of the Notetaker Smalltalk-78 (done mostly by Dan Ingalls and Bert Freudenberg from a rescued disk pack that Xerox had thrown away) to create a visual presentation for a tribute to Ted Nelson on his 70th birthday: [VIDEO] This particular system was a wonderful sweet spot for those days -- it was hugely expressive and quite small and understandable (my size). (This was the Smalltalk system that Steve Jobs saw the next year in 1979 -- though with fewer pictures because of memory limitations back then)."}],"r":[["H9B5mYfBPlY",1],["GNO_CYUjMK8",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["G0R09YzyuCI",1],["VZQoAKJPbh8",1],["6orsmFndx_o",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["NH1Tta7purM",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["D7Sd8A6_fYU",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["XkDEzfpdcSg",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["NqKyHEJe9_w",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1]]},"IlU-zDU6aQ0":{"wy":"HN recommends it for productivity, focus and working-from-home threads, since its advice on breaks and mental modes applies beyond studying.","q":{"x":"Marty Lobdell did a fantastic talk a while back [1] that changed the way I used to study","i":26977588,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26977588,"author":"grae_QED","month":"2021-04","story":"How I studied for 12 hours a day for over a year","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed how commenter studies; recommended for inefficient studiers","quote":"Marty Lobdell did a fantastic talk a while back [1] that changed the way I used to study","text":"I'm more curious if he was actually studying. A lot of people claim they can study for these absurd amounts of time when the vast majority of it is actually spent staring at a page or even rereading the same page. Marty Lobdell did a fantastic talk a while back [1] that changed the way I used to study and I'd recommend it to anyone who feels like they aren't studying efficiently. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41338230,"author":"james-revisoai","month":"2024-08","story":"Why Ed-Tech Startups Don't Scale (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Teaches effective studying techniques","quote":"there are some great teachers(lecturers) like Martin who teach effective studying techniques","text":"Some of the teachers I have worked with do this; and there are some great teachers(lecturers) like Martin who teach effective studying techniques: [VIDEO] Flashcards and Anki were incredibly effective for me. I am building a study tool which automatically generates them from lecture slides. Beyond that, it applies other psychology of learning techniques - It breaks up content into manageable chunks you can see your progress on (to boost your confidence of exam coverage) and has an exam-conditions like mode where it shows you relevant slides, then asks you questions and to freely recall by blurting onto a blank page everything you remember. It's then able to give you a realistic judgement of your learning and this way of learning by actively recalling freely is, though more intense, ultimately more effective than either reading, watching or quizzing in the long term. The part of learning that other flashcard apps miss, even Anki, is that students must be motivated. They must enjoy, visually engage, and be confident as they study. They must feel they are on a sustainable realistic path as they gather knowledge when on the way to their goals. And they don't have to study every day to do that - they just need to genuinely learn and be able to see the state of their progress... I am excited to be working on EdTech now[Revision.ai] which unveils and detects misconceptions to aid the student in eliminating them. Misconceptions are a massive opportunity - as much as spaced repetition"},{"id":39329273,"author":"james-revisoai","month":"2024-02","story":"How to Study (2023)","stance":"rec","why":"Timeless engaging one-hour lecture on effective study","quote":"a rather timeless one hour engaging lecture here","text":"This makes some great points, and Martin Lombell has covered something very similar in a rather timeless one hour engaging lecture here: [VIDEO] Some other diamonds I've uncovered or found while working on learning psychology are: - that students who organise fun and exercise on their schedule before studying, get better grades than the students who start by deciding when to study; - that most of us can learn much better with confusing, misconception-revealing content, over simple content - we all assume too much, as Veritasium covered: [LINK] - in principle, a desirable difficulty of varied content given in a medium that isn't lazy (read: you need to be able to get feedback, like a quiz, or rewriting, or tutoring) is the key way to establish viable long term learning; several other effects like Spaced Repetition only have benefits if you can stick to your motivation for learning, so you do need to prioritise motivation first. The article mentioned learning styles not being proven; in fact, one can argue the opposite is true; people who learn with mixed, varied content perform the best. But we also like the learning type which is comfortable for us; this boosts motivation. The problem is that studying students across a whole semester and multiple courses rarely happens, and studies focusing on how varying the learning content within a 10 minute window changed how much students learned, does not replicate the value of being familiar with a learning content style, nor the cost "},{"id":19343502,"author":"pedro1976","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: How do you stay focused when working from home?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how location shapes mental mode, so separate work space","quote":"I watched an interesting lecture by martin lobdell","text":"I watched an interesting lecture by martin lobdell - study less study smart [0] which says that your mind tends to be in a specific mental mode depending on the location. So when you try to work on your kitchen table, your mind is in the eating mode and you constantly think of that sandwich. Hence, separate as much as possible, separate room/table/laptop... [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18218225,"author":"pedro1976","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Getting your brain into the right mode for study","quote":"Hacking the mind, how to get your brain into the right mode","text":"Hacking the mind, how to get your brain into the right mode Marty Lobdell - Study Less Study Smart [VIDEO] AJ Jacobs: The Importance of Self-Delusion in the Creative Process” [LINK] -- On Machine Learning biases Aylin Caliskan - A Story of Discrimination and Unfairness [LINK] -- On scaling test automation, why scaling does not deliver the value you want GTAC2016: Scale vs Value - Test Automation at the BBC [LINK] -- On Future of Machine Learning Joscha Bach - Machine Dreams (33c3) [LINK] Joscha Bach - Computational Meta-Psychology (32c3) [LINK] Joscha Bach - From Computation to Consciousness (31c3) [LINK] [Edit] Layout"},{"id":16412508,"author":"piceas","month":"2018-02","story":"Ask HN: Is it 'normal' to struggle so hard with work?","stance":"rec","why":"Study skills lecture applicable to work focus; environment advice from 10:15","quote":"Study environment commentary from 10:15","text":"Managing distractions, clearing, and changing your environment are particularly effective for me. I was reminded of the following study skills lecture, some of which is applicable when trying to get going and keep going in a work context. Marty Lobdell - Study Less Study Smart ~60 min. Study environment commentary from 10:15 [VIDEO]"},{"id":12989065,"author":"Jemmeh","month":"2016-11","story":"How to start a company with no free time","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on scheduled breaks during long work","quote":"Marty Lobdell has a good talk about this on Youtube","text":"Any time you have to work hard for a long period of time, the trick is to take scheduled breaks. You don't need a long break either, just like 5 minutes every 30 minutes throughout the day will keep your energy up a lot. I'll come home, eat dinner while watching something fun, then get back using that pattern. Marty Lobdell has a good talk about this on Youtube: [VIDEO] I think for me the other thing is -- don't make it bigger than it is, like don't psych yourself out. Yes, it's hard to work from morning to night, but it's just hard, not impossible. And I think being physically good--eating right, sleep enough, and exercising, yes it's tired advice but that's how you get \"energy\"."},{"id":12010998,"author":"Jemmeh","month":"2016-06","story":"Ways to maximize your cognitive potential","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant talk on focusing and working effectively","quote":"Relevant talk on Youtube","text":"As a person who has trouble focusing-- A.) Minimize visual stimuli in your work area. Get everything off your desk, and shove your face in a corner. (I've turned my desk to face away from the hallway.) B.) Take notes as you work. I will write 15 minutes of code, take a note on what I'm doing, then get (purposefully) distracted for a minute or two. When I come back to my work, I can look at my note to see what I was doing and pick back up where I left off. It really minimizes the impact of distraction on my work. You can start out with lower chunks of \"productive\" time if you want. Practicing focus helps you to build up amount of time you can focus. Relevant talk on Youtube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11463907,"author":"BoysenberryPi","month":"2016-04","story":"Ask HN: How are you taking notes","stance":"rec","why":"Note taking system separating facts from concepts, with mnemonics for facts","quote":"a note taking system that separates facts and concepts that I really like","text":"There was a lecture by Marty Lobdell[1] where he talks about a note taking system that separates facts and concepts that I really like. Concepts are easy to get a handle on because it doesn't take much effort to think of a real word example to relate it to. Facts on the other hand are very difficult because they aren't easily related to knowledge you already have so for facts you use a mnemonic device. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10012013,"author":"Jemmeh","month":"2015-08","story":"Ask HN: How are some people exceptionally productive?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk eloquently covers taking breaks and studying effectively","quote":"Study Less, Study Smart is a talk by Marty Lobdell that eloquently discusses this.","text":"1.) Write things down. Get organized. Google Calendar and Trello mean I never forget to do anything and I don't exercise brain power on remembering it all. This helps balance by being more relaxed. At night I don't worry about all the things I have to do tomorrow, which makes a lot of people sleep less. I say, \"It's on my list, which I will tackle tomorrow.\" I sleep like a baby. I get things done. 2.) If you're looking for balance, take care of your body. 1 hour workout is 4% of your day. Make time for it, and do something active that you enjoy. (Even if that's playing DDR for an hour). Cook healthy meals in bulk ahead of time. Sick and dead isn't productive. 3.) Take breaks. When you feel your mind wandering, and you can't remember anything you've read in the past 5 minutes it's time to take a short break. Take 5 minutes, walk around the building, then sit back down. You might think you'll be so much more productive continuing to sit there working, but if you're working in circles or even making things worse, you are not being productive. Study Less, Study Smart is a talk by Marty Lobdell that eloquently discusses this. [VIDEO] 4.) Focus. Remove distractions. Close all the windows you don't need to work. Close your office door, answer your emails only once an hour. Whatever you need to do to focus."},{"id":38791680,"author":"rramadass","month":"2023-12","story":"Book list for streetfighting computer scientists (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"1) Read the ToC carefully to see what each chapter is about. Identify which chapters/sections are about \"Concepts/Ideas\" vs. \"Implementation/Technology\". 2) Read the book's Foreward/Preface/Introduction where the author(s) often lay out a plan of action for reading the book. This will give you the dependencies among the chapters and which are the most important so that you can decide on your path of action. 3) If the book has appendices/introductory chapter which lays out some needed background knowledge refresher in Mathematics/etc. read these first. 4) Go through each chapter's beginning introductory section and last summary section in sequence for all the chapters. Now you have an idea of what each chapter is about (motivation and summary) even though much is still unknown. Mark/Underline/Annotate key points/terms as needed. 5) Now from (1) & (2) you have a set of chapters/sections dealing with the fundamental \"Concepts/Ideas\" which is what you now start studying in sequence. Have paper and pencil handy to mark/underline/annotate the text and take notes as needed. You could use \"SQ3R\" ( [LINK] ) and \"Cornell Note-taking method\" ( [LINK] ) as techniques. 6) Make another pass over (5) to better grasp the \"Concepts/Ideas\". For difficult to grasp subjects this might not be enough. Make a note of why; perhaps you still lack some needed background knowledge/Mathematics which you need to brush up on. Make sure you don't go too deep into tangents; sometimes it can be treated as a "},{"id":11557034,"author":"Jugurtha","month":"2016-04","story":"Not All Practice Makes Perfect","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh, you're very welcome. This paper sent me down the rabbit hole: after graduating as an incompetent Electronics Engineer, I thought about the essence of competence and I stumbled upon this article. I'm always happy when I find a resource that addresses an abstract idea I'm thinking about but can't formulate for two main reasons: it always opens a door -if it has a name, it can be searched effectively- and it always amazes me how come I didn't think of it before. For instance, after graduation, I read a book titled \"How to Study in College\". Why didn't I ask such an obvious question before. I learned many things, one of which is the Cornell Note-taking method. I've always been interested in how to search for, acquire, and keep knowledge, how to apply it, how to manage it, and how to track its evolution -mental breadcrumbs, which idea lead to the other?- So it got me interested in how memory works: how we forget, how we remember, and what to do about it. It led to Hermann Ebbinghaus and \"spaced repetition\". I wasn't satisfied, though, for I wanted to know the underlying brain processes. The neuroscience behind it. This led me to Hebbian Theory and the concept of Long Term Potentiation. (The Brain from Top to Bottom - [LINK] ) which frankly blew my mind to a thousand shards: memory viewed in terms of ions . (Here's a very nice video from Carleton College \"Neuroscience - Long-Term Potentiation\" : [LINK] ) and here's a good presentation by Carleton's Dr. Matthew Hollahan: (\"How W"},{"id":11259266,"author":"Jemmeh","month":"2016-03","story":"Ask HN: Should engineering jobs have a nap time?","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in support of short breaks for long productive hours","quote":"","text":"This question is phrased badly because of the \"side jobs\". But anyways - I frequently sleep on my lunch breaks. Naps are great, clears my head. I'm most productive right when I get into work and right after lunch nap. Also to those using, \"Well you're only going to get 4 hours productive time anyways\" - I disagree. The key to working long productive hours is taking short breaks. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["eVtCO84MDj8",1],["Eq3bUFgEcb4",1],["RcGyVTAoXEU",1]]},"gNRnrn5DE58":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as a half-hour mini-documentary on how precision is bootstrapped from scratch, relevant to machining and chip manufacturing threads.","q":{"x":"The Origins of Precision\" is a half hour well spent","i":36795642,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36795642,"author":"hinkley","month":"2023-07","story":"Cold restart whole system after total outage","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Half hour well spent on origins of precision","quote":"The Origins of Precision\" is a half hour well spent","text":"There's a way to grind mirrors optics for optics with polishing stones that aren't even flat to the naked eye. Basically the system arrives at tiny tolerances via the process of using the system. And there's way to make three perfectly flat sharpening stones by starting with three raw pieces of natural sharpening stone, just by alternately rubbing the three stones together until they flatten each other out. Paul Sellers can teach you how to flatten a large board without a planer. He also has videos on how to get a wood plane perfectly flat using a large sharpening stone (which can be made as above or with float glass). And if memory serves, you to make something perfectly round you first need something perfectly flat. Once you have something perfectly flat and something perfectly round it's off to the races. Edit: \"The Origins of Precision\" is a half hour well spent [VIDEO]"},{"id":24522543,"author":"kazen44","month":"2020-09","story":"Iron, How Did They Make It? Part I, Mining","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on precision machining history and the lathe","quote":"i can highly recommend the youtube series \"machine thinking","text":"the lathe is basically the one machine required to kickstart the industrial revolution. (and a way to drive it ofcourse). Also, three surfaces that can be ground truely flat is also vital. i can highly recommend the youtube series \"machine thinking\" if you have interest in this[0], aswell as the book \"the perfectionist\" [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":44831853,"author":"anitil","month":"2025-08","story":"Historical Tech Tree","stance":"rec","why":"Building a flat plate from scratch using three-plate method","quote":"I find this video of theirs the most relevant","text":"I find this video of theirs the most relevant [0] where they go through how to start on a desert island and build a flat reference plate using the three plate method and the build up from there [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39046308,"author":"adolph","month":"2024-01","story":"What happened to the US machine tool industry?","stance":"rec","why":"Nice storytelling about precision tools","quote":"For a nice video/contextual storytelling, see Machine Learning channel's Origins of Precision","text":"Agreed and to clarify: > everything in inches, foot pounds, and what not doesn't translate very well internationally when you start outsourcing It translates pretty well for the last 90 years: In 1930, the British Standards Institution adopted an inch of exactly 25.4 mm. The American Standards Association followed suit in 1933. By 1935, industry in 16 countries had adopted the \"industrial inch\" as it came to be known, effectively endorsing Johansson's pragmatic choice of conversion ratio. [LINK] See also the paragraph above referencing the precision tools enabled by Swede Carl Johansson's \"Jo Blocks.\" For a nice video/contextual storytelling, see Machine Learning channel's Origins of Precision: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36757930,"author":"roelschroeven","month":"2023-07","story":"Backyard Metalcasting","stance":"rec","why":"Related Machine Thinking videos on precision","quote":"Also Machine Thinking's videos \"The 1751 Machine that Made Everything","text":"Also Machine Thinking's videos \"The 1751 Machine that Made Everything\" ( [LINK] ), \"Origins of Precision\" ( [VIDEO] ) and some others on his channel."},{"id":33204995,"author":"csours","month":"2022-10","story":"Why wasn’t the steam engine invented earlier? Part III","stance":"rec","why":"Example of a precision-focused documentary channel","quote":"I think it's fair to call these mini-documentaries.","text":"A YouTube channel primarily dedicated to precision: [LINK] Example video: [VIDEO] I think it's fair to call these mini-documentaries."},{"id":27957801,"author":"Lev1a","month":"2021-07","story":"TSMC eyes Germany as possible location for first Europe chip plant","stance":"rec","why":"Nice summary of building up precision from surface plates","quote":"Nice summary: (\"Origins of Precision\" by \"Machine Thinking\")","text":"From what I've seen as an amateur that is yet fascinated by mechanical engineering: iteratively work up to the precision limits with your current methods then try to find/research a (slightly) better way of measuring and/or manufacturing. Since humanity has worked up to this point over centuries/millenia, you wouldn't need to \"bootstrap\" it from the beginning anymore, just choose the appropriate level of (manufacturing/measuring) precision for your usecase. Otherwise start with a surface plate. Nice summary: [VIDEO] (\"Origins of Precision\" by \"Machine Thinking\")"},{"id":28022430,"author":"q3k","month":"2021-07","story":"Build Your Own Metal Working Shop from Scrap (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Video introduction to flat surfaces and precision","quote":"If you're interested in this sort of stuff and want a video introduction, see","text":"(Hand) scraping refers to [LINK] . With two surfaces you can still have some bias (convexity/concavity) even though they appear to be relatively flat, you need three surfaces for absolute flatness. See the Whithworth Method described earlier in this topic. If you're interested in this sort of stuff and want a video introduction, see: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27014744,"author":"zeotroph","month":"2021-05","story":"Machine Thinking on the Printing Press: The Screws of Disruption [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Channel's video on origins of precision","quote":"Also much recommended from that Chanel are the videos on the \"Origins of Precision\"[1]","text":"How the screw of the wine press was the central part of Gensfleisch/Gutenbergs invention which allowed all the other previously existing ones to finally come together (though I also read that certain advances in metallurgy were also essential). Also see the previous video on screws, a much underappreciated invention. It then describes how the availability of books made propagation of existing technology and newer developments possible and faster. The only other comparable moments in history are probably the evolution of spoken language, the invention of writing and most recently the internet. Also much recommended from that Chanel are the videos on the \"Origins of Precision\"[1], and \"The 1751 Machine that Made Everything\"[2]. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":26760972,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2021-04","story":"Why was cycling not included in the ancient Olympics? (2004) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how precision metal tools came about","quote":"stumbled over this[1] nice video, \"Origins of Precision","text":"> the precision metal tools I was wondering about how those came about not long ago, and stumbled over this[1] nice video, \"Origins of Precision\". [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20366681,"author":"nas","month":"2019-07","story":"Wringing","stance":"rec","why":"Nice video on measurement precision in manufacturing.","quote":"This is a really nice video if you are interested in measurement precision for manufacturing","text":"This is a really nice video if you are interested in measurement precision for manufacturing: Machine Thinking: Origins of Precision and first project introduction [VIDEO]"},{"id":34703410,"author":"adolph","month":"2023-02","story":"How cheap digital verniers work (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Leland quote about Johansson","quote":"From Machine Thinking video \"Origins of Precision\"","text":"\"There are only two people I take my hat off to. One is the president of the United States and the other is Mr. Johansson from Sweden.\" --Henry M. Leland, Founder of Cadillac and Lincoln automobile companies From Machine Thinking video \"Origins of Precision\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":27958318,"author":"Akronymus","month":"2021-07","story":"TSMC eyes Germany as possible location for first Europe chip plant","stance":"reference","why":"Three-plate flattening method","quote":"Obligatory machine thinking","text":"Obligatory machine thinking: [VIDEO] Altough, I disagree with the \"random fashion\". Alternating the pairs AB -> BC -> CA seems more logical to me."},{"id":22009311,"author":"peter_d_sherman","month":"2020-01","story":"The 1751 Machine That Made Everything","stance":"reference","why":"Related to rebuilding tools from scratch.","quote":"how to rebuild tools that produce other tools","text":"Related: Jacques de Vaucanson [LINK] Origins of Precision and first project introduction [VIDEO] (The last video, incidentally, is related to the problem of being stuck on a desert island, and how to rebuild tools that produce other tools...)"}],"r":[["djB9oK6pkbA",2],["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HUMGc8hEkpc",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["jhicDUgXyNg",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["fRMMSyCcTDI",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["85ZCTuekjGA",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["LA_DrBwkiJA",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["r3snVCRo_bI",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["Erp8IAUouus",1]]},"kF9KretXqJw":{"wy":"After the 2022 invasion, commenters spread it widely as an eye-opening explanation of Russian mindset, corruption and propaganda, though a few doubt the presenter's rigor.","q":{"x":"is fantastic since its from 2018 but still 100% valid","i":32925587,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32925587,"author":"saiya-jin","month":"2022-09","story":"President Putin has announced a partial mobilization in Russia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains Russian power structures and corruption; still valid despite being from 2018","quote":"is fantastic since its from 2018 but still 100% valid","text":"I really don't think he is facing any kind of true rivalry here. Read up a bit (ie [1] is fantastic since its from 2018 but still 100% valid) on the structures of power and ingrained potential (and limits) of corruption that he gives to those under him. Its not like there is any democracy, other hawks/oligarchs with big ego need to shut up and follow within their constraints or they are murdered or imprisoned, usually pretty visibly, sometimes with whole families. He did it in very smart way, I leave him that (plenty of KGB experience to learn from). Hitler and other dictators were proper amateurs compared to how Russia is run today. Nobody on their own has enough power to stand against him. He has a separate branch of paramilitary forces (on top of Wagner, GRU and KGB forces) that answer only to him and is there only to protect him. What it would require is massive conspiracy and cooperation of literally all of his underlings to topple him down, which is unrealistic to ever happen. Lone shooter/poison deliverer from his closest circle may have the best chance, but I presume he has multiple safeguards there too. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32210702,"author":"palmetieri2000","month":"2022-07","story":"A chess robot in Moscow has broken the finger of its human opponent","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture on Russian corruption hierarchies, Putin and propaganda by intelligence professor","quote":"He talks extensively about the corruption hierarchies in Russia as well as Putin and Propaganda, well worth a watch","text":"This videogame review from a YouTube channel called Warlockracy is actually also a really interesting perspective on at least some aspects of Russian subcultures and their prominence, I found it a surprisingly insightful social commentary wrapped up in an entertaining vid. 1. [LINK] (~45mins) The second video is more serious but also rather interesting (clips from it became very popular at the beginning of the Russian invasion into Ukraine). It is a lecture from a Finnish University Professor whom spent most of his career in Intelligence specializing in Russian relations and now teaches intelligence and security. He talks extensively about the corruption hierarchies in Russia as well as Putin and Propaganda, well worth a watch (and more recent videos have been released since found on the same channel). 2. [VIDEO] (~60mins) Operating under the assumption that the video isn't misrepresenting the cultural aspects of Russia I think we would both be surprised at how \"cartoonish\" things can be."},{"id":30538934,"author":"erddfre3423","month":"2022-03","story":"Research: ‘Ukraine war costs Russian military €20B per day’","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Finnish intelligence expert explains logic behind Russia's actions","quote":"Really awakening stuff at least for me.","text":"I recommend this video from a Finnish army intelligence expert & researcher to those trying to understand logic behind Russia's actions. It's in Finnish but English subtitles should be good enough. Really awakening stuff at least for me. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30538938,"author":"erddfre3423","month":"2022-03","story":"The implied poor Russian Army truck maintenance practices","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Finnish intelligence expert explains logic behind Russia's actions","quote":"Really awakening stuff at least for me.","text":"I recommend this video from a Finnish army intelligence expert & researcher to those trying to understand logic behind Russia's actions. It's in Finnish but English subtitles should be good enough. Really awakening stuff at least for me. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30539293,"author":"erddfre3423","month":"2022-03","story":"Psychological Operations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Finnish intelligence expert on Russian government mindset and tactics","quote":"Quite awakening stuff.","text":"I recommend people looking to understand Russian governments mindset and tactics to take look at this video by Finnish army intelligence expert & researcher. It has decent English subtitles. Quite awakening stuff. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30555783,"author":"asdswe","month":"2022-03","story":"Fire extinguished at Ukraine nuclear power plant, Europe's largest","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture helps understand deeper roots of Russia's actions","quote":"it really helps you to understand the situation much better","text":"I highly recommend this lecture by Finnish army intelligence expert to those looking to understand the deeper roots behind Russia's actions. Lecture in Finnish, but it has English subtitles. Truly wakening stuff, it really helps you to understand the situation much better. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30570361,"author":"kr433223","month":"2022-03","story":"Putin says Western sanctions are akin to declaration of war","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth look at how history shaped Russian culture and thinking","quote":"It was very enlightening at least to me","text":"I highly recommend this lecture by a Finnish military intelligence expert for anybody looking to understand Russia's actions better. It was very enlightening at least to me, goes in depth to how Russia's history has shaped its culture and thinking in bad ways. English subtitles available. [VIDEO] Based on the lecture it's evident that the Russian government only respects force. Soft measures will only encourage them."},{"id":30647482,"author":"mikkom","month":"2022-03","story":"Inside Putin's Circle","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Straight talk clarifying the Russian mindset","quote":"It's very straight talk and clarified russian mindset a lot for me at least.","text":"Evaluation of Russia by Finnish Intelligence Colonel (subtitles) [VIDEO] I think this is something you should watch if you are interested in how russia works. It's very straight talk and clarified russian mindset a lot for me at least."},{"id":30658878,"author":"waihtis","month":"2022-03","story":"Finnish govt agency warns of unusual aircraft GPS interference","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows Russia as a hierarchy of terror and lies","quote":"I have been spamming this lecture everywhere since the war started","text":"Suggesting Russians are playing a rational game here? I think the cover has been pulled on the Russian \"master strategy\".. it's literally a hierarchy of terror and lies, where everyone will do whatever it takes to please the irrational autocrat. I have been spamming this lecture everywhere since the war started and again I think anyone who's still questioning the Russian operative reality should watch it: Evaluation of Russia by Finnish Intelligence Colonel (subtitles) | December 3, 2018 [VIDEO]"},{"id":39122397,"author":"lcnPylGDnU4H9OF","month":"2024-01","story":"An illustrated guide to post-Orwellian censorship (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on Russian history and culture, including attitudes toward truth","quote":"time-stamped at the relevant portion but the whole talk is good","text":"> a shameless willingness to disseminate partial truths or outright fictions I remember an informative presentation about Russian history and culture which seems to go along with this, in particular the bit about \"two words for the truth and three for the lie\". [VIDEO] (time-stamped at the relevant portion but the whole talk is good)"},{"id":38163238,"author":"lapphi","month":"2023-11","story":"Who's Behind the Swat USA Reshipping Service?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why crime is ingrained in Russian culture","quote":"this lecture from a finnish intelligence officer is quite informative","text":"For anyone curious as to why crime in Russia seems to be so ingrained into the culture, this lecture from a finnish intelligence officer is quite informative. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32655220,"author":"Quekid5","month":"2022-08","story":"Mikhail Gorbachev has died","stance":"rec","why":"Nuanced talk on Russia; subtitles needed","quote":"I think this talk shows that it's probably a bit more complicated than that","text":"I think this talk shows that it's probably a bit more complicated than that: [VIDEO] Unless you happen to understand Finnish, subtitles are mandatory (and very accurate AFAICT). There is a link to a dubbed version in the comments if that is preferred."},{"id":31631502,"author":"eveningcoffee","month":"2022-06","story":"What the West (Still) Gets Wrong About Russian President Vladimir Putin","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into Russia from a former Finnish intelligence colonel","quote":"Martti J. Kari (former intelligence Colonel in the Finnish Defence Forces) gives a lot of insight","text":"Quite contrary. She provides much insight into the issue and all these points are worth consideration and discussion. Western politician try to project their view of the word on the other party and fail to see the issue from other point of view. People who have more intimate knowledge about Russia, for example Finns (1), can provide much more valuable insight. Especially so Russians with even more intimate knowledge about Russia. (1) Martti J. Kari (former intelligence Colonel in the Finnish Defence Forces) gives a lot of insight [VIDEO] Complementary material from Western point of view by historian and Holocaust researcher [LINK] I recommend to read especially this [LINK] Also Kamil Galeev from Russian Republic Tatarstan provides a lot of interesting insights [LINK] Perhaps the most interesting read from him is [LINK]"},{"id":31094799,"author":"simonh","month":"2022-04","story":"War of memes: Why Z-war won't end with peace","stance":"rec","why":"Former Finnish intelligence officer's 2018 strategic analysis of Russian thinking, with subtitles","quote":"strategic analysis of Russian thinking by a former Finnish intelligence officer, from 2018","text":"Very interesting taken alongside this strategic analysis of Russian thinking by a former Finnish intelligence officer, from 2018. He's approaching the topic from a bit of a different angle, but coming to very similar conclusions. There are subtitles in multiple languages. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30698007,"author":"jnsaff2","month":"2022-03","story":"Norwegian developer job board for Ukrainian refugees","stance":"rec","why":"Explanation of Russia from neighbors who live with the threat","quote":"A little explanation from our neighbors who also have to put up with the russian threat all the time","text":"Bullshit. The country is not 140M slaves without any agency. The system is effective, but people are complicit. Why they still are, I don't know. I grew up on soviet union and we escaped the first chance we got. But them behind the border decided to accept whatever they did. A little explanation from our neighbors who also have to put up with the russian threat all the time [VIDEO]"},{"id":30727603,"author":"Moru","month":"2022-03","story":"Mariupol's devastation: AP journalists document war's toll","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the propaganda behind what people are seeing","quote":"I think you need to watch this one[0], it's an explanation of what you are seeing.","text":"I think you need to watch this one[0], it's an explanation of what you are seeing. In general, that is the propaganda doing it's job. It's similar to all countries, we all have our propaganda and our view of our situation in the world. Russias is the classical \"It's us against them! And we are surrounded by our enemies!\". I'm sure North Korea is about the same only worse. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30686158,"author":"CRConrad","month":"2022-03","story":"Finnish govt agency warns of unusual aircraft GPS interference","stance":"reference","why":"Notes subtitle translation errors in the lecture","quote":"IMO the subtitles are somewhat weak on concluding the correct wording from context.","text":"Unfortunately the translation is not quite perfect. Hilarious bit, when he talks about the opera A life for the Czar: [1] \"...for the Tsar to be saved from the gut...\" No, from a swamp, I would think. (Unless it was the Biblical Czar Jonah?) Understandable, though: The Finnish for \"swamp\" is \"suo\"; for \"gut\", \"suoli\". Due to the agglutineration(?) Finnish grammar is based on, \"a bit of gut, some entrails\" becomes \"suol-ta\", while \"from [a|the] swamp\" becomes \"suo-lta\". Both of course spelled without the hyphen I put in here for illustrative purposes, and \"suolta\" pronounced identically in both cases. But still, which would a Czar need saving from -- a swamp, or someone's gut?!? So, TL;DR: IMO the subtitles are somewhat weak on concluding the correct wording from context. Oh, and I can't access your link at all. Locked/blocked/taken down? Perhaps there's a time limit on this ghostbin thingy? [EDIT:] Changed my somewhat un-charitable \"the translation is far from perfect\" to \"not quite\": They got \"Laiska Jaakko\" (\"Lazy Jacob\"[2]) --> \"Jacob de la Gardie\" right[3], bravo! [/EDIT] ___ [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30543778,"author":"frabbit","month":"2022-03","story":"The implied poor Russian Army truck maintenance practices","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the flat out bonkers statement","text":"[VIDEO] After he has already kicked off with the dubious statement that during the Mongol occupation \"the only ways to survive were lying, corruption and violence\" he follows up with the flat out bonkers statement: \"[...] as the Mongols who had previously ruled the country merged into the leading layers, which is still visible. When looking at genetic inheritance they are pretty, pretty dark eyes for example. There are not many flax heads in Russia.\" I hope no one flags the OP. I want this post to stay up so that we can all be aware of the racist, Russophobe undercurrents to much of the criticism of Russia's violent, unjustifiable aggression against the people of Ukraine."},{"id":30544604,"author":"frabbit","month":"2022-03","story":"The implied poor Russian Army truck maintenance practices","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The more I watch this the more I distrust the presenter.","text":"The more I watch this the more I distrust the presenter. Lines like¹ \"'A little warfare in the border areas is needed to maintain a patriotic spirit.' This was said in the 1800s century, bu this could have been said in the 2010s or 2018\" is wholly unconvincing. It appears that the presenter is projecting his racist animus, inserting words into the mouths of his perceived adversaries and generally carrying on in a non-intellectual, unintelligent manner which seeks not to understand, but to caricature. Again, what on earth is the academic institution allowing this drivel to be associated with their name? Why does YouTube take down other racist, hate speech videos while leaving this one up? (I am completely against taking any videos or posts down for any reason, but the selective nature of their actions suggests that some racism and misinformation is deemed not just acceptable to them, but even palatable.) 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30545307,"author":"frabbit","month":"2022-03","story":"The implied poor Russian Army truck maintenance practices","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Basically a hodgepodge of whatever he wants to believe, all cherry picked","text":"This video is the gift that keeps on giving¹. \"The notion of lie and truth works differently in Russia. Examples: The Terijoki government (Russian attempt to make puppet government to Finland in 1939) had a story that the working population of Finland was tired of the Mannerheim-Tanner (Marshal of the Finnish Defence Forces in the 1939) fascist junta and formed a government to liberate the Finnish people then when the Russians attacked they found out it wasn't quite the same as what the propaganda [had said].\" What is he insinuating here? This is in a section which does a \"fifty words for snow in eskimo\" attempt to claim that Russians are linguistically programmed to be liars. He has just discussed a picture which shows the \"technological backwardness of Russia\" in which the saviour is the German steamship coming up the the river in the far distance. Is he suggesting that all Finns were favour of the collaboration with the NAZIs². This is really weird. Then he goes on to mix in the Russian poisoning of Skripal, some probably true information about Russian forces operating in Donbass and Crimea with some never-before-heard conspiracy theory about all the people on MH17 being dead already and the hotly disputed DNC and Clinton were hacked by the GRU. Basically a hodgepodge of whatever he wants to believe, all cherry picked with the intent of his stated aims of showing that the Russian leadership are genetic Mongols who have linguistically programmed their boyar and working clas"}],"r":[["bJczLlwp-d8",2],["UKxQTvqcpSg",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["cIQ36Kt7UVg",1],["o1dgrvlWML4",1],["viJt_DXTfwA",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["DleXA5ADG78",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["AKGrmY8OSHM",1]]},"Mfsnlbd-4xQ":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as a delightful talk that makes music theory click and introduces Clojure and Overtone.","q":{"x":"Recommend this talk that made Music 'click' for me.","i":17121473,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":37002003,"author":"1MachineElf","month":"2023-08","story":"Overtone – programmable, live music in Clojure","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Overtone presentation engaging even to a non-programmer with music background","quote":"Recently I watched a great presentation[0] about Overtone by Chris Ford","text":"Recently I watched a great presentation[0] about Overtone by Chris Ford, delivered back in 2012. It was also the first time I saw my SO interested in a programming language talk - she isn't familiar with programming, but she does have a music background. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17121473,"author":"zphds","month":"2018-05","story":"Live Coding in Sporth: A Stack-Based Language for Audio Synthesis [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made music theory click via functional composition","quote":"Recommend this talk that made Music 'click' for me.","text":"Here's another similar library for Clojure. [LINK] Recommend this talk that made Music 'click' for me. Also fun if you are trying to read GEB and generate cannons. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9502132,"author":"kremlin","month":"2015-05","story":"Creative computing with Clojure","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Using Clojure to abstract and live-code music","quote":"Great, great talk linked to in the article, about using Clojure to abstract and live-code music.","text":"[VIDEO] Great, great talk linked to in the article, about using Clojure to abstract and live-code music. It actually helped me to more appreciate a funny episode in Star Trek: Next Generation where the hologram doctor becomes an esteemed musician among a species that previously didn't have music, because they saw it as an amazing mathematical feat."},{"id":6117566,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2013-07","story":"The Haskell School of Music (2012) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Builds a full theme from layered simple waves in Clojure","quote":"one talk about music and programming that I enjoyed a lot is 'functional composition' by chris ford, using clojure.","text":"I didn't read the haskell paper yet, but one talk about music and programming that I enjoyed a lot is 'functional composition' by chris ford, using clojure. [VIDEO] It simply groups and layers simple waves until he can describe a full theme. In case you didn't saw it and have 30 minutes of free time."},{"id":24445629,"author":"yayitswei","month":"2020-09","story":"The sound of Erlang: How to use Erlang as an instrument","stance":"rec","why":"Builds a Bach canon from pure sine waves with functional composition","quote":"Using functional composition to build a Bach canon out of pure sine waves.","text":"Reminds me of this Clojure talk- [VIDEO] Using functional composition to build a Bach canon out of pure sine waves."},{"id":7449285,"author":"grayrest","month":"2014-03","story":"Ask HN: Will soon have lots of time and little money – how to spend it?","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to Clojure through the Overtone music library","quote":"Learn Clojure via Overtone!","text":"Learn Clojure via Overtone! [VIDEO] I personally have to either wake up early, wake up and exercise, or start working outside my apartment (e.g. eat lunch out and take my computer) in order to not fall into slacking off all day. Assuming I don't fall into one of my slacking off patterns (e.g HN/Reddit/Twitter tech \"news\" all day), I've never had trouble staying engaged. I think of it as working every day but sometimes I get paid for working on other people's projects and others I get to enjoy working on my own. My personal priority list of things to do puts sleep and waking up without an alarm as the top priority."}],"r":[["lpu-3UF_b48",1],["5bQhGS8V6dQ",1],["2-oBNh0UkQc",1],["sCZJblyT_XM",1],["NVEgyJCTee4",1],["vuIlsN32WaE",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["1AjhFZVfB9c",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1],["m3svKOdZijA",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["j4kI2h3iotA",1]]},"zNVQfWC_evg":{"wy":"Called a favorite and a masterpiece by commenters as the clearest accessible explanation that there are only waves in fields, and what physics still doesn't know.","q":{"x":"is a masterpiece. Some very interesting facts, mostly about what we don't know.","i":43764552,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43764552,"author":"jaggs","month":"2025-04","story":"David Tong Lectures on Theoretical Physics","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture on quantum physics and what remains unknown.","quote":"is a masterpiece. Some very interesting facts, mostly about what we don't know.","text":"His quantum mechanics lecture on YouTube at the Royal Society I think, is a masterpiece. Some very interesting facts, mostly about what we don't know. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42630079,"author":"thekevan","month":"2025-01","story":"A video tour of the Standard Model (2021)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long talk explaining the Standard Model.","quote":"my mind immediately goes to my favorite YT video, which is Dr Tong's talk at the Royal Institution.","text":"When I see \"explanation of the Standard Model\", my mind immediately goes to my favorite YT video, which is Dr Tong's talk at the Royal Institution. I started watching this video and it took me a second to recognize the voice, it's him as well! If you want a long version: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32458914,"author":"Eupraxias","month":"2022-08","story":"What is quantum field theory and why is it incomplete?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tong's talk on quantum fields as the real building blocks.","quote":"David Tong's excellent video here","text":"I would love to hear any critical take on David Tong's excellent video here: [VIDEO] \"Quantum Fields: The Real Building Blocks of the Universe\" I'm interested to talk to others who want to think about QFT past the maths. I had my own stab at making sense of related ontological implications here: [LINK]"},{"id":34157386,"author":"Eupraxias","month":"2022-12","story":"The Zen Anti-Interpretation of Quantum Mechanics (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains electrons as waves of an underlying field.","quote":"All the electrons that are in your body are not fundamental. All the electrons that exist in your body are waves of the same underlying field.","text":"All of this makes more sense when understood in terms of Field theory: David Tong: Quantum Fields [VIDEO] \"All the electrons that are in your body are not fundamental. All the electrons that exist in your body are waves of the same underlying field.\" The next question is, if there are no individual things, what does math count? [LINK]"},{"id":32139016,"author":"Agamus","month":"2022-07","story":"Why Read Fichte Today?","stance":"rec","why":"Tong lecture arguing there are no particles, only waves.","quote":"Check out David Tang's lecture on QFT: \"there are no particles, only waves","text":"Agreed - mathematics is predicated on the idea that there are individual things, but as we are learning, through things like quantum field theory, there are no individual things, hence, no individuated 'things' to count. As such, mathematics is not the 'language of nature'. Check out David Tang's lecture on QFT: \"there are no particles, only waves\" [VIDEO] Mathematics still works (at our scale); the consequence is precision. Individuation of anything is a construct - including subjects and objects: [LINK]"},{"id":21845285,"author":"TheFiend7","month":"2019-12","story":"What Makes the Hardest Equations in Physics So Difficult? (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Royal Institution talk on fundamental building blocks of nature.","quote":"made by the Royal Institution (which I really enjoy watching), discussing the possibility of different fundamental building blocks of nature.","text":"Honestly I feel as though that's one of the most incredible aspects of theoretical physics I've seen. The fact that often times these underlying physics are danced around and even though they're unknown, we can tell something is not quite right. I believe this guy even talks about it a bit in this video made by the Royal Institution (which I really enjoy watching), discussing the possibility of different fundamental building blocks of nature. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32343650,"author":"Agamus","month":"2022-08","story":"We Need to Study Nothing","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Do any experts know if there is any overlap in this research with the study of quantum fluctuations in empty space? [VIDEO] (David Tong: Quantum Field Theory)"},{"id":31751788,"author":"Agamus","month":"2022-06","story":"Ten years after the Higgs, physicists face the nightmare of finding nothing else","stance":"reference","why":"Quantum field theory suggests there are no individual things.","quote":"If there are no individual things, as quantum field theory seems to suggest, what are numbers counting?","text":"I think this is correct. My sense is that the assumption of _individuation_ is at the core of logic, which then infects all rational thinking. (We add the predicates to \"things\", and then forget that we added them!) If there are no individual things, as quantum field theory seems to suggest, what are numbers counting? [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30031707,"author":"ianai","month":"2022-01","story":"Any single galaxy reveals the composition of an entire universe","stance":"reference","why":"Tong clip hinting at holographic relationships in the universe.","quote":"","text":"The cosmological background radiation, which is an image of the universe shortly after the big bang and before inflation, looks similar to the current layout of matter across the universe. This would seem consistent with a universe being able to be extrapolated from a region of it if there's some underlying holographic relationships. Based on this clip from David Tong. Starts here and goes on a few minutes: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kgCUn4fQTsc",1]]},"yNzQ_sLGIuA":{"wy":"Pointed to in threads about cancer screening, PSA tests, full-body MRIs and consumer diagnostics as an explanation of why more testing can hurt.","q":{"x":"This video does a good job also of covering the topic of over-testing/over-treating potential cancer","i":29098650,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":47019314,"author":"jamesbelchamber","month":"2026-02","story":"How often do full-body MRIs find cancer?","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on why screening catches more cancer without saving more people.","quote":"Rohin Francis does a good video on it","text":"It does, doesn't it! This is basically the reason scepticism in screening has risen (amongst scientists and medics, not the general population) - research seems to show that screening catches much more cancer but doesn't save many more people. Rohin Francis does a good video on it, which you don't have to watch because it has references underneath you can click straight through to (the video is good though): [VIDEO] I am frustrated by this because it seems obvious to me that \"more data == better\" but I guess it makes sense if you think of the scans as having high amounts of noise, and us having a poor understanding of the system we're monitoring (this never happens in tech, of course :))."},{"id":32717690,"author":"fooblaster","month":"2022-09","story":"Shocking hospital mistakes led to our daughter’s death","stance":"rec","why":"Deeper overview of negative consequences of overtesting.","quote":"A deeper overview","text":"I'd encourage you to think about the consequences of over testing. Although it is somewhat counter intuitive, there are many negative consequences to patient outcomes from overtesting. A deeper overview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29710341,"author":"eru","month":"2021-12","story":"The FDA's Restrictive Regulation of Home-Testing Devices (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows early detection is not straightforward; US versus UK prostate survival.","quote":"Compare and contrast this video from 'Medlife Crisis'","text":"Compare and contrast this video from 'Medlife Crisis': [VIDEO] > Trying to detect disease early seems like an obviously good thing to do. But reality isn't always quite so straightforward, especially in medicine. If It Ducks Like A Quack is a series where I try to go beyond the hype and explore how complicated the apparently simple phenomena in medicine are. One point from the video: five year survival rates of prostate cancer in the US are massively better than in the UK. However, roughly the same proportion of people die of prostate cancer in both countries. The US just screens a lot more."},{"id":29098650,"author":"Someone1234","month":"2021-11","story":"The paradox of the Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA) test","stance":"rec","why":"Covers over-testing and over-treating potential cancer.","quote":"This video does a good job also of covering the topic of over-testing/over-treating potential cancer","text":"This video does a good job also of covering the topic of over-testing/over-treating potential cancer (or creating \"Fake Disease\" as it terms it): [VIDEO]"},{"id":27060332,"author":"ejolto","month":"2021-05","story":"Are we screening too much for skin cancer? It's complicated","stance":"rec","why":"Covers dangers of over-screening for breast cancer and mastectomy side effects.","quote":"This one is even more relevant as he talks about dangers of over screening for beast cancer","text":"This one is even more relevant as he talks about dangers of over screening for beast cancer, and side effects of mastectomy+chemo: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23882125,"author":"capn_cabbage","month":"2020-07","story":"I lost my boyfriend to cancer 'conspiracy theories’","stance":"rec","why":"Informative on dangers of overdiagnosis, though poor fit for thread.","quote":"I still believe there are good bits of info provided in the video","text":"The opposite can be just as dangerous * . \"Medlife Crisis\" has an [informative video][1] that explains some of what can happen. * Edit: I shouldn't have equated the dangers here. This was a bad take. I still believe there are good bits of info provided in the video, but the relevance to this thread is less than I considered and I should have thought more about what I was sharing. Edit 2: I didn't mean to link to a specific timestamp of the video in the original link. That has been removed. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22420130,"author":"WillDaSilva","month":"2020-02","story":"Walmart Gets Into Health Care","stance":"rec","why":"Explains issues with letting anyone order any diagnostics.","quote":"this Medlife Crisis video that came out today seems appropriate","text":">Are there legitimate safety reasons these mundane diagnostics are so locked down? Healthcare is not my area of expertise either, but my understanding is that the short answer to your question is yes. For a longer introduction to the issues surrounding letting anyone get whatever diagnostics they want to pay for, this Medlife Crisis video that came out today seems appropriate: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37298062,"author":"rdedev","month":"2023-08","story":"Most cancer screenings don’t extend life, study finds","stance":"reference","why":"Screening not benign; positives can lead to unnecessary interventions.","quote":"screening is not completely benign and a positive screen might lead to more interventions","text":"I think the consensus on cancer screening is stil not fully there? Came across this video by a cardiologist a long time ago [VIDEO] I guess the main gist is that screening is not completely benign and a positive screen might lead to more interventions for what in the end could just be a benign tumor. Then there's the other point of detecting it late in life. Like treatment for cancer might not make much sense if you are already 89 years old"},{"id":31983645,"author":"i_cannot_hack","month":"2022-07","story":"Vets today are like doctors yesterday","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to show aggressive screening can hurt patients.","quote":"being overly aggressive with screening can really hurt patients.","text":"Are you implying that more active screening would cause more good than harm, and that the scientific evidence that shows otherwise is mistaken? What makes you think the evidence is wrong? Screening is a very complicated topic and being overly aggressive with screening can really hurt patients. [VIDEO] Or do you simply not support using scientific evidence and/or harm reduction to guide medical policy? What do you propose should be used instead in that case?"}],"r":[["2FMBSblpcrc",1],["7wC42HgLA4k",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["0f9GpMWdvWI",1],["udPY5rQVoW0",1],["JTfhYyTuT44",1]]},"vvZA9n3e5pc":{"wy":"HN points to it as the founders' own explanation of what Oxide is building and why, a frequent link whenever Oxide comes up.","q":{"x":"The talk lays out a history of servers, describes the problems with the servers that you can buy from vendors today","i":23979416,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":28989011,"author":"panick21_","month":"2021-10","story":"Kerla: Monolithic kernel in Rust, aiming for Linux ABI compatibility","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on what Oxide does and why","quote":"This is a great talk about what they do and why","text":"I think its an FPGA with a costume Open-Source Titan chip on it (RISC-V). It is not really a traditional BMC, its more like a service processor that does secure boot and gets you in the OS. It does a few other things but I think they really want it to have minimum functionality. This is a great talk about what they do and why: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26352398,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2021-03","story":"Compensation as a Reflection of Values","stance":"rec","why":"Talk covers what Oxide is doing, though dated","quote":"for what we're doing, I would recommend the talk I gave at Stanford about a year ago.","text":"To take these in order: for what we're doing, I would recommend the talk I gave at Stanford about a year ago.[0] It's a bit dated (and of course we have much much more detail now), but it remains broadly on point. For the parts of Sun that we want to take after: the good bits, of course! ;) There were things that have influenced us with respect to mission, and then also with respect to hardware/software co-design (we are, contrary to the impression you might have, doing a lot of hardware[1]). Lest I sound too rosy about Sun, it had a lot of problems too[2] -- so we're trying not to recreate those! ;) Thank you for the kind words on the podcast -- very much looking forward to getting back in the garage and recording some new episodes! Finally, on Spring: yes, I need to find it (we just moved) and then find a CD-ROM drive (feels easier) and then I'll get it uploaded! [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":24748283,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2020-10","story":"Rust After the Honeymoon","stance":"rec","why":"More detail on what Oxide is doing and why","quote":"more detail on what we're doing and why can be found in a (pre-COVID) talk I gave at Stanford","text":"Sorry if that's a bit thin; more detail on what we're doing and why can be found in a (pre-COVID) talk I gave at Stanford[1]. We're definitely not a Deep Stealth company, but we've also been very busy building; expect many more details over the coming year! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24266505,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2020-08","story":"Jensen Huang’s vision for data center dominance may destroy the Arm ecosystem","stance":"rec","why":"More detail on Oxide's approach, educational","quote":"you will find many aspects of the talk educational","text":"For whatever it's worth, the talk I gave at Stanford in February[1] goes into (much) more detail -- and given some of your other comments here, you will find many aspects of the talk educational. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23979416,"author":"steveklabnik","month":"2020-07","story":"The Soul of a New Machine: Rethinking the Computer [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Talk lays out history of servers and why better ones are possible","quote":"The talk lays out a history of servers, describes the problems with the servers that you can buy from vendors today","text":"The sibling comment is good. The problem that we're trying to solve is basically laid out on this slide: [VIDEO] The business is \"we will be selling servers.\" You can't buy any yet, but in the future, you'll be able to. The talk lays out a history of servers, describes the problems with the servers that you can buy from vendors today, and lays out why we think we can build better ones."},{"id":23528104,"author":"steveklabnik","month":"2020-06","story":"Today is my first day at Oxide Computer Company","stance":"rec","why":"Longer description of Oxide's goals from a founder","quote":"if you want a longer description, straight from the mouth of one of the founders, you may like","text":"So, I was trying to keep this post short and to the point, but if you want a longer description, straight from the mouth of one of the founders, you may like [VIDEO] > Are they trying to offer folks AWS or Azure quality servers for purchase? I think \"quality\" doesn't really capture 100% of it, but in a sense, yes. The goal is extremely quality, performant, robust servers. I will have to delegate the details to the above talk; I have a bunch of work to get started on, and would just end up re-typing out what it says above, ha!"},{"id":43966543,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2025-05","story":"RIP Usenix ATC","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker's own talk at EE380","quote":"I had honestly never heard of EE380 when they asked me to present[0]","text":"Oh no! I had honestly never heard of EE380 when they asked me to present[0], but I really appreciated the fact that it was (either implicitly or explicitly?) open to the public. I wish I had heard of it earlier, and sorry to see it go! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32057704,"author":"panick21_","month":"2022-07","story":"Oxide builds servers as they should be [audio]","stance":"reference","why":"Theory for why Oxide exists","quote":"That is at least the theory, see","text":"This company was created because somebody that actually built a large could with Dell/Supermicro suffered years and years of pain. If you do as you suggest you will have to do a whole lot more, at the minimum you need to set-up (and pay) VMWare. Then you will need to figure out how you do automated firmware upgrades, low level monitoring and if security is a concern you likely want to configure secure booting with attestation and things like that. Consider how much work it is to do what you suggest, vs buying this rack. And then consider how good (and repeatable) the end result is. That is at least the theory, see: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31031247,"author":"zerd","month":"2022-04","story":"What Flips Your Bit: Cosmic Ray Errors at Mozilla","stance":"reference","why":"Firmware hid corrected ECC errors until uncorrectable ones","quote":"Turns out that the firmware was hiding the fact that it had been correcting ECC errors the whole time.","text":"Bryan Cantrill mentions in this talk that they saw no correctable errors, until they suddenly got uncorrectable errors. Turns out that the firmware was hiding the fact that it had been correcting ECC errors the whole time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28858063,"author":"sanderjd","month":"2021-10","story":"So You Want to Rust the Linux Kernel?","stance":"reference","why":"Anecdote about btree library beating hand-written binary tree","quote":"Coincidentally I was just watching this talk from Bryan Cantrill","text":"Coincidentally I was just watching this talk from Bryan Cantrill: [VIDEO] . Around the 58 minute mark he shares an anecdote that is relevant to this thread. It boils down to him doing something where he would have written a simple bespoke balanced binary tree in C, but when writing a reimplementation in Rust used a \"naive\" library approach and was surprised to see a 35% speedup, which boiled down to the library using a btree instead of a balanced binary tree. This doesn't invalidate your point about there being use cases for binary trees that may necessitate you to write your own, I take you at your word that you've often had strong use cases for this, but I do think it illustrates something I believe to be true, which is that people reach for linked data structures too often, when contiguous ones are a better default."},{"id":27295695,"author":"ksec","month":"2021-05","story":"Servers as they should be – shipping early 2022","stance":"reference","why":"Found video explaining the company","quote":"Found a Youtube Video about it","text":"May be instead of asking target market or audience, who are their competitors? (Edit: Previous Discussions [LINK] ) Also thinking if the Website is not finished? All the \"Read More\" actually hide very little information, if so why hide it? And doesn't seems to explain the company very well. Seems like we need to listen to their PodCast to find out what is going on. ( Edit: Found a Youtube Video about it [VIDEO] ) >Get the most efficient power rating and network speeds of 100GBps without the pain of cable 100GBps would be impressive, 100Gbps would be ... not much? A interesting thing is all the terminal like graphics are actually HTML/CSS and not images."},{"id":24948622,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2020-10","story":"Requests for Discussion","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote citing the talk about being a computer company","quote":"We're a computer company[1]","text":"So, believe it or not, we have spent quite a bit of effort trying to make this process lightweight -- and having gone through it a bunch myself (and having a strong aversion to unnecessary process!), it doesn't actually feel unduly arduous. The reason a wiki won't work for us: it's far, far too loose for describing the things we're building and discussing the trade-offs. We're a computer company[1]: we are building a ton of hardware and low-level systems software that needs to be pretty well described and carefully considered (with revisions formally tracked). It's not aerospace or biomedical devices, but... it's also not a SaaS that is amenable to an MVP. So we do need more formalism than might be suitable elsewhere. In terms of newcomers: I actually think that's a tremendous strength of the RFDs: unlike many startups, we have a lot written down -- and newcomers (and indeed, even prospective Oxide employees) are able to read the stuff that often exists in people's heads. All of that said, it's definitely not perfect! If we could get a Google docs (or equivalent) in which the underlying artifact were stored as AsciiDoc and every modification appeared as a git commit (and the comments themselves were also tracked via a git commit), that would almost assuredly be better than the GitHub + custom infrastructure we've built. But already having outrageously broad ambition, we drew the line at not reinventing design document management... ;) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23530621,"author":"twic","month":"2020-06","story":"Today is my first day at Oxide Computer Company","stance":"reference","why":"Skimmed: operator control, no opaque binary blobs","quote":"The theme seems to be giving the operator of the computer a lot more control","text":"It seems that video has been posted to HN twice, but didn't attract any comments either time, sadly. I don't have the energy to watch a 90 minute talk, but some skipping around finds a bit more detail about what is in the plan: [VIDEO] The theme seems to be giving the operator of the computer a lot more control - eliminating opaque binary blobs, exposing the hardware more directly, etc. I can't tell if this is a particular bee in the founders' bonnets, or a real market demand, or if they know about some scary threat they can't talk about. Whilst this is definitely cool, and something that will get a lot of people on HN super pumped, it doesn't sound like something any company i have worked at would really care about. Unless these machines also deliver significantly better performance. But then, those companies were not hyperscalers."},{"id":23099674,"author":"mapgrep","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Is your company sticking to on-premise servers? Why?","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill explains offering hyperscaler-class on-prem servers","quote":"Bryan Cantrill gave a talk at Stanford on what they are trying to do","text":"Their co founder Bryan Cantrill gave a talk at Stanford on what they are trying to do, essentially offer on prem servers comparable to what “hyperscalers” like Google and Facebook put in their data centers — highly efficient and customizable (in low level software) iirc. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SKtQgFBRUvQ",1],["UGttmR2DTY8",1],["3wfNl2L0Gf8",1],["BKP1I7IocYU",1],["36myc8wQhLo",1],["79fvDDPaIoY",1],["hgN8pCMLI2U",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["4zG0uecYSMA",1],["c52QhiXsmyI",1],["EczW2IWdnOM",1],["rv-mFSoZOkE",1]]},"7G4ipM2qjfw":{"wy":"HN people link it as the go-to explainer for grid and inverter stability, especially in threads about blackouts and distributed solar.","q":{"x":"Just watch this Practical Engineering video [1], if you are not familiar with the topic.","i":43831699,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43831699,"author":"fuoqi","month":"2025-04","story":"Spain and Portugal power outage: what caused it, and was there a cyber-attack?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains inverter stability problems.","quote":"Just watch this Practical Engineering video [1], if you are not familiar with the topic.","text":"I haven't wrote that the government said that. But a lot of online commentators and even some news outlets immediately started to discuss \"cyberattacks\" (including the linked article) and asking \"maybe it was the Russians?\". Most people who are familiar with how electric grids operate are in agreement that high degree of renewable generation has likely made the situation worse not better. We know that right before the blackout the grid had a sudden dip in frequency, which is exactly the condition which leads to unstable work of inverters used by renewable plants. Just watch this Practical Engineering video [1], if you are not familiar with the topic. It discusses exactly the problems I wrote about. So renewables may have not been the direct reason for the blackout, but they are highly likely have helped to tip the gird behind the critical point. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44360082,"author":"fuoqi","month":"2025-06","story":"2025 Iberia Blackout Report [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Related issues with high renewable share.","quote":"Please watch this video if the last quote is gibberish to you.","text":"Temporary negative prices have been caused by the renewable generation which exceeded the grid demand at the time, which then evolved into the nasty feedback loop caused by the reaction of renewable generation to those conditions. You simply do not get such situation with traditional generation, it's the direct consequence of the intermittent nature of renewables and its high ratio in the total generation. Also, have you read after the market part? Please watch this video [VIDEO] if the last quote is gibberish to you. It discusses somewhat different issues, but the point still stands."},{"id":42633972,"author":"Lichtso","month":"2025-01","story":"Germans turn to balcony solar panels to save money","stance":"rec","why":"Why distributed generation is hard to coordinate.","quote":"","text":"Operating electrical power grids requires a very careful balance between production and consumption. Long term trends are regulated on the consumption side through pricing, short term jitter is regulated on the production side through switching power plants on and off. Traditionally, a power grid only had medium and large power plants, and they are not only connected to the power grid but also a control network. Adding many small power generators poses a problem in how you communicate and coordinate with them. \"Connecting Solar to the Grid is Harder Than You Think\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":42279269,"author":"HarryHirsch","month":"2024-11","story":"Sol-Ark manufacturer reportedly disables all Deye inverters in the US","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why load balancing is unavoidable.","quote":"Practical Engineering had a video on the subject not too long ago","text":"Practical Engineering had a video on the subject not too long ago: [VIDEO] The short answer is: it's for load balancing, it can't be avoided."},{"id":41320505,"author":"j16sdiz","month":"2024-08","story":"Australia to build the largest solar power plant","stance":"rec","why":"Explains grid integration problems of solar.","quote":"After watching \"Practical Engineering\"'s video on solar power[1]","text":"After watching \"Practical Engineering\"'s video on solar power[1], I start to think solar power plant of this scale is a bad idea -- it hate AC and just does not play nice with the rest of the power grid. A software bug can bring down the whole grid. .. and TFA is taking about expanding the grid. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8X7Ng75e5gQ",1],["7IsMeKl-Sv0",1],["nM9f0W2KD5s",1]]},"APZCfmgzoS0":{"wy":"Shared as an accessible, convincing case for intermittent fasting and its physiology.","q":{"x":"provide some compelling arguments for switching to intermittent fasting for many major systemic health benefits","i":17056940,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17056940,"author":"iglookid","month":"2018-05","story":"Things no one tells you before an Antarctic expedition (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compelling arguments for fasting versus eating less","quote":"provide some compelling arguments for switching to intermittent fasting for many major systemic health benefits","text":"The following sources provide some compelling arguments for switching to intermittent fasting for many major systemic health benefits. These videos by the channel \"What I've Learned\" are great: - Fasting vs. Eating Less: What's the Difference? [1] - Longevity & Why I now eat One Meal a Day [2] The channel [3] also has many other videos on the topic of nutrition, with similar emphasis. Also see the excellent and readable book \"The Complete Guide to Fasting: Heal Your Body Through Intermittent, Alternate-Day, and Extended Fasting\" by Dr Jason Fung and Jimmy Moore [4]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":28222599,"author":"seer","month":"2021-08","story":"What happens to your brain when you give up sugar","stance":"rec","why":"Channel explaining the biochemistry of processing different foods","quote":"What really opened me up to all of the complex biochem when processing different foods","text":"What really opened me up to all of the complex biochem when processing different foods was the “what I’ve learned” youtube channel [1]. What you describe was actually tested by the US government with the starvation experiment around ww2 and is consistent with your symptoms. Apparently when people are kept on a carb diet, but not given enough carbs, their live would suck quite a bit, they would loose muscle (but gain fat), not be able to think straight, etc. Its all fascinating stuff. I would suggest trying a full water fast for at least 2 days to see how it goes. For me its much less stressful, and after I get rid of all the carbs (24-48 hours, depending on what I was eating before) I would blissfully operate without any problems, including heavy fitness and programming tasks. And when I get to a point of too little glucose in my brain, because I had to fix a tricky bug, instead of searching for that chocolate treat, I’ll just sit for a but for 5-10 minutes, and let my body break down some of my fat to make more brain juice to operate normally. You are right though that people are different and this is a fricking complex stuff, and might not work for you, just wanted to mention that there might be more you can try. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22238190,"author":"ravenstine","month":"2020-02","story":"Ask HN: What Skills to Acquire in 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"Educational material convincing fasting is safe","quote":"here's some material I went over to educate myself and convinced me that it's perfectly safe","text":"I wouldn't say there's any one good resource. Most people preach intermittent fasting, which I've found is okay for maintenance and health but it's not nearly as beneficial nor will it lose you as much weight as fasting for days on end. But here's some material I went over to educate myself and convinced me that it's perfectly safe: Fasting vs. Eating Less: What's the Difference? (Science of Fasting) [VIDEO] Dr. Jason Fung - 'Therapeutic Fasting - Solving the Two-Compartment Problem' [LINK] Beginners Guide to Prolonged Fasting | 24-72 Hour Fasting Instructions [LINK] ^^ Not exactly a fan of Thomas DeLauer but there's some good information there. If you aren't faint of heart, check out some videos on the \"Snake Diet\" channel on YouTube. Cole is kind of a lunatic, constantly swears, and holds nothing back. He does this to be motivational. He's wrong about a few things(he thinks fasting cures everything and also thinks vaccines are bad), but when it comes to fasting properly he's pretty spot on. I just took from him what I thought made sense and left the rest behind. The best idea I got from him was the \"snake juice\", which is really just an electrolyte mixture you can make at home. It really makes all the difference. Overall, I wish there was someone who could preach this stuff without the Cole's level of insanity, but there you have it."},{"id":17874555,"author":"donpapa","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: What do you struggle with?","stance":"rec","why":"Video making the case that fasting works","quote":"I seriously hope this video can lead you to a better lifestyle.","text":"Yeah my man, I seriously don't know what to tell other than fasting works. Your metabolism isn't a something that breaks and is not fixable, and \"getting sick of fainting\" isn't a fucking excuse. If you're fainting, you're doing something wrong with your diet. You either getting too little from something, or too little. I seriously hope this video can lead you to a better lifestyle. and sorry if I sounded too harsh, lowkey it's targeted to myself as motivation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14697372,"author":"vijucat","month":"2017-07","story":"Can a Diet That Mimics Fasting Turn Back the Clock?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains fasting science including ghrelin and hunger waves","quote":"it's good to hear many of the points it raised validated by your experience","text":"I saw this video called \"Fasting vs. Eating Less: What's the Difference? (Science of Fasting)\" just yesterday, and it's good to hear many of the points it raised validated by your experience: [VIDEO] > hunger comes in waves, you'll get to a point where you feel super hungry, it'll usually pass after some time Matches what the video above talks about \"Ghrelin\" from 5:03 onwards."}],"r":[["tIuj-oMN-Fk",1],["IRTfhkiAqPw",1],["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["rhgwIhB58PA",1],["QyJZzq0v7Z4",1],["C25qzDhGLx8",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1]]},"d1EB1zsxW0k":{"wy":"Cited in energy threads for Gates's argument on why batteries and renewables alone fall short and nuclear is needed.","q":{"x":"which I highly recommend watching","i":19278318,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19278318,"author":"acidburnNSA","month":"2019-03","story":"Cheap renewables won’t stop global warming (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bill Gates discusses energy systems in depth","quote":"which I highly recommend watching","text":"This contains a link to the actual discussion [1] which I highly recommend watching. Bill demonstrates a sophisticated understanding of energy systems, guided by one of his favorite authors (Vaclav Smil) who recently wrote the incredible and humbling book, Energy and Civilization [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23579265,"author":"aksss","month":"2020-06","story":"Britain has gone two months without burning coal to generate power","stance":"rec","why":"Quick digestible Gates talk on nuclear","quote":"For a quick digestible bit of Gates on nuclear, watch the following.","text":"The Gates Foundation is one of the leading US investors in new nuclear tech right now. That documentary gets into the details of what they're working on and the hurdles they have in bringing new tech online. For a quick digestible bit of Gates on nuclear, watch the following. The documentary explores a lot more about the reactors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21168124,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-10","story":"Traveling Wave Reactor","stance":"rec","why":"Gates responds on battery cost","quote":"watch Bill's response to this","text":"I didn't set up that strawman. They're too expensive for a lot more than that. Seriously, watch Bill's response to this and I'd be interested if you or anyone has a better answer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20867918,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-09","story":"Why California May Go Nuclear","stance":"rec","why":"Gates does the math on batteries and reliability","quote":"TLDW: Batteries are several orders of magnitude too expensive to solve the reliability problem or make things like steel and concrete.","text":"Here, Bill Gates has done the math: [VIDEO] TLDW: Batteries are several orders of magnitude too expensive to solve the reliability problem or make things like steel and concrete."},{"id":19564865,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-04","story":"The Third Phase of Clean Energy Will Be Most Disruptive Yet","stance":"rec","why":"Gates argues solar and wind alone are not enough","quote":"Bill Gates argues a good case on why solar & wind are not enough","text":"Solar & wind are great, but no mention of nuclear? (look into Gen IV if concerned about nuclear's reputation) That's looking like what we need most of all if we want to seriously tackle the problem. If you're wondering why, Bill Gates argues a good case on why solar & wind are not enough: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23579178,"author":"aksss","month":"2020-06","story":"536 was ‘the worst year to be alive’ (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Many developed nations are reducing nuclear energy dependency as a matter of policy and preventing new reactors as a matter of regulation so dereg is a barrier that needs to be addressed for nuclear to become an alternative source in the highest carbon-impacting nations. The key parameters in a nuclear build are: 1) the construction time (impacted by regulatory regimes) 2) the cost of capital (interest rate of tying up money) 3) an uncertainty factor about getting shut down (impacted by regulatory regimes) In China, 1&2 are 3-4 years and 2% respectively. In the US they are 8-9 years, 15% and maybe 50% chance you'll be shut down before you can finish. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21166680,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-10","story":"Traveling Wave Reactor","stance":"reference","why":"Gates makes the case for nuclear","quote":"He's talked repeatedly about why nuclear","text":"It's shocking to me that the best solution we have for climate change gets downvoted on a site that's generally pro-science. But it seems to happen with every discussion about nuclear energy. Also surprising how even Bill Gates gets ignored on this issue. He's talked repeatedly about why nuclear[1][2][3], yet doesn't get a lot of traction. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] 3. [LINK]"},{"id":21371505,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-10","story":"Duck Curve","stance":"reference","why":"Gates says storage is orders of magnitude too costly","quote":"As Bill Gates and others who've done the math have repeatedly said[1], there is no storage solution anywhere on the horizon.","text":"Or we could just stop kidding ourselves. As Bill Gates and others who've done the math have repeatedly said[1], there is no storage solution anywhere on the horizon. Batteries are orders of magnitude too expensive. You have to consider the problem holistically. Steel and concrete production. Places like Tokyo that go days without power. Countries like India that are not going to pay a premium. If you're concerned about climate change, please take a serious look at nuclear energy[2][3][4]. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK]"},{"id":21085095,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-09","story":"How climate change is melting, drying and flooding Earth – in pictures","stance":"reference","why":"Gates on limits of wind and solar","quote":"Nuclear offers a solution and is deployable today.","text":"Unfortunately wind/solar aren't a complete solution[1]. Batteries are several orders of magnitude too expensive to solve for the Tokyo problem (days without power) and producing things like steel and concrete. Nuclear offers a solution and is deployable today. And yeah, really should've been done a long time ago. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20814214,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-08","story":"How do we cut down on emissions from steel, cement, and plastic?","stance":"reference","why":"Gates on battery cost versus needs","quote":"Bill Gates explains that batteries are still orders of magnitude too expensive to be a complete solution","text":"Bill Gates explains that batteries are still orders of magnitude too expensive to be a complete solution: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19716890,"author":"acidburnNSA","month":"2019-04","story":"U.S. Nuclear Power Plants Weren’t Built for Climate Change","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Phwew if only there was a reasoned thing to read on this topic. People on many sides of these issues are saying a lot very strongly that is in strong conflict, so it's not a great time to find something totally trustworthy. Most people agree with the consensus that nuclear and some renewables (wind/solar/hydro) are low carbon. Other renewables (biomass) are not low carbon. Then we quickly descend into a debate that effectively comes down to energy storage for the intermittent renewables vs. the cost of nuclear. Wind is at full power ~35% of the time in the US, solar is at full power ~25% of the time in the US. Recent nuclear builds in the US and Europe have been absolute boondoggles, though S. Korea remembers what the learned from Combustion Engineering and can build standard reactors very well now. Energy storage discussions span daily fluctuations (e.g. the duck curve) and batteries to seasonal fluctuations (much more challenging for know storage tech). Nuclear discussions also inevitability dip into nuclear waste (which has a scientific consensus solution in the deep geologic repository) to safety (where more people die every day from coal as-normal emissions than have been killed by nuclear accidents ever). Renewable discussions dip into land use, San Bernardino Co's recent ban on new large solar in the desert, migratory Hoary bats getting their lungs ripped out by wind turbines, and so on. I consider nuclear a total underdog right now because Wind and Solar are kicking b"},{"id":19280921,"author":"acidburnNSA","month":"2019-03","story":"Cheap renewables won’t stop global warming (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Gates on cost and scale of carbon capture","quote":"He says he's the biggest funder of various carbon capture technologies","text":"Bill mentions it at the 15 minute mark [1]. He says he's the biggest funder of various carbon capture technologies, liquids, solids, you name it. He then explains that it's 2 orders of magnitude non-economic and will have to be 10x the size of the current fossil industry to succeed at the rate people are saying is needed. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19269146,"author":"smt1","month":"2019-02","story":"New Reactor Designs for Revival of Nuclear Energy","stance":"reference","why":"Bill Gates on new reactor designs including traveling wave reactor","quote":"Bill Gates recently had an interview where he talks about new reactor designs","text":"Interesting, Bill Gates recently had an interview where he talks about new reactor designs, including the traveling wave reactor (commercialized in a prototype phase by a company he founded - TerraPower): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PhNUjg9X4g8",1],["GKYCA3UsmrU",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["OKY6BGcx37k",1],["oAHbLRjF0vo",1],["15RTC22Z2xI",1],["4Z2uzEM0ugY",1],["4Q7FTjhvZ7Y",1],["24R8JObNNQ4",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["gkj_91IJVBk",1],["Sp9H0MO-qS8",1],["Zk11vI-7czE",1]]},"ovJcsL7vyrk":{"wy":"HN commenters call it a favourite Veritasium video, praised for how a simple equation reveals surprisingly complex patterns.","q":{"x":"But this is the one I think people on HN need to watch most","i":28123217,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38007282,"author":"teaearlgraycold","month":"2023-10","story":"Why 10B snow crabs starved to death in the Bering Sea","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reveals fundamental patterns that are surprisingly complex","quote":"That’s my second favorite YouTube video.","text":"Here’s a rabbit hole to go down on that theme - [VIDEO] That’s my second favorite YouTube video. I love how it reveals some universal primitives are really complicated. The pattern in the video is as fundamental to the universe as circles are. Imagine how many other mind-boggling structures are ubiquitous but just outside our understanding."},{"id":29451727,"author":"Kaibeezy","month":"2021-12","story":"The 40th anniversary edition of Codex Seraphinianus","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Where fractals come from in 3D","quote":"hold on to your hat.","text":"Have you ever seen the Veritasium video on where fractals actually come from in 3D? If not, and since you already thought fractals were fascinating, hold on to your hat. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29504539,"author":"Kaibeezy","month":"2021-12","story":"How Wrong Is Veritasium? A Lamp and Power Line Story [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing 3D landscape under the Mandelbrot set","quote":"I’ll cut him endless slack after that mindsplosion.","text":"Did you see his one about the 3D landscape underlying the Mandelbrot set? I’ll cut him endless slack after that mindsplosion. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28123217,"author":"cgrealy","month":"2021-08","story":"The 3x+1 Problem [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Brain-breaking favourite among Veritasium videos","quote":"In terms of sheer brain-breaking... this is my favourite","text":"Absolutely. His \"gravity is not a force\" video led me through a whole range of emotions from \"this is ridiculous\" to \"actually...\" to \"holy crap, I've been wrong about this my entire life\". In terms of sheer brain-breaking... this is my favourite: [VIDEO] But this is the one I think people on HN need to watch most [LINK]"},{"id":26522501,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2021-03","story":"Misunderstanding Malthus’ mistake","stance":"rec","why":"Builds on Verhulst's model of population growth","quote":"This Veritasium video is about one small (but awesome) bit of that","text":"The original conversation happened almost 2 centuries ago I think (1830's). But sure! I think we throw away the baby with the bathwater when we say \"Malthus was wrong\". I think it's better to say \"Malthus inspired the beginning of an awesome idea\". Malthus' original idea was where he plotted population growth and food production in the same graph. When the 2 lines crossed, that's bad. That's obviously not very realistic, but actually he had the gist of it; it just needed a bit of refinement. Not much later, another Belgian by the name of Verhulst did indeed improve on Malthus' model. He came up with a formula where at first the population grows exponentially; and then later it slows down before it reaches the limit (carrying-capacity). In Verhulst's model, you don't have a catastrophic line-crossing. At that point, you basically have an actual Minimal Viable Product that you can use to predict and think about real-world population dynamics, and a whole lot more besides. Over the last two centuries people have built a lot of discoveries on top of that. This Veritasium video is about one small (but awesome) bit of that: [VIDEO] It's a good thing not everyone thought Malthus was (completely) wrong!"},{"id":24608433,"author":"pdfernhout","month":"2020-09","story":"You're enlightened – now what?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Parts of the video on resonance reminds me of this one","text":"Thanks for making that video emphasizing the idea of resonance and commercial design -- as well as social change. Parts of the video on resonance reminds me of this one: \"This equation will change how you see the world (the logistic map)\" [VIDEO] Some other parts on design remind me of videos by Charles and Ray Eames. You might want to consider adding Dialogue Mapping using IBIS to your design process if you have not already? [LINK]"}],"r":[["agZ0xISi40E",1],["vGAqYNFQdZ4",1],["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["d8RRE2rDw4k",1],["EYPapE-3FRw",1],["u6XAPnuFjJc",1],["VNvzZfsC13o",1],["3LopI4YeC4I",1],["QT0I-sdoSXU",1]]},"rvskMHn0sqQ":{"wy":"HN commenters link it to argue for cooperation and engagement over isolation, and as an accessible statement of positive-sum thinking.","q":{"x":"There is a video [0] I love that explains an argument for this well.","i":31381700,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31381700,"author":"axolotlgod","month":"2022-05","story":"Finding Van Tran, who brought female representation to games","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argument that more participants improve a field","quote":"There is a video [0] I love that explains an argument for this well.","text":"There is a video [0] I love that explains an argument for this well. The more people you have participating and iterating in a field, and more generally in life, the better that field/life ultimately becomes. Allowing more women in the video game field will ultimately make more satisfying and novel games for you and for everyone else. Modern video games are interactive art. People have emotional experiences from finishing games with stories and performances that speak to them. Part of this emotion comes from a deep understanding of the \"lived experience\" of people. There are parts of the lived experience that drastically differ for women and men, on the whole. Does that mean that men couldn't write a story that women find powerful and challenging? Of course not. But men aren't going to fully grok the little details of how society treats women, which means a lot of relevant themes relevant go unexplored if there aren't women on creative teams. Diversity of experience translates into diversity of ideas. Hell, I always thought that was a strength for FOSS, after all. --- [0]: [VIDEO] - Kurzgesagt"},{"id":29776400,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-01","story":"Bitcoin consumed 134 TWh in total during 2021","stance":"rec","why":"Kurzgesagt video on selfish altruism","quote":"I'm quite fond of the YouTube channel Kurzgesagt","text":"I'm quite fond of the YouTube channel Kurzgesagt which has deep videos that are targeted at a \"you don't need to be an expert in the subject to understand.\" There's a video on this subject - Can YOU Fix Climate Change? [LINK] You may also like A Selfish Argument for Making the World a Better Place – Egoistic Altruism [VIDEO]"},{"id":18735289,"author":"baby","month":"2018-12","story":"Huawei Could Divide the World","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for engagement over isolation of countries","quote":"There's actually a very interesting kurzgesagt video on the subject","text":"I think a lot of people (including me) want China to join the club of first world countries, not just economically, but also in terms of respect of human rights. How do you get there? By banning chinese products and heavily penalizing them? Or by inviting them to get their people richer and more educated? There's actually a very interesting kurzgesagt video on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17882090,"author":"arikr","month":"2018-08","story":"The 3 Tenets of Capitalism","stance":"rec","why":"Egoistic altruism explainer from Kurzgesagt","quote":"Egoistic altruism\" video by Kurzgesagt","text":"If you like this you may also like: - \"Egoistic altruism\" video by Kurzgesagt [VIDEO] , supported by the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation. - Stubborn Attachments by Tyler Cowen. Presents an argument for how contributing to positive sum economic growth is one of the best things you can do for humanity in the medium-to-long-run."},{"id":17023137,"author":"arikr","month":"2018-05","story":"Google Duplex: An AI System for Accomplishing Real World Tasks Over the Phone","stance":"rec","why":"Selfish argument for improving the world","quote":"See also","text":"This is amazing! I'm very excited for a future where more and more tasks can be automated, enabling humans to get higher and higher living standards with the same/fewer input resources. In short, technology is a wonderful thing that allows very low marginal costs. This is what we need to make the future a better place, given a consistent or growing population. \"Technology is miraculous because it allows us to do more with less. \" This is a perfect demonstration of that. See also: [VIDEO] \"A Selfish Argument for Making the World a Better Place – Egoistic Altruism\""},{"id":43265524,"author":"braiamp","month":"2025-03","story":"The Demoralization is just Beginning","stance":"reference","why":"Makes the non-zero-sum cooperation argument","quote":"There is a Kurzgezat video a couple of years back [1] that does understand this.","text":"> the other countries become less able to trade on good terms, and thus less able to buy US products/services There is a Kurzgezat video a couple of years back [1] that does understand this. They did the same argument that you are making here. Heck, the prisoners dilemma is about how cooperating is the most viable strategy for global benefits. The world isn't really a zero sum game. We get a tons of energy thanks to our star that is usually the thing that most closed systems desperately look for. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28870852,"author":"FredPret","month":"2021-10","story":"You Are Jeff Bezos","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among references that the system works","quote":"","text":"Fair point. In defense of my moral model, it's backed up by this idea: are things perfect? No. Is the system working? Yes, and astonishingly well. Is there a better way to get from here to there, where \"there\" is some improved state of prosperity and development? No. Human nature is what it is, and our systems must exploit our nature rather than combat it. It's all based on measurable positive outcomes, rather than some theoretical idea that being eye-wateringly rich is somehow bad - morally bad, or bad for society. That idea is rooted in the old, pre-growth, pre-industrial paradigm, when all wealth was seized by force in some way. Today, some wealth is from violence, but the overwhelming majority is new money that was created out of nothing but good old human intelligence. Here are some references backing up my idea that the system works. Here's one focusing on the most important metric, human development: [LINK] This one is from the World Bank. You have to go into the metrics and countries. There are metrics that aren't of interest here, but some are, like access to clean cooking fuels, or electricity. But it's easy to see that most of it is pointing in the right direction. [LINK] Here's a book about all the things that ordinarily fuel existential pessimism: [LINK] Here's a book about how innovation progresses exponentially, and what it means for the masses: [LINK] Here's that classic video by Hans Rosling giving us some perspective on the progress over the last century: [L"},{"id":17193215,"author":"zamalek","month":"2018-05","story":"Walmart will begin offering to subsidize college tuition for its U.S. workers","stance":"reference","why":"Source for selfish positive-sum reasoning","quote":"This is selfish positive sum reasoning[1], where doing good ironically results in more for you.","text":"> I wonder why they suddenly care? You would think it a silly idea: educating people into certifications that overqualify them and likely lead to their exit to greener pastures. There is the other end of the pipe to consider: students leaving high school would see Walmart employment as a way to afford their education - providing an everlasting stream of new employees. In addition, Walmart relies on successful innovators to stock their shelves with compelling products. This is selfish positive sum reasoning[1], where doing good ironically results in more for you. That's my best guess, at least. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["bLk1cjZ4ll0",1]]},"Pld8wTa16Jk":{"wy":"HN recommends Isaac Arthur as an entry point for far-future and thermodynamic-endgame questions.","q":{"x":"My go-to entry point for exploring those kinds of questions was Isaac Arthur.","i":21182040,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":30669915,"author":"yreg","month":"2022-03","story":"Red Dwarfs Will Be Humanity’s Last Home Before the Universe Dies","stance":"rec","why":"Series on civilizations at the end of time the commenter enjoys.","quote":"Isaac Arthur explores ideas like these in his videos / podcasts, I enjoy listening to it.","text":"Isaac Arthur explores ideas like these in his videos / podcasts, I enjoy listening to it. Actually he has a whole series on Civilizations at the End of Time.[0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23212988,"author":"cletus","month":"2020-05","story":"Timeline of the Far Future","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a list of must-see Isaac Arthur videos.","quote":"Mandatory Isaar Arthur links","text":"Mandatory Isaar Arthur links: - Civilizations at the End of Time: Iron Stars [1] - Civilizations at the End of Time: Black Hole Farming [2] - Boltzman Brains and the Anthropic Principle [3] [4] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":21182040,"author":"yourapostasy","month":"2019-10","story":"Not long ago, the center of the Milky Way exploded","stance":"rec","why":"Isaac Arthur as entry point on iron stars and far future.","quote":"My go-to entry point for exploring those kinds of questions was Isaac Arthur.","text":"My go-to entry point for exploring those kinds of questions was Isaac Arthur. He goes into the Kardashev scale [1], dying Earth [2], dying stars [3], farming black holes [4], and going past iron stars [5]. A Boltzmann Brain's description bears remarkable similarity to the Judeo-Christian concept of God before time begins, and has been fertile ground for ruminations [6], though none with any verifiable claims backing them in our current knowledge of physics [7]. Now if we could only restructure our implementation of capitalism to be pinned to advancements towards resolving physics questions to survive those kinds of timescales and challenges instead of locked down to a single planet's worth of baryonic matter and cognitive capacity... [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [VIDEO] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK]"},{"id":18346207,"author":"yourapostasy","month":"2018-10","story":"Astronomers Creep Up to the Edge of the Milky Way’s Black Hole","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on civilizations surviving the black hole epoch and Boltzmann Brains.","quote":"The really neat part of the lecture is towards the end, in his description of time winding down into thermodynamic equilibirum","text":"This brings to my mind Isaac Arthur's lecture \"Civilizations at the End of Time: Iron Stars\" [1]. He posits that a Kardashev Scale 3 civilization might use a galactic black hole to survive 10^100 entropic years of what he calls the \"Black Hole Epoch\". Hearing about this possible giant blob of plasma orbiting our galactic black hole makes me wonder if he and his team can come up with plausible explanations that the plasma is actually some construct of such a civilization, like perhaps a \"construction site generator\" as they're building their \"bunker against entropic time\" amongst the black hole. The really neat part of the lecture is towards the end, in his description of time winding down into thermodynamic equilibirum, a Boltzmann Brain fluctuating out of the equilibrium's randomness, and restarting the universe with another Big Bang. That's fascinatingly close to universe origin stories in Abrahamic religions, and echoes Hindu religious cosmological cyclicality. One aspect I didn't see touched upon is the possibility that our universe and we are simulations inside that Boltzmann Brain. And then there are the arguments that we do not want Boltzmann Brains [2]. Now if only the religious adherents in all our nations duking it out against other humans for not having the \"right\" religion could turn their cognitive surplus (poured into their religion) outwards to helping illuminate more upon these near-metaphysical physics challenges, we might actually get somewhere. After all, o"},{"id":18001129,"author":"spiralganglion","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: Favorite teachers on YouTube?","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested starting point for Isaac Arthur's fascinating space and futurism topics.","quote":"the some of the topics he explores are fascinating. I recommend starting with","text":"• Vi Hart (Math) [LINK] — I'm amazed nobody else listed her. She's my favourite math channel on YouTube (even though 3Blue1Brown is more technically informative, I just love Vi Hart's sense of style). • Isaac Arthur (Space / Futurism / Speculative Science) — [LINK] — The graphics are pretty cheeseball, but the some of the topics he explores are fascinating. I recommend starting with [VIDEO] • Aerotwist (Frontend / JS) — [LINK] — Paul Lewis works (or used to work?) on the Chrome team, and is an alum of the (good) HTTP203 podcast • LunchBox Sessions (Industrial hydraulic systems) — [LINK] — The videos are just screencasts of schematic simulations, but the presenter (Carl) has a good level of enthusiasm. (Disclosure — I contributed to these videos as an editor / software dev.)"},{"id":27753210,"author":"cletus","month":"2021-07","story":"Kepler telescope glimpses population of free-floating planets","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for staggering timeframes where a trillion years is a blink.","quote":"To be clear we're talking on truly staggering time frames here where a trillion years is a mere blink of an eye [1][2].","text":"> What's the point of interstellar colonization? Ultimately it's to claim matter, which is the ultimate limiting factor in how long a civilization lives. For example, you talk about engrossing digital worlds. Well, those worlds require energy and energy means matter. To be clear we're talking on truly staggering time frames here where a trillion years is a mere blink of an eye [1][2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14937820,"author":"jerf","month":"2017-08","story":"Can a Living Creature Be as Big as a Galaxy?","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in discussion of life on large slow scales.","quote":"","text":"Life on smaller, faster scales has been posited, but not in anywhere we can currently see. See, for instance, Dragon's Egg [1], which is a science fiction novel that very seriously tries to discuss life on the surface of a neutron star. I know it's fiction, but it's probably still one of the best treatments of the idea out there. Even if such a civilization would have zero interest in our resources we still certainly see no evidence of a star-faring galactic civilization of that type. Life on larger, slower scales rapidly hits the problem that 15 billion years turns out not to be all that long when you start dividing it by thousands or millions at a time. The problem is that if you're spread out that far, you're probably mostly running at the ambient temperature, which is very cold and much slower, so not only do you need to take 3-6ish orders of magnitude of speed off for dispersion in space, you need to take 3ish orders of magnitude off for being very cold and thus chemistry being very slow. And you may need to take another 3-6+ orders of magnitude off for waiting for your very dispersed, very cold molecules to encounter each other at all, in order to do whatever reactions are supposed to be supporting you. The problem you have there is that on that time scale, the galaxy is a very active and violent place, with supernovae popping off and galactic collisions and passages through the arms and all these stars flying about far faster than you can react to them (rather than the"}],"r":[["Qam5BkXIEhQ",2],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["zmCTmgavkrQ",1],["O5BJVO3PDeQ",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["rDPj5zI66LA",1],["E3URhJx0NSw",1],["8YUWDrLazCg",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["kCc8FmEb1nY",1],["iHzzSao6ypE",1],["WZskjLq040I",1],["o4-YnLpLgtk",1]]},"S2xHZPH5Sng":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever YouTube titles and thumbnails come up, both to defend creators and to criticize the drift toward clickbait.","q":{"x":"Derek of Veritaseum made a video laying out why \"clickbait-y\" titles like that are necessary, and I feel like it makes a pretty good case.","i":31142016,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":37638444,"author":"jerf","month":"2023-09","story":"How the Most Useless Branch of Math Could Save Your Life","stance":"rec","why":"Video preceded channel's improvement in quality","quote":"I feel like his channel has actually blossomed since then.","text":"The video in question for reference: [VIDEO] Interestingly, when I saw that I figured this channel for a goner because he was going to turn to the darkside, but quite contrary to expectations I feel like his channel has actually blossomed since then. His older videos are fine, but often just explaining concepts that you can find a lot of other explanations on, but some of his recent videos have rocked the boat even among physicists and IMHO he was ultimately proved to be correct for them. I particularly point at his videos about how nobody has measured the one-way speed of light ( [LINK] ), which was well-known among people very very deep in the field of relativity but not very well known outside of that (even amongst physicists in other fields, let alone lay people) and the electricity videos he's done in the last year ( [LINK] , [LINK] ) which attracted a whole whackload of \"debunking\" videos, which were themselves wrong (the best \"bunking\" video, if I may call it that, is probably AlphaPheonix's: [LINK] , since he actually set up the experiment, albeit on a smaller scale). They aren't all of that caliber, but the knot theory video was very good too and not something I've seen a hundred times elsewhere, and the airships video that preceded that one clearly had a high degree of research put into it beyond just the hype."},{"id":32786341,"author":"mierle","month":"2022-09","story":"The Next Chapter for Learning on YouTube","stance":"rec","why":"Creator perspective on clickbait and its purpose","quote":"His classification of clickbait into different types is also fascinating.","text":"Take a look at Veritasium's perspective on this issue [1]. Veritasium reflects on clickbait thumbnails from the content producer perspective, and balances that against the motivation behind YouTube's algorithms. His final conclusion is that cilckbait isn't all bad, and that it serves an important purpose for both viewers and content creators. His classification of clickbait into different types is also fascinating. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31142016,"author":"LordDragonfang","month":"2022-04","story":"I stopped working on black hole information loss","stance":"rec","why":"Makes a good case for why clickbait titles are necessary","quote":"Derek of Veritaseum made a video laying out why \"clickbait-y\" titles like that are necessary, and I feel like it makes a pretty good case.","text":"Derek of Veritaseum made a video laying out why \"clickbait-y\" titles like that are necessary, and I feel like it makes a pretty good case. [VIDEO] The tl;dw is that the most effective titles come right up against the edge of what an average target audience member would consider unacceptable on the axes of withheld information (to create a curiosity gap) and misleading sensationalism (to maximise the perceived importance of the topic); and you have to play the game of having effective titles because otherwise no one clicks on your video and it stops being recommended to people who would find it valuable."},{"id":29200732,"author":"timdaub","month":"2021-11","story":"Tips For Making a Popular Open Source Project in 2021","stance":"rec","why":"Optimizing thumbnails and headlines for click-through","quote":"Veritasium posted a brilliant way for optimizing thumbnails and headlines to improve click-through rate on Youtube","text":"Actually, Veritasium posted a brilliant way for optimizing thumbnails and headlines to improve click-through rate on Youtube: [VIDEO] I plead guilty and say that I've repeatedly posted the same link on HN but with different query parameters to understand its performance. But I've come to understand that users will notice this behavior and start to generally downvote. As a creator with a small audience, the only success with tapping into a high throughput feedback loop has been by painstakingly write articles and submit them one by one to HN. I've gotten much better at it already and frankly I've also noticed that engagement and \"likes\" isn't everything either. I've realized too that if you truly do what you believe in consistently, I've found that I suddenly start having \"impact\", which I think is different from engagement. How I see it: - Engagement: clicks, views, comments - Impact: The world changes, there's consequences and substantial changes as a reaction But I think you can't have one without the other. High engagement means you're a popstar. High impact means you're a politician. Ideally, I want to be able to pick my position on the gradient."},{"id":28350982,"author":"overcast","month":"2021-08","story":"Fake animal rescue videos have become a new frontier for animal abuse","stance":"rec","why":"Why creators constantly optimize titles and thumbnails","quote":"Veritasium does a great video on this.","text":"YouTube allowing creators to change Title and Thumbnails as many times as they want after a video has been uploaded, and providing realtime analytics after a change is what is driving this. They basically are constantly optimizing, until they find what sticks. The crap you describe obviously does very well. Veritasium does a great video on this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45283974,"author":"fknorangesite","month":"2025-09","story":"How to motivate yourself to do a thing you don't want to do","stance":"reference","why":"Creators forced into clickbait by the recommendation algorithm","quote":"Blame YouTube's recommendation algorithm, not the creators.","text":"It's funny you mention Veritasium in particular, since he hates the clickbait as much as anyone, and made a video specifically about it: [VIDEO] tldw You're basically forced to structure your thumbnails and titles that way if you want your videos to actually be seen. Blame YouTube's recommendation algorithm, not the creators."},{"id":41618104,"author":"Zren","month":"2024-09","story":"I Hacked My Friend's Phone to Show How Easy It Is [video]","stance":"reference","why":"YouTube title and thumbnail testing","quote":"Watch an older Veratasium video about Youtube's title+thumbnail clickbait","text":"Youtubers regularly swap the video thumbnail + title. Youtube even has tools to A/B test various thumbnails + titles nowadays for different users. I can confirm that the title \"I Hacked My Friend's Phone to Show How Easy It Is\" was the actual title when the video was uploaded. However you're right that it now looks like it changed to \"Exposing The Flaw In Our Phone System\" a day later. Watch an older Veratasium video about Youtube's title+thumbnail clickbait: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39831485,"author":"swyx","month":"2024-03","story":"Why Google failed to make GPT-3","stance":"reference","why":"Ethics of reach with professional standards","quote":"i mostly just subscribe to the veritasium school of ethics here","text":"as a content creator who mixes reach and professional standards i mostly just subscribe to the veritasium school of ethics here [VIDEO]"},{"id":36786242,"author":"gwd","month":"2023-07","story":"Why Oppenheimer Deserves His Own Movie [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Why he uses clickbait titles for educational videos","quote":"his goal is to make actually educational videos and have them seen","text":"> I would classify this as marketing and self-promoting hyperbole Or probably better, \"clickbait\". The author actually did a video about why he started using clickbait titles; basically, his goal is to make actually educational videos and have them seen, and clickbait titles means a lot more people see them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33720234,"author":"nayuki","month":"2022-11","story":"Standard Reference Materials [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Admits to changing titles to test viewer responses","quote":"Veritasium also admits to changing the title to test viewer responses","text":"The current HN post title of \"Standard Reference Materials\" is accurate and neutral. Veritasium's title as you mentioned is clickbaity. Veritasium also admits to changing the title to test viewer responses, as mentioned in [VIDEO] \"Clickbait is Unreasonably Effective\" [2021-08-17]. He also explains the problem with titling a video with a term that you would only understand after watching the video."},{"id":32582693,"author":"falcolas","month":"2022-08","story":"IFixit: Self Repair Program makes M1 MacBooks less repairable","stance":"reference","why":"Creators need to juice up titles","quote":"if you want to thrive by producing content, you need to juice up the title a little.","text":"Titles rarely do. And sadly, if you want to thrive by producing content, you need to juice up the title a little. Yeah, I hate it too. [VIDEO] But on topic, Apple's official stance, as provided by this repair manual, is that replacing a battery requires you to replace vast portions of the final product. Car analogy time: Got bad seals on your block? Send us the engine; who knows what kind of damage those bad seals have done. Oh, and only we, your car manufacturer can get you the tools and an authorized engine replacement. Can the battery be replaced without the assembly? iFixit says yes. Is this how Apple does it? Sure, but it's generating a lot of cost and waste. They can write both of. Can you?"},{"id":30989096,"author":"josephg","month":"2022-04","story":"Node.js packages don't deserve trust","stance":"reference","why":"Creator discusses title and thumbnail tradeoffs","quote":"Veritasium had a great video talking about this for his youtube thumbnails.","text":"Author here. I totally hear the criticism. I had \"Capabilities for nodejs packages\" or something like it as a draft title for the post. But its a boring title. And I believe this article would have had far less reach with a name like that. I hear you. I don't believe the answer is to just sensationalize everything to 110%. But I personally absolutely can't stand boring writing. Like, holding my attention on anything which isn't at least a bit human and engaging feels like torture. My job is 70% researcher, and one of the worst parts of my job is reading academic papers - I just can't hold my attention on a boring paper for more than a few sentences. My old supervisor used to tease me about using 2 weeks in the lab to save 5 minutes in the library. I think he's more right than he knows. So, I really don't have a good answer. What feels like sensationalist clickbait for you might seem like a catchy title to me, with a promise of engaging writing for someone else. And what sounds like an accurate title for someone else might turn me off completely because of its blandness. I suspect there's no middleground here where everyone is happy, and at the end of the day when I write its up to me and my judgement. And you, the audience to complain more loudly if you feel like like I'm taking it too far. I need that, because only have my own judgement for calibration. But thats pretty unsatisfying! Veritasium had a great video talking about this for his youtube thumbnails. I found it quite"},{"id":30557488,"author":"kgwxd","month":"2022-03","story":"We're building computers wrong [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why titles are clickbaity","quote":"there's a Veritasium video for that too","text":"And if you're curious as to why it's clickbaity, there's a Veritasium video for that too :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":30558439,"author":"CSSer","month":"2022-03","story":"We're building computers wrong [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Creator discussing more clickbait titles","quote":"","text":"Committed to, actually. He made a video about how they were going to start doing it more: [LINK] . Also, more recently: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":28723344,"author":"pininja","month":"2021-10","story":"What a solid made of electrons looks like","stance":"reference","why":"Hook new people, then teach them","quote":"do whatever it takes to hook people in that have never heard the topic before, then teach them something new.","text":"I think this is the same argument made by Veritasium - poorly summarized: do whatever it takes to hook people in that have never heard the topic before, then teach them something new. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28311613,"author":"avnigo","month":"2021-08","story":"The Clickbaitification of Netflix","stance":"reference","why":"Thumbnail and title experimentation on platforms","quote":"Veritasium talks about thumbnail and title experimentation on YouTube","text":"Veritasium talks about thumbnail and title experimentation on YouTube, and argues for the need to do so on platforms that surface up and recommend content to users: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28350929,"author":"james-skemp","month":"2021-08","story":"Fake animal rescue videos have become a new frontier for animal abuse","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on YouTube clickbait","quote":"Veritasium did a video about this recently, actually.","text":"Veritasium did a video about this recently, actually. I got the picture and didn't finish it, but it helped me realize how YouTube has pushed junk to me, despite trying to subscribe to quality channels. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38238956,"author":"matjet","month":"2023-11","story":"Google's New \"Help Me Script\" Automation","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"From \"Clickbait is bad, and I don't do it.\" to \"I am working to efficiently clickbait people.\" Reminds me of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31137219,"author":"4oo4","month":"2022-04","story":"The Man Who Accidentally Killed the Most People in History","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The video titles ironically seemed to get more clickbait-y after he released this video","text":"I suppose this one isn't too bad in that regard, I think I was mainly thinking of this video: [LINK] This video about leaded gasoline makes a solid case and the science and history are there to back up that Kettering caused the lead poisoning on this scale, but I think it's a stretch, or a gross oversimplification to say he killed the most people in history. I guess I expect slightly more scientific rigor and caution from someone like him (though, I understand that YouTube videos aren't expected to withstand the scientific method). With this video, I think it comes down to the title. Saying he killed the most people is a reach, I think saying he poisoned the most people is probably more accurate. That's not saying anything to discredit how bad lead poisoning is and that it does kill people, but if you're going to accuse someone of being a mass murderer I think you need to make your claims really precisely and have data that really clearly shows a causation and not just correlation. The video titles ironically seemed to get more clickbait-y after he released this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30560053,"author":"fsh","month":"2022-03","story":"We're building computers wrong [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"At some point he started A/B testing them to maximize his revenue.","text":"I think he is easily one of the smartest people on YouTube and is producing some of the very best videos. He is also a brilliant educator. For examples, his videos on special [1] and general relativity [2] were real eye-openers for me, even though I have studied physics and have seen this stuff explained a million times before. What I really don't like are the new video titles. At some point he started A/B testing them to maximize his revenue. He even made a video about it [3]. Of course, this selects for the most outrageous clickbait in order to get that sweet engagement. I am starting to wonder if he took into account, how much this is starting to alienate long-term followers. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"UawS3_iuHoA":{"wy":"HN points to it as the classic overview of why TLS certificate trust is flawed, and it resurfaces whenever CA or HTTPS security comes up.","q":{"x":"I've found Moxie Marlinspike's talk [1] to be a great overview of these sorts of issues.","i":34675741,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34675741,"author":"8organicbits","month":"2023-02","story":"Weakening TLS protection, South Korean style","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great overview of TLS and certificate trust issues","quote":"I've found Moxie Marlinspike's talk [1] to be a great overview of these sorts of issues.","text":"I've found Moxie Marlinspike's talk [1] to be a great overview of these sorts of issues. IIRC, he built an alternate system discussed in the talk. But I believe that's defunct. (Tangent, anyone know the postmortem on that?) [VIDEO]"},{"id":16799061,"author":"mashedvikings","month":"2018-04","story":"Mythology About Security","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moxie recounts talking with an SSL developer about early design assumptions","quote":"you should definitely watch Moxie Marlinspike's (lead dev of Signal) talk","text":"\"Often hear that the reason today’s Internet is not more secure is that the early designers failed to imagine that security could ever matter.\" Related to this, you should definitely watch Moxie Marlinspike's (lead dev of Signal) talk where he tells about his discussion with Kipp Hickman, a developer of SSL: [VIDEO] (until 16:33)"},{"id":25491919,"author":"mbakke","month":"2020-12","story":"Security expert warned SolarWinds in 2019 that anyone could access server","stance":"rec","why":"Funny account of the 2011 Comodo hack and its attribution","quote":"Moxie (of Signal fame) has a hilarious account of the story","text":"Whenever I hear statements like \"incredibly complex and sophisticated attack\" I'm reminded of the 2011 Comodo (now Sectigo) hack where the company said it had \"clinical accuracy\" and \"likely to be a [Iranian] state-driven attack\". Days later, the perpetrator released a \"manifesto\" (and several other documents) all but confirming that it was the work of a lucky amateur. Moxie (of Signal fame) has a hilarious account of the story in his 2011 Blackhat talk \"SSL and the future of authenticity\" (starts at around 5:00): [VIDEO]"},{"id":21426088,"author":"maqp","month":"2019-11","story":"Encrypted web traffic now exceeds 90%","stance":"rec","why":"Explains TLS problems beyond cryptanalysis","quote":"Moxie has good points about the problems TLS has.","text":"Moxie has good points about the problems TLS has. And they are not about breaking TLS via cryptanalysis: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48344956,"author":"cyphar","month":"2026-05","story":"Parallel Reconstruction of Lawful TLS Wiretapping","stance":"reference","why":"Moxie argues CA trust is controlled by the wrong people","quote":"Moxie Marlinspike spoke about this at length back in the early 2010s","text":"> One of Web PKI's security holes is the fact that any CA can issue valid certs for any domain. The only official \"mitigation\" for that is voluntary and can be defeated. In case you were not aware, Moxie Marlinspike spoke about this at length back in the early 2010s[1]. His view was that the problem is that certificate authority trust is controlled by the wrong people (web hosts, not users -- or browsers, as a proxy for user wishes) and is not possible to revoke because once a web host uses a particular CA you are stuck trusting them forever otherwise the internet will break. > The solution to that is to rearchitect the Web PKI ecosystem to use domain registrars as the sole source of truth for which CA is allowed to issue valid certs, in addition to cryptographic fingerprints of the source of the originator and issuer. I won't rehash it here but it's not technically difficult and would make it so only the domain owner could issue certs, and valid certs could only come from the CA the domain owner authorizes. Unfortunately, this is problematic for a bunch of other reasons. Yes, this means that a classic Comodo or DigiNotar attack might be blocked (though it is also just as likely they would've been included on the allow-list for American websites), but it also means that registrars could force you to use VeriSign and you would have no choice in the matter -- that is what originally happened with TLS and was what originally happened with DNSSEC too. It seems prudent to me to av"},{"id":45583868,"author":"cyphar","month":"2025-10","story":"Let's Not Encrypt (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Moxie's critique that distrusting big CAs is impractical","quote":"the contents of which are sadly just as true today as they were then","text":"> the CA would be immediately distrusted by browsers, not as punishment but to deter state actors This is not practically possible for browsers to do, as it would also cause all of the legitimate certificates signed by that CA to become distrusted and break large swathes of the internet. This was one of the main complaints Moxie Marlinspike had in his 2011 talk on TLS (the contents of which are sadly just as true today as they were then)[1]. In fact, there is fairly credible evidence that the NSA did actually do this already back in 2011 with the DigiNotar hack to steal the contents of Iranian emails[2]. This case was so egregious that DigiNotar did get distrusted by browsers, but other hacks like that of Comodo did not result in their CA certificates being distrusted. The CAB does apparently block CAs more aggressively than they did a decade ago, but I wonder if they would actually block a big CA like LetsEncrypt if it came out they did something shady or got hacked. It just seems incredibly unlikely they would flip the \"turn off >60% of the internet\" switch regardless of what LetsEncrypt hypothetically did (for reference, in 2011 Comodo signed only 20-25% of website certificates). [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":6733224,"author":"arantius","month":"2013-11","story":"Moving forward on improving HTTP's security","stance":"reference","why":"Talk behind an alternative to the certificate authority system","quote":"removes the certificate authority oligopoly from the picture without reducing security","text":"Recently learned of [LINK] (talk: [VIDEO] ) which sounds quite promising. It's not perfect (private, non routable sites?) but removes the certificate authority oligopoly from the picture without reducing security (AFAICT)."},{"id":25572057,"author":"1cvmask","month":"2020-12","story":"YouTube removed a Cybersecurity channel without much reason","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Will they remove Moxie videos then because he makes cybersecurity videos funny and points out the inherent failures in SSL? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["jh8supIUj6c",2]]},"j5v8D-alAKE":{"wy":"Commenters link it as the engaging go-to explainer on planned obsolescence and the lightbulb cartel.","q":{"x":"Veritasium made a video that reviews the history and presents the topic in an engaging way.","i":26945731,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":31432210,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2022-05","story":"Engineers investigating NASA’s Voyager 1 telemetry data","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers the light bulb cartel with fines for long-lasting bulbs.","quote":"Veritasium has an awesome video on planned obsolescence here.","text":"Veritasium has an awesome video on planned obsolescence here. [1] The problem in many cases isn't that it costs so much more to make products that last, but rather that making products that break is just way more profitable when you can effectively control the market to such a degree that you don't need to worry about some party pooper (1) coming in and making stuff that does genuinely last and (2) gaining significant marketshare. The example of the light bulb cartel that had unquality assurance, spot testing, and a formal structure of fines and penalties for any members who made too long of lasting lightbulbs is just hilariously dystopic. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":32373652,"author":"deltasevennine","month":"2022-08","story":"Electronics are built with death dates. Let’s not keep them a secret","stance":"rec","why":"Story of how companies colluded to shorten lightbulb life.","quote":"Worth watching if you have 15 minutes","text":"I don't think you understand the extent of what's going on. It's not that people are trying to make hardware last forever. It's that these devices are being deliberately designed to break down in a couple years. There are actual design decisions to force consumer behavior into purchasing new things every couple of years. This doesn't just apply to things that follow moores law. Almost every product in existence nowadays is literally designed to break earlier then they usually do. Companies in certain cases actually spend more money creating a design that ensures that a product will break early so that consumers will buy a new thing within some years. This includes cars, computers, phones, microwaves, lightbulbs. Etc. Your personal need to buy a new car, new phone and new clothes is the result of market manipulation over the last 10 decades or so... morphing our culture from one where we kept tools around for years into one where we need to buy new things all the time. It was not like this at least 1 or 2 generations ago. The result of this endless buying behavior is good for business and the economy but it has devastating effects on the environment and our resources. Worth watching if you have 15 minutes: [VIDEO] The actual story of how companies colluded to make lightbulbs not last as long."},{"id":32373683,"author":"deltasevennine","month":"2022-08","story":"Electronics are built with death dates. Let’s not keep them a secret","stance":"rec","why":"Short interesting video on planned obsolescence.","quote":"To learn more here's an interesting short video about it","text":"This is called planned obsolescence, and it's not just electronics. Practically every product nowadays are designed with a death date to perpetuate a repeating buying cycle from consumers. It's very real. To learn more here's an interesting short video about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31435869,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2022-05","story":"Engineers investigating NASA’s Voyager 1 telemetry data","stance":"rec","why":"Video's comments hold many industry anecdotes on planned obsolescence.","quote":"The comments for the linked video [1] are awesome because it has so many people from different areas of industry (and academia) sharing anecdotes.","text":"In modern times planned obsolescence is a normal part of business. The comments for the linked video [1] are awesome because it has so many people from different areas of industry (and academia) sharing anecdotes. So cartel? Probably not. Built to fail? Almost certainly. If we view things only through the lens of profit, then MTTF (mean time to fail) is just another curve to maximize against profit. If it's too short then your reputation will suffer and you'll lose money. If it's too long, then you're just leaving money on the table. In our society the brilliant engineer is not the one who makes a thing that last for 50 years, but one that lasts for just long enough to avoid damaging your brand image, and also fails in a way that makes the consumer more incentivized to buy a new thing than try to repair their current one. The guy who came up with the idea of slowing old iPhones \"to save battery life\" is who the bonus+promotion is going to. One of the biggest problems that has to be understood is that there is every incentive for money-driven governments to actively support planned obsolescence. Planned obsolescence drives factors that we believe are indicators of a strong economy, like GDP. If people were able to stop endlessly buying the same things over and over, the \"economy\" would take an absolutely massive hit. And with it there would also be declines in employment, tax revenue, and everything else. This is one of the many examples where the socially detrimental relation"},{"id":30661286,"author":"ComradePhil","month":"2022-03","story":"EU to make it mandatory to use customer-replaceable batteries in household items","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting video on planned obsolescence.","quote":"Veritasium video on planned obsolescence which is interesting","text":"This is a very good start. If they did the same for screens, I wouldn't be surprised if the phone sales dropped to half or even less. There is no way companies like Apple and Samsung will be OK with this. I expect big pushback from them. If these steps go through, it will be interesting to see what other planned obsolescence [1] methods will these companies go for. The one big tool Apple has is the full control of the software ecosystem. They can simply make newer apps unavailable for old phones for example. That will need to be tackled at some point. [1] Veritasium video on planned obsolescence which is interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26945731,"author":"infogulch","month":"2021-04","story":"Phoebus Cartel","stance":"rec","why":"Engaging review of the Phoebus cartel history.","quote":"Veritasium made a video that reviews the history and presents the topic in an engaging way.","text":"Veritasium made a video that reviews the history and presents the topic in an engaging way. \"This is why we can't have nice things\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":41188933,"author":"plewd","month":"2024-08","story":"East Germany invented 'unbreakable' drinking glasses","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Not exactly the most reliable supporting evidence, but Veritasium has a video which talks about this stuff.","text":"Not exactly the most reliable supporting evidence, but Veritasium has a video which talks about this stuff. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35376000,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-03","story":"There's something off about LED bulbs","stance":"reference","why":"Documents the Phoebus cartel as a real conspiracy.","quote":"This goes into the \"actual conspiracy - not a theory\" category.","text":"This goes into the \"actual conspiracy - not a theory\" category. Veritasium - This is why we can't have nice things - [VIDEO] The Great Lightbulb Conspiracy [LINK] > On 23 December 1924, a group of leading international businessmen gathered in Geneva for a meeting that would alter the world for decades to come. Present were top representatives from all the major lightbulb manufacturers, including Germany’s Osram, the Netherlands’ Philips, France’s Compagnie des Lampes, and the United States’ General Electric. As revelers hung Christmas lights elsewhere in the city, the group founded the Phoebus cartel, a supervisory body that would carve up the worldwide incandescent lightbulb market, with each national and regional zone assigned its own manufacturers and production quotas. It was the first cartel in history to enjoy a truly global reach. [LINK]"},{"id":34042877,"author":"quaintdev","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Anyone tired of everything being a subscription now?","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary-style resource on planned obsolescence.","quote":"There are nice documentaries about this","text":"This exact behavior drives planned obsolescence. This has led to so much waste over past century that it's going to be very hard to go back. I was baffled that we invented a bulb that could last 100 years over a century ago and I still have to change bulbs every year or two. There are nice documentaries about this [0] The end of ownership [LINK] [1] This is why we can't have nice things [VIDEO]"},{"id":33799475,"author":"lgl","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Are iPhones designed to last for a couple of years?","stance":"reference","why":"Video on planned obsolescence.","quote":"There's a particular video from Veritasium [1] that comes to mind regarding this.","text":"Most likely yes, as it is also done by many electronics companies. It's called \"planned obsolescence\" [0]. There's a particular video from Veritasium [1] that comes to mind regarding this. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32474043,"author":"anthropodie","month":"2022-08","story":"Android 13","stance":"reference","why":"Resource on planned obsolescence.","quote":"","text":"We need to find a way to move on, once something is perfected. Because if we don't we kinda ruin what we have already perfected. It's called planned obsolescence and below are some resources on the same. It is widely used across industries for over a century and kinda plays a huge role in climate change. Veritasium [VIDEO] DW (mostly about phone) [LINK]"},{"id":30497221,"author":"adhesive_wombat","month":"2022-02","story":"Someone should probably start a bright home lighting company (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Lightbulb lifetimes were driven down artificially via a cartel.","quote":"Lightbulbs have a long and very successful history of artificially driving lightbulb lifetimes down though a cartel system","text":"There are in fact LED bulbs that aren't overdriven to a deliberate and premature thermal death, but you can only buy them in Dubai: [LINK] Lightbulbs have a long and very successful history of artificially driving lightbulb lifetimes down though a cartel system: [VIDEO] If you're building your own LED fixtures, you can probably underdrive the LEDs and run them at a radically cooler and slightly more efficient operating point, at a higher upfront cost: you need more LEDs for the same output."},{"id":27334619,"author":"neonological","month":"2021-05","story":"Overkill objects for everyday life","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why consumer products are designed to fail.","quote":"Consumer grade products are deliberately designed to fail. This is why","text":"Consumer grade products are deliberately designed to fail. This is why: [VIDEO] If you're older, like really old, you can actually remember a time where products were of a much much higher durability before the industry figured out that low durability products were more profitable. So in actuality some of the suggestions on that site aren't necessarily overkill as industrial products aren't deliberately engineered to fail."},{"id":26778733,"author":"qart","month":"2021-04","story":"A campaign to make environmental destruction an international crime","stance":"reference","why":"History of the lightbulb cartel and planned obsolescence.","quote":"The cartels were for incandescent light bulbs [1], rather than other kinds of lighting.","text":"The cartels were for incandescent light bulbs [1], rather than other kinds of lighting. It's harder for cartels to exist now, given that we can buy alternatives from other countries on Ebay and AliExpress. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26791810,"author":"kenmacd","month":"2021-04","story":"How Government Wrecked the Gas Can","stance":"reference","why":"Shows old bulbs burned out quickly due to lack of regulation.","quote":"","text":"> Light bulbs don’t illuminate. I find this one especially funny as it's pretty much the lack of regulation that made the 'old' bulbs burn out much too quickly [1]. And before someone says \"but my LED bulbs keep burning out\", also the free-market/lack of regulation. To see how they should be built watch Clive on how bulbs are made for Dubai[2]. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK]"}],"r":[["5MxH1sfJLBQ",1],["2A2NY29iQdI",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["p8u_k2LIZyo",1],["Ecy1FBdCRbQ",1],["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1],["Y9FZ4igNxNA",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["ne99laPUxN4",1],["hTsrDpsYHrw",1],["rQmqVVmMB3k",1],["5fG_lyNuEAw",1],["8JrqH2oOTK4",1],["6bqBsHSwPgw",1],["wbSwFU6tY1c",1],["5eW6Eagr9XA",1]]},"kmFOBoy2MZ8":{"wy":"Recommended as a thorough, speculative-but-grounded overview of the magnitude of colonizing Mars.","q":{"x":"Highly recommended video on Colonizing Mars by Isaac Arthur","i":15249748,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15249748,"author":"leanthonyrn","month":"2017-09","story":"Asteroid Mining Is Our Best Hope for Colonizing Mars","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Highly recommended Isaac Arthur video on colonizing Mars.","quote":"Highly recommended video on Colonizing Mars by Isaac Arthur","text":"Highly recommended video on Colonizing Mars by Isaac Arthur [VIDEO] Equally amazing Asteriod Mining by Mr. Arthur [LINK]"},{"id":34064651,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2022-12","story":"What ever happened to the first cryogenically frozen humans?","stance":"rec","why":"Part of an Isaac Arthur playlist on near-Earth space living.","quote":"Isaac Arthur's YouTube channel is full of speculation and plans for all kinds of possible or nearly-possible futures","text":"The Sun radiates about 400 trillion trillion Watts in all directions, and only a couple of billionths of it crosses Earth. There's a lot more radiant energy spreading out into the void waiting to be Dyson Swarmed. There's no way we're going to rocket launch billions to Mars, and there isn't another planet worth living on without unreasonable amounts of energy for Terraforming. But we are capable of rebuilding the atoms of Earth into much larger living environments - there are so many atoms it wouldn't mean destroying the Earth to do it. A Space Elevator doesn't need diamond nanowire, it could be built with enough steel chain. An orbital ring around the Equator would be enormous and would take approximately nothing of the Earth's matter to construct. Isaac Arthur's YouTube channel is full of speculation and plans for all kinds of possible or nearly-possible futures, as well as more distant dreams. e.g. the playlists: - \"The future of Energy, Industry and Agriculture\" - [LINK] - \"Earth v2.0\" - [LINK] - \"Outward Bound\" - near-Earth space exploration and living - [VIDEO] - \"Visions of Earth\" - arcologies, megacities, ecumenopolises, geoengineering, weather control - [LINK] And somewhere in there, megastructures such as Dyson Spheres, ringworlds, etc."},{"id":22225132,"author":"keanzu","month":"2020-02","story":"James Lovelock: 'I Would Not Waste My Fortune on Colonizing Mars'","stance":"rec","why":"Conveys the magnitude of works needed for Mars.","quote":"Isaac Arthur does great stuff on Youtube which gives an idea of the magnitude of the works necessary.","text":"The sum total of human activity in the last 100 years also eclipses the sum total of all human activity prior (300k years worth). Humanity didn't do a lot for a long time. But yes, Mars would be a mega project. Isaac Arthur does great stuff on Youtube which gives an idea of the magnitude of the works necessary. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15039657,"author":"devrandomguy","month":"2017-08","story":"Living on Mars: The Stuff You Never Thought About","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth look at colonizing Mars.","quote":"Isaac Arthur just kicked off Outward Bound with an in depth look at colonizing Mars.","text":"Perfect timing! Isaac Arthur just kicked off Outward Bound with an in depth look at colonizing Mars. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1],["uxykI30fS54",1]]},"ojm74VGsZBU":{"wy":"HN points to it as a short, funny look at how cities stand in for other places on screen, and at the film industry's local effects.","q":{"x":"There's also a much shorter, funnier one called \"Vancouver Never Plays Itself","i":14112492,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":21186381,"author":"crummy","month":"2019-10","story":"Apple Hides Taiwan Flag in Hong Kong","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Movies shot in Vancouver rarely set there","quote":"One of my favourite Youtube channels, Every Frame A Painting","text":"One of my favourite Youtube channels, Every Frame A Painting, talks about this in Vancouver - except that the movies are rarely set in Vancouver: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32269236,"author":"mynameisvlad","month":"2022-07","story":"That time when I accidentally social engineered myself to a film set","stance":"rec","why":"Film industry's local business effects","quote":"Great Youtube video on this topic","text":"> The idea is that this would promote more use of local businesses. It does not usually work out that way though. Great Youtube video on this topic, especially since you're likely talking about Vancouver: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21099255,"author":"tobr","month":"2019-09","story":"When Filming in New York, the City Is Always the Star","stance":"rec","why":"How a city is made to appear not itself on screen","quote":"it’s still an interesting demonstration of how a city is made to appear not to be itself on screen.","text":"This made me rewatch Vancouver Never Plays Itself [1] from Every Frame a Painting. It didn’t have as many examples of Vancouver playing New York as I remembered, but it’s still an interesting demonstration of how a city is made to appear not to be itself on screen. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14112492,"author":"JBlue42","month":"2017-04","story":"Walking in L.A.","stance":"rec","why":"Shorter, funnier video on cities standing in for others","quote":"There's also a much shorter, funnier one called \"Vancouver Never Plays Itself","text":"I recommend \"Los Angeles Plays Itself\" to any interested in seeing the juxtaposition of what you see in movies/tv with the reality. There's also a much shorter, funnier one called \"Vancouver Never Plays Itself\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":49165619,"author":"PetitPrince","month":"2026-08","story":"Hollywood Stopped Making Movies in Hollywood","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Vancouver never playing itself","quote":"","text":"\"Vancouver never plays itself\" as Tony Zhao and Taylor Ramos explain in on of their later Every Frame a Painting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45874617,"author":"Ragnarork","month":"2025-11","story":"Ask HN: What Are You Working On? (Nov 2025)","stance":"reference","why":"Reminded of the video","quote":"","text":"This reminded me instantly of Every Frame a Painting's video \"Vancouver never plays itself\"[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lD9FAOPBiDk",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["GFE4rkSaKOY",1],["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["XfQUOHlAocY",1]]},"xMP_AfiNlX4":{"wy":"Recommended as a gold-standard free course for anyone learning to build precision mechanical things.","q":{"x":"series by Dan Gelbart on YouTube which is pure gold","i":29813641,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29813641,"author":"dejv","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: Resources for “Learning” Manufacturing?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gold for learning to build precision mechanical things","quote":"series by Dan Gelbart on YouTube which is pure gold","text":"There is \"Building Prototypes\" [1] series by Dan Gelbart on YouTube which is pure gold for anybody that wants to learn how building mechanical things, mostly precision/instrumentation things. Part 12 for plastic forming and casting. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28350720,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: What problem are you close to solving and how can we help?","stance":"rec","why":"Series on building prototypes","quote":"Also, see Dan Gelbart's 18 video series about building prototypes","text":"It sounds like you need to add a \"stage\" to help position your sample. Flexures are systems that bend to perform motion, and can do surprising things that you can't do with joined together machined pieces. Here's an open source stage project using flexures that will likely help [LINK] Also, see Dan Gelbart's 18 video series about building prototypes [VIDEO]"},{"id":22899519,"author":"kbr2000","month":"2020-04","story":"Using a 1930 Teletype as a Linux Terminal [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Short course on how to build stuff","quote":"another interesting 18-part series of videos about prototyping","text":"Thanks, first time I've heard of them, will be going through those (they seem to be available at archive.org [0]) soon! I'd like to point out another interesting 18-part series of videos about prototyping (in his words \"a short course on how to build stuff\" :)), by engineer/inventor/professor Dan Gelbart: [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10039279,"author":"plumeria","month":"2015-08","story":"High precision air bearing CNC lathe and grinder [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Series on building prototypes","quote":"His Youtube series about \"Building Prototypes\" looks interesting","text":"Nice, thanks for sharing this. His Youtube series about \"Building Prototypes\" looks interesting: [VIDEO] Here is a bio about Dr. Daniel Gelbart: [LINK]"}],"r":[["PaypcVFPs48",1],["viJt_DXTfwA",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["36myc8wQhLo",1],["ajGX7odA87k",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["Th4VlqQyVr4",1],["7W33HRc1A6c",1],["Dddbe9OuJLU",1]]},"QOEMv0S8AcA":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the foundational talk behind FreedomBox and the case against centralized web services.","q":{"x":"It's worth the watch/listen to get the idea in full.","i":3555482,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":3555482,"author":"bootload","month":"2012-02","story":"Facebook Is Using You","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the idea behind FreedomBox: own your own logs.","quote":"It's worth the watch/listen to get the idea in full.","text":"\"... I follow a few high profile tech news source, but I never heard of Freedombox. ...\" That's why it's important to read HN. Eben Moglen is one of the few lawyers I bother to listen too. It helps he has a CS background. Moglen did a talk at New York Technology Council & Internet Society, Friday, February 5, 2010 that outlined the idea behind the freedom box. I remember watching & listening to the recording just after the speech. It's worth the watch/listen to get the idea in full. The idea in short, \"Own your own logs\" . Third parties can't infer what they don't have. The talk is located here - [LINK] - [VIDEO] or here for alternative AV formats ~ [LINK]"},{"id":2579137,"author":"vikingux","month":"2011-05","story":"G8 agenda calls for \"civilized Internet\": monitored, governed, controlled, taxed","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moglen talk on freedom in the cloud.","quote":"Stop listening to Bob Marley. Listen to Eben Molgen.","text":"Stop listening to Bob Marley. Listen to Eben Molgen. Youtube: \"Eben Moglen - Freedom in The Cloud\": [VIDEO] Stop going to Starbucks. Send money to The Freedom Box Foundation. [LINK] Otherwise they may prove Richard Stallman correct."},{"id":7895355,"author":"logn","month":"2014-06","story":"YunoHost: Aiming to make hosting accessible","stance":"rec","why":"Speech on what centralized services cost us.","quote":"Eben Moglen gave a great speech on what we're giving up by using centralized services.","text":"Eben Moglen gave a great speech on what we're giving up by using centralized services. His call to action was to basically build what YunoHost now is. This video of the speech is an hour long but a good one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16798760,"author":"jancsika","month":"2018-04","story":"FreedomBox","stance":"reference","why":"Moglen's 2010 proposed FreedomBox features.","quote":"Here are two potential features Eben Moglen mentioned[1] back in 2010","text":"Here are two potential features Eben Moglen mentioned[1] back in 2010: * it should know how to go and collect your stuff out of all the social networking places where you've got it * it should know how to send an encrypted backup of everything to your friends' servers (in the video you can hear two developers making \"mmm\" noises at that feature) Eight years later, does Freedombox have either of those two features? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8180992,"author":"bootload","month":"2014-08","story":"It's not too late to ditch the ad-based business model and build a better web","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Relinquishing control of server logs is the source of the problem.","text":"\"... The idea that software should be free means that only software that isn't free gets polished to the level that anyone uses it. ...\" Linux kernel? \"... The Internet, which was supposed to be our great liberator and engine of learning, became George Orwell's bidirectional TV set. It's a TV set that watches you. ... and it all goes back to free. ...\" Telephony isn't free and is spied on. Related but not quite. Relinquishing control of server logs is the source of the problem. cf: Eben Moglen, \"Freedom in the Cloud\" , Feb 5th 2010 @ NYU ~ [VIDEO]"},{"id":4174711,"author":"bootload","month":"2012-06","story":"‘FreedomBox’ Is in Danger of an Early Death","stance":"reference","why":"Background talk to the Freedom Box.","quote":"the background to the freedom box can be found in this talk","text":"the background to the freedom box can be found in this talk \"Freedom in The Cloud\" , by Eben Moglen at NYU 2010Feb05 ~ [VIDEO]"}]},"ZCuYPiUIONs":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as the clearest explanation of how Fiber and React 16 work.","q":{"x":"ReactConf 2017 is excellent","i":14142336,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":16120073,"author":"acemarke","month":"2018-01","story":"Show HN: Nerv – A fast React alternative, compatible with IE8 and React 16","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic presentation for understanding React 16 changes.","quote":"fantastic ReactConf presentation","text":"Yeah, the React team takes backwards compatibility very seriously. They did finally drop some deprecated methods from the main package in React 16, but made them available as separate packages, and even wrote codemods to automatically replace usages. I can recommend several resources for understanding the changes in React 16: - Lin Clark's fantastic ReactConf presentation on \"A Cartoon Intro to Fiber\": [VIDEO] - The official announcements for React 16: [LINK] and [LINK] - A good summary of the major changes: [LINK] - Two articles looking at the user-facing features and changes: [LINK] and [LINK]"},{"id":14064167,"author":"acemarke","month":"2017-04","story":"React v15.5.0","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk explaining how React Fiber works.","quote":"ReactConf 2017 is excellent","text":"For those who are interested in some of the details of the work that's going on, Lin Clark's recent talk on \"A Cartoon Intro to Fiber\" at ReactConf 2017 is excellent [0]. There's a number of other existing writeups and resources on how Fiber works [1] as well. The roadmap for 15.5 and 16.0 migration is at [2], and the follow-up issue discussing the plan for the \"addons\" packages is at [3]. I'll also toss out my usual reminder that I keep a big list of links to high-quality tutorials and articles on React, Redux, and related topics, at [LINK] . Specifically intended to be a great starting point for anyone trying to learn the ecosystem, as well as a solid source of good info on more advanced topics. Finally, the Reactiflux chat channels on Discord are a great place to hang out, ask questions, and learn. The invite link is at [LINK] . [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":14142336,"author":"acemarke","month":"2017-04","story":"Facebook Announces React Fiber, a Rewrite of Its React Framework","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk explaining how React Fiber works.","quote":"ReactConf 2017 is excellent","text":"I'll repeat the comment I made in the \"React 15.5\" thread a couple weeks ago ( [LINK] ): For those who are interested in some of the details of the work that's going on, Lin Clark's recent talk on \"A Cartoon Intro to Fiber\" at ReactConf 2017 is excellent [0]. There's a number of other existing writeups and resources on how Fiber works [1] as well. The roadmap for 15.5 and 16.0 migration is at [2], and the follow-up issue discussing the plan for the \"addons\" packages is at [3]. I'll also toss out my usual reminder that I keep a big list of links to high-quality tutorials and articles on React, Redux, and related topics, at [LINK] . Specifically intended to be a great starting point for anyone trying to learn the ecosystem, as well as a solid source of good info on more advanced topics. Finally, the Reactiflux chat channels on Discord are a great place to hang out, ask questions, and learn. The invite link is at [LINK] . [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":15076378,"author":"pmlamotte","month":"2017-08","story":"Inside a fast CSS engine","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on React Fiber by a gifted explainer.","quote":"Her talk on react fiber this year is quite good as well","text":"I believe she just has a talent for it, she's an engineer as far as I know and has other related cartoon descriptions: [LINK] Her talk on react fiber this year is quite good as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14142289,"author":"mklarmann","month":"2017-04","story":"Facebook Announces React Fiber, a Rewrite of Its React Framework","stance":"rec","why":"Beautiful description of React Fiber.","quote":"React Fiber was beautifully described by Lin on the last reactconf.","text":"React Fiber was beautifully described by Lin on the last reactconf. [VIDEO] Congratulations to the release."},{"id":18732622,"author":"carlmungz","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Can you suggest educative react projects for newbies to make?","stance":"reference","why":"Part of a reading list for understanding React internals.","quote":"Study the React source code and implement your own mini-version.","text":"Study the React source code and implement your own mini-version. It's a great way to deepen framework specific knowledge (if that's what you are after) and also learning more about the language used to create the framework. Start with these articles: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":16493840,"author":"swyx","month":"2018-03","story":"Beyond React 16 by Dan Ambramov – Async Rendering and React Suspense","stance":"reference","why":"Early demo of time slicing at ReactConf.","quote":"Lin Clark basically demoed Time Slicing a -year- ago at ReactConf","text":"typo in the title - its Abramov not Ambramov It's interesting that Lin Clark basically demoed Time Slicing a -year- ago at ReactConf: [VIDEO] and in the first 10 mins of Dan's talk you see basically the same thing with user interaction applied. createFetcher is brand new and here's the metaconversation on Twitter/Github: [LINK] and [LINK] with a couple implementations here ( [LINK] ) and here ( [LINK] ) // style discussion below one thing I really appreciate about this talk is hard it is to even communicate asynchronous problems and the solutions to them. Personally the lagging animationFrame clock display (for time slicing) and the loading state devtools (for \"react suspense\") were actually the bigger \"wow\" moments. (Yes I know this talk is not about the tools or the charts) he does another thing I'm a fan of - starting with a question. this helps frame the rest of the talk: explaining why the question is a big deal, breaking the question down, proposing some solutions, showing how the solutions answer the question. bottom line - there's more to learn from this talk than just React."}]},"k1BneeJTDcU":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up in threads about the internet, attention economy and smartphones, mostly as a favorite, with a few dissenters.","q":{"x":"This is one of my favourite songs now","i":27491999,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":47123075,"author":"Imustaskforhelp","month":"2026-02","story":"Hacker News.love – 22 projects Hacker News didn't love","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Song showing the extremism and attention economy of the internet","quote":"Bo Burnham is amazing.","text":"This reminded me of this song which actually shows the extremism of things in Internet. Welcome to the Internet - Bo Burnham: [VIDEO] \"Y'know wasn't always like this, not very long ago.. just before your time.. right before the towers fell.. circa 99 this was catalogues, travel blogs a chatroom or two, we set our nights and spent our nights waiting for you.\" \"It was always the plan to put the world in your head.\" \"Could I interest you in everything all of the time? Apathy is a tragedy and boredom is a crime. Everything and anything all of the time\" Bo Burnham is amazing. Another (interesting?) song is jefferey bezos song [LINK] [Bo Burnham: Inside - Jeff Bezos] Recommend everyone to watch both music if you have the time and the first one for sure."},{"id":27491999,"author":"zubspace","month":"2021-06","story":"I Miss the Old Internet (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Song about internet culture from a funny, sad special","quote":"This is one of my favourite songs now","text":"I just watched Inside [1], where Bo Burnham creates a wonderful, funny, sad and thought-provoking \"Special\" about our internet culture. Highly recommended. This is one of my favourite songs now: [2] It's a bit crazy when I think about how different our generations grow up and it makes me a bit sad that our children will have a completely different understanding of what the internet is. For me it was a place of wonder, freedom and self-expression. It's different today, but even I have problems explaining how it changed. But it definitely lost some if it's playfulness on the way. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28043136,"author":"Pyrodogg","month":"2021-08","story":"Life before smartphones (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Song capturing how the internet and mobile devices suck up attention","quote":"hits this right on head","text":"\"Apathy's a tragedy and boredom is a crime\" - Welcome to the Internet by Bo Burnham[1] This new song from Bo's covid-lockdown inspired special \"Inside\" hits this right on head. The Internet, particularly when paired with mobile devices just tries to suck up so much attention, because that's exactly what we made it do. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27462622,"author":"sodality2","month":"2021-06","story":"iOS 15 Humane","stance":"rec","why":"Bo Burnham song about internet culture","quote":"I absolutely loved it. Especially \"Welcome to the Internet","text":"Yep- it's totally different from his previous work but I absolutely loved it. Especially \"Welcome to the Internet\", because I can see firsthand what the song is about. Here's the video on Bo Burnham's own channel: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42320003,"author":"normalaccess","month":"2024-12","story":"Lead exposure in last century shrank IQ scores of half of Americans","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant social commentary on devices hijacking attention","quote":"Relevant social commentary (timestamped)","text":"100% correct. Nature does not blink and boop. Just like a drugs out devices hijack people's natural feedback mechanisms and produce an experience that's hyper-real. A two year-old with an iPad becomes unable to handle reality because it's dull by comparison. Relevant social commentary (timestamped): [VIDEO]"},{"id":40096124,"author":"aebtebeten","month":"2024-04","story":"Personal Machines and Portable Worlds","stance":"reference","why":"Lyrics used to illustrate the shallow monetised web","quote":"Which Power Ranger are you? Take this quirky quiz Obama sent the immigrants To vaccinate your kids","text":"What makes a \"world\" for me is that there are a few basic rules from which all the gameplay can be derived; something where the basic axioms don't generate a rich theory is just \"on rails\" and at best a trivial world (something where the basic axioms generate anything and everything is even worse: a degenerate world). What makes a machine \"personal\" for me is that I need to engage by putting in some creative input in to get anything out; doodling on a sheet of paper is personal, but clicking on and desultorily reacting to some external stream is impersonal. In these senses, the \"engagement\" of the monetised web is neither personal nor a world: Which Power Ranger are you? Take this quirky quiz Obama sent the immigrants To vaccinate your kids [VIDEO]"},{"id":40202651,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-04","story":"30% of Children Ages 5-7 Are on TikTok","stance":"reference","why":"Art produced by the internet generation","quote":"It occasionally produces art","text":"It occasionally produces art: [VIDEO] EDIT: Mr. Burnham is vintage 1990, so he's a bit too old to have been weaned onto it"},{"id":32951329,"author":"poszlem","month":"2022-09","story":"Are You Ready for the Internet? (1994) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Satirical take on the modern internet","quote":"Kind of makes me wonder what would those people say if we played them Bo Burnham's Welcome to The Internet from 2021","text":"Kind of makes me wonder what would those people say if we played them Bo Burnham's Welcome to The Internet from 2021: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30052538,"author":"andrewxhill","month":"2022-01","story":"Blue verification checkmark vs. hexagonal PFPs on Twitter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Why stop at 2000 though? The internet is still full of terrible things today, many of which are the most visible, and most of which are happening because, money. [VIDEO] Terrible things on the internet don't make all things built on the internet guilty by association. Just like scammers and grifters finding opportunities in web3 don't make all NFTs bad ideas."},{"id":45838147,"author":"sabatonfan","month":"2025-11","story":"ChatGPT terms disallow its use in providing legal and medical advice to others","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Welcome to the internet! Have a look around","text":"Welcome to the internet! Have a look around ( [VIDEO] ) Another obligatory song named Jeff bezos by Bo Burnham ( [LINK] ) C'mon Jeffrey you can do it! (I mean you can break half the internet with AWS us-east-1, but in all seriousness both songs are really nice lol)"},{"id":41960406,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-10","story":"Teens Want Less Sex in Movies and TV Shows, Study Finds","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Lagniappe","text":"I'm with [LINK] on this point. In a world where teens are a click away from the referents to \"Ma'am I do my own plumbing\" and \"Wh-what are you doing, steppe peoples?\", I'd guess they don't see sex as negative so much as see gratuitous sex, sprinkled in by writers who can't even quote a single midget donkey porn trope properly, as negative. We pay about $60 for an evening for 2 at the movies; for that I'd appreciate having 90-120 minutes of, you know, story. Lagniappe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46334721,"author":"metalman","month":"2025-12","story":"Faith in the internet is fading among young Brits","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"thanks for the \"recomendation\" 168 million views","text":"thanks for the \"recomendation\" [VIDEO] 168 million views edit: thinking about this video, it occurs to me that what is criminalised as subversion, may just be an instinctual reaction to non survial behavior on the part of our ruling class"}],"r":[["Uo08uS904Rg",1],["sG9UMMq2dz4",1],["prXuyMCgbTc",1],["rFejpH_tAHM",1],["TPpoJGYlW54",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["yftBiNu0ZNU",1],["1sl5KJ69qiA",1]]},"s_osQvkeNRM":{"wy":"HN commenters post it in swearing and profanity debates as the best statement against taking offense at profanity.","q":{"x":"Stephen Fry says it best","i":5694969,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":12229771,"author":"bhrgunatha","month":"2016-08","story":"Why swearing is good for you","stance":"rec","why":"Fry on swearing with delightful bleeping.","quote":"Stephen Fry, whose intellect and vocabulary are way beyond mine, on swearing.","text":"Stephen Fry, whose intellect and vocabulary are way beyond mine, on swearing. The bleeping on the second video is so deliciously germane. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":7457977,"author":"anu_gupta","month":"2014-03","story":"WTF, HTML and CSS?","stance":"rec","why":"Fry defending swearing as rich part of English.","quote":"Profanity is an essential and rich part of the English language.","text":"From the fucking lovely Stephen Fry \"The sort of twee person who thinks swearing is in any way a sign of a lack of education or a lack of verbal interest is just a fucking lunatic.\" Profanity is an essential and rich part of the English language. It's really only prudes and people who need to feel morally superior have a problem with it, and they can fuck right off, frankly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5694969,"author":"shocks","month":"2013-05","story":"I Don't Want Your Fucking App","stance":"rec","why":"Fry addresses offense at profanity.","quote":"Stephen Fry says it best","text":"And as usual, any HN post that contains profanity is immediately overrun by 'offended' fuckheads that simply don't understand the joke and are offended by fuck all. Stephen Fry says it best: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3212887,"author":"stevelosh","month":"2011-11","story":"Think twice about that F-bomb in your next talk","stance":"rec","why":"Stephen Fry defends swearing against vocabulary snobbery.","quote":"I'm with Stephen Fry on this one","text":"I'm with Stephen Fry on this one: \"There used to be mad, silly, prissy people who used to say swearing was a sign of a poor vocabulary. Such utter nonsense.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":3217725,"author":"glenjamin","month":"2011-11","story":"Swearing","stance":"rec","why":"Sums up the casual attitude to swearing.","quote":"The following video sums it up nicely IMO","text":"I'm rather bemused by this whole thing, I'd be intrigued to know the demographics of the people who take such, issue with use of swear words. Perhaps it's an American thing, but in Britain I'm not aware of anyone I know who'd react in hysterics at the thought of a strong word appearing in something as public as a slide deck! Swearing at someone can be extremely rude, but using such words passively to add emphasis seems perfectly normal to me. The following video sums it up nicely IMO [VIDEO]"},{"id":7140104,"author":"Crito","month":"2014-01","story":"Rename GTFO to GOAWAY","stance":"reference","why":"Fry on the notion that swearing is unnecessary.","quote":"On the ridiculous notion of \"unnecessary","text":"The word \"fuck\" evolved past \"sexual acts and bodily functions\" _centuries_ ago. Do you have the same objection of \"screwing off\"? I anticipate you claiming that you have objections to the phrase, but do you have the same magnitude of objections? Or is one worse than the other? Second hand smoke smells. That is an actual reason to not blow smoke into somebodies face, it isn't just tradition. Putting weird political rants into your documentation would presumably not serve a purpose. The word fuck, despite your disapproval of it, has versatile meaning and can be used in a variety of ways in general speech. If you want a political equivalent, it would be like referencing luddism in documentation. Luddism, originally referring to a specific political movement, has long since transcended its literal connotations. It would not be considered particularly bizarre to use the word \"luddism\" or \"Luddite\" in documentation. On the ridiculous notion of \"unnecessary\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["YKuRkGkf5HU",1]]},"5guXaWwQpe4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a grounded, data-driven counter to hype about quick energy revolutions.","q":{"x":"I really suggest watching this talk by Smil","i":17707063,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17707063,"author":"Scea91","month":"2018-08","story":"Seaweed could be scrubbing more carbon from the atmosphere than expected (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insightful talk on how little energy technology has changed","quote":"I really suggest watching this talk by Smil","text":"Exactly, we haven't achieved that much actually. Most of the technology we use for harvesting energy is not much further from what people could imagine in 19th century. Turbine was invented in the 19th century. I really suggest watching this talk by Smil [VIDEO] and if you are interested reading some books from him about energy and oil. Really insightful."},{"id":24514400,"author":"lcamach84","month":"2020-09","story":"How can we, as web professionals, help to make the web more energy efficient?","stance":"rec","why":"Vaclav Smil's view on clean energy","quote":"I encourage you to look at the work of Vaclav Smil","text":"I encourage you to look at the work of Vaclav Smil [1] . There's no such thing as clean energy. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14249281,"author":"tedsanders","month":"2017-05","story":"The Future of Energy Isn't Fossil Fuels or Renewables, It's Nuclear Fusion","stance":"rec","why":"Smil argues energy transitions are slow","quote":"","text":"That is a bizarre claim to make. Energy will not be far cheaper and more abundant in 10 years. Trillions of dollars are invested in infrastructure that has lifetimes of 50+ years. It is not economically or technologically feasible to undergo an energy revolution that quickly. Even if we gathered the political and financial will, our production facilities and supply chains could not feasibly handle the load of rebuilding the world's energy infrastructure in a single decade. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34124522,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2022-12","story":"Deadly, dirty, indispensable: the nitrogen industry has changed the world","stance":"reference","why":"Smil on Haber-Bosch feeding much of humanity","quote":"","text":"I get where you're coming from, folks like Vaclav Smil have talked about how the haber bosch process is arguably what's kept 2/3 of human population alive ( [VIDEO] ). The issues are also: - Thinking beyond the anthropocentric and realize we've created many ocean dead zones: e.g. [LINK] . Think about what this'll mean in terms of ocean temperature in life within our lifetimes. - Many farmsites simply use way too much nitrogen, possibly impacting human drinking water (one good person to follow in Iowa is Chris Jones ( [LINK] ) and Silvia Secchi ( [LINK] ) - Despite the above the predicament of deciding to use nitrogen inputs during adverse weather conditions (curious to hear about the alternatives here, from what I see even smallholder farmers that try to minimize nitrogen input still use a bit of it). - The haber bosch process itself, and industries seeking to find alternatives to produce ammonia gas (iirc likely used in making diesel DEF fluid urea - [LINK] ). - The article also cites norman borlaug, of which there's plenty of questions to ask around the economic and ecological narratives around that work - e.g. [LINK] and likely 'the violence of the green revolution'"}],"r":[["5MdSE-N0bxs",1],["vpTHi7O66pI",1]]},"7gDKbT_l2us":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a thorough, nuanced perspective on the Rowling controversy and on cancel culture from a trans creator.","q":{"x":"This is a good, calm, nuanced discussion about why LGBTQ people and our allies don't like Rowling as much as we used to.","i":33847686,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33847686,"author":"ReactiveJelly","month":"2022-12","story":"The Truth Matters and Secular Humanists Should Defend It","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Calm, nuanced discussion of why LGBTQ people soured on Rowling.","quote":"This is a good, calm, nuanced discussion about why LGBTQ people and our allies don't like Rowling as much as we used to.","text":"The Internet has the same incentive landscape as bathroom stall graffiti. That's why you're so mad, Robyn. This isn't a back-and-forth between two real people who mutually respect each other. If I was talking to Robyn in real life, over coffee, for 2 or 3 hours, we'd probably get something productive done. But online, it's a hit-and-run dumpster where the truth matters a little, and humor and presentation matter _much more._ Robyn writes their wounded-gazelle piece whining about the woke cancel mob, and I try to 'clap back' without 'seething'. Wittgenstein would roll in his grave to see the level of MLG language gamers we have online these days. Nuance gets buried online. I am not gonna sit here and write a boring screen-long essay expressing my nuanced thoughts on the rights of trans adults and trans children, to an audience that might literally be reading this on the shitter. It works fine for less-controversial topics like Rust vs C. It just doesn't work for gender. They who write on shit-house walls... P.S. - If you have 2 hours to spare for nuance, I highly recommend the Contrapoints video about J. K. Rowling. This is a good, calm, nuanced discussion about why LGBTQ people and our allies don't like Rowling as much as we used to. [VIDEO] If you don't have 2 hours to spare, then you aren't gonna get nuance, sorry to tell you."},{"id":29052295,"author":"geraldalewis","month":"2021-10","story":"Reasons for Speaking Out on Sex and Gender Issues (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Contrapoints' perspective on J.K. Rowling, long but great.","quote":"ContraPoints' videos are really great. Here's her perspective on J.K. Rowling","text":"It's 90 minutes, but ContraPoints' videos are really great. Here's her perspective on J.K. Rowling: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25934421,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2021-01","story":"Agent fired from literary agency for using Parler and Gab","stance":"rec","why":"Thorough deep dive analysis of Rowling's views.","quote":"Contrapoints just did a pretty thorough deep dive analysis[1].","text":"> Rowling's views are not 'dated' (that's your opinion) They are. Bigotry is not static. It's a dynamic reaction to progress being made in the rest of society. Her views are not a reaction to fringe whackjobs on twitter screaming for some rights that the rest of society is not willing to give them. Her views are a reaction to changes in government policy that do not happen overnight, are themselves a result of decades of progress made in conjunction with medical and scientific subject matter experts. > they are thought out, and reasonable. They are not. They are based on strawmen interpretations of trans people's lived experiences, and their desired outcomes. It would take a really long time to go through all her points and point out the various logical flaws in it, but the main issue is they are founded on her own personal beliefs, anxieties, and prior trauma, not a reasonable detached view of the status quo. Contrapoints just did a pretty thorough deep dive analysis[1]. > The issue is not 'people not buying her books' - the issue is that people are trying to suppress and silence her An author's interaction with their audience, and their \"platform\" goes far beyond the books they publish. They have other channels, and other ways to reach people - in Rowling's case millions of people, primarily children. While not everyone would agree with me on this, but if Rowling was quietly harboring transphobic beliefs, and made no further follow-up, I wouldn't care, and there would be no"},{"id":26489663,"author":"benrbray","month":"2021-03","story":"GitHub, fuck your name change","stance":"reference","why":"Incidental link in a discussion of cancel culture.","quote":"","text":"The argument \"I haven't met a single...\" is equally as strong as an unqualified \"You are misinformed.\", which I was responding to. Note that I asked for clarification :) Since I have you here, would you mind elaborating a bit more about your stance on \"cancel culture\"? I'll also elaborate a bit more on my stance. I think most people would agree that mobs by definition have no united agenda. It's a bunch of disorganized people with their own goals and motivations who all briefly get fired up about the same topic. Twitter mobs are a tornado of confirmation bias, where people in echo chambers spin up hot takes of current events to confirm their own worldview. The amount of meaningful debate that can be had in 280-character chunks is negligible. When people talk about \"cancel culture\", my impression is not that they think there is any sort of coordinated attack on right-leaning figures by prominent left-leaning figures -- only fringe conspiracy theorists believe that George Soros is sending out weekly lists of names that should be \"cancelled\" this week. It's that they believe Twitter is a place which has developed a culture of criticizing and ridiculing other users, public figures especially. I think it's undeniable that any time a public figure missteps, a vocal minority of people (e.g. angsty teenagers) on Twitter calls for the person to be fired or otherwise deplatformed, even before they have a chance to respond. Some people also receive death threats. So, I don't think it's "},{"id":26143352,"author":"monopoledance","month":"2021-02","story":"Brendan Eich on the Lex Fridman Podcast [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Natalie Wynn offers a better critical take on cancel culture.","quote":"There is a far, far better (critical) take on it within \"marxist\" culture, e.g. from Natalie Wynn.","text":"He is not talking about cancel culture. He is taking this very overloaded term as premise, as self-evident \"fact\" for the degeneration of academia and \"the public discourse\", to pave the way for injecting his super deep ideology about salvation by Love, Elon and Ayn Rand. I am not sure, if you are familiar with Joscha Bach, but he once said something about something, and people think he's super intelligent! None of these podcasts talking critical about cancel culture. There is a far, far better (critical) take on it within \"marxist\" culture, e.g. from Natalie Wynn. If Lex wants to talk cancel culture, why not invite her for example? She is an academic, smart and wrote and talked extensively about it. But that would be a little too open-minded wouldn't it? [§] [LINK] [$] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aDMjgOYOcDw",1],["kh88fVP2FWQ",1],["UBc7qBS1Ujo",1]]},"IkdziSLYzHw":{"wy":"HN readers recommend it as an intuitive explanation of neural networks that shifts how people think about AI and language.","q":{"x":"Richard Sutton takes a different view in an interview with Dwarkesh Patel[2]","i":45805599,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45805599,"author":"j1mr10rd4n","month":"2025-11","story":"The Case That A.I. Is Thinking","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hinton argues human language use is analogous to backpropagation-trained neural networks.","quote":"Geoffrey Hinton's recent lecture at the Royal Institute[1] is a fascinating watch.","text":"Geoffrey Hinton's recent lecture at the Royal Institute[1] is a fascinating watch. His assertion that human use of language being exactly analogous to neural networks with back-propagation really made me think about what LLMs might be able to do, and indeed, what happens in me when I \"think\". A common objection to LLM \"intelligence\" is that \"they don't know anything\". But in turn... what do biological intelligences \"know\"? For example, I \"know\" how to do things like write constructs that make complex collections of programmable switches behave in certain ways, but what do I really \"understand\"? I've been \"taught\" things about quantum mechanics, electrons, semiconductors, transistors, integrated circuits, instruction sets, symbolic logic, state machines, assembly, compilers, high-level-languages, code modules, editors and formatting. I've \"learned\" more along the way by trial and error. But have I in effect ended up with anything other than an internalised store of concepts and interconnections? (c.f. features and weights). Richard Sutton takes a different view in an interview with Dwarkesh Patel[2] and asserts that \"learning\" must include goals and reward functions but his argument seemed less concrete and possibly just a semantic re-labelling. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":45720269,"author":"tim333","month":"2025-10","story":"YouTube Just Ate TV. It's Only Getting Started","stance":"rec","why":"Hinton explaining AI and neural networks, interesting.","quote":"watching Hinton explain AI and neural networks at the mo.","text":"I muck about on HN and get ~80% of my video content on youtube. I think it's great overall. The rest ~5% twitter, 0% TV although I have one sitting switched off with a £174 bbc fee. 5% pirated maybe. If you go with the official HN guide of \"anything that gratifies one's intellectual curiosity\" and avoid \"stories about politics, or crime, or sports, or celebrities\" then youtube is much better - watching Hinton explain AI and neural networks at the mo. [LINK] and [VIDEO] which is kind of interesting."},{"id":44882291,"author":"calf","month":"2025-08","story":"Is Chain-of-Thought Reasoning of LLMs a Mirage? A Data Distribution Lens","stance":"rec","why":"Hinton's intuitive explanations shifted the commenter's views despite some disagreement.","quote":"his intuitive explanations helped shift my views somewhat as well.","text":"It was a Royal Institution public lecture, \"Will AI outsmart human intelligence? - with 'Godfather of AI' Geoffrey Hinton\", [VIDEO] Ultimately I somehwat disagreed with some of Hintons points in this talk, and after some thought I came up with specific reasons/doubts, and yet at the same time, his intuitive explanations helped shift my views somewhat as well."}],"r":[["7k1ehaE0bdU",1],["YMqgiXLjvRs",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["baFaEvBywGc",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1],["9NOncx2jU0Q",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1],["gCWtkvDQ2ZI",1]]},"N6xLMUifbxQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it for its comparison of tank factory efficiency and the Soviet approach of building cheap tanks with short lifespans.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend it","i":19562023,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19562023,"author":"reitzensteinm","month":"2019-04","story":"Tanks Are Mighty Fine Things (1946)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compares US, Soviet, German tank factory efficiency in man-hours.","quote":"I highly recommend it","text":"I've been to the tank museum in Munster (not to be confused with Münster if you ever decide to go!). Looking up close, comparing the American and German WWII tanks to the Russian ones is absolutely night and day. The alignment of the panels and the welding on a Tiger tank looks (to an untrained eye) pretty much as good as you could make a mass produced vehicle back then. A T34 by contrast has haphazard misalignments connected by welding that looks like mine did when I was learning in high school. So you can see with your own eyes that they essentially put the bare minimum of quality in to them. This is a very interesting talk on the manufacturing efficiency of the US, Russia and Germany in WW2, which drills down into the details of how the factories were organized and the difference in man hours spent on each (which I'll spoil the bottom line of here: US 10k, SU 35-50k, DE 300k). We've all heard stories about how Russian tanks in certain cases were driven out of factories partially completed straight in to combat, but up until I watched it I hadn't heard of Tiger tanks being rejected by the Wehrmacht because they didn't latest gear stick design. I highly recommend it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24954478,"author":"chewz","month":"2020-10","story":"Photos from inside a Typhoon class submarine","stance":"rec","why":"Compares German, Soviet, US tank production philosophies.","quote":"Very enlightening.","text":"In the video I have linked below, in third lecture Jonathan Parshall compares industrial doctrines of tank manufacturing by Nazi Germamy, Soviet Union and USA during WW2. Very enlightening. Three different systems, three different manufacturing philosophies. - Germany - pre-industrial artisan manufacture (250 modifications during 2-year production run of 1347 units, 300 thousand man-hours per tank vs American 10 thousand man-hours) - USA - Detroit style horizontal mass production (US scaled down tanks production in 1944 due to overproduction in 1943) - Soviet Union - cost-optimized, vertical mass production (the average tank lasted only 14 hours in battle anyway) [VIDEO] Tank Production: A Comparative Study of output in Germany, the US, and the USSR Jonathan Parshall."},{"id":18642588,"author":"avar","month":"2018-12","story":"The price of the Manhattan Project (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on WWII as a battle of factories.","quote":"enlightening on this point","text":"I found this conference talk about WWII being at its heart a \"battle of the factories\" enlightening on this point: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37549504,"author":"michaelt","month":"2023-09","story":"USA built 151 Aircraft carriers in WWII","stance":"reference","why":"Source for WWII tank production figures.","quote":"I saw a very interesting video a few years ago","text":"> At the end of the battle they mention that by the end of the war the US was completing multiple carriers per month, and they were just orders of magnitude ahead of every other country when it came to manufacturing power and steel production. I saw a very interesting video a few years ago [1] - by the end of WW2, tank production figures were: Russia built 106,000 tanks America built 102,000 tanks Germany built 47,000 tanks Britain built 36,000 tanks Needless to say, once you know one side had 5x as many tanks, the outcome becomes something of a foregone conclusion.... [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33604544,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2022-11","story":"How the War Was Won","stance":"reference","why":"Further material from Kursk presentation.","quote":"","text":"> The counter-argument was that this was propaganda, but I don't accept that. Research seems to indicate that over one-third of the tanks used by the Russians in defence around Moscow during Barbarossa (in Dec 1941) were British: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] (via) More from the 2013 Kursk presentation at the [US] National WWII Museum: * [VIDEO] I've heard the expression: Word War Two [in Europe] was won by British intelligence, Russian blood, and American steel."},{"id":22665833,"author":"michaelt","month":"2020-03","story":"On Killing Tanks","stance":"reference","why":"Soviet tanks needed only short lifespans.","quote":"I saw an interesting talk about Soviet WW2 tanks","text":"I saw an interesting talk about Soviet WW2 tanks [1] and one of the things it said was the Soviets had done research and determined that a tank survives an average of 14 hours in combat, and in its entire life only needed an engine and transmission able to drive 1500 km [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19561862,"author":"michaelt","month":"2019-04","story":"Tanks Are Mighty Fine Things (1946)","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Soviet tank lifespan analysis.","quote":"","text":"One of the interesting things I've heard the Soviets did [1] was operational research: The lifespan of a tank on the Eastern front was less than 6 months, and once in combat was less than 14 hours. Less than 1000 miles driven. Knowing they didn't need a tank that lasted more than 1000 miles let them lower the number of parts and man-hours for each tank, allowing them to get them off the production line that much faster. You can imagine what an advantage it was that the Allies produced four times as many tanks as the Axis powers. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NWSZ4c9yqW8",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["0rSMt1pAlbE",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["ZPoqNeR3_UA",1],["AbSehcT19u0",1]]},"UIKGV2cTgqA":{"wy":"HN brings it up whenever curriculum fads or Common Core complaints appear, as a funny precedent from the 1960s.","q":{"x":"My favorite Tom Lehrer song is probably New Math.","i":16775251,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16775251,"author":"MrMember","month":"2018-04","story":"Tom Lehrer at 90: a life of scientific satire","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clever, rapid-fire satire that still sounds fresh","quote":"My favorite Tom Lehrer song is probably New Math.","text":"My favorite Tom Lehrer song is probably New Math. [VIDEO] With its clever lyrics and rapid fire delivery it sounds like it could have come out last year and not fifty years ago."},{"id":40462556,"author":"123894893","month":"2024-05","story":"Why did Tom Lehrer swap fame for obscurity?","stance":"rec","why":"Animated version showing the different subtraction methods in the song","quote":"Here's a good animated version of the song that shows the different methods.","text":"Yes, he's mocking the idea of teaching borrowing. He gives the way he thinks subtraction should be taught in the intro. It's a way that, as far as I can tell, hasn't been taught in America in over half a century. After the intro, he goes on to mock the concept of borrowing, which was (apparently) part of New Math. Then in the final verse he mocks the idea of teaching bases. Here's a good animated version of the song that shows the different methods. You'll notice that the \"silly\" New Math method is the way that makes sense to Americans today, and the \"simple\" method preferred by Lehrer is very confusing for anyone who's not used to it: [VIDEO] Here's more of the intro which makes it clear what's going on. My explanations in brackets: > Some of you who have small children may have perhaps been put in the embarrassing position of being unable to do your child’s arithmetic homework because of the current revolution in mathematics teaching known as the “New Math”. > So as a public service here tonight, I thought I would offer a brief lesson in the New Math. Tonight, we’re gonna cover subtraction. [ New Math is confusing, and he's going to go over how confusing it is in a humorous way.] > Consider the following subtraction problem, which I will put up here: 342 minus 173. Now, remember how we used to do that… > Three from two is nine, carry the one, and if you’re under 35 or went to a private school you say seven from three is six but if you’re over 35 and went to a public school "},{"id":40464271,"author":"OskarS","month":"2024-05","story":"Why did Tom Lehrer swap fame for obscurity?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the old and new subtraction methods side by side","quote":"I think the song is very funny and charming","text":"Yeah, ultimately it's the same algorithm, it's just a question of what are the details of the procedure, which makes it confusing if you've learned one way and not the other. The whole point of the song is that Lehrer thinks this is a new, less good way of doing subtraction. Look at this video if you haven't already: [VIDEO] The first way he does it seems INCREDIBLY confusing to people who learned arithmetic in the last 30 or so years (the sentence \"8 from 4 is 6\" is nonsensical to most such people), but the \"modern\" way he's mocking is perfectly understandable. I think the song is very funny and charming, but I do think this is a rare case where Lehrer is just wrong, the \"borrowing\" style is a much better and clearer way to explain subtraction. It is just as fast, and it gives you a much better intuition about what's actually happening, instead of just learning the steps by rote."},{"id":6658305,"author":"mturmon","month":"2013-11","story":"A ridiculous Common Core test for first graders","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Lehrer's song on 1960s New Math and base 8","quote":"Worth a listen.","text":"It's inevitable to link to this now: [VIDEO] It's Tom Lehrer, about 50 years ago, explaining subtraction in the context of the \"new\" math (the base-8 stuff starts at 2:40). Worth a listen."},{"id":45632356,"author":"bitwize","month":"2025-10","story":"Why do Stanford math professors still use chalk? (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Song references pounding dust out of erasers","quote":"this is referenced in the Tom Lehrer song \"New Math\"","text":"Pounding the dust out of the erasers was something American students often found fun, especially as elementary school students; this is referenced in the Tom Lehrer song \"New Math\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":36113960,"author":"_dain_","month":"2023-05","story":"What if nobody is bad at maths?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A whole lot of \"I think\", \"I believe,\" \"I feel\". No reference to existing educational research on what works and what doesn't. >This isn’t a call to give up, though: instead, we should teach maths differently, in a way that doesn’t churn out mathophobes. When five-year-olds first encounter the subject, it’s as a creative, open-ended activity, involving play and exploration. They learn about numbers using colourful blocks that join up in different ways. They fit these shapes together and tell different stories with them. Just a year or two later, though, maths becomes a discipline with strict rules and a forbidding regime of right or wrong answers. Instead we should try to maintain that sense of exploration and open-endedness, of trying out different approaches to a problem and seeing what works. What’s important about times tables, for example, is not the answers, but the different possible relationships between numbers. What’s important about equations is not the solution, but the techniques we use to untangle a problem using logic. Some of these exercises could be presented more like a Sherlock Holmes mystery, where the focus is on how the clues are pieced together and not just on what the answer must be. Where's the evidence that this would actually be a superior teaching method? The point of drills is to counteract the natural tendency to forget things. Fluency is gained through practice and repetition. There's no way around it. Think of an actual Sherlock Holmes mystery "},{"id":28410203,"author":"remexre","month":"2021-09","story":"Finger counting gives away your nationality","stance":"reference","why":"Compares 1965 New Math complaints to Common Core ones","quote":"comparing the complaints in \"New Math\" (from 1965) to those offered about Common Core is educational","text":"I think it depends on what curriculum your school's using; non-base-10 math is a punchline in Tom Lehrer's \"New Math\" [1]. Common Core might be getting rid of it? As a side note, comparing the complaints in \"New Math\" (from 1965) to those offered about Common Core is educational :) [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27476881,"author":"lkbm","month":"2021-06","story":"The Teaching of Arithmetic I: The Story of an experiment (1935)","stance":"reference","why":"Famous, delightful song ridiculing New Math","quote":"Tom Leher has a famous and delightful song about it","text":"It's not quite the same, but there've been several efforts in the US to shift math education away from \"memorize this algorithm for addition, this one for multiplication, etc.\" and instead get kids to understand our number system. New Math was ridiculed for it[0], and now Common Core[1]. \"Why are you teaching kids these inefficient ways of multiplying instead of memorizing the standard algorithm?\" Despite it being the focus of Tom Leher's song, my go-to test for \"Do you understand our number system?\" is \"Can you work out a multiplication problem in base 8?\" If you understand the concept of digits, you can solve multiplication in other bases, and likely have solid grasp on how our number system work. From there, you'll understand both the standard arithmetic algorithms and will be able to figure out your own shortcuts. If you lack this understanding, you're likely stuck just mindlessly using the algorithm you were taught. (One other thing I'd also like for us to add is Fermi problems. Physics students learn to estimate everything from the mass of the earth to the number of dogs in their city. Seems like something everyone would benefit from practicing and that kids would really enjoy.) [0] Tom Leher has a famous and delightful song about it: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] (This is more a defense of Common Core, but in response to the common complaint.)"},{"id":24280179,"author":"bryanrasmussen","month":"2020-08","story":"Tom Lehrer's Mathematical Songs (1951)","stance":"reference","why":"Notes the song missing from the linked list","quote":"doesn't have New Math","text":"doesn't have New Math [VIDEO] growing up we had two Tom Lehrer albums (I guess my parents had their good points)"},{"id":22941390,"author":"virmundi","month":"2020-04","story":"Why are Soviet math textbooks so hardcore in comparison to US textbooks? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Comedy song about 1950s-60s New Math","quote":"Here's a comedy song about it from that era.","text":"America did this too. There was the New Math trend in the 50-60s which feels like common core. Here's a comedy song about it from that era. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28239697,"author":"sam_goody","month":"2021-08","story":"California’s proposed new math curriculum defies logic","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Tom Lehrer complained about \"New Math\" some 60 years ago.","text":"Tom Lehrer complained about \"New Math\" some 60 years ago. [VIDEO] (To be honest, \"new math\" makes sense to me. But whatever, for us oldies this is as obligatory as a XKCD) He had no idea about where the world would go with \"woke\", though."},{"id":25149136,"author":"hprotagonist","month":"2020-11","story":"Whatever Happened to New Math? (1990)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(and remember kids, base 8 is just like base 10, really — if you’re missing two fingers)","text":"it’s so simple/so very simple/that only a child can do it! (and remember kids, base 8 is just like base 10, really — if you’re missing two fingers) [VIDEO]"},{"id":24833967,"author":"pg_bot","month":"2020-10","story":"Tom Lehrer releases song lyrics to public domain","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I find it funny that his \"New Math\" has now been replaced by an even newer math which parents can't understand.","text":"I find it funny that his \"New Math\" has now been replaced by an even newer math which parents can't understand. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16406209,"author":"hprotagonist","month":"2018-02","story":"Just teach my kid the math","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"remember, base 8 is just like base 10, really, if you’re missing two fingers.","text":"[VIDEO] “remember, base 8 is just like base 10, really, if you’re missing two fingers. Now, anyone who gets this right can stay after class and help clean the erasers...”"}],"r":[["6SYHamnHqU8",1],["EfGD2qveGdQ",1]]},"WYPNjSoDrqw":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the definitive documentary on BASIC's origins, and it resurfaces whenever BASIC or Kurtz comes up.","q":{"x":"The Birth of BASIC documentary that Dartmouth did for the 50th anniversary is excellent and features him prominently","i":42206637,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42206637,"author":"nettework","month":"2024-11","story":"Thomas E. Kurtz has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"50th anniversary documentary featuring Kurtz prominently","quote":"The Birth of BASIC documentary that Dartmouth did for the 50th anniversary is excellent and features him prominently","text":"I interviewed Tom Kurtz in 2017. He was so generous, and as brilliant as you'd expect. He wrote me a memo on \"Grammatical Simplifications in BASIC.\" He loved memos--was an academic mathematician at heart, even at 89! It was his idea to make computing accessible--he (modestly) credits Kemeny with all the other ideas about BASIC and the Dartmouth Time-Sharing System (DTSS). I wrote about the interview here: [LINK] . The Birth of BASIC documentary that Dartmouth did for the 50th anniversary is excellent and features him prominently: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40227237,"author":"WoodenChair","month":"2024-05","story":"BASIC turns 60","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on the creation of BASIC at Dartmouth","quote":"There's a really good documentary on the creation of BASIC at Dartmouth (made by Dartmouth)","text":"There's a really good documentary on the creation of BASIC at Dartmouth (made by Dartmouth): [VIDEO] If you don't want to watch the documentary, we did a shorter podcast episode about it: [LINK]"},{"id":26249669,"author":"ArtWomb","month":"2021-02","story":"BBC Micro at 40: How it inspired a generation of coders [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Related documentary on BASIC's origins","quote":"Related doc on youtube: \"Birth of BASIC","text":"I enjoy messing around in the Owlet browser-based BBC BASIC editor. 8-bit graphics still has a place in modern web design ;) [LINK] Related doc on youtube: \"Birth of BASIC\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":32015012,"author":"russellbeattie","month":"2022-07","story":"Ed is the standard text editor (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"BASIC's inception at Dartmouth","quote":"which makes sense given BASIC's inception at Dartmouth [2]","text":"For some reason it took me years to understand the need for a line editor. It didn't make any sense in my head. Then one day I saw the famous picture of Bill and Allen as kids in front of a teletype [1] and it finally clicked. The way it worked was like this: As you keyed in your program, letter by letter, it was printed out on the paper immediately like a typewriter. If you ran the program and there was a problem, you'd just read (or re-read) the printout, find the line with the bug, then use a line editor to fix just that line. You might mark the paper with pencil to keep track of the change. After a while if you needed to see the latest version, you'd just print the whole program out, or just the lines you needed. This realization finally clued me into what was going on when I was 10yo and programming BASIC on a TRS-80 Color Computer. It was essentially simulating a teletype (which makes sense given BASIC's inception at Dartmouth [2]), where you would type each line individually, then if there was a problem, you would edit the program individual lines at a time. I always thought it was an odd until I understood how actually using a teletype actually worked. Also explains why the command to write letters to the screen is called \"PRINT\". 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["qI_g07C_Q5I",1]]},"ZfuierUvx1A":{"wy":"HN people use it as a showcase of what fits in 64 kilobytes.","q":{"x":"probably the coolest and most impressive one","i":2858279,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2858279,"author":"jarin","month":"2011-08","story":"Low-level x86 code compression for 64k intros","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Impressive 64k intro generating graphics and music from code.","quote":"probably the coolest and most impressive one","text":"In case you haven't seen a 64k intro, \"Chaos Theory\" by Conspiracy is probably the coolest and most impressive one I've ever seen (fullscreen it in 720p for maximum awesomeness): [VIDEO] I think it's probably one of the few I've seen that goes beyond being just impressive coding and actually seems to evoke the feeling of a post-singularity civilization, or something like that. The graphics and music are all generated on the fly from 64k of code. I think it actually uses the code compression from the linked article."},{"id":39116551,"author":"0x000xca0xfe","month":"2024-01","story":"Making Rust binaries smaller by default","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what is possible in 64 kilobytes.","quote":"let the demoscene blow your mind","text":"I work in embedded software and we fight for every megabyte of NAND flash. Multiple smaller executables add up, too. Reducing the binary size is usually more important than performance (and often more important than memory safety, if we are totally honest...). Personally, I hate bloated software just as much as slow software. It's crazy what you can do with 64 kilobytes, let the demoscene blow your mind: [VIDEO] Would be awesome if compilers could do the same :)"},{"id":8231534,"author":"lloeki","month":"2014-08","story":"A Single Div","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as a better 64k demo than Farbrausch.","quote":"there are comparatively much better realisations both in terms of art and technique.","text":"I don't get the fascination with Farbrausch. While I respect and admire the feat which I'm unable to pull of myself, there are comparatively much better realisations both in terms of art and technique. See the following 64k demos: - chaos theory - [VIDEO] - turtles all the way down - sub dream [LINK] - the timeless — [LINK] - turtles all the way down - brain control - [LINK]"}],"r":[["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["oAVjF_7ensg",1],["AD5aAd8Oy84",1],["3GVO-MfIl1Q",1],["msN87y-iEx0",1]]},"czgOWmtGVGs":{"wy":"Linked whenever year numbering or the Holocene calendar comes up as the accessible introduction to the idea.","q":{"x":"Kurtzgesagt has a really great video about the subject","i":45668468,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45668468,"author":"daeken","month":"2025-10","story":"Greg Newby, CEO of Project Gutenberg Literary Archive Foundation, has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Really great video on the Human Era calendar","quote":"Kurtzgesagt has a really great video about the subject","text":"I've become an unironic proponent of HE, the Human Era. For CE dates, we just add 10000 (I'm writing this from 12025). But for BCE dates, they're subtracted and it makes things so much clearer when it comes to telling how long ago something was. E.g. the sundial was invented around 6000 HE, steel was developed around 9000 HE. Kurtzgesagt has a really great video about the subject: [VIDEO] There's also a timeline in HE that covers many major historical events: [LINK]"},{"id":16441891,"author":"ASalazarMX","month":"2018-02","story":"Anthropocene began in 1965, according to signs left in world’s ‘loneliest tree’","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of the Holocene calendar","quote":"here's a good explanation of it","text":"I'm all for leaving a religious calendar and using the Holocene calendar. Just add 10,000 to your dates and you're set. [LINK] If you don't mind videos, here's a good explanation of it [VIDEO]"},{"id":16235841,"author":"doctoboggan","month":"2018-01","story":"Jawbone of Earliest Modern Human Out of Africa Discovered in Israeli Cave","stance":"rec","why":"Well-sourced, generally reliable videos on the topic","quote":"His stuff is well sourced and generally reliable.","text":"It might not be what you were thinking, but the YouTube channel Kurzgesagt has 2 very good videos on the topic: [LINK] [VIDEO] His stuff is well sourced and generally reliable."},{"id":43463920,"author":"tomrod","month":"2025-03","story":"Deregulated energy markets accelerate solar adoption","stance":"reference","why":"Suggests labeling years in the Human Era","quote":"I suggest considering labeling even as 11973","text":"As a sidenote, I love that you reference 1973 as 01973. I suggest considering labeling even as 11973, based on some reasonable humanist-based information.[0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29739565,"author":"awiesenhofer","month":"2021-12","story":"Long Now Years: Five-Digit Dates and Y10K-Compliance at Home (02013)","stance":"reference","why":"Kurzgesagt episode on the Human Era calendar","quote":"An episode of kurzgesagt about this topic","text":"Reminds me of the Holocene Calendar, which argues for setting the year 0 ten thousand years earlier to include all of major human history. So instead of writing 02022 AD it would become 12022 HE, for Human Era. It would mean to update these date libraries fast though... An episode of kurzgesagt about this topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28469231,"author":"mLuby","month":"2021-09","story":"No One Ever Saw the Snow Cruiser Again (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"8-minute video on the Holocene calendar","quote":"An 8min video by Kurzgesagt on it","text":"If we're using 5 digits, add 10K years: we're in year 12021 of this Holocene calendar. (Happy palindrome year!) 0 is pre-history, some of the very first permanent construction. An 8min video by Kurzgesagt on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25958126,"author":"rendall","month":"2021-01","story":"Göbekli Tepe","stance":"reference","why":"Case for adding 10,000 years to the year count","quote":"Cesare Emiliani and Kurzegesagt make the case that we think of world civilization as much newer than it is","text":"Cesare Emiliani and Kurzegesagt make the case that we think of world civilization as much newer than it is, because our year count is now only to 2021. They argue for instead bumping our year by 10,000 to more truly reflect the age of our civilization, to 12,021, and (arbitrarily) commemorating Göbleki Tepe as its foundation [LINK] [VIDEO] (2016 or 12016)"},{"id":22913691,"author":"Green_man","month":"2020-04","story":"Our Long Bets and Predictions about 02020","stance":"reference","why":"Proposal to add a leading 1 to the year","quote":"a proposal to add a leading 1 to our year","text":"This reminded me of a proposal to add a leading 1 to our year[2020 -> 12020]: [VIDEO] Cesare Emiliani proposed that the Construction of the Göbekli Tepe 12,000 years ago could represent a \"start of civilization\", creating a \"holocene calendar\". [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["gSKzgpt4HBU",1],["kixAljyfdqU",1],["dGiQaabX3_o",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1]]},"zeLToGnjIUM":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the clearest explanation of how Zig achieves colorless async/await.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching Andrew Kelley's video","i":26941390,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26941390,"author":"kbd","month":"2021-04","story":"Red and blue functions are a good thing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Andrew Kelley explains color-less async/await in Zig","quote":"I highly recommend watching Andrew Kelley's video","text":"I really can't agree with this premise after seeing how Zig implements color-less async/await: [LINK] I highly recommend watching Andrew Kelley's video (linked in that article) on this topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26111847,"author":"jorangreef","month":"2021-02","story":"Comparison of Rust async and Linux thread context switch time and memory use","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how Zig async/await avoids function coloring","quote":"Zig's async/await does not force function coloring on you to do all of this","text":"I recently wrote an IO abstraction over io_uring using Zig's async/await. Here's how you would do a write()/fsync()/read() with this ( [LINK] ): const offset: u64 = 0; const bytes_written = try io.write(fd, buffer_write[0..], offset); try io.fsync(fd); const bytes_read = try io.read(fd, buffer_read[0..], offset); Other sync functions can use this asynchronous IO completion code in a synchronous style (as this snippet shows) and still get all the zero-syscall and asynchronous performance of io_uring. What this is actually doing under the hood is filling SQEs into io_uring's submission queue ring buffer and then later reading completion events off io_uring's completion queue ring buffer, so it's fully asynchronous in the I/O sense but this hasn't spilled out and leaked over into the control flow. The control flow is as it should be, nice and simple and synchronous. Beyond this, Zig still allows you to explicitly indicate concurrency with the `async` keyword, for example if you wanted to run multiple async code paths concurrently. But the crucial part is that Zig's async/await does not force function coloring on you to do all of this: [VIDEO] Pretty incredible on Zig's part to be able to pull this off. Huge kudos to Andrew Kelley. Also, thanks to Jens Axboe and io_uring, what you saw above was first-class single-threaded or thread-per-core, there's no threadpool doing that for you, it's pure ring buffer communication to the kernel and back, no context switches, no expensive coor"},{"id":23234002,"author":"bvrmn","month":"2020-05","story":"What Color Is Your Function? (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Zig's approach to colorless async by explicitly kicking call frames","quote":"There is an interesting approach in Zig to explicitly kick call frames to achieve colorless asyncness.","text":"There is an interesting approach in Zig to explicitly kick call frames to achieve colorless asyncness. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23224921,"author":"pron","month":"2020-05","story":"State of Loom","stance":"reference","why":"Zig asynchronous I/O talk linked in a Java threading debate","quote":"","text":"> Code that's meant to be pinned (and may behave incorrectly if not pinned) looks no different from any other code. Same goes for async/await. The decision to keep you running on the same native thread is up to the scheduler. > Java isn't positioned as a high-level, high-abstraction language and that's not, IME, the user community it has. I beg to differ. It aims to be a good compromise between productivity, observability and performance. Every choice, from JIT to GC, is about improving performance for the common case while helping productivity, not improving performance by adding fine-grained control. There are a few cases where this is not possible. Primitives is one of them, and, indeed, 25 years later, we're \"expanding\" primitives rather than find some automatic way for optimal memory layout. > It's always been advertised as a safer alternative to C++ - rather like Rust. I think you're mistaken, and in any event, this is certainly not our position. Java is designed for a good blend of productivity, observability and performance, and unless the circumstances are unusual, it opts for improving common-case performance with high abstractions rather than worst-case performance with low abstractions like C++/Rust. Roughly speaking, the stance on performance is how do we get to 95% with the least amount of programmer effort. Anyway, regardless of what I said above, the constraints on the design of usermode threads other than philosophy are also very different for Java than for C"}],"r":[["NRoUC9P1PmA",1],["mwrA5IRGpfU",1],["w3WYdYyjek4",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1]]},"04Wk9Oi_Fsk":{"wy":"Commenters hold it up as proof that 2600 programming is alive in the demoscene and as one of the most impressive demos ever made on the hardware.","q":{"x":"2600 programming is alive in the demo scene. This was just created in 2014 and is completely insane","i":8408516,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":10998500,"author":"unimpressive","month":"2016-01","story":"Jailbreak firmware turns cheap digital walkie-talkie into DMR scanning receiver","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the most impressive demos, beyond baseline 2600 games","quote":"this is one of the most impressive demos I've ever seen","text":"On that note, this is one of the most impressive demos I've ever seen: [VIDEO] (When you're familiar with what a baseline atari 2600 game looks like, this is just beyond the pale.)"},{"id":8408516,"author":"apaprocki","month":"2014-10","story":"Dirty Assembly Tricks in NES Assembly (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Astonishing 2600 demo made in 2014","quote":"2600 programming is alive in the demo scene. This was just created in 2014 and is completely insane","text":"2600 programming is alive in the demo scene. This was just created in 2014 and is completely insane: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19041111,"author":"basementcat","month":"2019-01","story":"8bit Workshop","stance":"reference","why":"Example of extreme demos on limited hardware","quote":"it is pretty clear that the hardware that I work on is ridiculously overpowered.","text":"After seeing demos like Bang!/XayaX [1] and Chiphead/Altair [2], it is pretty clear that the hardware that I work on is ridiculously overpowered. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["QTJRwKOFddc",1],["YyIQKBzIuBY",1]]},"A0VYsiMtrNE":{"wy":"HN users share it as a delightful example of people making real music from obsolete electronics and self-built instruments.","q":{"x":"It's this band from Japan who make incredible music with instruments they made themselves.","i":47702058,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47702058,"author":"solstice","month":"2026-04","story":"Ask HN: Any Interesting Niche Hobbies?","stance":"rec","why":"Japanese band making incredible music with self-made instruments","quote":"It's this band from Japan who make incredible music with instruments they made themselves.","text":"I dig the electroduotone. Congrats! Have you heard of electronicos fantasticos? It's this band from Japan who make incredible music with instruments they made themselves. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41149981,"author":"jamdav16","month":"2024-08","story":"p5.js","stance":"rec","why":"Japanese group making music from repurposed old electronics","quote":"Super interesting stuff!","text":"Reminds me of Electronicos Fantasticos (Japan) who re-purposes old items (TV's old fans, barcode scanners etc...) and makes electronic music. Super interesting stuff! [VIDEO]"},{"id":25453465,"author":"entire-name","month":"2020-12","story":"Transmits AM radio on computers without radio transmitting hardware","stance":"rec","why":"Japanese group making electromagnetic-themed music","quote":"I was browsing YouTube the other day, and got recommended something similar","text":"I was browsing YouTube the other day, and got recommended something similar: [VIDEO] Very interesting indeed! The title of the video translates to \"Electromagnetic festival music\"."},{"id":39573624,"author":"kaycebasques","month":"2024-03","story":"Wintergatan Marble Machine (2016) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Group playing TVs and a space heater as guitars","quote":"They also figured out how to somehow play TVs and a space heater as guitars??","text":"Actually I did stumble across it on my \"weird music instruments\" rabbithole last night! They also figured out how to somehow play TVs and a space heater as guitars?? [VIDEO]"}]},"K_oET45GzMI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a detailed documentary on the Soros-versus-Bank-of-England episode.","q":{"x":"This wonderful BBC documentary explains everything on detail.","i":21456568,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21456568,"author":"elmar","month":"2019-11","story":"When George Soros Broke the British Pound (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"BBC documentary explaining the incident in detail.","quote":"This wonderful BBC documentary explains everything on detail.","text":"This incident was the main reason the UK didn't enter the Euro. This wonderful BBC documentary explains everything on detail. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13863155,"author":"shitgoose","month":"2017-03","story":"SEC Rejects Rule Change for Bitcoin Exchange-Traded Fund [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of origins of the crisis and political mistakes.","quote":"a good overview of the origins of the crisis.","text":"[VIDEO] a good overview of the origins of the crisis. how politicians for their own shortsighted gains made one poor decision after another and led the country into financial crisis."},{"id":7752957,"author":"loso","month":"2014-05","story":"The Trade of the Century: When George Soros Broke the British Pound","stance":"rec","why":"Good BBC documentary on Black Wednesday from the government side.","quote":"A pretty good documentary from the BBC about Black Wednesday.","text":"A pretty good documentary from the BBC about Black Wednesday. Shows more from the government side and the mistakes they made than Soros part but still gives you a good understanding of what happened. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21455734,"author":"hogFeast","month":"2019-11","story":"When George Soros Broke the British Pound (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on the BoE and Soros trade.","quote":"Documentary on this -","text":"The only reason he could put on the trade in the size that he did was because the BoE took the other side (they sold $27bn worth of £ and Soros had a $10bn position) and believed they were omnipotent (even amongst British civil servants, the BoE was unusually stacked with \"god complex\" types...and still is to a certain extent). Documentary on this - [VIDEO]"}]},"QVBlnCTu9Ms":{"wy":"Recommended in estimation and sprint debates as a well-reasoned account of where waste is and isn't in agile.","q":{"x":"If you want a well-reasoned discussion of where the waste is and isn't, I highly recommend Allen Holub's talk #NoEstimates","i":33096998,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33096998,"author":"coryfklein","month":"2022-10","story":"I don’t believe in sprints","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well-reasoned discussion of where waste is and isn't in agile.","quote":"If you want a well-reasoned discussion of where the waste is and isn't, I highly recommend Allen Holub's talk #NoEstimates","text":"One could write a solid critique of sprints and how they help or don't, and where time is wasted, but this article is overzealous and comes off as motivated more by anger than reasoning. If you want a well-reasoned discussion of where the waste is and isn't, I highly recommend Allen Holub's talk #NoEstimates [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19549979,"author":"Vinalin","month":"2019-04","story":"Deadlines Are Killing Us, and Almost Everything Else I Know About Leadership","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues estimates cause impossible deadlines and shows predictive alternatives.","quote":"I found it extremely interesting and highly recommend it.","text":"There's an interesting video by Allen Holub that I've seen on the somewhat related topic of ridding software development of estimates (#NoEstimates)[1]. He argues that they're responsible for deadlines that are impossible to meet due to them being based completely on a guess. He then goes on to talk about how we can still get predictive power without giving estimates. I found it extremely interesting and highly recommend it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46185708,"author":"hal-eisen","month":"2025-12","story":"Estimates are difficult for developers and product owners","stance":"reference","why":"NoEstimates talk cited against estimation.","quote":"But estimation sets everyone up for disappointment.","text":"[VIDEO] #NoEstimates Yes, be agile. Yes, measure all the things. But estimation sets everyone up for disappointment."},{"id":38192437,"author":"codr7","month":"2023-11","story":"Hard-to-swallow truths they won't tell you about software engineer job","stance":"reference","why":"Supports tracking backlog tasks for projections instead of estimates.","quote":"","text":"Planning doesn't mean estimation though; we all know it's just guesswork, and basing business decisions on guesswork is madness. On top of that it's a lot of wasted time and effort. Milestones and projections is a much more constructive approach, simply tracking the number of tasks in your backlog over time will give you an increasingly correct projection of when the project will be finished. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32051831,"author":"azemetre","month":"2022-07","story":"Microsoft researched what made employees happy","stance":"reference","why":"Shows estimating makes no difference to cumulative-flow diagrams, at 26m15s.","quote":"Money segment is at the 26m15s mark.","text":"Apparently if you do zero estimating (or just count the amount of stories), it makes ZERO difference when calculating your cumulative-flow diagram. [VIDEO] Money segment is at the 26m15s mark. The wasted effort of trying to estimate stories provides zero business value in this context."},{"id":26190373,"author":"tekkno89","month":"2021-02","story":"How I started believing in Cycle Time over Estimation","stance":"reference","why":"Conference talk on why past data does not predict new work.","quote":"he also links to a conference talk about this.","text":"he also links to a conference talk about this. \"you can make a claim that estimates are based on past behavior, but the fact is that what you're implementing is something that hasn't been implemented before. So any kind of measurement that you've made of something that has happened in the past is not going to impact what you're doing now\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":26190724,"author":"snorberhuis","month":"2021-02","story":"How I started believing in Cycle Time over Estimation","stance":"reference","why":"Distinguishes data-driven prediction from estimation.","quote":"He tries to make the distinction between estimation and prediction is that the latter is only data driven.","text":"Thank you for your comment! > But I don't get how they went from that to \"estimations are bad\". It seems to me that they're just doing better estimations now by looking at historical data? For me personally, I went from estimations are just really poor even for the most simple things and not worth the effort. It is correct that is can still be seen as migrating to a better form of estimation. If you look at the conference talk by Allen Holub[1]. He tries to make the distinction between estimation and prediction is that the latter is only data driven. > Maybe the team leader is confused because they thought this was a man-hour estimation? And they're now afraid that one developer will work full-time five days on it? That could be the case. I never looked at it that way. > Doesn't this imply that their 10 minute estimation was done completely without asking the team? Yes, it was done with just the team lead and me. We migrated away from Scrum at that point and we did not do full team refinement with estimations sessions pre-sprint. We would collaborate on the technical solution for any story when we would pick it. This could be full team to two engineers. The engineer that brought up the bug would probably bring up the bug. > Really? You couldn't consider the complexity of a release process you designed yourself? You honestly thought this was a 10 minute task even though you knew you were dealing with a legacy system? Yes, I honestly did! It was just a simple code change in our"}]},"cTsEzmFamZ8":{"wy":"Suggested as a starting point for understanding Yang's capitalist case for UBI, though some note it is long and dated.","q":{"x":"The Joe Rogan interview is a good place to start","i":21154643,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21154643,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2019-10","story":"Stockton, CA Tested Universal Basic Income. Here’s How Recipients Spent It","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point on Yang's capitalist case for UBI.","quote":"The Joe Rogan interview is a good place to start","text":"A public jobs/infrastructure program would work something like this: various states would lobby for money to spend in their states, $billions in large contracts would be awarded to politically connected corporations that would bill at $100/hr keep a large percentage and subcontract the work at $40/hr to smaller businesses that in turn would keep a percentage and hire people to do the work for $15/hr. That is trickle down economics. If instead you distributed the $billions to every citizen in the country, the vast majority of the money would be spent in local economies on things like day care, car or home repair, taking your kids in for dental cleaning. That is a trickle up economy. I too reasoned that UBI was liberal social nonsense. But I've been following Andrew Yang's presidential campaign and his justifications make sense from a capitalist point of view. As Yang points out - this is capitalism that doesn't start at zero. It has benefits. Check out his long interviews on Youtube. The Joe Rogan interview is a good place to start [VIDEO] . If you don't have the time check out his talk with the AARP [LINK] or the AFL-CIO [LINK]"},{"id":20269619,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2019-06","story":"Sanders to propose canceling entire $1.6T in US student loan debt","stance":"rec","why":"Long-form interview on Yang's proposals.","quote":"You should checkout his proposals. There are long form interviews on Youtube.","text":"That is candidate Andrew Yang's proposal. 120K to every American over 10 years. You should checkout his proposals. There are long form interviews on Youtube. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19295853,"author":"hanniabu","month":"2019-03","story":"France Plans 5% Digital Tax as Governments Chase Internet Giants","stance":"rec","why":"Yang on value-added tax, automation and basic income.","quote":"there's a lot of other interesting discussions here that are related to this topic, such as automation and basic income.","text":"I was listening to Presidential candidate Andrew Yang in his podcast with Joe Rogan. His plans, since there's a lot of avoidance, is to attempt a different route called a \"value added\" tax where there's a tax for trucking mileage and online transactions. He mentioned other countries to both of these, but don't remember which he mentioned off the top of my head. Here's the podcast for those interested, there's a lot of other interesting discussions here that are related to this topic, such as automation and basic income. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19525614,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2019-03","story":"Boston Dynamics’ new robot stacks boxes [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Longer exposition of Yang's UBI ideas.","quote":"for a longer exposition of his ideas see","text":"This might be good point to mention the only 2020 presidential candidate who is talking about how UBI could address this seemingly inevitable future that is closer than we think. Andrew Yang. See his discussion with students at Georgetown Univ. [LINK] or for a longer exposition of his ideas see [VIDEO]"},{"id":22134771,"author":"godelski","month":"2020-01","story":"The Yang Gang and Its Bots","stance":"reference","why":"Often-suggested podcast, but considered outdated and long.","quote":"Lots of people suggest the Joe Rogan podcast [0], but that is old, things have changed in his campaign, and it is long.","text":"It was a poor joke making a critique of the Medium post. > It does make me more interested in hearing what Yang has to say because it seems honest and like a statement from someone who hasn't been doing political activism for years. Lots of people suggest the Joe Rogan podcast [0], but that is old, things have changed in his campaign, and it is long. I rather like the Now This interview [1] (30 mins). Others like the Iowa Town Hall[2] (hour). The reasons I personally like Yang are #1, climate (I'm a scientist myself and work in labs with many climate scientists. I don't think his plan is going to solve everything, but I like it more than the other candidates. It is clear to me he has talked to experts), #2 I think he's trying to answer an important question that I've been curious about for a long time \"How do we transition into a post scarcity society\". Or specifically \"how do you transition into an economy where you do not need a large number of workers. What do you do if 10% of your population is unemployable, not because of lack of skills, but because lack of demand?\" It is the reason I am interested in UBI in the first place (where I've read a lot about here on HN). So I rather like his talk about changing the rules of the economy and making them human-centric. I do personally see a drastically changing economy and society, and I think he is asking the right questions about it. I have also seen him change positions after talking to experts, which I think is an essential q"}],"r":[["OmJ-4B-mS-Y",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["S9JGmA5_unY",1],["vBiIDwBOqQA",1],["dW8Cy6WrO94",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1]]},"lWZ2Bz0tS-s":{"wy":"Recommended as a companion to reading the dense book.","q":{"x":"They’re great to watch as you work through the book","i":21556220,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":26285061,"author":"xtiansimon","month":"2021-02","story":"The Art of Reading More Effectively and Efficiently","stance":"rec","why":"MIT video series helps with a difficult book","quote":"You may even need the assistance of this MIT Open CourseWare video series on YouTube [2] about the book.","text":"> \"...interesting trend in our discourse...\" > \"...more and more moralizing and virtue signaling. So much ... \"motivation porn\"...\" > \"..aspirational message - imagine yourself rich, happy, driving a Lamborghini..\" > \"...too many people have come to see reading as a lofty...\" > \"reading is just a hobby\" > \"Making it into such a self-serious pursuit kills most of the joy.\" > \"...you read books, we all read books. Everybody poops, and everybody reads books.\" WHAT?? This comment is apoplectic with contradictory statements and false beliefs. What could possibly be accomplished by it? It's an unapologetic rant on reading and writing on, of all places, one of the most text focused news and discussion sites on internet. Sheesh! Clearly, for some people reading is not just for entertainment, or 'joy'. And, clearly, for some of these people, their joy is also writing about reading (hence the OP). I do agree on one thing suggested by the response, though not said directly. Reading and Writing may converge into a bizarre infinite loop. If you find this maddening, rage inducing, then consider yourself warned! There is one book which you must not read. Dare I say it's name? YES! I DARE! Godel, Escher, Bach by Douglas Hofstadter [1]. It is not an easy read for the shitter. You may even need the assistance of this MIT Open CourseWare video series on YouTube [2] about the book. Read this book at your own PERIL! Extreme brain expansion may occur. WHEW. (Now I'm going to site quietly and medit"},{"id":21556220,"author":"azhenley","month":"2019-11","story":"Gödel, Escher, Bach","stance":"rec","why":"MIT course recordings to watch alongside reading the book","quote":"They’re great to watch as you work through the book","text":"MIT had a course on the book with recordings on YouTube. They’re great to watch as you work through the book: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14286461,"author":"dugditches","month":"2017-05","story":"Show HN: 100M Books – Open a new tab, discover a new book","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent MIT course on the book, all lectures on YouTube","quote":"There's an excellent MIT course for it.","text":"I personally found it 'heavy'. There's a lot to think about and take in. As it's not like reading typical technical books or papers. There's a great charm/fun and cleverness once things fall into place. There's an excellent MIT course for it. All the Lectures on Youtube as well. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["DxL2HoqLbyA",1],["mbyG85GZ0PI",1],["gRRvjZ_XNog",1]]},"nsJZ_kKjXcE":{"wy":"HN users recommend it as an amusing, practical overview of padlocks from cheap to high security.","q":{"x":"Here's an amusing overview of available padlocks, starting with crap Master Locks and moving upward.","i":13920411,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15352183,"author":"javajosh","month":"2017-09","story":"Coding in the open: how to do it securely","stance":"rec","why":"Compares lock designs and why they differ in pick resistance.","quote":"In this amusing video this guy goes over details of different locks","text":"Some locks with open designs are difficult, or impossible to pick. In this amusing video this guy goes over details of different locks, and explains why they are not all created equal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13920411,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-03","story":"The Document Which Used to Be Called the MIT Lockpicking Guide (1992)","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of padlocks from cheap to high security.","quote":"Here's an amusing overview of available padlocks, starting with crap Master Locks and moving upward.","text":"The top of the line in padlocks is the Sargent & Greenleaf 951 padlock, which is about $1300.[1] DoD uses these on weapons storage. Resists grinders, saws, liquid nitrogen, etc. Here's an amusing overview of available padlocks, starting with crap Master Locks and moving upward.[1] The ones that are hard to force or cut have large-diameter shackles, which probably won't fit a gym locker. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12282171,"author":"Zancarius","month":"2016-08","story":"Hacker Unlocks ‘High Security’ Electronic Safes Without a Trace","stance":"rec","why":"Guide to choosing a high security lock.","quote":"I'd recommend these videos","text":"Spend some time on bosnianbill's Youtube channel and you'll definitely get some tips. In particular, I'd recommend these videos: Buying Security: [LINK] Choosing a high Security Lock: [VIDEO] (Be ware of about 2 minutes 30 seconds of sarcasm in the last one!) Otherwise, browse through the rest of his videos, skipping the challenge locks unless it's something that fascinates you: [LINK] Essentially, cheap locks can be bypassed very easily (almost anything by Masterlock). More expensive locks ($60+) will have more pins, making them harder to pick, and often have replaceable cores. Of course, security isn't just about the lock, which is addressed in the first video. Hope this gives you a starting point!"}],"r":[["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["Uj-rK8V-rik",1],["3UxZDJ1HiPE",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["JOLtS4VUcvQ",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1],["tX4H_ctggYo",1],["KUdHIatS36A",1],["1tSqSMOyNFE",1],["xixM_cwSLcQ",1],["aoNOVlxJmow",1]]},"H2tuKiiznsY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a concise explanation of what caused the 737 Max crashes.","q":{"x":"VOX has a good explainer video on what caused the 737 Max to crash twice for anyone who doesn't know the details.","i":20376029,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21293808,"author":"atonse","month":"2019-10","story":"Boeing 2016 internal messages suggest employees may have misled FAA on 737 MAX","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows MCAS design was fundamentally flawed, not just pilot error","quote":"Watch this video","text":"So you want to use their historical record as some kind of justification for their dismal record with the 737 MAX, as if one is related to the other? Cold comfort to the 300+ lives, and families destroyed. Watch this video: [VIDEO] This isn't just some \"oops, these were badly trained pilots\" – the whole premise of MCAS is seriously screwed up and smacks of non-engineers running the place."},{"id":20376029,"author":"open-source-ux","month":"2019-07","story":"Boeing loses big order for 737 Max aircraft","stance":"rec","why":"Explainer on what caused the two 737 Max crashes","quote":"VOX has a good explainer video on what caused the 737 Max to crash twice for anyone who doesn't know the details.","text":"VOX has a good explainer video on what caused the 737 Max to crash twice for anyone who doesn't know the details. Essentially, Boeing installed sensors and software to automatically push the plane nose down if the plane angle was too steep as it ascended. This software/sensor combination is implicated as the cause of the recent crashes in Indonesia and Ethiopia. The real reason Boeing's new plane crashed twice : [VIDEO]"},{"id":20291602,"author":"joering2","month":"2019-06","story":"FAA Finds New Risk on 737 Max, Orders Boeing to Make Changes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The whole video - if correct, paints a grim picture of profits above PAXes...","text":"Meanwhile he claims 737 will be their safest plane ever: [LINK] Edit: unsure if this video is correct, but the MCAS system is classical \"solution\" to how in software a junior programmer would patch their software by adding more complexity to the code without realizing what that changes would further affect. It felt like they gave up on some solid solution and just decided to added a sensor who would overwrite pilot manuver. A similar situation would be downgrading a size of tires on a new model of a car (to be competitve with other auto makers that lower the price) and simply adding software blockage that you cannot turn wheels as violent as you could before because on thin thread that would lead to a car tipover. The whole video - if correct, paints a grim picture of profits above PAXes... [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["iuumnjJWFO4",1],["LsGZ_2RuJ2A",1],["Unzc731iCUY",1],["otAcmD6XEEE",1],["5FWWe2U41N8",1]]},"dX649lnKAU0":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an especially good explanation of analog TV and CRT shadow masks.","q":{"x":"This one on \"how analog tv works\" I thought was especially good.","i":18968519,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":18968519,"author":"canthonytucci","month":"2019-01","story":"Odd History of the SD Logo [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Especially good explanation of how analog TV works.","quote":"This one on \"how analog tv works\" I thought was especially good.","text":"So happy to see Technology Connections here, one of my absolute favorite YouTube channels and one that I have learned a lot from. This one on \"how analog tv works\" I thought was especially good. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47639854,"author":"AlexDragusin","month":"2026-04","story":"Why Inventing Color TV Was So Difficult [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Entire series on how analog color TV works, plus a playlist.","quote":"Alec Watson from Technology Connections did an entire series on this subject","text":"Alec Watson from Technology Connections did an entire series on this subject: (How Analog Color TV Works: The Beginnings) [VIDEO] Then the playlist on TV stuff: [LINK] Don't blame me for the rabbit hole :P"},{"id":24541021,"author":"s_gourichon","month":"2020-09","story":"Good Quality DOSBox Video Capture","stance":"reference","why":"Explains shadow masks and why CRTs were not that crisp.","quote":"this is explained e.g. on \"How Analog Color TV Works: The Beginnings\" by \"Technology Connections","text":"It is good that this article exists. It explains how to convert screenshot and video capture provided by DOSBox into web-oriented formats, with some welcome considerations about pixel aspect ratio. This is a good basis! > but in these screenshots we want to retain the crisp and jagged edges that is typical of DOSBox and the old CRT monitors. Therefore we use the -sample option that does not introduce any new colours. Instead it uses nearest-neighbour interpolation (point sampling) to decide the colours of the scaled image. There are 2 ironic things: * modern world drifts away from pixel-to-pixel rendering, and indeed the web page presenting the captures constrains it (img in a div in a main with max-width:42em. So, web viewing does introduce some smoothing * the CRTs-based screens of the days were not that crisp! Especially the color ones, they had a \"shadow mask\", a grid of RGB spots which had no reason to be aligned with the pixel locations (this is explained e.g. on \"How Analog Color TV Works: The Beginnings\" by \"Technology Connections\" [VIDEO] or jump straight to [VIDEO] for explanations and [VIDEO] for close-to-screen example ) > The screenshots obtained in this manner have an aspect ratio of 8:5 which makes the output look stretched horizontally. Because modern format and/or modern displaying program tend to assume square pixels, which originals were not. Same issue on Amiga (pixel aspect ratio was even different on North-American Amigas and European Amigas) and many ot"}],"r":[["HxaD_trXwRE",1],["f6kdp27TYZs",1],["3DtUzH3zoFo",1],["tfAnxaWiSeE",1]]},"1AcEt073Uds":{"wy":"HN people cite it to debunk oven-reflow and reballing repair folklore, and as part of Rossmann's critiques of Apple hardware quality.","q":{"x":"It's pretty funny. He thinks oven reflows are a bad idea.","i":19665383,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19665383,"author":"ScottFree","month":"2019-04","story":"Baked MacBook Air: A cautionary recipe","stance":"rec","why":"Rossmann argues oven reflow and reballing GPUs is a bad idea","quote":"It's pretty funny. He thinks oven reflows are a bad idea.","text":"Has anyone watched Louis Rossman's video series on this topic? It's pretty funny. He thinks oven reflows are a bad idea. Reballing flip chip GPUs is BULLSHIT - the truth about dead laptop GPUs & repairing them. - [VIDEO] Luckily, he also shows the proper way to do it: Linus Attempts BGA Graphics Chip Repair! - [LINK]"},{"id":17603356,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2018-07","story":"Apple releases software fix for MacBook Pro slowdown","stance":"rec","why":"Rossmann on laptop GPU failures and repair","quote":"Watch a bunch of Louis Rossman YouTube videos.","text":"Watch a bunch of Louis Rossman YouTube videos. He knows all about the seedy underbelly of Macbook hardware design mistakes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19665456,"author":"zitterbewegung","month":"2019-04","story":"Baked MacBook Air: A cautionary recipe","stance":"reference","why":"Debunks the oven reflow technique as a myth","quote":"The above technique is a myth","text":"The above technique is a myth . See [VIDEO] . Trying to randomly unsolder and put back resistors or other components when you don’t know what the error is is another bad idea. The author should have tried to actually attempt to fix the logic board first. But before all that he should have inspected the board and use a voltmeter to see what the issue is. But even doing that he might need a replacement part."},{"id":18424300,"author":"bunnycorn","month":"2018-11","story":"Apple Admits to Hardware Quality Problems with iPhone X, MacBook Pro","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here, he said that Apple puts a chip to break MacBooks after 3 years.","text":"Yes. How many do you want? Here, he said that Apple puts a chip to break MacBooks after 3 years. [VIDEO] Here, he says that \"Apple\" (actually a Customs office) sent a letter to him to pay for batteries that were legitimate, later, on Reddit, he admitted he bought fake batteries from China that had Apple logos (and therefore, should be, as they did) caught by the customs (because saying otherwise would get him in jail), his friends at Reddit, deleted the comments, but there are still copies of what he said: [LINK] Also, in his video, he says that Apple makes products \"vintage\" or \"obsolete\" after 4 or 5 years. The newest \"vintage\" Apple product in late 2018, is from 2011: [LINK]"}],"r":[["n691pLhQBkw",1],["PsPX0nElJ0k",1],["rE3j_RHkqJc",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["RNFesa01llk",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["IrbJYsep45E",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1]]},"9q3uNcJh4pc":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible demonstration of how anyone can collect micrometeorites at home.","q":{"x":"Here's a good video running through one way to do this at home","i":41176259,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41176259,"author":"aaron695","month":"2024-08","story":"Does space dust fall on the roof of my house?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows a way to collect micrometeorites at home with real science","quote":"Here's a good video running through one way to do this at home","text":"Here's a good video running through one way to do this at home - [VIDEO] (I really like the ending, it's real science which is rare these days) There are a heap of better links to micrometeorites stories than this stackexchange. Discussed many times on HN."},{"id":29008755,"author":"jrockway","month":"2021-10","story":"Project Stardust: A Photographer Scours Rooftops for Minuscule Cosmic Particles","stance":"rec","why":"One of several videos on hunting micrometeorites on rooftops","quote":"Here's a few videos on the subject","text":"I feel like everyone is scouring their roof for micrometeorites these days. Here's a few videos on the subject: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29011020,"author":"atombender","month":"2021-10","story":"Project Stardust: A Photographer Scours Rooftops for Minuscule Cosmic Particles","stance":"rec","why":"Great video about Larsen's work collecting microscopic meteorites","quote":"Here [1] is a great video about Larsen and his work on collecting microscopic meteroites.","text":"Here [1] is a great video about Larsen and his work on collecting microscopic meteroites. Larsen's book, \"In Search of Stardust: Amazing Micrometeorites and Their Terrestrial Imposters\" [2] is also great, a gorgeous coffee table book with lots of high-quality photos of meteorites of the kind that you see on the web site. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":19180683,"author":"nkurz","month":"2019-02","story":"Tiny meteorites are everywhere. Here's how to find them [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Cites a specific moment in the video about electron microscope confirmation","quote":"","text":"I don't think the video mentioned anything about an electron microscope. It's not emphasized, and not present at all in the audio, but there is \"mention\" within the video. At 8:40 in the video ( [VIDEO] ), when showing the response from Larsen, the sentence after the highlighting reads \"a scanning electron microscope (SEM) examination ... would be necessary\". So you are right that it wasn't done, but it apparently would have been the next step for a confirmation."}],"r":[["uqH_Y1TupoQ",1],["5_A8dq2fA5o",1],["Klkpk_hO4FQ",1],["ve4q-1D_Ajo",1],["zT58pzY41wA",1],["Wpzvaqypav8",1],["cIcbAMO6sxo",1],["wSdV1M7n4gQ",1]]},"EgMqhEMhVV8":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an analysis of PewDiePie's popularity and of how YouTube's recommendations favored gaming content.","q":{"x":"Here's a video with some interesting theories about how PewDiePie, and other gaming channels, are so popular","i":7902712,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7902712,"author":"Kronopath","month":"2014-06","story":"YouTube's Biggest Draw Plays Games, Earns $4 Million a Year","stance":"rec","why":"Theories on why gaming channels suit YouTube promotion","quote":"Here's a video with some interesting theories about how PewDiePie, and other gaming channels, are so popular","text":"Here's a video with some interesting theories about how PewDiePie, and other gaming channels, are so popular because they're effectively hyper-tuned for Youtube's promotion algorithms: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7806602,"author":"Houshalter","month":"2014-05","story":"Check your Google history","stance":"rec","why":"Theory that YouTube's watch-time recommendations favored let's plays","quote":"There is an interesting theory on how that happened","text":"There is an interesting theory on how that happened: [VIDEO] Basically Google changed their algorithm to recommend videos based on time watched instead of just views. Which massively favored let's plays because they are so long. They also promote channels based on geographical location. Pewdiepie had an advantage somehow because he had a fanbase in Sweden and moved to Italy. This might also explain parent comment's problem (recommendations favoring let's plays because they are longer.)"},{"id":15676840,"author":"cerealbad","month":"2017-11","story":"The jobs of the future are already here, and some are weird","stance":"reference","why":"12-minute video on streamer and YouTuber economics","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] (12mins) [LINK] (13mins) if you prefer to read instead. Online Multiplayer Video Games: Peak Daily Users 5,000,000 League of Legends (Riot Games 2009, Free / Cosmetic microtransactions) 2,552,565 PLAYER UNKNOWN'S BATTLEGROUNDS (Steam / Bluehole Inc. 2017, $30 / Cosmetic microtransactions) 727,479 Dota 2 (Steam / Valve Corporation 2013, Free / Cosmetic microtransactions) 547,883 Counter-Strike: Global Offensive (Valve Corporation 2012, $15 / Cosmetic microtransactions) -in-built audience of about 500k-5million -low barrier of entry high skill ceiling -former professional players stream their games daily and give advice -livestream audience of 10k-20k concurrent viewers -streamer can earn up to 5 million USD a year from: monthly subscription model for cosmetic chat features, ad and merchandise revenue streams There are many intersections between genres, because teenagers are interested in a lot of things, fashion, music, video-games, tv shows. There is also a certain infantisation of 20-30's due to the increasingly common choice to put off starting a family until late 30s. A good example is the two brothers Logan (22) and Jake (20) Paul. Former viners(1) who were on a Disney tv show (2) moved to youtube (3) bringing over their fans from vine and disney and adding new ones due to clever self-promotion. Any 5 to 15 year old in America knows their name and about 5-10 million watch their videos daily. What is their talent? In a post-modern twist, the story of their success "},{"id":9847302,"author":"Spellman","month":"2015-07","story":"YouTube gaming star PewDiePie 'earned $7M in 2014'","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of PewDiePie's popularity","quote":"Here's someone's analysis of why he got so popular.","text":"Plus he has moved to 3 different countries, picking up viewers from them all. And how YouTube's Recommended Videos system works. Here's someone's analysis of why he got so popular. Looks, editing skills, and personality also helped build him a niche. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_YysNM2JoFo",1],["zQGOcOUBi6s",1],["HKQDSgBHPfY",1],["sULa9Lc4pck",1],["nsjDnYxJ0bo",1]]},"IjJmTeBSEzU":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on military AI as a vivid depiction of where autonomous warfare could lead.","q":{"x":"I think that scifi shorts have the duty and power to show us the paths that certain developments entail.","i":16761679,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":35768427,"author":"jareklupinski","month":"2023-05","story":"Necrobrands – Digital End-Stage Capitalism","stance":"rec","why":"Short on ceding decisions to a power that decides for us.","quote":"i love a reason to bring up this short on what will happen when we just cede everything to a power that makes decisions for us","text":"i love a reason to bring up this short on what will happen when we just cede everything to a power that makes decisions for us: [VIDEO] - \"LAST DAY OF WAR\" - by Dima Fedotov | TheCGBros"},{"id":28645144,"author":"jareklupinski","month":"2021-09","story":"Apple's Power Move to Kneecap Facebook Advertising Is Working","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter loves this kind of CGI animated short.","quote":"I love these kinds of stories","text":"I love these kinds of stories > CGI 3D Animated Shorts : \"LAST DAY OF WAR\" - by Dima Fedotov [VIDEO]"},{"id":34797406,"author":"jareklupinski","month":"2023-02","story":"The US Air Force successfully tested AI-controlled jet fighter","stance":"reference","why":"Endgame of AI-controlled warfare: machines keep following orders.","quote":"The last war broke out. All the people are dead, but the machines continue dutifully to follow orders.","text":"Don't forget the endgame [VIDEO] > The last war broke out. All the people are dead, but the machines continue dutifully to follow orders."},{"id":26550527,"author":"bartmika","month":"2021-03","story":"DARPA now has AI-controlled F-16s working as a team in virtual dogfights","stance":"reference","why":"Sci-fi short depicting machines fighting on after humanity is gone.","quote":"Here are two science fiction short videos of an artist depicting such a future","text":"> What puzzles me is that as of yet, there doesn't seem to be any talk of using \"AI\" (ie Neural Net stuff) for planning and strategy, which seems like a MUCH more powerful application than this stuff. It'll probably be so effective, it'll still be fighting long after all of humanity has destroyed itself and left an irradiated environment. Here are two science fiction short videos of an artist depicting such a future: \"LAST DAY OF WAR\" - by Dima Fedotov | TheCGBros - [VIDEO] \"Fortress/Крепость\" - by Dima Fedotof | TheCGBros - [LINK] (edit: typofix)"},{"id":16761679,"author":"fabianhjr","month":"2018-04","story":"Google Workers Urge C.E.O. To Pull Out of Pentagon A.I. Project","stance":"reference","why":"Sci-fi short showing where autonomous warfare leads.","quote":"I think that scifi shorts have the duty and power to show us the paths that certain developments entail.","text":"The problem emerges when an actor most become a monster to win, sort of like Mutually Assured Destruction. I think that scifi shorts have the duty and power to show us the paths that certain developments entail. For example: - Slaughterbots: [LINK] - Last Day of War: [VIDEO]"}]},"IoL8g0W9gAQ":{"wy":"HN points to it as an accessible explanation of why the USPS struggles financially, especially in threads on mail delays and international rates.","q":{"x":"Jon Oliver does an excellent job of explaining","i":24153471,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24153471,"author":"chb","month":"2020-08","story":"Save the USPS","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why the USPS cannot turn a profit","quote":"Jon Oliver does an excellent job of explaining","text":"For those wondering why the USPS cannot turn a profit, Jon Oliver does an excellent job of explaining: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23963895,"author":"lexicality","month":"2020-07","story":"Amazon gets priority while mail gets delayed, say US letter carriers","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how defunding left the USPS with less money","quote":"John Oliver did an interesting video on this","text":"John Oliver did an interesting video on this: [VIDEO] TL;DR: Deliberate government action to defund them has resulted in them having way less money than you would expect."},{"id":24003224,"author":"teh_klev","month":"2020-07","story":"Pending Postal Service Changes Could Delay Mail and Deliveries","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver segment boiling down USPS finances and politics","quote":"love him or hate him John Oliver boils this down to","text":"It has for many years: [LINK] Discussed here: [LINK] And love him or hate him John Oliver boils this down to: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28730470,"author":"RedShift1","month":"2021-10","story":"International Mail Service Suspensions","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver episode on rules that push USPS into losses","quote":"John Oliver had an episode about this","text":"No other company has to do this. They created this rule specifically to make the USPS run at a loss so they would have a reason to gut it. And that's what they're doing now, gutting it. John Oliver had an episode about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23677627,"author":"m000","month":"2020-06","story":"The U.S. can now set its own rates for mail from China and other countries","stance":"reference","why":"Explains USPS pricing law and international mail rates","quote":"Learned about it in a John Oliver's video [1]","text":"> why can't I choose a shipping option that costs something less than 50 cents to anywhere in the US? Not an American, but I believe there's a 2006 bill that prevents USPS from selling services below the cost. Learned about it in a John Oliver's video [1], mentioned somewhere else in this thread. I'd guess that Chinese epackets are part of an international agreement, so they are exempt. > The system was illogical. It would be illogical if USPS had flexible costs. But the USPS costs are more or less fixed. The mailman will go his round every day and will get his salary, even if he has little to deliver. So, you may as well have him deliver Chinese epackets, for whatever price the Chinese are willing to pay. And that's a good deal for the public. Otherwise the $1 trinket for China would be $6 (if Chinese had to pay full shipping), or $10 (if bought through a US-based reseller). [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SrU9YDoXE88",1],["pdJQ8iVTwj8",1],["I14b-C67EXY",1]]},"cDA3_5982h8":{"wy":"HN uses it to teach kids computational thinking and as a joke whenever specs, requirements, or LLM prompts are taken too literally.","q":{"x":"this video is a great way to show how difficult it is to teach a machine to do something and how clear instructions can help get things done","i":19746134,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19746134,"author":"srirangr","month":"2019-04","story":"Ask HN: How to Teach Coding?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how hard it is to teach a machine and why clear instructions help.","quote":"this video is a great way to show how difficult it is to teach a machine to do something and how clear instructions can help get things done","text":"1. Tell them what to do but do not tell them how to do it. (Just give a few pointers otherwise they'll be lost.) 2. Give them a playground to fail - Offload any non-business critical tasks and let them make mistakes. No one I know ever learnt programming without making any mistakes. Immediately tell them about best practices and how to avoid such mistakes in future. 3. Show them the impact of their work - There's nothing more motivating than seeing the impact of one's work. 4. Build curiosity - Answer as many questions as you can answer. Admit when you don't know any answers and start looking it up on the web right in front of them. People pick habits by looking at their superiors. If it's absolute beginning to programming, this video is a great way to show how difficult it is to teach a machine to do something and how clear instructions can help get things done: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13675528,"author":"zyang","month":"2017-02","story":"Ask HN: How should I teach code to kids?","stance":"rec","why":"Segue into technical thinking for young kids.","quote":"I thought it was a great segue into technical thinking for young kids.","text":"Have you seen the exact instruction challenge on youtube? I thought it was a great segue into technical thinking for young kids. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44399053,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-06","story":"Qwen VLo: From “Understanding” the World to “Depicting” It","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"As a ML researcher and a degree holding physicist, I'm really hesitant to use the words \"understanding\" and \"describing\" (much less hesitant) around these models. I don't find the language helpful and think it's mostly hateful tbh. The reason we use math in physics is because of its specificity. The same reason coding is so hard [0,1]. I think people aren't giving themselves enough credit here for how much they (you) understand about things. It is the nuances that really matter. There's so much detail here and we often forget how important they are because it is just normal to us. It's like forgetting about the ground you walk upon. I think something everyone should read about is Asimov's \"Relativity of Wrong\"[2]. This is what we want to see in these systems if we want to start claiming they understand things. We want to see them to deduction and abduction. To be able to refine concepts and ideas. To be able to discover things that are more than just a combination of things they've ingested. What's really difficult here is that we train these things on all human knowledge and just reciting back that knowledge doesn't demonstrate intelligence. It's very unlikely that they losslessly compress that knowledge into these model sizes, but without very deep investigation into that data and probing at this knowledge it is very hard to understand what it knows and what it memorizes. Really, this is a very poor way to go about trying to make intelligence[3], or at least making intellig"},{"id":17669565,"author":"ygra","month":"2018-08","story":"CS Unplugged: Computer Science Without a Computer","stance":"reference","why":"Father does exact-instructions exercise with kids.","quote":"a father does this with his kids","text":"I've seen this video a while ago, where a father does this with his kids: [VIDEO] Lots of frustration about the stupid robot and I guess your school kids weren't much different."},{"id":39867617,"author":"wigiv","month":"2024-03","story":"Edgar, build a Dyson swarm","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of the \"Exact Instructions Challenge\" videos where kids write instructions for some everyday task","text":"Reminds me of the \"Exact Instructions Challenge\" videos where kids write instructions for some everyday task - like making a PB&J sandwich - and parents, hilariously, follow the instructions verbatim. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39478151,"author":"ygra","month":"2024-02","story":"The Xylophone Maze: Screen-free coding for children","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Sounds like it could devolve similarly to what's being done here","text":"Sounds like it could devolve similarly to what's being done here: [VIDEO] I sometimes try with my children. Having one on my shoulder and pulling either the left or the right ear to tell me where to go. The way home from kindergarten can then take a while, depending on how attentive they are. Of course, parent stumbling into hedges makes for a good laugh, so that may also sometimes be intentional."},{"id":36601058,"author":"NKosmatos","month":"2023-07","story":"The hardest part of building software is not coding, it's requirements","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think about the funny “exact instructions” video :-)","text":"Whenever I read/hear about project/software requirements that need to be followed exactly, I think about the funny “exact instructions” video :-) [VIDEO]"},{"id":35228640,"author":"illiarian","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: Why do many CS graduates lack foundational knowledge?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory PB & Jelly Exact Instructions Challenge video","text":"Obligatory PB & Jelly Exact Instructions Challenge video: [VIDEO] The answer is: it runs on dad, poorly :)"},{"id":27159113,"author":"chromanoid","month":"2021-05","story":"A URL Lengthener","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this :D","text":"\"Monkey Paw Development\" was a new one for me :) Thank you! Great analogy. Reminds me of this :D [VIDEO]"}]},"evQsOFQju08":{"wy":"HN points people to it as an accessible explanation of what qualia are.","q":{"x":"Vsauce did a good video on it","i":23592745,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23592745,"author":"behindsight","month":"2020-06","story":"“You want to know something about how bullshit insane our brains are?” (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on qualia and subjective color","quote":"Vsauce did a good video on it","text":"You might be interested in the concept of qualia [0] Vsauce did a good video on it, \"Is Your Red The Same as My Red?\" [1] 0: [LINK] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20836864,"author":"0xdeadb00f","month":"2019-08","story":"Organoids Are Not Brains. How Are They Making Brain Waves?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what qualia is","quote":"wondering what \"qualia\" actually is, I found an old episode of VSause about it","text":"For anyone who read this comment and was wondering what \"qualia\" actually is, I found an old episode of VSause about it: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":41433266,"author":"danbruc","month":"2024-09","story":"Is My Blue Your Blue?","stance":"reference","why":"Whether your blue might be like my red","quote":"whether your perception of blue might be like my perception of red","text":"I expected this to be about the question whether your perception of blue might be like my perception of red. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18446414,"author":"mygo","month":"2018-11","story":"Food taste 'not protected by copyright' rules EU court","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"well different people have different retinas, how are you going to make sure there is a universally agreed representation, of a colored trademark =p [VIDEO]"}]},"iN1MsCXkPSA":{"wy":"Shared in Crafting Interpreters threads because it shows the care behind the book and is satisfying to watch.","q":{"x":"the time-lapse video of the entire process is like ASMR for geeks","i":22788833,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":30335732,"author":"vishnugupta","month":"2022-02","story":"Crafting Interpreters – Web Version","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the hand-drawn illustrations in the book being made.","quote":"The author's love for their craft oozes through their work.","text":"Related. The author's love for their craft oozes through their work. If I'm not wrong every single illustration in the book is hand-drawn ; like literally. Take a look: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22788833,"author":"airstrike","month":"2020-04","story":"Crafting “Crafting Interpreters”","stance":"rec","why":"Time-lapse of hand-drawing the book's illustrations.","quote":"the time-lapse video of the entire process is like ASMR for geeks","text":"> I used to do graphic design, and I have this weird tic where any time I see something that looks handwritten, I look for multiple instances of the same letter to see if they are different or if the design just used a handwriting font. It’s almost always a handwriting font and I die a little inside to see the illusion evaporate. Not a graphic designer, but I do the exact same thing and feel the exact same way... Happy to see I'm not crazy alone And his look like really neat hand-drawn diagrams! I certainly appreciate the degree of craftsmanship and care displayed throughout both books As a someone put it on YouTube, the time-lapse video of the entire process is like ASMR for geeks [VIDEO]"},{"id":39486276,"author":"munificent","month":"2024-02","story":"Why Writing by Hand Is Better for Memory and Learning","stance":"reference","why":"Author's video of drawing the illustrations on paper.","quote":"Here's a video I put together showing the process","text":"You're welcome! People always assume I drew them digitally somehow, but nope, literally on paper with pencil and pen. Here's a video I put together showing the process: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27997954,"author":"munificent","month":"2021-07","story":"Crafting Interpreters is available in print","stance":"reference","why":"Author shows the process of hand-drawing the book's illustrations.","quote":"Here's a video of me making one","text":"Literally hand drawn with my hands. Here's a video of me making one: [VIDEO] It's, like, comically time consuming. But also pretty satisfying."}],"r":[["WHj2GaPuEhY",1],["KRcecxdGxvQ",1],["aeRVdYN6fE8",1],["I8LbkfSSR58",1],["b9IkpUYlOx8",1]]},"j9uRckJLqLk":{"wy":"Linked whenever the Curta or mechanical calculators come up, as a look at a working 3D-printed version.","q":{"x":"Adam unboxes his 3D-printed Curta on YouTube at","i":16040461,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":46076632,"author":"beeforpork","month":"2025-11","story":"Designing a Mechanical Calculator","stance":"rec","why":"A 3D printable Curta as inspiration for a mechanical calculator.","quote":"There's a 3D printable version of it.","text":"Maybe have a look at the Curta for inspiration of a mechanical calculator. There's a 3D printable version of it. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":18285849,"author":"function_seven","month":"2018-10","story":"The Slide Rule","stance":"rec","why":"A 3:1 scale 3D printed Curta replica.","quote":"A 3:1 scale replica of the Curta, 3D printed.","text":"I'm sure you've seen this already, but if not: [VIDEO] A 3:1 scale replica of the Curta, 3D printed."},{"id":16040461,"author":"jshprentz","month":"2017-12","story":"ReCurta: Our goal is to build the first Curta calculator since 1972","stance":"reference","why":"Adam Savage unboxes a 3D-printed Curta built by Marcus Wu.","quote":"Adam unboxes his 3D-printed Curta on YouTube at","text":"Marcus Wu designed, 3D-printed, and assembled a 3x scale working Curta calculator. He shared his models and progress on Thingverse at [LINK] His YouTube channel includes many videos of the development, assembly, and operation of his calculator. Watch a short demonstration at [LINK] Adam Savage commissioned Marcus to build a copy. Adam unboxes his 3D-printed Curta on YouTube at [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8VqXBA-sGHA",1],["64cEmjtwRgw",1]]},"o25I2fzFGoY":{"wy":"HN reaches for this clip whenever a thread involves euphemism or corporate and sanitized phrasing.","q":{"x":"George Carlin has a funny skit about how messaging has gotten softer over the years that you might enjoy.","i":37706351,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37706351,"author":"barelysapient","month":"2023-09","story":"Leaked screenshot shows Amazon now tracking individual employee attendance","stance":"rec","why":"Funny skit on how messaging has gotten softer","quote":"George Carlin has a funny skit about how messaging has gotten softer over the years that you might enjoy.","text":"In our entitled and highly litigious society I call this Corporate Normal Form. First Corporate Normal Form is messaging derogatories, or things perceived as such, to have a unoffensive coating and makes speaking about them not immediately trigger your fight or flight response. On a related note, George Carlin has a funny skit about how messaging has gotten softer over the years that you might enjoy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20402475,"author":"keldaris","month":"2019-07","story":"Living without the modern browser","stance":"rec","why":"Brilliant, thoughtful Carlin sketch on euphemistic language","quote":"George Carlin has a brilliant sketch on this very issue [1], which is both thoughtful and inoffensive","text":"If there existed some form of global consensus across the English-speaking world on what the inoffensive version is, I'd be happy to abide by it. In the meantime, suggesting that people on a venue as global as HN have some sort of a responsibility to follow and observe the random cultural variations in whichever narrow bubble you were referring to strikes me as presumptious and absurd. I also wish more people valued clear language over these battles of ever more contrived euphemisms. George Carlin has a brilliant sketch on this very issue [1], which is both thoughtful and inoffensive (specifically references \"handicapped\" around the 5:30 mark). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47478655,"author":"Quarrelsome","month":"2026-03","story":"Bored of eating your own dogfood? Try smelling your own farts","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's famous piece on losing meaning through softened language","quote":"George Carlin's famous piece on it","text":"because you lose the meaning over time. Emotional aspects of language can be useful. George Carlin's famous piece on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43179835,"author":"latexr","month":"2025-02","story":"Tell HN: Y Combinator backing AI company to abuse factory workers","stance":"reference","why":"Sterile phrasing recalls Carlin's soft language bit","quote":"it immediately made me think of George Carlin’s bit on soft language.","text":"> I'm biased of course, but when I read \"$X is backing $Y to do $Z\", I read $Z as what $X is specifically and intentionally backing $Y to do. Fair! I agree with you and I hadn’t realised the sentence as is was accusing Y Combinator in that sense. My intention was something along the lines of “Tell HN: Y Combinator backing AI company whose product aims to abuse factory workers” but it was too long and some nuance was lost in the trimming. > Nor is the phrase neutral; it's obviously accusatory. Again, I agree. But as I mentioned before, I also don’t see a non-critical stance which would make the situation worth commenting on, and I do believe it should be commented on. The only reason it’s worth commenting at all is because it’s bad. > Some commenters have suggested things like \"production line monitoring\". Which is a phrase so sterile and euphemistic it immediately made me think of George Carlin’s bit on soft language. [VIDEO] Also, I disagree with that description because it fails to capture the point (see below). > I'm sure it's true that technology like this can turn into a Taylorist nightmare, but there surely are also ways to use it humanely The crux of the matter to me is that this is inhumane by design . It’s not just the video, their page on YC’s website makes it patently clear via the written word they care about factory owners but not the workers . This is neither a marketing problem nor an issue of bad clients abusing the product—the founders plainly described how t"},{"id":42542212,"author":"fiforpg","month":"2024-12","story":"Toronto man creates tiny mobile homes to help unhoused people escape the cold","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin railing against soft language in his 1990 special","quote":"Here is a YT clip of George Carlin railing against it in his 1990 special","text":"Looks like this kind of soft / euphemistic language dates back to even earlier than that. Here is a YT clip of George Carlin railing against it in his 1990 special: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35624962,"author":"vincnetas","month":"2023-04","story":"MRI Brain Images Just Got 64M Times Sharper","stance":"reference","why":"Reminded of Carlin by wordy euphemism","quote":"This reminded me of \"George Carlin on soft language","text":"Off topic: \"high net-worth\" is word \"rich\" not politically correct anymore or what is the thinking behind using three words instead of one? This reminded me of \"George Carlin on soft language\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":33965265,"author":"throwawayacc3","month":"2022-12","story":"PhilTel is looking to install free payphones in Philadelphia","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin calling out euphemistic language decades ago","quote":"George Carlin called this specific phenomenon out decades ago [0].","text":"It's not \"imposing the necessity of the word\", it's calling out the nonsensical euphemistic language that gets churned out by the most neurotic of society and then imposed upon everyone. George Carlin called this specific phenomenon out decades ago [0]. You can kvetch about using words that signal your virtue, but homelessness has a codified definition [1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":34107338,"author":"dmix","month":"2022-12","story":"Are Progressives in Denial About Progress? Yes, but So Is Almost Everyone Else","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's soft language bit, relevant to modern speech norms","quote":"I’ve often wondered how George Carlin would react against the modern progressive movement towards gatekeeping speech.","text":"I’ve often wondered how George Carlin would react against the modern progressive movement towards gatekeeping speech. Especially with his soft language and dirty words bits. [VIDEO] Sometimes it’s easy to get so wrapped up in your well intentioned culture where you end up being ridiculous and detach yourself from reality."},{"id":9970106,"author":"dpc_pw","month":"2015-07","story":"GitHub threatens to shut down a repository for using the word 'retard'","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin bit used against euphemism","quote":"Always relevant George Carlin","text":"Idiocy of political correctness taking it's toll. Always relevant George Carlin: [VIDEO] In 20 years, next Github will be taking down projects that have \"cognitively impaired\" in it, because that's what we're going to be using to insult each other."}],"r":[["MO0r930Sn_8",1],["2DIl3Hfh9tY",1]]},"rQhzEnWCgHA":{"wy":"HN commenters reach for it whenever cargo bikes come up as a practical car replacement for families.","q":{"x":"I had my mind blown about Dutch-style cargo ebikes","i":35232322,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35232322,"author":"Rendello","month":"2023-03","story":"Cyclists Now Outnumber Motorists in City of London","stance":"rec","why":"Shows cargo ebikes hauling cargo or three kids","quote":"I had my mind blown about Dutch-style cargo ebikes","text":"I had my mind blown about Dutch-style cargo ebikes, bakfietsen . The cool thing is that you can use the bikes to haul cargo, or put three kids in the front in special harnesses when you need to: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32409930,"author":"audunw","month":"2022-08","story":"Electric Vehicles Are Going to Suck; Here's Why [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Not Just Bikes video on electric cargo bikes","quote":"Not Just Bikes has an excellent new video on this","text":"I didn't watch the whole thing, but did she mention electric bikes? IMO, Electric cars, or cars in general, are just fine. We just did a family road-trip around Denmark with our Ioniq 5 and it's absolutely perfect for that. The thing is, we should be renting them when we need them, and use bikes more the rest of the time. Electric bikes are quickly becoming a viable alternative to cars in many bike-friendly cities, and we're seeing an explosion in the choices of electric cargo bikes. Not Just Bikes has an excellent new video on this: [VIDEO] I rode my electric bike to work today in fact. Instead of our EV, ironically. But our family uses only one car at least. We don't necessarily need simpler cars, we need better electric bikes. Or electric motorbikes/scooters. And better public transportation. Something like this would be a nice option for some usecases: [LINK] GetAround is making renting cars more viable too. We do actually have a second car now, but I've been renting the old one out until we're ready to sell it and until we're sure we can handle two kids in kindergarten without it. It's been way more popular than I anticipated."},{"id":32457911,"author":"coalteddy","month":"2022-08","story":"Electric vehicles are way more energy-efficient than internal combustion","stance":"rec","why":"Shows bakfiets covering nearly all errands","quote":"I suggest you watch this video[1] by the awesome channel \"Not Just Bikes\".","text":"I suggest you watch this video[1] by the awesome channel \"Not Just Bikes\". Bakfiets are a dutch cargo bike you can literally use for 99% of all your errands. People in a city do not need personal cars. And even Cargo bikes are only useful for 10-20% of occasions, depending on if you have big shopping tours or kids. For the 1 or 2 occasions a year where a car is needed, car sharing is the way to go. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47189329,"author":"Mawr","month":"2026-02","story":"Cardiorespiratory fitness is associated with lower anger and anxiety","stance":"reference","why":"Biking kids around as car alternative","quote":"","text":"Calling everything but cars exclusionary is mildly hilarious. First of all, you're simply forgetting about public transport. That's needed too and pretty much covers all your concerns. However... 1. The disabled? How many of those do you think are disabled in just the right ways to still be able to drive? You know \"disabled\" doesn't just mean \"missing a limb\", right? So uh, if the only option is to drive and someone's disabled and therefore can't drive, what do they... do? Anyhow: [1] 2. Kids? How many kids do you see driving cars around? You know 10+ year olds can just... bike on their own, right? Like, to school, etc.? [2] [3] [4] Below that age, just bike them around? [5] ;) > Forcing You know nobody's forcing you to do anything, right? Like, you can still own and use your car, there are some very valid use cases best served by them. For example, around half of the Dutch own a car. They just don't use them as much for really dumb purposes, like driving 5km to a grocery store, because the nearest one is within walking or cycling distance. The problem is that driving has been so heavily subsidized, that we've come to take it for granted and are now truly shocked when asked to pay more to even somewhat offset the real costs. And I'm afraid there's no viable path forward that doesn't involve eventually paying in full. > Forcing people to bike and walk everywhere sounds a lot better if you're in a moderate climate where bad weather means you need to pack a light jacket and wait"},{"id":41619987,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-09","story":"Love of cargo bikes is changing how we deliver goods in our cities","stance":"reference","why":"Slaughter preferred two-wheeled bakfiets over three-wheelers","quote":"Jason Slaughter of Not Just Bikes preferred two wheels with the big box upfront","text":"Does anyone have experience on two- versus three-wheeled cargo bikes? When he made a video on the topic, Jason Slaughter of Not Just Bikes preferred two wheels with the big box upfront ( bakfiets ): * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"},{"id":40695688,"author":"ralfd","month":"2024-06","story":"'The big problem is water': UK ebike owners plagued by failing motors","stance":"reference","why":"Parents zipping children around on cargo e-bikes","quote":"Then family mum and dad zipping their children around","text":"> Don't people bike because they want to get fit? In my city people bike because they want to get from A to B. E-bikes are great for that! They enable reasonable fast biking without breaking into sweat (good for commuting) and in hilly areas. First they enabled senior citizens to bike fast and longer. Then came the mountain e-bikes and made it cool. Than the city hipsters. Then family mum and dad zipping their children around: [VIDEO] > If not, get a proper motorbike. Motorbikes are loud and heavy and more expensive. You need a helmet and have to use the road instead of bike lanes. You need a driving license."},{"id":40369054,"author":"Mawr","month":"2024-05","story":"Parking reform legalized most of the new homes in Buffalo and Seattle (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Bring kids along on a cargo bike","quote":"you could either bring your kids along on a cargo bike","text":"Wouldn't it be nice if the only other car traffic on the road was there because of use cases similarly niche to yours? Public transportation is great at eliminating the need for using a car for completely overkill trips like commuting and basic shopping. Having said that, given good cycling infrastructure, chances are you could either bring your kids along on a cargo bike [1] or they could just ride there themselves [2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":38192515,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-11","story":"Spain lives in flats: why we have built our cities vertically","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence cargo bikes carry passengers and shopping","quote":"","text":"- Ebikes let you cover great distances with little effort. - You can have someone ride on the backseat or in the cargo space of a cargo bike. [1] - You'd be hard pressed to come up with a shopping load that won't fit on a cargo bike. [1] Then there's the cost vs a car [2], which will likely make up for any inconvenience. Of course, you do need at least decent bike infrastructure for this to be realistic. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38057294,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-10","story":"Cruise pauses driverless operations across all cities","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how much a cargo bike can carry","quote":"","text":"> A bicycle is trash (fun to ride, not that useful). Entirely dependent on the infrastructure: [LINK] > What are you going to carry on your bike? Five bags? Seems like a lot of groceries to buy at a time, but if you really want to: [VIDEO] > I don’t want to go 5 miles away, I want to go 200. You cover 200 miles every day? [LINK] Regardless, you can own a car for those longer journeys just fine and still make most trips by bicycle. > With bikes and walking, your world is small. Yeah, that's why public transport is important too. Cars as well, although deprioritized. > Electric cars powered by renewable energy get rid of most of the negative externalities. They get rid of some of the noise and almost all of the harmful exhaust. That's about it. They're still 2 ton+ massive steel cages that are incredibly dangerous to everyone else, take up a ridiculous amount of space, and require extremely expensive infrastructure. As for the rest, sure, I'm not really criticising your particular choices, it sounds like you live in a rural area where a car is necessary anyway. However, there's no way for everybody to be able to live like you do. There are just too many people. We need to live with long-term sustainability in mind."},{"id":37021982,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-08","story":"Driving is more expensive than you think (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Not Just Bikes creator raises two kids with cargo bike","quote":"","text":"> How many kids do you have? Jason Slaughter of Not Just Bikes has two: * [VIDEO] See also: > Heather Moore-Farley was pregnant with her third child when the family’s car died on route home from vacation. Her husband’s office was walkable from home, as was a Zipcar share. So to help save both money and the environment, the family decided to see how long they could go without a car. Two days before they welcomed their youngest child, “we ordered a cargo bike and just kept going,” said Moore-Farley, who lives in Oakland. That was 10 years ago. They still haven’t replaced the family car. * [LINK] > Traipsing 3 kids around to school, daycare, and extracurricular activities wouldn’t be easy. How would they grocery shop to fill all those mouths? What about family vacations, weekend trips away, or heading out to the movies – could they survive? * [LINK] > Biking with kids is all the rage in Portland these days, but biking with six kids between the ages of 2 and 11? That’s something I never would have thought possible before I met southeast Portland resident Emily Finch. * [LINK]"},{"id":36731475,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2023-07","story":"S.F. says incidents by Cruise, Waymo driverless taxis are ‘skyrocketing.’","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch riders using bakfietsen in snow","quote":"","text":"> I think the young, healthy people who live in moderate climates with good weather, short commutes, and little need to haul anything The old dutch people riding their bakfietsen around in the snow show this is much more a cultural thing than a hard fact of weather and human nature - and culture can change as the need arises. [VIDEO] As far as short commutes - that's as much a factor of public policy as are trains and other forms of public transport."},{"id":35834585,"author":"menus","month":"2023-05","story":"Squad Solar City Car","stance":"reference","why":"Shows cargo bike carrying kids and shopping","quote":"A cargo bike like in the video above is sufficient.","text":"> Or you might need to carry kids. Or carry shopping/parcels [VIDEO] There is a huge misunderstanding in what bikes are trying to achieve. No sane one is suggesting you use a bike for a 50 mile work commute. The criticism is against giant SUVs and pickup trucks for in-city travel. You dont need to burn 2 gallons of fuel to carry a gallon of milk and dozen eggs from 2 miles away. A cargo bike like in the video above is sufficient. Most cars have 1 passenger in them currently. How do I know that? Because HoV lanes are always empty."},{"id":35234341,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-03","story":"Cyclists Now Outnumber Motorists in City of London","stance":"reference","why":"Cargo bike as answer to carrying children","quote":"","text":"> - might be impossible depending on work or children; For the latter, perhaps see \"The Car-Replacement Bicycle (the bakfiets)\": * [VIDEO] > - weather might make the ride unpleasant; I live in Toronto, Canada, and in the Before Times I cycled everday between ~April and December, rain or shine. The simplest solution is to just always have bad weather gear: it's like and doesn't take up much volume. Having it in a pannier means you always have it and don't have to think about it. The simplest solution for rain is a poncho, though I went with jackets and rain pants. I found the threat of rain is more of an obstacle that actual rain. If your commute is <40 minutes, odds are pretty good it won't actually rain during your ride. It's the possibility of it that tends discourage people, in which case some gear works to counter it. That said, if you ride >80% of the time when the weather isn't too bad that's still a good improvement over not riding at all."},{"id":34377038,"author":"ignormies","month":"2023-01","story":"EPA Proposes to Strengthen Air Quality Standards","stance":"reference","why":"Cargo bikes as hope for car dependence","quote":"","text":"The process is a feedback loop though. People drive because everything is so spread out. The corner stores die because people don't walk past anymore. Rinse and repeat. Electric cargo bikes are the last bastion of hope here. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32388665,"author":"jrussino","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: What's the next big thing that few people are talking about?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of cargo bike replacing cars","quote":"","text":"I think the recent electric scooter boom and subsequent pushback by communities shows that: - There is (IMHO huge ) pent-up demand for last-mile style electric transportation: scooters, bicycles, etc. - The electric drive technology is starting to reach a point where it can support a whole new space of designs that make this sort of transportation appealing to a wide variety of users (i.e. not just fit young adventurous people; the elderly, young children, etc.) - Sadly, infrastructure, laws, and social norms move way too slowly and are the limiting factor for what could be a major quality-of-life improvement for many many people. Note that \"EV's that are half the width of a normal car lane ... probably less than $10k.\" is a pretty good description for the electric cargo bikes that are already starting to emerge. I would love to see cities and towns adapt faster to accommodate things like this: The Car-Replacement Bicycle (the bakfiets) [VIDEO] How to Transport Kids by eBike [LINK] This American Mayor is Creating the Ultimate Biking City [LINK]"},{"id":32566292,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-08","story":"France is now offering a €4k e-bike subsidy to people who trade in their car","stance":"reference","why":"Compares two-wheeled and three-wheeled cargo bikes","quote":"two-wheeled cargo bikes are much easier to cycle than three-wheeled cargo bikes","text":"> a two-wheeled cargo bikes are much easier to cycle than three-wheeled cargo bikes. See video \"The Car-Replacement Bicycle (the bakfiets)\" * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"}],"r":[["UCgoxQCf5Jg",1],["IroPQ150F6c",1],["RSTNhvDGbYI",1],["wlvKAT7SZIQ",1],["vHWiDx_l4V0",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["ARKbfWk4Xyw",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["VVUeqxXwCA0",1],["KSWqx8goqSY",1],["tt2AYafET68",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["fuFlMtZmvY0",1],["zb7Bs98KmnY",1],["AE1gaNO9nj0",1]]},"tInaI3pU19Y":{"wy":"HN uses it as concrete data in game engine debates, showing a custom engine far outperforming Unity at several times the effort.","q":{"x":"A custom 3D engine performs immensely better than a comparable Unity project, but it took the author 3 to 4 times the effort.","i":27590777,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":23274389,"author":"jokoon","month":"2020-05","story":"What Unity Is Getting Wrong","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates Unity bloat versus a custom engine.","quote":"A great demonstration of how unity is bloated","text":"A great demonstration of how unity is bloated: [VIDEO] Unity is targeting students and gamedev wannabees. You don't make a game with a framework. Programming is not simple. Same debate of framework vs libraries. I'm even against Godot, which you cannot even use as a rendering library. You end up being too dependent on those platforms and all their assets and plugins. Of course if you intend to make a game that's not too ambitious, if it revolves around the graphics, unity might be ok, but it's just a sophisticated RPG maker 3D. I strongly advise against learning unity or godot. A huge problem is that unity/unreal made it difficult for classic engines/renderers to keep thriving, and it's hard to find a good engine nowadays. There are some, of course, but their community is non-existent, which makes things much harder in open source."},{"id":22184965,"author":"jokoon","month":"2020-01","story":"Godot 3.2","stance":"rec","why":"Benchmarks Unity against a handbuilt engine.","quote":"Here is a good video about benchmarking unity","text":"If one doesn't understand how a game works, a framework is not going to help. This person will probably shoot himself in the foot. Here is a good video about benchmarking unity [VIDEO]"},{"id":29129050,"author":"KronisLV","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How were video games from the 90s so efficient?","stance":"reference","why":"Same project built with and without an engine.","quote":"Similar differences show up when you try to build the same project, once while using an engine and another without it","text":"> ... it greatly decreases your time-to-market to do things in a straightforward way ... I'd actually like to link the YouTube channel of a person who is writing their own game engine and game at the same time: [LINK] You can see how using Godot, Unity or Unreal (or most other engines) would have been much faster in regards to time to market. Similar differences show up when you try to build the same project, once while using an engine and another without it, the performance can be much better if you write your own optimized code (supposing that you can do so in the first place), however the development still takes much longer, for example: [VIDEO] Now, whether that matters to you or not is a different matter entirely: some care about learning a lot more about the lower level stuff, others just want to develop games and care more about the art/story/etc., while others care about selling them ASAP."},{"id":27551546,"author":"qayxc","month":"2021-06","story":"Stride Game Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Fair comparison of handbuilt versus engine effort.","quote":"Let's not guess and simply checkout a fair comparison, like this gentleman did","text":"Let's not guess and simply checkout a fair comparison, like this gentleman did: [VIDEO] From his perspective as an experienced developer, it took 3.4x more effort to implement a very basic project versus using a fully featured engine. And that's ignoring the editor part, not to forget. I'd call a factor of 3.4 quite significant. In more general terms, it's the difference between actually finishing a game in a 3½ months vs a full year. In other words, you could finish 3 projects in the time it takes you to fully handcraft one, with the added advantage of OOTB multi-platform support. So reality seems to disagree with your \"not really saving you that much much time\" assumption. It's the small conveniences that add up quickly: exporters/importers for assets, in-game editors, readily available prefabs/blueprints for all kinds of components, stuff like that. Of course it's not rocket surgery to use SDL and a handcrafted engine for a quick little project. Yet, I've witnessed far more game projects die (especially by beginners) that went the handcrafted all-the-way route versus people using game engines."},{"id":27590777,"author":"ajconway","month":"2021-06","story":"Your CPU may have slowed down on Wednesday","stance":"reference","why":"Custom engine outperforms Unity but takes 3 to 4 times the effort.","quote":"A custom 3D engine performs immensely better than a comparable Unity project, but it took the author 3 to 4 times the effort.","text":"> In many cases, this software doesn't even do anything that is functionally different from 10 or 20 years ago, but it still consumes many more resources I would argue that in most cases the choice is not between fast, optimized and slow, unoptimized software. The choice is between having a functioning program (using a universal, \"reasonably\" efficient stack) and not having one at all (because the amount of resources it takes to develop a customized, low-level implementation is prohibitively high). A good example: [VIDEO] A custom 3D engine performs immensely better than a comparable Unity project, but it took the author 3 to 4 times the effort."},{"id":22293029,"author":"jokoon","month":"2020-02","story":"“I made an operating system UI within Unity”","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of Unity's performance.","quote":"Here is an analysis of unity's performance","text":"Here is an analysis of unity's performance [VIDEO] My take on your comment is that the problem of electron is that it uses HTML."},{"id":22377432,"author":"jokoon","month":"2020-02","story":"Create a First-Person Shooter In Godot","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I never managed to like unity or godot because they remove freedom from the developer. Your code becomes a second class citizen. Apparently level design people and artists like it, which makes sense, of course, since indie gamedevs and small teams who are full of ideas and content, want to work as quickly as possible. But as a developer, I just loathe those tools. They sort of make sense for non-developers who want to make a prototype game and \"tweak\" things by writing as little code as possible while getting bleeding edge graphics. In reality it just enables unambitious projects so that \"anybody can make games\". It's like comparing LEGO to mechanical engineering. Of course, if you're pedantic, a LEGO car fits the definition of a car. You can sell a lego car, and then you realize how twisted the indie video game market is right now. But if you want to make a game, those WYSIWYG things are horrible. They're viable, but if you have some issue or limitation with the tool, you're really stuck. If there's a performance problem, it becomes more difficult. If you want to release on console, there are additional steps and work to do. You cannot \"use what you want\", because it's a huge generic blob of things that you cannot really understand how it's made. I watched a video about the internals of godot and was not convinced at all. Of course it's open source but it's a giant mess. Unity is not even open source. I will always show this video each time those WYSIWYG tools are talked abo"},{"id":22184769,"author":"jokoon","month":"2020-01","story":"Godot 3.2","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I tried to learn it a little but I did not manage to understand how it works, I don't really like the whole WYSIWYG thing, it forces the developer to surrender control, and you waste a big amount of time teaching yourself how it works internally, time that could be spent being productive with a simple renderer. It also seems to support c++ as \"gdnative\", although it requires another compiler to link against godot's binaries. It seems awkward because you still need to use the godot editor, and there is still a lot of interfacing to write. It seems like a good alternative to unity, but in the end, all I want is an engine, not a \"framework\" or some awkward scene editor with some kind of Node hierarchy/interface. I could say the same thing about UE4. I hear everywhere that's it's the place to go, that \"everybody uses it and it's the norm in the industry if you want to make a game from scratch\", but the reality is that those frameworks are too big and too feature rich. It sounds like they want to attract young developers to lock them into those awkward frameworks, because they advertise it as being full-featured engines, but small developers might not really need to focus on bleeding edge rendering, so it doesn't make sense. I know there are engines like Ogre3D, but I would rather spend time learning an actual universal graphics API that works on all hardware, which has more value on a resume, and I can just use what I need. All other things like audio, animation, inputs, physics,"}]},"yY96hTb8WgI":{"wy":"HN uses it as the short, canonical explainer of Norman doors and affordances, linked in design and UX discussions.","q":{"x":"The Vox vodcast(?) in [1] goes a lot further into the problem, starting from a very similar door.","i":43018751,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43018751,"author":"crote","month":"2025-02","story":"The subtle art of designing physical controls for cars","stance":"rec","why":"Goes deep on push/pull door design problems and their simple fix.","quote":"The Vox vodcast(?) in [1] goes a lot further into the problem, starting from a very similar door.","text":"Yeah, but it's not supposed to. With good design it is immediately obvious whether the door is push or pull - even when the frame doesn't have any clear indications. For example, the door in [0] (apologies for the YT short) has no immediately clear hints if you look at the frame or handle. Both sides look exactly the same, so how are you supposed to know? Add a \"push/pull\" sticker you'll miss 90% of the time? The Vox vodcast(?) in [1] goes a lot further into the problem, starting from a very similar door. The solution is pretty simple, in hindsight: if they designed the door properly, you won't even be able to pull on a push door. Have a pull handle on the pull side, and a push plate on the push side. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28958145,"author":"WhitneyLand","month":"2021-10","story":"Magit, the magical Git interface (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Short Vox explainer on what a Norman door is.","quote":"What is a Normal door - 5 min Vox short","text":"You probably don’t feel like you’re fighting your front door when you walk out of your house or apartment also right? All that means is that git tools, and your front door, work well once you have mastered them. The ease of use and discoverability of tools over the life of their learning curve is what determines if they are a Norman door. For lots of people, using a Unix command line or Git for the first time feels like a Norman door. This is why other interfaces exist (like guis) for the same tools. What is a Normal door - 5 min Vox short [VIDEO]"},{"id":41524455,"author":"godelski","month":"2024-09","story":"The Modern CLI Renaissance","stance":"reference","why":"Norman door analogy for user mistakes.","quote":"","text":"> If there were some kind of usage study or telemetry This still wouldn't convince me because it's not what my argument is about. (Fwiw, I think one of the reasons BS is upset is how frequent the issue is brought up. You can see his frustration in the GitHub issues too. And how he points to it being at the top of the readme and it still doesn't get through to people. Just like a sign on a door saying pull. There's a reason that doesn't work. Why literate people keep failing. Ask why and how it can be prevented.) I know I'm miscommunicating but I'm really not sure how. Because it isn't about what most people do. It isn't about what most people what. I know this sounds odd, and maybe that's what's getting in the way. Maybe I shouldn't say error because you're thinking the tool has errored? How about user mistake? When user uses the tool wrong? When something unexpected happens? Any of these will substitute. It's all about when I user does something and it's not what they think they're doing. Similarly I understand how it appears I'm arguing for verbose by default, and in a way I am (I'm not blind to this lol), but I'm also not really. But when talking about verbosity in terms of programs I think we'd need to distinguish number of words from what kind of words. Though I think it's not uncommon for debug or warnings to be misclassified as info haha. Design is about communication. Any command you enter gives you feedback. Returning nothing or returning too much are feedback, the t"},{"id":40852792,"author":"godelski","month":"2024-07","story":"Code reviews do find bugs","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how door signs and design can conflict.","quote":"It's not always dumb when a person pulls on a door that says push","text":"Sometimes feature requests are actually bugs and can be illustrative of one not properly understanding design. But I think it is important how user feature requests are interpreted. They have a frustration that you might not be aware of but they aren't aware of all the code and constraints. It can even be in design, which is still important. Very often there is a way to resolve a feature request that is not what the user explicitly asks for. But to do that you have to read between the lines, and carefully. Of course, some people go completely the wrong way with this and cough Apple cough decide that they know what is best for the user. It's totally a hard balance to strike, but I think it is very common for it to be framed much simpler. There's the joke that the user is dumb, and maybe they are, but that doesn't mean the issue they face is. It's not always dumb when a person pulls on a door that says push, because it may actually be that the sign and design are saying different things[0]. And personally, I like when users suggest methods of resolving the problem. I might throw that in the garbage, but it can often give me better context clues as to what they're trying to ask for and really does tell me if they're thinking hard about the problem that they care about the product. They just don't have the same vantage point that I do, and that's okay. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23644533,"author":"gav","month":"2020-06","story":"Points of contact – a short history of door handles","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Norman doors and affordances.","quote":"I often use doors as the introduction to understand affordances.","text":"I often use doors as the introduction to understand affordances. It's crazy that we've had doors for 5000 years and we still make them hard to open. See \"Norman Doors\": [VIDEO] The Design of Everyday Things: [LINK]"},{"id":21788915,"author":"Isinlor","month":"2019-12","story":"The Cybertruck is not brutalist","stance":"reference","why":"Video on bad door design tied to The Design of Everyday Things.","quote":"I care about design e.g.","text":"You take position of authority, criticize, but you are not educating. > The fields of art and aesthetics have a long, complex history and have been studied and written upon by thousands of intelligent scholars, designers, artists, philosophers, and religious figures. So, let me ask you, why should I care? I care about design e.g. \"The Design of Everyday Things\": [VIDEO] But what is it about aesthetics that is not subjective?"}],"r":[["pYXy-A4siMw",1],["L5pUA3LsEaw",1],["XfQUOHlAocY",1],["G-J86Ka9MkQ",1],["7IsMeKl-Sv0",1]]},"zaUCDZ9d09Y":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a contrarian critique of Starlink's viability; some defend its economics argument while others dismiss it as repetitive.","q":{"x":"Well, the economics are discussed in this video","i":46166430,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46166430,"author":"spwa4","month":"2025-12","story":"SpaceX in Talks for Share Sale That Would Boost Valuation to $800B","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses the economics of satellite internet and Starlink.","quote":"Well, the economics are discussed in this video","text":"Probably a good idea to do it now, because Trump has made sure SpaceX is about to have yet another European, a Chinese and an Indian competitor soon. 2 out of 3 have already demonstrated landing a rocket, as has Blue origin in the US with the New Glenn launch + landing. Plus a few countries are thinking about it, at least Switzerland, South Korea and Israel if you can believe it. Also the EU has setup a working Starlink competitor (by approving the feature on \"old\" satellites), and China is already doing launches and theirs should be at least partially operational. Russia claims to have a working Starlink competitor and India is building one. Oh and as for profitability ... not that Starlink hasn't been tried 10 times before, with the most spectacular crash being Iridium, but that was far from the only attempt+bankruptcy building Space internet. Well, the economics are discussed in this video: [VIDEO] TLDR: SpaceX is bankrupt, Starlink is a pets.com \"We lose on every sale but make it up on volume\" style move. So yes, high time to sell the stock indeed. Oh, and Blue Origin has beat SpaceX to Mars and will be the first private company getting a payload to Mars soon (the \"ESCAPADE\" mission). As in payload is on the way and there's no way SpaceX can catch up anymore. In fact it's pretty tough finding another rocket manufacturer that has not launched a mission to Mars. Boeing has launched payloads to Mars. Blue origin has. Arianespace has. Russia has. Not especially economically r"},{"id":41908784,"author":"sfblah","month":"2024-10","story":"Can SpaceX land a rocket with 1/2 cm accuracy?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues Starlink is a bad business rather than physically impossible.","quote":"I'd actually love it if you could find a single thing he says in that video that's provably false.","text":"[LINK] Edit: Just out of curiosity, I looked up his video about Starlink, and his point was it's a bad business, not that it's physically impossible to do it: [VIDEO] I'd actually love it if you could find a single thing he says in that video that's provably false. I'm quite confident history will judge Elon Musk properly, as the \"Too Big To Fail\" version of Theranos and Elizabeth Holmes."},{"id":41909440,"author":"mardifoufs","month":"2024-10","story":"Can SpaceX land a rocket with 1/2 cm accuracy?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This entire video is just the same thing over and over.","text":">They claim they're going to get these laser communications between the satellites which will make things faster for a long distance... [this is because light travels faster in a vacuum than through fiber optic cable you up to London a very important one for the Global Financial system Starlink latency is under 50 milliseconds while the current Internet is around 70 milliseconds] yeah Starlink can't do any of that at the moment. >Probably something to do with the fact that the satellites are hundreds of miles or kilometers apart and you're trying to hit a tiny moving Target from another moving target with a laser and then and chaining those together that doesn't sound very easy but they're promising to launch some satellites that can do it in the next generation [getting close to launching satellite 1.5 which has laser interest satellite links] >Now where have I heard that before... Let's just call me skeptical on this one [VIDEO] This entire video is just the same thing over and over. As is usual with him, he doesn't actually make any strong enough claim and just goes on endless sneering. But in the context of the video, it is absolutely clear that he's saying it is just another lie and that it's basically impossible. Remember, this was mere months before the laser network went up. He also claimed that the bandwidth is never going to be usable, but it absolutely is. Also, that's just the fallacy fallacy. This isn't some sort of debate club, it makes perfect sense to discredi"},{"id":30176372,"author":"kisstheblade","month":"2022-02","story":"Starlink Premium","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"See for example a \"busted\" video","text":"Does this make starlink any more feasible? I thought that the math just doesn't work out. Not to mention the massive amount of space garbage (40k satellites replaced every 5 years?) See for example a \"busted\" video [VIDEO]"},{"id":30268263,"author":"joering2","month":"2022-02","story":"Starlink lost 40 satellites to a geomagnetic storm","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I recently started researching into Starlink as my next (cheap) internet solution and stumbled upon these 2 videos below.","text":"I recently started researching into Starlink as my next (cheap) internet solution and stumbled upon these 2 videos below. The worst part for me is that once this becomes more popular the bandwidth will die down to crumble. I don't buy they will be able to upkeep with bandwidth sharing it from satellite to satellite using a.. laser beam (is that even possible to sync 2 satellites and send signal thru laser beam that has radius of 10 nm at distance of 1,000 km from one object to another traveling at 20,000km/s ?) [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"4Tr0otuiQuU":{"wy":"Mostly cited as an example piece in audio and AI-music discussions, with one commenter urging people to hear the whole sonata, especially the third movement.","q":{"x":"anyone who's never heard the whole thing owes it to themselves to give it a listen.","i":23463432,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23463432,"author":"Splognosticus","month":"2020-06","story":"Toto’s “Africa” Played on Tesla Coils","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full Moonlight Sonata, third movement especially","quote":"anyone who's never heard the whole thing owes it to themselves to give it a listen.","text":"> Beethoven: Sonata No 14 (aka Moonlight Sonata), 1st Movement Speaking of, anyone who's never heard the whole thing owes it to themselves to give it a listen. The 3rd movement is off the chain. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33182903,"author":"p1esk","month":"2022-10","story":"AI language models are struggling to “get” math","stance":"reference","why":"Original piece that AudioLM output resembles","quote":"notice the similarity of the last 7 seconds to Moonlight sonata","text":"I don't see a lot of progress in AudioLM compared to results from 2018: [LINK] What's more interesting and concerning - listen carefully to the first piano continuation example from AudioLM, notice the similarity of the last 7 seconds to Moonlight sonata: [VIDEO] I'm afraid we will see a lot of this with music generation models in the near future."},{"id":33183155,"author":"p1esk","month":"2022-10","story":"AI language models are struggling to “get” math","stance":"reference","why":"Original piece that AI sample copied from","quote":"It definitely copied a piece of Moonlight sonata in the last 7 seconds of the first continuation sample","text":"It definitely copied a piece of Moonlight sonata in the last 7 seconds of the first continuation sample: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8690506,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2014-12","story":"24/192 music downloads are silly","stance":"reference","why":"Example music piece in a hypothetical bit-depth listening test","quote":"","text":"The correct way to attack this isn't by attacking the theory. It's to gather a lot of people and ask them to press a button indicating whether the audio they hear is 16-bit or 24-bit. If the results are no better than chance, then 24-bit doesn't matter, regardless of how sound the underlying argument is. EDIT: The experiment would also be extremely difficult to design. For example, you'd need to run this test with music, not simple sounds. So the question is, which music? I think whatever is most popular at the time would be a good candidate, because if people are listening to music they hate, they won't care about the fine details of the audio. But that introduces an element of uncertainty and noise into the results which is hard to control for. Some people might deliver accurate results with [LINK] but not with [VIDEO] whereas for others it's the opposite. Or, it could be the exact opposite: Maybe you can only detect whether a sound is 24-bit when it's a simple tone, and not music. Age is also a factor. My hearing is worse than a decade ago. The headphones used by the test are another factor. If you feed 24-bit input to headphones, there's no guarantee that the speakers are performing with 24-bit resolution. In fact, this may be the source of most of the confusion in the debate. I'm not sure how you'd even check whether speakers are physically moving back and forth \"at 24-bit resolution\" rather than a 16-bit resolution."}]},"FaAZAII2x0w":{"wy":"HN cites it as a rare public look at Google's internal network design and how it scales to many VMs.","q":{"x":"If you're interested in datacenter networking, can't miss ONS.","i":11261335,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11261335,"author":"mikecb","month":"2016-03","story":"What's the Best Cloud? Probably GCP","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Google's datacenter network hardware, 5 Pb/s bisection bandwidth","quote":"If you're interested in datacenter networking, can't miss ONS.","text":"If you're interested in datacenter networking, can't miss ONS. Amin Vahdat, the guy in charge of networking at Google, has keynoted the past two years, with hardware in 2015[1] and software in 2014[2]. Mark Russinovich also spoke about Azure's SDN, but it doesn't come close to Amin's presentation. Every once in a while you'll hear some random spec from one of these companies, and it's always pretty surprising, but Amin's team has achieved 5 Petabit/s in bisectional bandwidth. It's more than surprising. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":14038644,"author":"zaroth","month":"2017-04","story":"Espresso – Google’s peering edge architecture","stance":"reference","why":"Amin's 2015 keynote, unwatched by commenter","quote":"","text":"For some reason they didn't actually link to the talk, which I haven't watched, but presumably actually tries to start answering those questions. Quick search is showing the 2015 keynote that Amin gave, haven't found the 2017 one yet... [1] - 2015 ONS Keynote [VIDEO]"},{"id":12257148,"author":"whatupmd","month":"2016-08","story":"Google Compute Engine Incident #16015","stance":"reference","why":"Google's internal network uses OpenFlow for determinism","quote":"","text":"They switched to openflow on internal networks for this reason, deterministic: [VIDEO] External network is BGP though and sounds like they didn't detect it until user reports hit. They can't predict the problem and there detection isn't working well either."},{"id":10853698,"author":"jsolson","month":"2016-01","story":"Non-volatile Storage: CPUs no longer more performant than I/O devices","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in discussion of storage and network performance","quote":"","text":"The numbers in this are daunting, but I personally believe massively multi-core systems make the problem a lot less daunting than the article makes out. Core counts in big servers can get up over 100 per server for Intel (see Amazon's new EC2 offerings for public evidence of this). Intel's Xeon Phi series of processors offer core counts approaching ~300. Going to 300x takes the required latencies per request from microseconds up to the millisecond range. POWER systems can go even higher. Moreover, for many workloads that actually leverage this sort of compute you can do something horrifying with the new DRAM-addressable persistent storage: DMA directly from the NIC into block storage. Some (many?) high performance network adapters offer the ability to filter packets to distinct Rx queues; buffers can be posted with addresses in the storage mapped region allowing direct NIC->storage transfer. If you bake more intelligence into the NIC, you can even do things like Mellanox's NVMe Fabrics: [LINK] This is particularly relevant to the JBOD example. Now, there's the question of what you're actually going to do with all of that data, but in a lot of cases it's likely a durable read-mostly cache that's effectively a materialized view optimized of some (hopefully much slower write-rate) transactional store (say, product data on Amazon -- detail pages served up at some absurdly high rate, but a relatively low mutation rate). Other workloads I can think of fall into a category I tend to"},{"id":9802043,"author":"jsolson","month":"2015-06","story":"Is the Network the Limit? Dealing with Weave, CoreOS and Azure","stance":"reference","why":"Why Google's network should scale to many VMs","quote":"Amin Vahdat recently shared some content publicly about why it should scale to lots of VMs","text":"Hi Joe :) Conveniently, Amin Vahdat recently shared some content publicly about why it should scale to lots of VMs: [VIDEO] (And, to others, disclaimer: I work on some bits of GCE networking at Google)"}]},"YXehBx0Yc_w":{"wy":"Recommended in threads about practical versus CGI effects as a fun, instructive attempt to match a classic effect on a small budget.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend to watch their video.","i":29221998,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29221998,"author":"sakopov","month":"2021-11","story":"The bullet effects in Terminator 2 weren’t CGI","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Corridor Crew tries to recreate T-1000 bars scene with modern tools.","quote":"Highly recommend to watch their video.","text":"There is YouTube channel called Corridor Crew which tried to use new technology available to film makers to reproduce the scene in Terminator where T-1000 (played by Robert Patrick) walks through metal bars of a jail cell. To their surprise, the results they got were pretty underwhelming and worse than what digital effects artists were able to come up with decades ago. Highly recommend to watch their video. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30955456,"author":"torginus","month":"2022-04","story":"“I believe CGI is getting worse even as the tech advances.”","stance":"reference","why":"Example of modern budget recreation falling short of original effect.","quote":"The fact that they were able to recreate the effect on a shoestring budget speaks volumes of their talent","text":"I think CGI is getting worse for the same reason video games are getting worse - reaching 70% of a result can be done with 10% of the effort it took decades ago, but reaching 100% is not getting easier - you need tons of incredibly talented and dedicated engineers who have a very deep understanding of CG and come up with a bespoke solution. An example in movies would be the video the Corridor guys did on the Terminator 2 shot where the T-1000 phases through the prison bars - the VFX team on the movie essentially built a fluid simulation engine to get it right. The fact that they were able to recreate the effect on a shoestring budget speaks volumes of their talent and the evolution of tooling, but it doesn't look nearly as good: [VIDEO] Another example from the field of video games would be Back 4 Blood vs Left 4 dead - the insane amount of care and effort Valve put into minute details cannot be replicated by faster tools and more computing power: [LINK]"},{"id":28035941,"author":"codetrotter","month":"2021-08","story":"fSpy: Open-source still image camera matching","stance":"reference","why":"Video mentions fSpy while recreating a Terminator 2 scene.","quote":"This software was mentioned briefly in the Corridor Crew video where they recreate a scene from Terminator II","text":"This software was mentioned briefly in the Corridor Crew video where they recreate a scene from Terminator II [VIDEO] For macOS users that have Homebrew installed, you can install fSpy with: brew install --cask fspy"}],"r":[["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["W88Sact1kws",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1]]},"0f9GpMWdvWI":{"wy":"Cited in grid, smart thermostat and energy storage threads as an accessible explanation of load shifting.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections has an interesting video about pre-cooling","i":28901498,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28901498,"author":"eru","month":"2021-10","story":"The booming business of knitting together the world’s electricity grids","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting explanation of pre-cooling a house to shift load.","quote":"Technology Connections has an interesting video about pre-cooling","text":"Technology Connections has an interesting video about pre-cooling: [VIDEO] The author also made a good point that despite wind and solar being intermittent, they are actually fairly predictable a or less day in advance (thanks to weather forecasts)."},{"id":35147864,"author":"nixonpjoshua1","month":"2023-03","story":"Launch HN: Electric Air (YC W23) – Heat pump sold directly to homeowners","stance":"reference","why":"Example of optimizing home heating and cooling around energy use.","quote":"","text":"That really isn't the same product, as I understand this is an HRV combined with heat pump system where they are software integrated to PID controls that optimize the level of fresh air and weather aware energy utilization, with a system like this and a startup I'm sure optimizations like this [VIDEO] should be possible - this is like Dropbox, can't expect people to roll their own - and you need a fully integrated system to really make this work well without comfort, even simpler if cooling/heating is more efficient via bringing in fresh air this system could choose to do that vs running the heat pump at all!"},{"id":32504596,"author":"ReactiveJelly","month":"2022-08","story":"Tesla Virtual Power Plant","stance":"reference","why":"Over-cooling a house at night as thermal storage comparable to a Powerwall.","quote":"Technology Connections does a similar thing, over-cooling his house at night as a thermal battery.","text":"Technology Connections does a similar thing, over-cooling his house at night as a thermal battery. Video: [VIDEO] Reddit discussion: [LINK] He estimates it at 16 KWh of storage. Same ballpark as a Powerwall, right?"},{"id":32096683,"author":"evanreichard","month":"2022-07","story":"Smart thermostats inadvertently strain electric power grids","stance":"reference","why":"Argues for proactive cooling using the house as a battery.","quote":"Technology Connections goes into this more [1]","text":"Ecobee has eco+ which has \"Community Energy Savings\" [0]. It was just way too intrusive. It seemed to be a reactive system vs proactive. Technology Connections goes into this more [1], but I believe they should be providing a system that proactively cools when there is less demand _prior_ to an expected increase in demand. Effectively using your house as a \"battery\". When I had it enabled, I was _always_ too hot (in the summer) during peak hours. Sorry, not sorry: I'm not willing to sacrifice my comfort for the ~$10/mo incentive. ___ [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29563883,"author":"GuB-42","month":"2021-12","story":"Why battery costs have plunged since 2010","stance":"reference","why":"Thermostats that account for electricity cost and pre-cool.","quote":"there is a video by Technology Connections on that","text":"Yes, I think it is a missed opportunity. Hopefully, we are slowly getting there. We have smart meters, smart thermostats, smart everything, all these devices should be able to negotiate for the best time to run. The big red display can be a thing, but I don't want to micromanage my power consumption when it can be done automatically. We already have boilers that turn off during peak hours, but you can also have your thermostats take the cost of electricity into account, there is a video by Technology Connections on that ( [VIDEO] ). You should also have a \"run when it is cheap\" button on your appliances, most already have a delayed start feature, we can be a little smarter. Electric cars are even better because not only you can schedule charging but they are also batteries. We already have the technology to do all that, at most, all we need is a standardization effort. Power line communication seems like a good fit. It is already used in smart meters to automate readings, and this way, any device that is plugged in knows how much it costs and can adjust, including the big red display if you want one."},{"id":27910732,"author":"Nzen","month":"2021-07","story":"The Colorado River is shrinking","stance":"reference","why":"Using a house as a thermal battery to shift energy use off-peak.","quote":"recently published a video about using his house as a thermal battery [1]","text":"While the effect of residents is small, the usage of other sectors is, ultimately, in service of residents. People raise cattle in response to or anticipation of demand. Those cattle use more water than if we raised chickens or wheat for seitan. I consider it unlikely that a top down campaign to ration beef, in service of water usage, would succeed without a change in residential culture. I am more apt to agree with Hank Green [0] that success in major social restructuring requires involvement at the individual level, too, because this encourages secondary behaviors that reinforce the restructuring. People who are voluntarily quitting hamburgers and steaks are more apt to write to their representatives or donate to NGOs that do in support of this issue, initially and to check in later on that promises are kept. [0] [LINK] As another example, Alec, who publishes youtube videos as _Technology Connections_, recently published a video about using his house as a thermal battery [1] so he can offset his energy use to night time, rather than running his AC at peak hours. He suggests that utilities involve residents to widen this strategy, in favor of brownouts or other centralized tools for shedding load in extreme circumstances. A policy solution that did not burden individuals with brownouts, ex by overprovisioning generation, would fall back on their shoulders in the form of higher utility rates. I allege the same would be the case for adverse climate change or overallocated wate"}],"r":[["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["udPY5rQVoW0",1],["yNzQ_sLGIuA",1]]},"2YoPoNx3L5E":{"wy":"HN cites it in Rust-versus-C++ and Ada discussions as a real industrial case of choosing SPARK for safer embedded code.","q":{"x":"The HN community may be interested in this 1 hour presentation [1] on Nvidia's adoption of Ada","i":30846348,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30846348,"author":"tnballo","month":"2022-03","story":"High Assurance Rust: Developing Secure and Robust Software","stance":"rec","why":"NVIDIA's adoption of Ada and why it chose it over Rust","quote":"The HN community may be interested in this 1 hour presentation [1] on Nvidia's adoption of Ada","text":"Author of the book here, thank you for your feedback! Though not an Ada expert, I have used the SPARK subset. And I’ve worked on security assessment of military systems with components written in Ada. Although we looked at binaries and not source code, because our assurance model did not consider the Ada compiler to be part of the chain of trust (so we test the artifact the CPU will execute). I really like Ada and hope to use more of it in the future! The HN community may be interested in this 1 hour presentation [1] on Nvidia's adoption of Ada, including why they chose it over Rust in 2019 (regulatory certification was one factor). There’s also a great academic paper [2] on the recent advances SPARK has made in heap memory verification, inspired in part by Rust’s type system. There are currently efforts underway to certify Rust for usage in safety critical systems via an alternate, certified toolchain (among other pieces of the puzzle). Two related initiatives are linked through in FAQ question #7 (which someone already shared). I’ll be sure to add the Ferrous Systems and AdaCore collaboration when I have a chance to batch together some updates. I, like many others, believe Rust is a technology that can deliver value in safety and mission critical verticals due to reduction in undefined behavior via principled static analysis (a type system for which there has been early success in formal verification of safety claims[3]). Looking forward to digging into Rust research tools "},{"id":48098561,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2026-05","story":"CUDA-oxide: Nvidia's official Rust to CUDA compiler","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here's a recording of a 2020 presentation (\"Securing the Future of Safety and Security of Embedded Software\")","text":"Here's a recording of a 2020 presentation (\"Securing the Future of Safety and Security of Embedded Software\") from NVIDIA at the AdaCore conference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45112502,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2025-09","story":"Introduction to Ada: a project-based exploration with rosettas","stance":"reference","why":"NVIDIA working with AdaCore on Ada/SPARK firmware","quote":"Here’s a related talk","text":"Here’s a related talk: “NVIDIA - Securing the Future of Safety and Security of Embedded Software” [VIDEO] > NVIDIA is working with AdaCore to implement Ada and SPARK programming languages into certain firmware elements to reduce this potential error. In this webinar you will learn how the combination of NVIDIA hardware with Ada and SPARK delivers robustness and security, improving efficiency and safety in the development pipeline."},{"id":44233834,"author":"adezxc","month":"2025-06","story":"Ada Continues to Climb in June Tiobe Index and PYPL","stance":"reference","why":"How NVIDIA used Ada/SPARK for embedded software","quote":"There's this talk by AdaCore and a SWE@nVidia on how nVidia utilized Ada/Spark for Embedded software","text":"There's this talk by AdaCore and a SWE@nVidia on how nVidia utilized Ada/Spark for Embedded software: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33032711,"author":"kevlar700","month":"2022-09","story":"Is it time to retire C and C++ for Rust in new programs?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Nvidia also chose Spark; a built in subset of Ada as being more cost effective for secure code than C++.","text":"Ada offers security and simplicity and is already part of GCC but also has llvm now. Linux kernel drivers have also been demonstrated. Perhaps it would be healthier for Rust to have competition? [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Nvidia also chose Spark; a built in subset of Ada as being more cost effective for secure code than C++. [VIDEO] Assembly is also supported by Ada and I found it far easier to add than with C even. C interface support is also excellent."},{"id":33032720,"author":"kevlar700","month":"2022-09","story":"Is it time to retire C and C++ for Rust in new programs?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Nvidia also chose Spark; a built in subset of Ada as being more cost effective for secure code than C++.","text":"Ada offers security and simplicity and is already part of GCC but also has llvm now. Linux kernel drivers have also been demonstrated. Perhaps it would be healthier for Rust to have competition? [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Nvidia also chose Spark; a built in subset of Ada as being more cost effective for secure code than C++. [VIDEO] Assembly is also supported by Ada and I found it far easier to add than with C even. C interface support is also excellent."},{"id":33032747,"author":"kevlar700","month":"2022-09","story":"It's time to halt starting any new projects in C/C++","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Nvidia also chose Spark; a built in subset of Ada as being more cost effective for secure code than C++.","text":"Ada offers security and simplicity and is already part of GCC but also has llvm now. Linux kernel drivers have also been demonstrated. Perhaps it would be healthier for Rust to have competition? [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Nvidia also chose Spark; a built in subset of Ada as being more cost effective for secure code than C++. [VIDEO] Assembly is also supported by Ada and I found it far easier to add than with C even. C interface support is also excellent."}]},"D2vj0WcvH5c":{"wy":"Linked as a data point on the state of humanoid robot manipulation in 2023.","q":{"x":"Watch their latest update","i":38838326,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38838326,"author":"KiwiJohnno","month":"2024-01","story":"Nvidia AI Lead: We are ~3 years from the ChatGPT moment for physical AI agents","stance":"rec","why":"Latest update shows real progress","quote":"Watch their latest update","text":"Yeah 100% the initial announcement of Optimus was waaay to early, typical Elon bullshit to generate hype. But it is a real thing they are working on, and they have come a long way (considering they came from a guy in a spandex suit...) Watch their latest update: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39479257,"author":"loceng","month":"2024-02","story":"Nvidia adds $277B in market value, Wall Street's largest one-day gain in history","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"How about addressing and countering any of my specific points, ideally each one in sequence so you can't cherrypick to avoid the ones where I'm certainly correct - rather than what's essentially ad hominem as your response? [LINK] [VIDEO] etc. Perhaps you're not thinking from first principles and extrapolating from there?"},{"id":38413210,"author":"nopinsight","month":"2023-11","story":"Autonomous Excavator Constructs a Six-Metre-High Dry-Stone Wall","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of robot manipulation progress","quote":"See demo from Sep 2023 here","text":"> Imagine a several hundred kilo machine stepping on your toes with its full weight just because it mis-estimated your movements. \"Specifications. Tesla Bot is planned to measure 5 ft 8 in (173 cm) tall and weigh 125 lb (57 kg).\" [LINK] I'm aware that Tesla tends to be over-optimistic in its timeline regarding AI and robotics. I do not endorse the estimates without question and yes, some other companies might have made more progress than they did. The citations were meant as examples of the development in the domain. (And Boston Dynamics doesn't seem to plan a consumer launch of a humanoid robot any time soon, so I didn't cite them.) What made me more optimistic these days are recent progress in multimodal large foundation models which allow robots to perform much better, esp on manual manipulation. See demo from Sep 2023 here: [VIDEO]"}]},"DzRGBAUz5mA":{"wy":"Referenced in clickbait and YouTube-algorithm debates as a candid creator explanation of why good channels use poor thumbnails.","q":{"x":"He talks about the youtube algorithm being the primary driver for the shitty thumbnails","i":30426269,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":26210098,"author":"bilegeek","month":"2021-02","story":"United B772 at Denver on Feb 20th 2021, engine inlet separates from engine","stance":"rec","why":"Linus candidly discusses thumbnails.","quote":"I'll give him credit for being straight about it.","text":"Here's the video where Linus goes into it: [VIDEO] I'll give him credit for being straight about it."},{"id":36439751,"author":"Nathan2055","month":"2023-06","story":"DeArrow – Solving Clickbait on YouTube","stance":"reference","why":"LTT's experiment showing clickbait boosts engagement.","quote":"Linus Tech Tips famously ran an experiment several years ago","text":"While I’m just as annoyed about the clickbait problem as everyone else, it is worth noting that it’s hard to blame it on the individual YouTubers. If you don’t follow whatever the algorithm demands that given week, you’re unlikely to get very many views at all. Even subscription notifications are fed into the algorithm now, and YouTube has been proven to be perfectly willing to not send out notifications for videos it seems unworthy. This even affects subscribers who turned the bell on, a feature that was supposedly released to mitigate the first time they implemented that change by supposedly offering an “always force notifications for this channel’s uploads” option, but YouTube’s guidance now says that the bell only guarantees receiving all notifications generated by a channel while also stating that not all uploads will generate a notification.[1] Linus Tech Tips famously ran an experiment several years ago (and I believe have rerun it a few times since then) and determined that, even though they hated doing it, videos which used clickbait titles and thumbnails got consistently got 20% higher engagement than ones that didn’t use clickbait.[2] I’ve also heard anecdotal numbers even higher than that, and there’s a knock-on effect as YouTube tends to promote channels with consistently high performance way more frequently (this is why big Game Theory uploads instantly and consistently show up in everyone’s recommendations). This isn’t a complaint about your tool at all, don’t "},{"id":32787724,"author":"cube00","month":"2022-09","story":"The Next Chapter for Learning on YouTube","stance":"reference","why":"Creators justify misleading thumbnails as necessary.","quote":"","text":"The way YouTube refuses to enforce their \"misleading thumbnail\" policy says it all about the content they want to encourage. Most creators (rightly or wrongly) justify because \"everyone else does it, so we need to do it as well\" [1] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33020651,"author":"astura","month":"2022-09","story":"On Mr. Beast and being alone in a circle for 100 days","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a good channel forced into clickbait thumbnails.","quote":"videos are severely punished for not having them.","text":"How do you know if a channel has content worth consuming if you block it without watching it? Literally hundreds of good channels worth consuming have thumbnails like that. I find it off-putting too, but videos are severely punished for not having them. This is the channel mentioned in the sibling comment (Linus Tech Tips) - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31952147,"author":"daemoens","month":"2022-07","story":"Severe thermal throttling discovered in Apple's M2 MacBook Pro","stance":"reference","why":"Defends LTT's clickbait as necessary for viewership.","quote":"Their videos are still high quality and that's what actually matters.","text":"They don't. They released a couple videos a few years ago and there was a significant decrease in viewership. Ultimately, theyre a company with almost 50 employees and everyone needs to be paid. Clickbait is annoying, but it's just that. Annoying. Their videos are still high quality and that's what actually matters. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30426269,"author":"chrisan","month":"2022-02","story":"I tested four NVMe SSDs from four vendors – half lose FLUSH’d data on power loss","stance":"reference","why":"Linus blames the algorithm for ugly thumbnails.","quote":"He talks about the youtube algorithm being the primary driver for the shitty thumbnails","text":"He talks about the youtube algorithm being the primary driver for the shitty thumbnails [VIDEO] vs his traditional one that got buried. Not sure about his other stuff you claim, I'm not a super big video guy for tech things (just let me skip and search ahead easily) but this came up with my friends a few years ago after people started noticing many videos from various creators going to this format of thumbnail."},{"id":26381565,"author":"leoc","month":"2021-03","story":"Popular YouTubers who are building their own sites","stance":"reference","why":"LTT is open about thumbnails and the algorithm.","quote":"Sebastian and Linus Media Group seem to be pretty frank and open about a lot of this","text":"Sebastian and Linus Media Group seem to be pretty frank and open about a lot of this: see for instance [VIDEO] on putting up gurning thumbnails and navigating the YT algorithm (and that whole playlist, including a revenue breakdown [LINK] ), the Gamers Nexus video on LTT's production process [LINK] and the coverage LTT regularly lavishes on its own production setup and process, from a nearly-12m video about the awkwardness of ingesting video from their new 12K cameras [LINK] to seemingly quite genuine fly-on-the-wall coverage of temporarily losing their data to a RAID failure [LINK] . Obviously you always have to take anyone's self-presentation with a grain of salt. But they're certainly not hiding the fact that there's a more grounded reality behind the \"on\" personas."},{"id":21804510,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2019-12","story":"Floatplane – Linus Tech Tips launches their own video hosting platform","stance":"reference","why":"LTT explains changes made to appease the YouTube algorithm.","quote":"how they don't like some of the changes they have had to make to stay popular on youtube","text":"I've followed them for a while now on youtube and it has been interesting to watch them evolve their format, content, and presentation to appease youtube's algorithms. They have been pretty open [1] about how they don't like some of the changes they have had to make to stay popular on youtube. I hope they roll back some of the more annoying changes. It appears that the ugly thumbnails sadly still persist in on floatplane [2]. It would also gladly pay for a return to 5 minute videos vs the now standard 15+ min length that gets a middle youtube ad-break and increased revenue. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":36439801,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2023-06","story":"DeArrow – Solving Clickbait on YouTube","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"that argument is undercut a bit by the fact that they use the same playbook on floatplane.","text":"Linus as well. [VIDEO] They claim that they only create clickbait because the platform requires it, but that argument is undercut a bit by the fact that they use the same playbook on floatplane."}],"r":[["f8ZqBLcIvw0",1]]},"eGffUODWWSE":{"wy":"HN cites it as a deeper look at Tesla's integrated thermal management system, from a commentator respected for auto industry knowledge.","q":{"x":"Sandy generally has great commentary on the auto industry.","i":29301363,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29301363,"author":"verdverm","month":"2021-11","story":"Tesla, electric SUVs get poor scores from Consumer Reports","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Tesla thermal management system","quote":"Sandy generally has great commentary on the auto industry.","text":"[VIDEO] (octopump) It's not that heat pumps won't work, it's about pump technology. This is actually the thermal management system, used for both heating and cooling. Sandy generally has great commentary on the auto industry. He's quite genuine Every auto company misleads, at least Tesla is being a leader The point about accident rates is in context of unwitting beta testers. There are many drivers on the road, many of who make the road unsafer. That fact should also be considered when discussing road safety. If FSD beta is better on average, then the roads are getting stepwise safer."},{"id":26042780,"author":"nickik","month":"2021-02","story":"Elon Musk admits Tesla car quality flaws, says mass production is “hell”","stance":"reference","why":"Octovalve as Tesla innovation","quote":"Fully integrated heating/cooling system 'Octovalve'","text":"- Mega Castings, casting have been used before, but never at the size Tesla does. Tesla has costume co-designed machines and they developed their own alloy to make it work. See: [LINK] - Structural Battery Cell (Packs are structural in some cars, not cells) Eventually these two things together will basically be the main part of the car. This is nothing like anybody else does it: [LINK] - Fully integrated heating/cooling system 'Octovalve' ( [VIDEO] ) - Unifying chips to central computer - Stainless Steel Exoskeleton You can see a full breakdown of a last year Model Y here (same guy that did this interview with Elon, was very critical during Model 3 and impressed now): [LINK] That is of course only for cars, the improvement in battery production that Tesla has made are arguably more interesting. A whole video series on this: [LINK]"},{"id":23535695,"author":"SEJeff","month":"2020-06","story":"Model S Long Range Plus: Building the First 400-Mile Electric Vehicle","stance":"reference","why":"Deeper look at the octovalve heat pump","quote":"This segment dives much deeper into the octovalve / heat pump for you to see","text":"> That's a function of battery size. The maximum battery size of the current Kona, Niro, and Soul is 64 kWh. Batteries cost money. The Kona, Niro, and Soul are all less expensive than the top end Teslas. The Model 3 LR battery size is 75 kWh. Are you telling me a 9 kWh difference say ~15% is the sole reason for an almost 25% increase in range? If it is only a matter of the battery size, the range would be linear. It is a function of battery size, total mass, and efficiency. A more or less efficient heat pump is absolutely a defining part of this equation, especially in the cold, as the press release you linked stated. It is why the longer, wider, and taller Model Y has only slightly less range than the Model 3 when it should have less due to aerodynamics and increased mass. > Not the current model. Sorry, you are right. The 2020 Kia Soul EV range is 243 miles. The google card result is out of date. > Sure there is. Tesla used to be the #1 BEV maker in Europe. Now they're #3. That's competition for you: I was referring to competition when it comes to overall efficiency and range. The ID.3 and any EVs built on VW's EV chassis are the closest competition to Tesla has in that area. When it comes to price, it isn't hard to beat Tesla so you're right on this, but I wasn't being specific enough. > But none of this answers the original question. In what way is Tesla's heat pump better than Hyundai's? This segment dives much deeper into the octovalve / heat pump for you to see: [VIDEO"}],"r":[["hJP5GqnTrNo",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["WNWYk4DdT_E",1],["zVhQOhxb1Mc",1]]},"jltnBOrCB7I":{"wy":"HN uses it as an example of cost-benefit reasoning about product safety in threads on recalls, security spending and Boeing.","q":{"x":"There is an entertaining video of Milton Friedman discussing the same issues with an audience member","i":9448941,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9448941,"author":"baddox","month":"2015-04","story":"The Engineer’s Lament","stance":"rec","why":"Friedman debates safety trade-offs with an audience member.","quote":"There is an entertaining video of Milton Friedman discussing the same issues with an audience member","text":"There is an entertaining video of Milton Friedman discussing the same issues with an audience member: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14924553,"author":"randyrand","month":"2017-08","story":"Uber Knowingly Leased Unsafe Cars to Drivers","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman on Ford not recalling the Pinto.","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of this video from the 70s/80s about Ford knowingly distributing an unsafe car - \"Milton Friedman on Ford not recalling the ford pinto\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":12598056,"author":"wsc981","month":"2016-09","story":"Defending Against Hackers Took a Back Seat at Yahoo, Insiders Say","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman discussion related to recall cost calculation.","quote":"","text":"Narrator: A new car built by my company leaves somewhere traveling at 60 mph. The rear differential locks up. The car crashes and burns with everyone trapped inside. Now, should we initiate a recall? Take the number of vehicles in the field, A, multiply by the probable rate of failure, B, multiply by the average out-of-court settlement, C. A times B times C equals X. If X is less than the cost of a recall, we don't do one. The example reminds me of this discussion[0] between Milton Friedman and a student. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19451817,"author":"75dvtwin","month":"2019-03","story":"Doomed Jets Lacked 2 Safety Features That Boeing Sold as Extras","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I think there is something wrong with Friedman's argument.","text":">consumer shopping for a car can decide how much safety they want to pay for I have seen this argument before, by a Nobel Memorial prize in economics, Milton Friedman. [1] [VIDEO] Overall he said, that it is not possible, as a principle, to put 'infinite' economic value into individual life. -- Perhaps the argument can be made, that in a different situation the 'other data' pilots were to use, would increase safety. -- Either way, I think there is something wrong with Friedman's argument. 'Optionality' -- when related to safety features, should be treated very different, than optionality in, say, comfort features. Passengers themselves should not be expected to make choices which optional safety features, on a car, a bus, or plane should be purchased by the drivers/operators. Drivers, should not be expected to make these choices, either. My thinking here, is that An optionality in safety device, should change the whole major (not minor) vehicle model number designation, for example. This would drive different visible profiles: different training, cost, marketing and so on."}],"r":[["DgVS-zXgMTk",1],["TruCIPy79w8",1],["oJTwQvgfgMM",1],["6NlI4No3s0M",1],["jA1C9VFqJKo",1],["jTYkdEU_B4o",1]]},"nfRouGH0oMg":{"wy":"HN cites it as a primary source for Lamport's relativity inspiration behind logical clocks in distributed systems threads.","q":{"x":"This short clip [0] from that interview is worth a watch.","i":39495959,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39495959,"author":"chatmasta","month":"2024-02","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"Short clip of Lamport discussing writing his clocks paper","quote":"This short clip [0] from that interview is worth a watch.","text":"The article itself isn't great, but it does briefly mention Leslie Lamport's 1978 paper \"Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of Events in a Distrib­uted System.\" In a 2016 interview, Lamport talked about his experience writing this paper and its association with special relativity. This short clip [0] from that interview is worth a watch. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36618585,"author":"myk9001","month":"2023-07","story":"Vector Clocks: So what time is it?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Lamport's relativity inspiration quote","quote":"","text":"This isn't a coincidence. Vector clocks, roughly speaking, build upon the idea of Lamport Clocks. This isn't to say that one type of clock is better than the other. The choice between them depends on the task at hand and involves a set of trade-offs. Anyway, Leslie Lamport, who is considered the founding father of distributed systems as we know them today, introduced the concept of Lamport Clocks in his 1978 paper \"Time, clocks, and the ordering of events in a distributed system\" (for which he later won the Turing Award). According to Lamport, that paper was directly inspired by the special theory of relativity. In his own words [^1]: \"[...] special relativity, in particular, the four-dimensional space-time view developed by Minkowski in the famous 1908 paper. I realized that the problems in distributed systems are very much analogous to what's going on in physics [...]\" So, I don't know if these algorithms were reinvented or (re)discovered because, as you rightly say, they happen to operate in our universe. [^1]: [VIDEO] \""},{"id":34750346,"author":"LAC-Tech","month":"2023-02","story":"A Gentle Introduction to CRDTs","stance":"reference","why":"Lamport says special relativity helped inspire his clock concept","quote":"Lamport himself says that his knowledge of special relativity helped him come up with the concept.","text":"Even with terrestrial applications that have very low clock skew, it can be very helpful to have a notion of which events could be effected by previous events and which couldn't have. But you're bang on the money with the physics analogy. Lamport himself says that his knowledge of special relativity helped him come up with the concept. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ESXOAJRdcwQ",1],["9FdHq3WfJgs",1]]},"pVI_smLgTY0":{"wy":"HN mentions it as a sign of Linux reaching millions of non-technical viewers, with some finding it funny and others fascinating.","q":{"x":"Try PewDiePie. around 6.7M people now know something like Linux and Hyprland exists","i":45221759,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45221759,"author":"mindcrash","month":"2025-09","story":"Jeena's Hyprland Demo","stance":"rec","why":"Introduced millions of viewers to Linux and Hyprland","quote":"Try PewDiePie. around 6.7M people now know something like Linux and Hyprland exists","text":"> maybe in part due to DHH promoting it a bit Try PewDiePie. around 6.7M people now know something like Linux and Hyprland exists, and that in 2025 it might even work better than MacOS or Windows and whatever desktop environments on Linux came before. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45722858,"author":"pizlonator","month":"2025-10","story":"What happened to running what you wanted on your own machine?","stance":"reference","why":"PewDiePie telling people to install Linux","quote":"This was a fascinating thing to watch for me (pewdiepie telling people to install Linux)","text":"> we could genuinely lose general purpose computing. > At the moment, anyone can use Linux; it's better and easier than ever. Maybe Linux will save us. This was a fascinating thing to watch for me (pewdiepie telling people to install Linux): [VIDEO] My bet is that the momentum is strong enough that: - A critical mass of PC makers will continue to offer a Linux preinstalled option, or at least some path to installing Linux. - If Windows and macOS take more rights away, it'll just help Linux's market share. So Linux's share will probably grow not only because Linux is getting better but because the corpo OSes trying to take away general purpose computing"},{"id":44023764,"author":"AlienRobot","month":"2025-05","story":"If nothing is curated, how do we find things","stance":"reference","why":"PewDiePie enthusiastically promoting Linux freedom","quote":"I watched that PewDiePie video[1] of him using Linux and I found it extremely funny","text":">Actual businesspeople are pretty ruthless in getting people to using their product. Open source people aren't, by nature (except for Lennart Poettering) I watched that PewDiePie video[1] of him using Linux and I found it extremely funny that this guy was like \"Linux is awesome! Freedom! (literal USA flag in the background) You are a god now!\" and then every Linux-focused channel reacting to that video was like \"yeah, you can play games, but not all of them. Yeah, it works, except... Yeah, you can customize but he will grow out of it when he needs to get work done...\" Linux people are downers. It's like I was watching the second coming of Stallman selling to everyone the idea that you get to control what your computer does, unlike on Windows. You can customize it, you can optimize it yourself, you can remove everything and add anything. And that he had been having so much fun doing it. The man literally told his audience that everyone should use Linux so Linux gets better. While the people who should be promoting it the most insist that \"Linux is not for everyone.\" It really feels like I peered into some sort of alternative reality in that video and it was really refreshing. And I think that's the problem with FLOSS, what FLOSS truly lacks. FLOSS tends to be merely \"an alternative.\" It should be more than that. It should be freedom. It's absolutely ridiculous to me that I can change the font and color of the text of my post on Tumblr, a proprietary closed source social media,"},{"id":43820781,"author":"dijit","month":"2025-04","story":"Widespread power outage in Spain and Portugal","stance":"reference","why":"PewDiePie video on switching to Linux","quote":"leading to this[0] recent video from Pewdiepie...","text":"Agreed. It's crazy how momentum can carry a business. To use a potentially controversial example, Microsoft products (Office, Windows) are still extremely entrenched despite the overwhelming majority of knowledgable people agreeing that they're on a steep downward trajectory and the alternatives have long since surpassed them.. leading to this[0] recent video from Pewdiepie... [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["7cC3_jGwl_U",1],["cidchWg74Y4",1]]},"ubDMLoWz76U":{"wy":"HN points to it as a practical reference for the Linux-VM-on-Mac workflow and as an entry point to NixOS.","q":{"x":"My general setup mirrors this[1] pretty closely","i":33768953,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31098721,"author":"indiv0","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: What’s a good laptop for software development at around $2k?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows running NixOS in a VM on a Mac as dev workflow.","quote":"Just run Linux in a VM! It's my preferred workflow nowadays.","text":"Just run Linux in a VM! It's my preferred workflow nowadays. See: [LINK] [VIDEO] Only real limitations are lack of ML because no server/desktop-grade GPU and some missing ARM-based docker containers. But the latter is slowly being resolved and the former isn't gonna happen on a laptop anyways."},{"id":39778587,"author":"xyst","month":"2024-03","story":"My 2024 Setup","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a NixOS VM-on-Mac development setup.","quote":"Similar to hashicorp founder setup found here","text":"Personally been working on replacing “brew” with “nix” for package management. Eventually using VMs spun up with qemu to emulate nixOS on my Mac for primary dev environment. Similar to hashicorp founder setup found here (except hoping to not use “parallels or vm ware fusion”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33768953,"author":"bsnnkv","month":"2022-11","story":"LXD containers on macOS at near-native speeds","stance":"reference","why":"Setup walkthrough the commenter's own workflow closely mirrors.","quote":"My general setup mirrors this[1] pretty closely","text":"My general setup mirrors this[1] pretty closely, though of course on Windows I can just use WSL2. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30994013,"author":"xisthesqrtof9","month":"2022-04","story":"Impressions from a first-time Mac user","stance":"reference","why":"Inspired NixOS VM setup on Mac.","quote":"Inspired by Mitchell Hashimoto's VMWare setup[0].","text":"3 of the 4 items that the author mentioned can be solved with using NixOS inside a VM on your mac :) Inspired by Mitchell Hashimoto's VMWare setup[0]. I setup my own computer in such a way, I now have the best of both worlds. Developing on a linux machine, where I can control everything if I wanted (down to the OS) and the ease of Notes/iMessages whenever I need it. Window management is a pita because of internal APIs and the fact that Apple doesn't cater to people that actually care about these tools. Check out Yabai[1] which btw requires you to disable SIP (System Integrity Protection) if you want to use its full potential. Instead you can run NixOS and choose your favourite window/tiling manager (i3). Package manager: I still run Nix but I am not that happy with it. Either I need to spend some more time or look for an alternative. One of the problems is the ability to easily pin older versions. [0] - [VIDEO] [1] - [LINK]"},{"id":30777107,"author":"alekun","month":"2022-03","story":"Firefox now only available via snap on Ubuntu","stance":"reference","why":"Sparked interest in NixOS.","quote":"I got interested in NixOS after watching","text":"I got interested in NixOS after watching [LINK] and [VIDEO] I use fedora since long time and so far is quite stable without any non-sense"}],"r":[["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["8SkdfdXWYaI",1]]},"w8c_m6U1f9o":{"wy":"Posted as a joke or satire whenever a company sells out its users or surveillance comes up.","q":{"x":"This Onion talk put it really well","i":6574574,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6574574,"author":"smokinn","month":"2013-10","story":"The Story Behind a Radical New Idea: A Social Network for Academia","stance":"rec","why":"Satire on companies selling out their communities.","quote":"This Onion talk put it really well","text":"One of the problems with for-profit is that the company is likely to sell its community out if they think that'll make them more money. See: Elsevier buying Mendeley and the backlash from that: [LINK] This Onion talk put it really well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18466647,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-11","story":"Facebook Applied for Patent to Predict Who’s in House Based on Family Photos","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Words on their own are not worth much in the long run. It's easy to pursue well-meaning plans on good days when everything is working like it's supposed to and everywhere you go people seem to be talking about the amazing stuff your \"unicorn trajectory\" will create. Cory Doctorow had a useful analogy[1]: if you're trying to lose weight, most of the time it isn't very difficult to resist tempting siren's call from the box of cookies in your pantry. Obviously not buying the tempting snack would have been a better (and simpler) idea, but willpower is plentiful on good days. Obviously that's a poor risk assessment: of course it's easy to make the annoying-bug-beneficial choice when it doesn't cost much. The actual test is what happens on a very bad day when you're exhausted and worried about other problems. Most people eventually end up caving and \"cheating\" on their diet, simply because we're terrible at judging risks and overestimate our ability to resist temptation when it isn't actually being tested. The solution to this problem is to have the humility to admit our weaknesses in assessing risks and how easy it can be to overestimate our own abilities. From that point of views, prudence suggests a good idea might be using the benefits available on good days to prepare for the bad days and moments of weakness. Don't allow the temptation into the house so succumbing to temptation isn't possible or involves a much higher cost. Words that promise that you won't fall to temptation "},{"id":8518218,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2014-10","story":"Ad blocker that clicks on the ads","stance":"reference","why":"Satire about selling out users to advertisers.","quote":"The Onion was right[1].","text":"Paying directly changes the relationship significantly - a business is generally going to pay attention to their customers that produce their revenue than the \"free\" accounts that are the merely the product being sold to advertisers. The Onion was right[1]. In the rush to sell out their \"users\" to to whomever is willing to pay, a lot of people seem to have come to believe that advertising is the only way to the internet can work. The internet enabled many new ways of publishing due it removing most of the per-transaction costs. I suspect we haven't even seen most of these methods. While \"Kickstarter\" style funding and Wikipedia's \"public television style\" requests for donations, while interesting experiments, are only the first generation of what is enabled by the internet. Unfortunately, untested and unproven (by somebody else) ideas do imply some amount of risk, which scares a lot of people back into the traditional method where the advertisers get to paint over everything. edit: forgot URL [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39321729,"author":"jowea","month":"2024-02","story":"Reddit beats film industry again, won't have to reveal pirates' IP addresses","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Oh yes one my favourite ~~TED~~ Onion talk business case studies","text":"Oh yes one my favourite ~~TED~~ Onion talk business case studies: \"The Power Of Selling Out: Your Customers As Political Capital - Onion Talks - Ep. 9\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18323411,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-10","story":"Google’s smart city dream is turning into a privacy nightmare","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Onion was also prescient about the end game for surveillance based companies","text":"The Onion was also prescient about the end game for surveillance based companies: [VIDEO] (this video is especially interesting in light of Google's recent involvement in Chinese censorship)"},{"id":15117710,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-08","story":"Facebook says Pages that regularly share false news won’t be able to buy ads","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Another infamous Onion video in a similar style","text":"Another infamous Onion video in a similar style is \"The Power Of Selling Out: Your Customers As Political Capital\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":9900830,"author":"8ig8","month":"2015-07","story":"Let's talk content. AMA","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"As usual, The Onion has this covered (2012)","text":"As usual, The Onion has this covered (2012): [VIDEO] \"The Power Of Selling Out: Your Customers As Political Capital - Onion Talks\""}]},"z-q8ehzHeQQ":{"wy":"HN links it as a neat breakdown of an unusual CRT/LCD color technology.","q":{"x":"There's a neat breakdown of the tech on this YT channel","i":23662339,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23662339,"author":"tootie","month":"2020-06","story":"Show HN: Rainbow – an attempt to display colour on a B&W monitor","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of JVC's LCD/CRT hybrid color display technology","quote":"There's a neat breakdown of the tech on this YT channel","text":"JVC built a working model with that approach. There's a neat breakdown of the tech on this YT channel: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43897745,"author":"grishka","month":"2025-05","story":"A Tektronix TDS 684B Oscilloscope Uses CCD Analog Memory","stance":"reference","why":"Field-sequential color CRT technology beyond oscilloscopes","quote":"this particular CRT technology was not only used in oscilloscopes","text":"Modern video projectors also display color channels sequentially and also exhibit that effect. Also, this particular CRT technology was not only used in oscilloscopes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32660607,"author":"grumbel","month":"2022-08","story":"Advantages of CRT over LCD","stance":"reference","why":"JVC hybrid adds color to a black and white screen","quote":"There is also an interesting LCD/CRT hybrid from JVC[2]","text":"The Sony Watchman[1] had the electron gun at the bottom shooting at a tilted screen instead of shooting at it from the back. This allowed to make a GameBoy-sized portable CRT TV. There is also an interesting LCD/CRT hybrid from JVC[2], it's not flat by itself, but the way it adds color to an otherwise black&white screen could be used in slim designs. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30862834,"author":"xattt","month":"2022-03","story":"Why is the red primary color oriented at 104° on a vectorscope?","stance":"reference","why":"Studio monitor using an LCD overlay on a monochrome CRT","quote":"looked at a studio monitor that worked on the principle of cycling an LCD overlay over a monochrome CRT","text":"Tangential but Alec Watson of Technology Connections looked at a studio monitor that worked on the principle of cycling an LCD overlay over a monochrome CRT. When I was growing up in Toronto, I want to say that I remember seeing these in use by “LiveEye” crews of CityTV whenever I went by an on-location shoot. (1) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aw7fUMhNov8",1],["15RTC22Z2xI",1]]},"-VcWF8dIDj4":{"wy":"Cited in threads about earthquakes and tsunami warnings as a visceral view of what a tsunami wave looks like at sea.","q":{"x":"Here's a visual of your thoughts from the Fukushima event","i":44730787,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":49311607,"author":"zamadatix","month":"2026-08","story":"Magnitude 7.7 Earthquake – 68 km NNW of Ende, Indonesia","stance":"reference","why":"Longer unedited source footage of the tsunami","quote":"Longer & unedited video from cited source","text":"Same video with normal controls: [LINK] Longer & unedited video from cited source: [VIDEO] . based on the translation of what folks are saying, this is a 10 meter wave at 38 meter sea depth."},{"id":44730787,"author":"x______________","month":"2025-07","story":"M8.7 earthquake in Western Pacific, tsunami warning issued","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of a ship riding a tsunami wave","quote":"Here's a visual of your thoughts from the Fukushima event","text":"Here's a visual of your thoughts from the Fukushima event: [VIDEO] Japan, Tsunami. Coast Guard ship rides over the tsunami waves. 日本 - 津波 4.1M views · 14 years ago"},{"id":41190394,"author":"isoprophlex","month":"2024-08","story":"Tsunami Alert Issued for Japan","stance":"reference","why":"Coast guard ship riding over tsunami waves offshore","quote":"pretty scary vid, for sure.","text":"IIRC the water displacement on open seas is super modest, only when the thing hits shallower water near the coast does it become a problem Edit: [VIDEO] They're a few miles off the coast, ~10 m high waves, 38 m deep water according to some comment that translates the audio. pretty scary vid, for sure."}]},"1QD9XQm_Jd4":{"wy":"Mentioned mainly as the place to hear Yegge discuss Rhino on Rails, a project that was never released.","m":[{"id":2146260,"author":"megaduck","month":"2011-01","story":"The Next Big Language","stance":"reference","why":"Yegge discussing his Rails clone on Rhino","quote":"The first big one was a rails clone on Rhino, which he talked about but never released","text":"Looking at Yegge's projects, I think NBL was just \"javascript\". The first big one was a rails clone on Rhino, which he talked about but never released ( [VIDEO] ). After that, he started building a system for extending emacs with JS, named \"ejacs\". It never got fully baked, but the effort produced the very nice js2-mode. Details here: [LINK] So, I think NBL was just \"Javascript\". Awesome call. Despite the problems with the standards process, JS has exploded in popularity over the past three years."},{"id":1517580,"author":"riprock","month":"2010-07","story":"Blogger Finger","stance":"reference","why":"Yegge interview on Rhino on Rails project","quote":"I wonder what happened to his Rhino on Rails project","text":"I wonder what happened to his Rhino on Rails project...I would love to hear a follow up on that. [LINK] [VIDEO] (2008)"},{"id":195571,"author":"brooksbp","month":"2008-05","story":"Ask YC: Django vs Ruby on Rails","stance":"reference","why":"Steve Yegge interview on Rhino on Rails","quote":"there's a video on Youtube...","text":"+1 Obviously haven't read much Yegge when making statements like that. Ontop of the above defense, there's a video on Youtube... [VIDEO]"}]},"2HZs-v0PR44":{"wy":"Posted as a reference clip in geopolitical threads about Israel and US policy, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":47925645,"author":"pazimzadeh","month":"2026-04","story":"Iran caused more extensive damage to U.S. military bases than publicly known","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as support that Iran sees Israel as a US proxy.","quote":"","text":"> it's worth understanding motivations of people on both sides. 100% agree. > Iran doesn’t have bases or any presence near US borders but Iran does have proxies (with IRG advisors) surrounding Israel That's the way you see it but the way Iran sees it is that Israel is a proxy of the US. [VIDEO] The US also chose to fight Taliban alone and lost, rather than continuing their post-9/11 collaboration with Iran - Iran being local to the region would have been a great asset, as they were right up until Bush's \"axis of evil\" doctrine. > Like you said, when Khomeini stepped off the plane he was flanked by a US ambassador and a rabbi, US policy was once different. You mean to say that the US read Khomeini's book which talks about implementing Sharia law, violence against \"corrupt people\", destruction of non-Islamic religious centers, the West wanting to steal Iran's resources, etc.. but were willing to take the risk because they were so afraid of the left taking power in Iran. Then they tried to fix their mistake by having Saddam attack Iran, which backfired and created another 50 years worth of resentment towards the US. And they likely ensured another 20+ years of distrust with their recent actions. > Iran's actions may not be as evil as what the US has done to Iran Glad to hear you say this - wish you said this earlier as I would not have guessed it from your previous comments. If you truly think that then I'm not sure why you're surprised at the slogans and Iran's defensive measur"},{"id":42205956,"author":"jumping_frog","month":"2024-11","story":"ICC issues warrants for Netanyahu, Gallant, and Hamas officials","stance":"reference","why":"Biden clip on Israel.","quote":"This video is relevant.","text":"This video is relevant. US President Joe Biden: “If there were not an Israel, we’d have to invent one.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":49127255,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-07","story":"The bond market isn't buying what Fed Chair Warsh is selling","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's this [1]. From 1986: > If there were not an Israel, we’d have to invent one It's lying about working on a ceasefire [2][3]. It's sending more and more weapons to Israel afer October 7, going so far as to split them into up to 100 smaller packages to avoid the need for Congressional approval [4]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK]"}]},"AGYn7ER5U_0":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the canonical example of an intelligence official lying to Congress under oath without consequence.","q":{"x":"James Clapper blatantly lied to Congress, is anything ever going to happen to him?","i":20001759,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":25145770,"author":"inquirerofsorts","month":"2020-11","story":"NSA Spied on Denmark as It Chose Its Future Fighter Aircraft: Report","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"repeatedly lie under sworn oath.","text":"> But evvvveryone spies on everyone The NSA has a budget bigger than entire countries economic output. Not everyone spies on everyone to the same extent. Not everyone breaks their own laws constantly to the same extent. Not everyone hides behind some bullshit moral argument to justify their actions to same extent. The rot is real. They now have more power than any elected official including the President and repeatedly lie under sworn oath. These people are no longer public servants they are public masters. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20001759,"author":"sarcasmatwork","month":"2019-05","story":"Wikipedia case against NSA internet snooping returns to court","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"James Clapper blatantly lied to Congress, is anything ever going to happen to him?","text":"Few questions after reading this: Did NSA really stop the warrantless searches? They hinted in April they would. What is NSA going to do with their Utah data center? Tax payers paid for it right? [LINK] James Clapper blatantly lied to Congress, is anything ever going to happen to him? [VIDEO]"},{"id":14358340,"author":"ChristianBundy","month":"2017-05","story":"Chelsea Manning Is Expected to Leave Prison, 28 Years Early","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Wyden: \"Does the NSA collect any type of data at all on millions or hundreds of millions of Americans?\" Clapper: \"No, sir. Wyden: \"It does /not/?\" Clapper: \"Not wittingly. There are cases where they could inadvertently perhaps but not wittingly.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"Aaxw4zbULMs":{"wy":"Cited as the canonical visual demonstration of coupled oscillators synchronizing, applied to analogies from brain waves to LLM populations.","q":{"x":"Everything that oscillates at roughly the same frequency and has some coupling synchronizes.","i":36377696,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":45625409,"author":"roenxi","month":"2025-10","story":"The Majority AI View","stance":"reference","why":"Metronomes synchronising and desynchronising.","quote":"You might find some inspiration in this video of metronomes synchronising and desynchronysing","text":"You might find some inspiration in this video of metronomes synchronising and desynchronysing [0]. It doesn't take much to cause things to happen at the same time, in the case the CEOs are likely all responding to the same market signals at the same time for the same reason. Probably interest rates. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44026172,"author":"Animats","month":"2025-05","story":"Emergent social conventions and collective bias in LLM populations","stance":"reference","why":"Metronomes self-synchronizing as a pervasive phenomenon.","quote":"Watch this video of metronomes self-synchronizing.","text":"That may be reading too much into this behavior. Watch this video of metronomes self-synchronizing.[1] That's a pervasive phenomenon. Anything with similar oscillation frequency and coupling will do this. (Including polling systems with fixed retry intervals.) Are you sure that's not just this effect? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36377696,"author":"Animats","month":"2023-06","story":"Brain Waves Synchronize When People Interact","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates coupled oscillators synchronizing.","quote":"Everything that oscillates at roughly the same frequency and has some coupling synchronizes.","text":"Everything that oscillates at roughly the same frequency and has some coupling synchronizes.[1] No surprise here. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15989845,"author":"carapace","month":"2017-12","story":"Acoustic Denial of Service Attacks on HDDs","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates oscillators syncing through coupling.","quote":"Huygens synchronization, oscillators sync up through resonance...","text":"resonance is a hellofa thing Can carry information, cf. Huygens synchronization, oscillators sync up through resonance... [VIDEO]"}]},"CzwtCBj5jEs":{"wy":"HN cites it as the go-to video on the little-known MD Data format, mostly in MiniDisc and obsolete-media threads.","q":{"x":"LGR did an Oddware video about it a few years back","i":34910211,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34910211,"author":"goosedragons","month":"2023-02","story":"Toshiba HD-DVD firmware archive","stance":"reference","why":"LGR Oddware video on MD Data, which flopped.","quote":"LGR did an Oddware video about it a few years back","text":"They did. MD Data was a thing. It just flopped super hard. It was way too expensive over Zip disc for a minor storage increase and size decrease. LGR did an Oddware video about it a few years back: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33651914,"author":"fredoralive","month":"2022-11","story":"Dissecting a Dummy Promo MiniDisc","stance":"reference","why":"LGR looks at a Sony MiniDisc data drive.","quote":"Sony did release drives, DGR looks at one in this video for example","text":"Sony did release drives, DGR looks at one in this video for example [VIDEO] . It's yet another example in the weird graveyard of replacements for the 1.44MB floppy that didn't catch on."},{"id":22701525,"author":"dijit","month":"2020-03","story":"Dumping MiniDisc Media","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as an unfamiliar example, uncertain if it is MiniDisc.","quote":"","text":"Seems like you're right but I never-ever saw this: [VIDEO] Although it seems like it's not really minidisks."}]},"Frkw6aurVUY":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up in EV-adoption threads as an answer to how people without driveways can charge on the street.","q":{"x":"Pop-up chargers are at least hidden when not in use","i":31378143,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":45899537,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2025-11","story":"Using street lamps as EV chargers","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier example of pop-up street chargers","quote":"See also pop-up chargers from six years ago","text":"Robert Llewellyn (previously of Red Dwarf fame) covered this idea eight years ago (July 2017) on his 'electrify' channel: > The simple and very commonplace lamp post will soon become a ubiquitous charge point for electric cars. They charge at about 5 kW, or 16 amps, not super fast but overnight charging is all most drivers need. Ubitricity is a German based company who've come up with a simple, cheap to install and well managed system for more people on more streets to adopt electric cars. * [LINK] See also pop-up chargers from six years ago: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":31378143,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2022-05","story":"Shell to install 100K charge points in the UK by 2030","stance":"reference","why":"Pop-up chargers that stay hidden when unused","quote":"Pop-up chargers are at least hidden when not in use","text":"Wireless charging has the lowest footprint and is the best option when there isn't much room, though not many EVs can charge wirelessly yet: [LINK] Pop-up chargers are at least hidden when not in use: [VIDEO] Utility pole chargers do a good job of staying out of the way: [LINK]"},{"id":29239775,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-11","story":"Electric vehicles could fully recharge in 5 minutes with new cable design","stance":"reference","why":"Shows pop-up chargers as a street charging solution","quote":"","text":"Fully Charged , based out of the UK, had episodes on street lamp plugs, and pop-up chargers: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] Each area/street will probably have to examined individually to find the right solution for it."},{"id":25876630,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-01","story":"Electric vehicles close to ‘tipping point’ of mass adoption","stance":"reference","why":"Pop-up charger option for street parkers","quote":"I'm a bit skeptical.","text":"> The number of people in the UK and Ireland that have to park overnight on the street is way too high to start banning ICE vehicles within the next decade. You're not wrong (and I too am a \"garage orphan\"—neither a garage or even a driveway), but there are some options being developed. The YT channel Fully Charged featured two a while back: * Street lamps: [LINK] * Pop-up charger: [VIDEO] I'm a bit skeptical."},{"id":25102766,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-11","story":"UK to ban sale of new petrol and diesel cars from 2030: FT","stance":"reference","why":"Shows pop-up chargers for streets without poles","quote":"See also pop-up chargers for streets without poles","text":"> All new street lights (as of a couple of years ago) have to have charging points built-in […] If anyone wants to see an example of light pole charging, the Fully Charged YT channel had an episode of one example: * [LINK] See also pop-up chargers for streets without poles: * [VIDEO]"}]},"G9D8dzl4zGk":{"wy":"HN cites it as the source of the released UFO footage, though commenters often debate whether the video shows anything beyond camera artifacts.","m":[{"id":19829090,"author":"soulofmischief","month":"2019-05","story":"What Is Going on with UFOs and the Department of Defense?","stance":"reference","why":"Released IR footage of unexplained maneuvers","quote":"is incredible and, if real, proves that high-G maneuvers without exhaust or aerofoils exist","text":"Isn't this most likely just a response to the surge of drones over the last half decade? I imagine UFO sightings have proportionally increased and thus the number of ad-hoc reported sightings within the Navy finally crossed the threshold. It could even be a foreign government or spy agency doing amateur reconnaissance with new tech. As the article states, > The lack of a structured procedure and classification system, and the nebulous fear of being stigmatized by reporting things like UFOs [is] something that has long plagued the military and private sectors alike There is also the nebulous fear of people overreacting whenever you finally decide to implement these common-sense policies. You must be hiding something, even if you were ironically just trying to be more transparent. I don't mean to sound like a denier. The released IR footage from the same observation period [0] is incredible and, if real, proves that high-G maneuvers without exhaust or aerofoils exist and we are still lacking some very fundamental understanding about the nature of gravity. I also found the article to be well thought-out and that it covered most of the most obvious answers without trying to paint a particular picture. I'll have to pay more attention to Rogoway. Speculating on the shape of the aircraft, would the round tic-tac shape provide the least average air resistance in every direction provided an aerofoil is not needed for flight? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19749626,"author":"themodelplumber","month":"2019-04","story":"U.S. Navy drafting new guidelines for reporting UFOs","stance":"reference","why":"Pentagon UFO program news and released incident videos","quote":"","text":"I wonder if this video release impacted the decision? [VIDEO] I remember when it came out it caused quite a stir. Edit: Here's the Wikipedia page: [LINK]"},{"id":20070854,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2019-06","story":"Carrier Group in Recent UFO Encounters Had New Air Defense Tech","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the video looks like the camera software is trying to track something that's on or within the camera","text":"It was. But the video looks like the camera software is trying to track something that's on or within the camera, thus doing a steady rotation at roughly uniform rate. In plane's frame of reference, this footage would correspond to an object trajectory shaped like a circle centered around the plane. That's a pretty weird trajectory to have. See for yourself: [VIDEO] . And tell me, why would the UFO start rotating exactly at the moment the camera is about to cross 0 degrees, and persist through what looks like camera axis reorientation, continuously rotating at the rate about 2x of the rate camera axis rotates?"}]},"H8eP99neOVs":{"wy":"HN uses it as the canonical source for the 'focus is saying no' quote, cited in threads on product decisions such as dropping old hardware support.","q":{"x":"the article got its headline from, which it credited to Steve jobs in the first paragraph, from WWDC ‘97","i":33258394,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":47833809,"author":"nntwozz","month":"2026-04","story":"Why macOS27 won't be supporting Intel anymore","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs quote on focus used to contrast with Microsoft.","quote":"Focusing is about saying no. — Steve Jobs (WWDC '97)","text":"Focusing is about saying no. — Steve Jobs [VIDEO] (WWDC '97) This is something Microsoft will never learn, it's not in their DNA."},{"id":33258394,"author":"sixstringtheory","month":"2022-10","story":"Focus is saying no to good ideas","stance":"reference","why":"Original WWDC '97 source of the article's headline quote.","quote":"the article got its headline from, which it credited to Steve jobs in the first paragraph, from WWDC ‘97","text":"Funny enough the next suggested play after that video is this one where the article got its headline from, which it credited to Steve jobs in the first paragraph, from WWDC ‘97: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32961709,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-09","story":"Get in zoomer, we're saving React","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That was the vision of OpenDoc, and the component based CyberDog web browser that Apple built with it. [LINK] >OpenDoc is a defunct multi-platform software componentry framework standard created by Apple in the 1990s for compound documents, intended as an alternative to Microsoft's proprietary Object Linking and Embedding (OLE).[1] It is one of Apple's earliest experiments with open standards and collaborative development methods with other companies. OpenDoc development was transferred to the non-profit Component Integration Laboratories, Inc. (CI Labs), owned by a growing team of major corporate backers and effectively starting an industry consortium. In 1992, the historic AIM alliance launched between Apple, IBM, and Motorola—with OpenDoc as a foundation. With the return of Steve Jobs to Apple, OpenDoc was discontinued in March 1997. [...] >After three years of development on OpenDoc itself, the first OpenDoc-based product release was Apple's CyberDog web browser in May 1996. The second was on August 1, 1996, of IBM's two packages of OpenDoc components for OS/2, available on the Club OpenDoc website for a 30 day free trial: the Person Pak is \"components aimed at organizing names, addresses, and other personal information\", for use with personal information management (PIM) applications, at $229; and the Table Pak \"to store rows and columns in a database file\" at $269. IBM then anticipated the release of 50 more components by the end of 1996.[7] WWDC 1996 Cyberdog demo [LIN"},{"id":18393341,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2018-11","story":"At least it wasn't Oracle","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Then there were Apple and IBM's two joint ventures: Taligent and Kaleida (where I worked on ScriptX until it shut down). [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Working at Kaleida was like having parents who couldn't get along and argued all the time, so no hard decisions ever actually got made. ScriptX supported QuickTime on MacOS, Windows and OS/2, but Apple was shy about IBM and other companies having access to the source code. IBM desperately wanted everyone to use and love OS/2 (so they renamed it Warp so the cool kids would dig it), while Taligent was tasked at solving the problem of Apple's shitty operating system. Taligent also had their own weird incompatible object model, as well as CommonPoint's \"people, places and things\" metaphor, which they shamelessly ripped off from Schoolhouse Rock. [LINK] CHRP was the Common Hardware Reference Platform for the PowerPC, which IBM and Apple collaborated on. IBM made a CHRP PowerPC Thinkpad that ran OS/2, but out of pride refused to sell one that ran MacOS, which kind of missed the whole point of CHRP. IBM just couldn't imagine why anyone would want to run anything but OS/2 on them. I would have loved to have a MacOS powerbook running on wonderful Thinkpad hardware, because Apple's laptop hardware was pretty crappy at the time. It would have been the best of both worlds, but it was never to be. [LINK] And then there was OpenDoc. There were actually two different incompatible implementations of OpenDoc, one from Apple on MacOS and one from IBM on "},{"id":4288625,"author":"ryannielsen","month":"2012-07","story":"Apple To Release Mountain Lion On July 25","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on focus as saying no, applied to dropping old hardware support.","quote":"As SJ himself said, focus is about saying \"no\".","text":"It isn't like these support issues are a surprise to them, they just have different priorities than fixing them. Exactly! Like I said, I'm sure they felt their limited resources could be better spent elsewhere. Supporting 6 year old hardware is a very, very low priority, so they didn't spend any resources on it. As SJ himself said, focus is about saying \"no\". This video discusses OpenDoc, but it could just as easily apply to supporting 6 year old hardware: [VIDEO] Apple is always pushing forward. Sometimes perhaps a bit too aggressively, but they're always pushing forward."}]},"I25UeVXrEHQ":{"wy":"Used as a recurring gag or jab at Stallman's social eccentricities; not recommended for its content.","m":[{"id":24882434,"author":"BigBubbleButt","month":"2020-10","story":"The Coming Civil War over General Purpose Computing (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"For anyone interested in the toe cheese incident","text":"> What's more worrying is people I speak to regularly now don't even know who he is. And if they do it's because of the toe cheese incident. I've known who Stallman is for a long time, but I had never heard about the toe cheese incident. I just looked it up. My reaction: [LINK] For anyone interested in the toe cheese incident: [VIDEO] I'm not trying to malign Stallman here, but you can't deny this is interesting."},{"id":2329523,"author":"getsat","month":"2011-03","story":"Richard Stallman: Cell phones are 'Stalin's dream'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"His lack of concern for social norms is... intriguing.","text":"Not antagonising you, but you are aware that he did something similar during a lecture once, right? His lack of concern for social norms is... intriguing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46256471,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2025-12","story":"Apple has locked my Apple ID, and I have no recourse. A plea for help","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"there are funny things about the guy, such as the video where he eats something from his foot.","text":"Well, there are funny things about the guy, such as the video where he eats something from his foot. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26652303,"author":"Rochus","month":"2021-03","story":"The FSF unionised because of RMS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"he is nevertheless an expert for certain culinary specialities","text":"> He's not leading a culinary institute No, but he is nevertheless an expert for certain culinary specialities (see e.g. [VIDEO] ). Essentially, of course, this is also about good manners. Anyway, it seems to be a law of nature that a guru will always find suitable devotees."},{"id":18876585,"author":"ddebernardy","month":"2019-01","story":"So Long, Macbook. Hello Again, Linux","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Is Linux that popular with youngsters after they've left university? When I think about laptop Linux users it's the archetypical greasy bearded GNU'er [1] that springs to mind... [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11657126,"author":"doener","month":"2016-05","story":"New GNU Emacs website","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"a guy, which eats things from his foot.","text":"So this is obviously an attempt to let Emacs look like a software that is not mainly developed by a guy, which eats things from his foot.^^ [VIDEO]"},{"id":3756637,"author":"spwmoni","month":"2012-03","story":"Interview with Richard Stallman, Founder of Free Software Foundation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I wonder if he realizes that he's already got a youtube presence?","text":"\"We apologize for the inconvenience caused with watching the video of the interview. Richard did not want to upload this interview in youtube as it is not free ('free' as in free speech/freedom, not price). He also wanted us to use a non proprietary video format like ogg theora and not the patented formats like mp4. When we chose to use dropbox to stream the video, dropbox suspended our accounts for generating excessive traffic. Kindly download the video from the icon below.\" How fitting that an interview with RMS should be preceded by this. I wonder if he realizes that he's already got a youtube presence? [VIDEO]"},{"id":1386324,"author":"kevinelliott","month":"2010-05","story":"Richard Stallman agrees to do an AMA on reddit","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If it wasn't for this article pointing to the reedit post, I would have never discovered this juicy, delicious, and distasteful video of Stallman eating his foot pickings. [VIDEO] That literally brightened my day. Brilliance is questionable if it means this is the end result!"},{"id":35254432,"author":"someotherperson","month":"2023-03","story":"Web fingerprinting is worse than I thought","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the guy who sits down, takes off his socks and eats his toenails/toe skin in the middle of a Q&A[0]","text":"That's right, the guy who sits down, takes off his socks and eats his toenails/toe skin in the middle of a Q&A[0] shouldn't be held to some ambiguous standard of how to navigate society. It's exactly this eggshell stepping that people are expected to adhere to that has people treating him the way they are. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18562605,"author":"SEJeff","month":"2018-11","story":"Amazon Timestream – Fast, scalable, fully managed time series database","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"tendency to actually eat his toenail gunk[1]","text":"RMS has said publically that proprietary software should be illegal, and that free software guarantees user freedoms. This is a philosophy, and one that opines every user wants to become a developer. For things like emacs, and much of GNU, this is true, for most of the rest of the world, this is not. I eschew free software because I'm not about forcing my views on others (which is literally the mission of GNU). I'm about developing software to be the best it can be, and maybe meeting some friends along the way. Open source, being the best software development model overall, allows me to meet that goal. You could almost say some of RMS's more extreme quirks border on authoritarian (see the example with the abortion joke in the libc manual he FORBADE removal of and demanded be re-added when a dev simply overruled him). He's not acting in a manner that encourages \"freedom\", but as a simple and obvious dictator of all things GNU or claiming to be GNU. He's frequently tried to shape the path of GNOME (which I am a former foundation member and was on the sysadmin team) in areas he literally has no business weighing in on. Then there are some more gross personality problems, like his sexism, or tendency to actually eat his toenail gunk[1], or to ever refuse to be wrong on anything, even when an entire community disagrees with him. Dr Stallman has done a great deal of good for the world with Free Software, however like the VAX and PDP-11, his time has passed. Open source won just lik"},{"id":5773456,"author":"btilly","month":"2013-05","story":"US entertainment industry to Congress: make it legal for us to deploy rootkits","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"videos like show him as eccentric for reasons that go well beyond sticking to principle.","text":"As much as I appreciate Stallman, videos like [VIDEO] show him as eccentric for reasons that go well beyond sticking to principle."},{"id":3966027,"author":"unimpressive","month":"2012-05","story":"FreeBSD 10 to use Clang, GCC will be deprecated","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"which no doubt refers to Stallmans's legendary hygienic practices.","text":"For bonus points, in the actual recording they note that Hypocrites \"Smells like thousand year old goat cheese.\". which no doubt refers to Stallmans's legendary hygienic practices. [0] Needless to say, the BSD guys aren't really fans. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3178872,"author":"hugh3","month":"2011-10","story":"Dave Winer: Why I stand up for Stallman","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here is a video of Richard Stallman, in the middle of a talk, eating something he's just picked off his foot","text":"Some folks miss the point. We only make fun of Stallman in an attempt to change his behaviour in a way that benefits both him and others. For instance, rms smells bad. If he could be persuaded to smell less bad, then everybody would benefit. Ridicule is a valuable social mechanism for giving negative feedback to those who behave inappropriately. For those who say \"but can't we give this feedback in a non-hurtful way?\", the answer is no, for the same reason that your brain can't make pain non-painful -- some people are too damn stubborn to listen to non-painful feedback. Here is a video of Richard Stallman, in the middle of a talk, eating something he's just picked off his foot: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1559508,"author":"bosch","month":"2010-07","story":"Richard Stallman answers Reddit's top 25 questions.","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I lost all respect for the guy.","text":"I don't care what this guy has to say anymore. After he pulled his sock off, picked his foot and ate whatever the fuck it was he picked off, all in front of what appears to be a lecture class sized audience - I lost all respect for the guy. [VIDEO] No wonder computer people have a brutal rep for social interaction. WTF."},{"id":1476348,"author":"blasdel","month":"2010-06","story":"GNU HURD: Altered visions and lost promise","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Now he just eats detritus from his foot","text":"\"like giving the Han Solo award to the Rebel Fleet\" -- he was a terrific speaker a decade ago, even if he did get off the rails quickly raving about GNU-slash-Linux. Now he just eats detritus from his foot: [VIDEO]"}]},"KP_bKvXkoC4":{"wy":"Cited in FreeBSD vs Linux debates as the source for why Netflix chose FreeBSD, with commenters reading the GPL as the main factor.","q":{"x":"From both of those sources I would say avoiding GPL was the primary consideration.","i":23820945,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":26055202,"author":"nix23","month":"2021-02","story":"FreeBSD 13.0 Beta1 Now Available","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">I cannot help being a bit sad that contribution like this will only benefit the few people that use FreeBSD Same could be said the other way around, the thing is Linux can implement it and even take the code, the other way is rather difficult....so much for freedom. And not sure what you mean with \"few\" people, the few Playstation 3/4/5 users? Juniper? OpnSense/Pfsense installations? Or the few Truenas/Freenas users? >Or not something else enough? [VIDEO]"},{"id":23820298,"author":"jaekash","month":"2020-07","story":"Tutorial sites treating FreeBSD like a Linux distro","stance":"reference","why":"Netflix talk on choosing FreeBSD; no claim of better performance.","quote":"This presentation also makes no indication that FreeBSD was chosen because it had better performance than Linux","text":"This presentation also makes no indication that FreeBSD was chosen because it had better performance than Linux: [VIDEO] Seems GPL already ruled Linux out."},{"id":23820469,"author":"jaekash","month":"2020-07","story":"Tutorial sites treating FreeBSD like a Linux distro","stance":"reference","why":"Netflix presenter on FreeBSD choice and GPL concerns.","quote":"Seems GPL was a problem for them.","text":"Both of these things suggest they had many other reasons to chose FreeBSD, and neither suggests FreeBSD was faster than Linux: - [LINK] - [VIDEO] Seems GPL was a problem for them. EDIT: another source suggesting GPL avoidance was the primary reason for choosing FreeBSD over Linux: [LINK] - and the presenter also says that the performance comparisons they have done between Linux and FreeBSD is not exactly fair because they did not do all the engineering work on Linux that they did on FreeBSD."},{"id":23820945,"author":"jaekash","month":"2020-07","story":"Tutorial sites treating FreeBSD like a Linux distro","stance":"reference","why":"Netflix talk suggests avoiding GPL drove FreeBSD choice.","quote":"From both of those sources I would say avoiding GPL was the primary consideration.","text":"> There are plenty of talks, papers, etc from both Juniper, Netflix, etc on why they made their choices, but your response is merely \"could also be they did not understand how to configure Linux\" without any evidence. Here are the only sources I could find that gave some reasons for Netflix's choice: - [LINK] - [VIDEO] Neither of them suggest FreeBSD was faster than Linux in their tests. From both of those sources I would say avoiding GPL was the primary consideration. If there is something to back your claim up, link to it. If you cannot link to it then how the heck do you expect me to find it. I'm not the person claiming it exists."},{"id":23244939,"author":"nix23","month":"2020-05","story":"OpenBSD 6.7","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why Netflix chose FreeBSD.","quote":"But Netflix chose it because of that","text":"Well the answer why Apple chose BSD is pretty simple...license and maturity. But Netflix chose it because of that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11420570,"author":"toomanythings2","month":"2016-04","story":"FreeBSD 10.3","stance":"reference","why":"Netflix's use of FreeBSD for its CDN.","quote":"Netflix delivers all video streaming content via FreeBSD","text":"Netflix delivers all video streaming content via FreeBSD which makes up 37% of all internet traffic. [LINK] They also contribute to FreeBSD core. It is not just an \"appliance\" as some people seem to want to point to. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] In addition, a guy I met, who worked at Yahoo, told me about 25% of Yahoo still runs FreeBSD as of two years ago."}]},"PH1BKPSGcxQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as a demonstration of Windows' long backwards compatibility.","q":{"x":"due to continuous backwards compatibility as demonstrated in videos such as","i":14176130,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":27216078,"author":"stordoff","month":"2021-05","story":"Internet Explorer 11 (IE11) to be retired on June 15, 2022","stance":"reference","why":"Follow-up upgrading Windows 1 to Windows 10.","quote":"Follow up - Windows 1 to Windows 10","text":"Windows 1 to Windows 7: [LINK] (set colour schemes disappear at XP, but programs continue to work) Follow up - Windows 1 to Windows 10: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16319364,"author":"GSimon","month":"2018-02","story":"What I learned from watching my iPad's slow death","stance":"reference","why":"Shows upgrades from earliest Windows with software staying compatible.","quote":"showing Windows upgrades from earliest versions to newest","text":"> Apple neither forces OS updates, nor forces app updates In practice they do. I recently deleted a timelapse app from my new phone (se) because the app wouldn't work with the new update. That may not be the best airtight example as the new OS update I did 'choose' to upgrade to which did somehow break the app, and the creator didn't release an updated version for the new OS, but in a real world scenario are you going to discontinue OS updates on a device you purchased months earlier for a few apps? Meanwhile there's decade old computers running windows xp that have no problems starting photoshop 7.0. There's also this video: [VIDEO] showing Windows upgrades from earliest versions to newest and software like excel being compatible from earliest versions onward."},{"id":14176130,"author":"trunchbull","month":"2017-04","story":"“Users will only be able to view patents via HTTP. HTTPS will no longer work”","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates Windows' continuous backwards compatibility.","quote":"due to continuous backwards compatibility as demonstrated in videos such as","text":"It's a similar story with Windows, due to continuous backwards compatibility as demonstrated in videos such as: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13882534,"author":"miles","month":"2017-03","story":"Man Puts Computer Through Hell by Installing Every Major Windows Upgrade","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"a direct link to the video","text":"The article is not coming up for me, but here's Google's cache: [LINK] a direct link to the video: [VIDEO] and a link to the original 2011 experiment: [LINK]"}]},"PzHM6PVTjXo":{"wy":"HN uses it as a real-world test of anti-collision systems in discussions of Tesla Autopilot and self-driving safety.","q":{"x":"Here's a road test, from 2013, of three anti-collision systems.[1]","i":18134094,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":19806733,"author":"tim333","month":"2019-05","story":"Tesla sued in wrongful death lawsuit that alleges Autopilot caused crash","stance":"reference","why":"Tests auto-braking at 55 and 60mph; Volvo works well.","quote":"Here's a TV show trying them at 55mph and 60mph","text":"Here's a TV show trying them at 55mph and 60mph, though with a slow moving object. The Volvo still works quite well at 60. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18134094,"author":"Animats","month":"2018-10","story":"Honda will use GM’s self-driving technology, invests in Cruise","stance":"reference","why":"Road test of three anti-collision systems showing wide performance variation.","quote":"Here's a road test, from 2013, of three anti-collision systems.[1]","text":"A lot of cars have Lane Keep Assist and Automatic Cruise Control, but most are not nearly good enough to rely on. Yes. Here's a road test, from 2013, of three anti-collision systems.[1] These things are all over the place on performance. As many Tesla crashes and deaths have demonstrated, Tesla's system won't stop for stationary obstacles. (Road barrier, street sweeper, stalled car, fire truck - the list goes on.) There needs to be a minimum performance standard for these things. A reasonable minimum goal is to mitigate collisions down to a 35MPH difference or less. With seat belts, air bags, and crush depth, almost anything below 35 MPH is survivable now. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17268157,"author":"jaclaz","month":"2018-06","story":"NTSB: Autopilot steered Tesla car toward traffic barrier before deadly crash","stance":"reference","why":"Older Fifth Gear anti-collision test with an inflatable dummy car.","quote":"I simply love the inflated \"dummy car\".","text":"JFYI, a (five year old) video from \"Fifth Gear\": [VIDEO] I simply love the inflated \"dummy car\"."},{"id":12469064,"author":"tim333","month":"2016-09","story":"Apple Is Said to Be Rethinking Strategy on Self-Driving Cars","stance":"reference","why":"TV show testing auto-braking systems as a model for testing.","quote":"Here's a TV show testing auto-braking systems for example.","text":"There are a bunch of ways you can test the systems apart from just looking at fatality numbers. Here's a TV show testing auto-braking systems for example. [VIDEO] You could easily extend that to a test track with all sorts of stuff self driving cars find hard - oil spills, diversions, people / animals / plastic bags jumping out and so on."},{"id":11775196,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-05","story":"Four hundred miles with Tesla’s autopilot forced me to trust the machine","stance":"reference","why":"Top Gear/Fifth Gear style comparison of anti-collision systems.","quote":"Top Gear compares the various systems as of 2015.[2]","text":"It's surprising that Tesla made that choice. BMW's anti-collision system is on even during manual driving.[1] BMW will brake even if the driver is pressing the accelerator. So will Volvo. Top Gear compares the various systems as of 2015.[2] Volvo did best; Mercedes didn't stop soon enough and hit the dummy car. VW's city car only does this up to 18MPH. Maybe Tesla has a false-alarm problem that BMW and Volvo have overcome. More to the point, this behavior needs to be standardized, or people shifting from one car to another will do the wrong thing. The National Transportation Safety Board is working on this.[3] In a few years, anti-collision may be as standard as anti-skid is now. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":11776579,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2016-05","story":"Four hundred miles with Tesla’s autopilot forced me to trust the machine","stance":"reference","why":"Tests auto-braking in Volvo, Mercedes and VW cars.","quote":"they say the auto-braking systems only engage by braking hard at the last second","text":"Another user linked a Fifth Gear video testing the auto-braking systems in Volvo/Mercedes/VW cars. In it, they say the auto-braking systems only engage by braking hard at the last second specifically so you don't get blasé about it as if it were a smooth controlled braking. It's not a mode you want to use because it really feels like you're going to crash. [VIDEO]"}]},"QgkB6T6LrhQ":{"wy":"HN uses it as evidence that Apple's Touch Bar idea was not new, and that Lenovo had already tried and dropped it.","m":[{"id":29131266,"author":"alexvoda","month":"2021-11","story":"The “Ultimate” ThinkPad (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the X1 Carbon's adaptive function row.","quote":"Thankfully it was only used on the Gen 2 of the X1 Carbon.","text":"Than, to your horror, here is how it looks (1). Thankfully it was only used on the Gen 2 of the X1 Carbon. The thing is, the concept of the touchbar (especially as technically implemented by Apple with a matte texture) is actually great. The great mistake was having it replace the Fn-row. If it was placed above the Fn-row, the touchbar would have been great. (1) [VIDEO]"},{"id":24337508,"author":"philliphaydon","month":"2020-09","story":"App Review process updates","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Lenovo had a similar adaptive key row earlier.","quote":"Wow so this isn't even 'new' on the Macbook","text":"[VIDEO] Wow so this isn't even 'new' on the Macbook... I had no idea the gen 2 carbon did this..."},{"id":12810056,"author":"basch","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: Would You Upgrade to the New MacBook Pro?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"they already went back on it because it wasnt apple quality.","text":"it already is a copy [LINK] [VIDEO] they already went back on it because it wasnt apple quality. apple always waits until its refined, but you cant give them credit for creating the idea."}]},"Qhm7-LEBznk":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a cautionary example of missing basic conceptual understanding in debates about math education and hiring.","m":[{"id":6571838,"author":"DanBC","month":"2013-10","story":"Only a few countries are teaching children how to think","stance":"reference","why":"Viral video showing students struggling with the meaning of speed.","quote":"See also the viral video \"meaning of MPH","text":"It's depressing but giving people \"s = d / t\" then asking questions like \"Bob travelled for 2 hours at 50 mph. How far has Bob travelled?\" is still tricky for some people to work out. We don't want people to learn formulae. We don't particularly need people to do mental arithmetic. We want people to understand problem solving and gathering information and relationships between different bits of the formula. See also the viral video \"meaning of MPH\" ( [VIDEO] ) There's some unpleasant \"HURR DURR GURLS ARE STOOPID\" in some videos (and don't for the love of god read the comments) ( [LINK] )"},{"id":27085695,"author":"username90","month":"2021-05","story":"How to Teach Math?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The problem here is that she don't know what speed is.","text":"Why do you assume that students know what speed is? Have you seen this video? [VIDEO] The problem here is that she don't know what speed is. She knows a lot of formulas, but not what speed actually is. That is a typical student, if you add speed in a formula you will make them confused and the problem will get harder."},{"id":3964263,"author":"mistercow","month":"2012-05","story":"Why I ask \"how many golf balls fit on a bus?\" in job interviews","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is what you're trying to filter out","text":"How do you mean \"subjective\"? There is an objective answer to the question \"how many golf balls can fit in a double decker bus\" and while I suppose you could call it \"trivial\", being able to make that kind of estimate efficiently really is an important skill for programmers (and scientists, and engineers) to have. That said, this is another FizzBuzz sort of test, and should not be made unnecessarily complicated. This is what you're trying to filter out: [VIDEO]"}]},"RXeOiIDNNek":{"wy":"HN links to specific segments of the keynote to back up claims about features like Passwords, Math Notes and the ChatGPT handoff.","m":[{"id":42431997,"author":"015a","month":"2024-12","story":"Most iPhone owners see little to no value in Apple Intelligence so far","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The new features in 18.2 GA release are a total shit-show. I've never seen Apple software this rushed out the door before, probably to beat the end-of-year shutdown. Image Playground is full of so many bugs and weird UX patterns. - The app is called \"Playgrounds\". Weird, especially considering Apple already has an app called \"Playgrounds\" (full name: Swift Playgrounds). Fine, weird, and especially weird if you put yourself in the shoes of someone who has no idea what AI is or what Apple is building. - When you launch it, it has to download models, a process which took over an hour on my internet connection, but it gives you no indication the models are downloading except one informational popup when you first launch the app; nor the download's progress. - Instead, I was placed into the full app experience, where I was allowed to try to generate images. The generations would fail with an error. - However, the error is totally unreadable because its presented as a toast at the top of the screen, rendering underneath the dynamic island. All I could see was the first few and last few characters. - I've heard confusion from two friends (techy-bubble of course) on \"why can I only generate variations on these four friends\" Its because the list of people you can generate images of seems to come from the list of facial recognition targets in Apple Photos. Yup. None of my friends who have tried this pay for Apple Photos, so their photos aren't being backed up, their list of generation "},{"id":42093067,"author":"mlajtos","month":"2024-11","story":"My Notes on Apple Math Notes","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote demo of draggable graph sliders","quote":"Jenny Chen demoed a graph that could be changed by dragging a slider of one of the variables","text":"It is true that in the WWDC24 keynote, Jenny Chen demoed a graph that could be changed by dragging a slider of one of the variables [0], but as you can see in demo in the article, graphs are dead – they do not respond to changing variables. Or maybe I am just doing something wrong... [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40637574,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-06","story":"Apple unveils 'Passwords' manager app at WWDC 2024","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote confirming Windows sync for Passwords app","quote":"","text":"> Pointless if it doesnt have cross platform. As mentioned in another news article on the topic: > It also syncs with PCs via the iCloud for Windows app. * [LINK] and in the keynote itself: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":40643512,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2024-06","story":"macOS 15.0 supports Nested Virtualization on M3 chips","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote segment on the calculator app","quote":"Just watch the darn video from this morning, its only 7 minutes long","text":"Just watch the darn video from this morning, its only 7 minutes long and you are using a device that is tailored for this very use case ha ha [VIDEO] FYI they even purposely made fun of themselves regarding the calculator app."},{"id":40733279,"author":"glhaynes","month":"2024-06","story":"Even Apple cannot explain why we need AI in our lives","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote demo showing ChatGPT handoff","quote":"Starting at 01:36:09","text":"It certainly appears so; every demo they showed makes it very apparent when you’re taking the step of starting to interact with ChatGPT. Starting at 01:36:09: [VIDEO]"}]},"SoxsMMV538U":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of perfect memory as an illustration that it can be a burden rather than a superpower.","q":{"x":"it is more a curse than a superpower","i":4408727,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":43674569,"author":"nthingtohide","month":"2025-04","story":"Nominal Aphasia: Problems in Name Retrieval","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on living with perfect memory","quote":"","text":"> On the plus side I live in today and dont dwell on the past. It is like a superpower for happiness. This is true on many levels. 4:10 @ The Woman Who Could Not Forget - Jill Price [VIDEO] Perfect Memory = Perfect Inability to Forgive | House M.D. [LINK]"},{"id":28186230,"author":"shannifin","month":"2021-08","story":"What’s it like living with a phenomenal memory and can it be learned?","stance":"reference","why":"Woman with autobiographical memory describing obsessive tendencies","quote":"this woman talks about being somewhat obsessive-compulsive about her past","text":"> Glowacki is on the autism spectrum and has superlative memory and calendar recall skills. I've also known several autistic people who have great memories; I don't know if the right word would be \"interest\", \"obsession\", or \"compulsion\", but they have an inclination to go over certain facts in their heads over and over, so of course developing a good memory for those facts is natural. I've seen similar articles and TV segments about people who particularly remember autobiographical facts, what they were doing everyday for decades. Again, it's not \"automatic\" memory, as it tends to be sensationalized, but rather the result of an interest or obsession to continually think about it. For instance, this woman talks about being somewhat obsessive-compulsive about her past: [VIDEO] > Unfortunately, it didn’t translate into academic exam success in his weaker subjects like maths and science: “I only remember things I like.” When you're interested in something, you will naturally think about it more often. So the memory itself really isn't the phenomenon so much as the relative rarity of someone being interested in such details in the first place. --- The real magic would be to be able to remember something without effort, interest, or attention. I'd love to be able to read a book while daydreaming and then reread it in my head later for processing. Or remember some detail from another day that I didn't pay attention to at all at first, rewinding it in my head like a video. And thoug"},{"id":4408727,"author":"olalonde","month":"2012-08","story":"Why Can Some People Recall Every Day Of Their Lives? Brain Scans Offer Clues","stance":"reference","why":"Jill Price describes perfect memory as a curse","quote":"it is more a curse than a superpower","text":"According to A.J. (aka Jill Price) herself, it is more a curse than a superpower: [VIDEO] \"We can say time heals all wounds,\" said Price's mother. \"Doesn't for her.\""}]},"UqBmdZ-BNig":{"wy":"Linked in lift-theory arguments as a clear demonstration that air over the top of a wing does not need to catch up with air underneath.","q":{"x":"This[1] video has a very good demonstration of this.","i":27649007,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":44828618,"author":"timr","month":"2025-08","story":"GPT-5","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> right, the other is that if you remove every incorrect statement from the AI \"explanation\", the answer it would have given is \"airplane wings generate lift because they are shaped to generate lift\". ...only if you omit the parts where it talks about pressure differentials, caused by airspeed differences, create lift? Both of these points are true. You have to be motivated to ignore them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29024133,"author":"dmkirwan","month":"2021-10","story":"Incorrect Lift Theory","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates actual airflow over an aerofoil","quote":"This video from Cambridge University illustrates what happens to the airflow over an aerofoil","text":"Funnily enough, there's another web page on the NASA domain that outlines the \"longer path\" or \"equal transit\" theories as the correct cause of lift on an aircraft wing: [LINK] When I was studying for my PPL, they taught us using the equal transit theory, though the instructor couldn't help himself from telling us that it was a simplification. This video from Cambridge University illustrates what happens to the airflow over an aerofoil: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27649007,"author":"slavak","month":"2021-06","story":"Why we don’t understand heavier-than-air flight","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates air over a wing does not follow equal transit time","quote":"This[1] video has a very good demonstration of this.","text":"You're right- there isn't, and it doesn't. In fact a parcel of air moving over the top of the wing will beat its counterpart moving below it to the trailing edge. This[1] video has a very good demonstration of this. (Relevant part begins around 0:25.) So saying that air on top of the wing moves faster, creating a pressure differential and thus lift, is absolutely correct. The problem begins when some people try to come up with an intuitive explanation for _why_ the air would need to speed up. \"Because this is the lowest energy state that conserves energy, momentum, and mass\" isn't a very satisfying answer; neither is \"because this system of PDEs say so\"; so they came up with the \"equal transit time\" explanation, which is simple, intuitive, and completely wrong. Hopefully aeronautical engineers at P&W didn't actually believe that last bit? [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"VUoXtddNPAM":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a joke or passing reference when conversation touches on conformity or identical houses.","m":[{"id":37853896,"author":"Animats","month":"2023-10","story":"Why Are There So Many Colourful Houses in Bristol?","stance":"reference","why":"Cites the song Little Boxes, about Daly City tract houses.","quote":"It refers to Daly City, incidentally.","text":"Little boxes on the hillside Little boxes made of ticky tacky Little boxes on the hillside Little boxes all the same There's a pink one and a green one And a blue one and a yellow one And they're all made out of ticky tacky And they all look just the same \"Little Boxes\", 1961 [1] It refers to Daly City, incidentally. Multicolored tract houses were a 1950s California thing. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40262983,"author":"labrador","month":"2024-05","story":"PHP Doesn't Suck Anymore","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I volunteered to help .netart artists in the early 2000's to get their code working. They were able to do some very creative things with PHP that I know they didn't have the coding skills to do otherwise. Like grabbing random images based on keyword searches from poems and creating montages with them. The industrial programmers would shudder in horror at the PHP code they wrote but it did the job. PHP is for the people. It'd be nice if the bureaucratic programmers would keep on making their proper factory factory singletons they learned at University without turning up their noses at PHP in their boxed world Malvina Reynolds - Little Boxes [VIDEO]"},{"id":18549275,"author":"DoubleCribble","month":"2018-11","story":"The Insect Apocalypse Is Here","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You have no idea. Many Master Planned Communities have very restrictive CC&R's (Covenants, Codes & Restrictions) that you must contractually agree to abide to in order to buy a property there. I've seen all kinds of fun rules like only being allowed to paint your house one of the approved seven shades of beige, only using one type of roofing material, only planting trees and shrubs in any publicly facing frontage/backage/sideage from an approved list, maintaining no publicly visible weeds, maintaining a green front lawn year round and even put up a minimum amount of holiday decoration (lights) during December. Little Boxes, indeed[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"WGRnhBmHYN0":{"wy":"Mentioned only in passing in political threads, mostly critically.","m":[{"id":31341236,"author":"incomingpain","month":"2022-05","story":"The second American civil war is already happening","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">Things don't necessarily go hot all at once. To me there's no such thing as a cold civil war. If it's not hot, it's not a civil war. The USA has not crossed the line into civil war, it's not too late to exit your echo chambers and start talking to each other again. I wholly agree with that wiki page on debate of a second civil war starting about 2008. Honestly havent seen that page in some time and it's much more verbose. I think from a quick skim it's quite good. \"First black president\" in the USA was going to be rife with identity politics. No amount of effort would fix this, but you have to deal with the consequences of bringing identity politics back. Not only was there no attempts at all to deal with those consequences... they went even further. Anyone who opposed Obama's policies became a white supremacist. Ben Shapiro for example is a notorious nazi until he was outed as a jew. The next election polling basically showed Clinton was going to crush Trump. The far east blue states showed they were going blue surprising nobody and they declared the election for clinton. Later to find out trump won and the polls were quite wrong. Clinton went around the country and abroad saying trump stole the election. In reality, the rhetoric of calling republicans a bunch of fascist white supremacists made people lie to pollsters. It wasn't safe to admit the truth you were going to support the republicans. They might come for you. Then comes the next election and biden literally said h"},{"id":29484085,"author":"sleepysysadmin","month":"2021-12","story":"Young Dems more likely to despise the other party","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"that's not a gaffe","text":">This is insane. One side is doing everything they can to rig elections The democrats? [VIDEO] that's not a gaffe. If you assert it's a gaffe, you must now explain a ton more. >And then the other side is judged for being taken aback by that? Are we supposed to pretend like nothing is happening? Someone could blame the media but that's beside the point. The USA is more divided today that I have ever seen it. According to articles ive read, the divide is larger today than it has been since the civil war. Thusly probable that another civil war is coming but oh boy is this one ever going to be one sided."},{"id":26497280,"author":"TheBobinator","month":"2021-03","story":"Across the Internet, a Game of Whac-a-Mole Is Underway to Root Out Extremism","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Stuff like this doesn't help the optics.","text":"If they had the kind of sway over the public you say they had, then Trump wouldn't have recieved a record 72 million votes in 2020. They spent 4 years lambasting him over every single thing they could and the impact was an election about 90 million people in the US Think was rigged. Stuff like this doesn't help the optics. [VIDEO] Nor does Zuckerberg spending $500 million on ballot harvesting initiatives and skirting the law where he could. The one thing this last election prooved is that when rich and powerful people feel threatened with losing power or fortune, they often decide to use whatever power they have and spend whatever fortune they've got to maintain what they have. We'll find out, as the forensic investigations drag on, exactly how compromised this election was. Suffice to say, an awful lot of people on the left are acting like they have nothing to lose which is a strong signal indictments and convictions are on the road ahead. Look at the MSM's SEC 8k and 10k filings. [LINK] Free news is done to sell advertising, which also finds its way into their subscription businesses and even if you pay for WSJ, the articles are often paid for or done as political favors."}]},"Zz95_VvTxZM":{"wy":"HN people link it as a striking illustration of atmospheric pressure crushing vessels, in threads on vacuum airships, hyperloop and the Titan sub.","q":{"x":"it effectively demonstrates how violent and quick an implosion would be","i":36437785,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36398177,"author":"pfdietz","month":"2023-06","story":"Titanic tourist sub goes missing sparking search","stance":"reference","why":"Example of sudden buckling implosion of a tank car.","quote":"Example of implosion of a railroad tank car","text":"> a buckling mode, which is inherently unstable. As soon as it goes, it goes all at once. Example of implosion of a railroad tank car: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36411117,"author":"Ruthalas","month":"2023-06","story":"Missing Titanic Sub Faced Lawsuit over Depths It Could Safely Travel To","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a vessel failing at just 1 bar.","quote":"Here's a pressure vessel failing at only 1 bar","text":"This is likely less the case when the pressure differential is ~480 bar. Failure is going to occur on the order of fractions of a second at those huge pressures. (Fortunately?) Here's a pressure vessel failing at only 1 bar: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36437785,"author":"andrewmunsell","month":"2023-06","story":"Oceangate Statement, “We now believe that [all crew] have sadly been lost.”","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates how violent and quick an implosion is.","quote":"it effectively demonstrates how violent and quick an implosion would be","text":"There's obvious differences between the Oceangate sub and this (1 atm difference, this is a thinner steel(?) railroad tanker not a sub), but I think it effectively demonstrates how violent and quick an implosion would be: [VIDEO] Video contents: An experiment where the vacuum safety valves were removed from an oil tanker railroad car, and a vacuum was placed inside the car, resulting in it crumpling in an implosion. There is no gore or other specific disturbing content"},{"id":36438170,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2023-06","story":"Oceangate Statement, “We now believe that [all crew] have sadly been lost.”","stance":"reference","why":"One-atmosphere implosion for scale comparison.","quote":"Someone else on the thread posted a video of a one atmosphere implosion","text":"The point is the extreme, rapid force involved. Imploding or exploding, nine atmospheres is gonna suck. 400 atmospheres is... like standing under a nuclear bomb. The good news is it doesn't hurt, at all. Someone else on the thread posted a video of a one atmosphere implosion ( [VIDEO] ). 400x the pressure here."},{"id":34774878,"author":"pontifier","month":"2023-02","story":"Vacuum Balloons","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a violent train car implosion.","quote":"Train car imploding","text":"I can't believe nobody has shown how violent vacuum implosions can be. Here are some of my favorite vacuum implosions: Train car imploding: [VIDEO] Vacuum chamber imploding: [LINK]"},{"id":30501560,"author":"parasense","month":"2022-02","story":"Hyperloop lays off half of its employees as it pivots away from passenger travel","stance":"reference","why":"Shows reality of atmospheric pressure on vacuum vessels.","quote":"Here is your reality","text":"Here is your reality: [VIDEO] You're not entirely wrong however. There is effectively one atmosphere differential at sea level, but framing the difference that way is at worst intellectual dishonest, or at best a rather naive understanding. In actual reality there is a very long tube, and all the surface area is under pressure. In metric is 10,000 kg per square meter, multiplied by all the square meters down the length of tube. Of course this simplified understanding ignores the weight of the transport capsule inside, which causes a momentary higher differential. And, the hyperloop won't work at sea level, as others have pointed out, it would have to be underground to avoid thermal expansion issues... meaning higher pressure."},{"id":28371197,"author":"immmmmm","month":"2021-08","story":"The Tesla Dojo Chip Is Impressive, but There Are Some Major Technical Issues","stance":"reference","why":"Shows problems of holding vacuum in a large vessel.","quote":"i found this interesting video of some of problems one might get trying to put vacuum in a pipe","text":"not fully related but i was doing some reading on various \"new sustainable ways of transportation\" and, since they're building the biggest hyperloop test track near my place, i found this interesting video of some of problems one might get trying to put vacuum in a pipe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26485831,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2021-03","story":"Early SpaceX engineer crawled inside an imploding rocket on a jet","stance":"reference","why":"Shows what happens when pressure differential goes the wrong way.","quote":"Here's an idea of what can happen","text":"Airplanes aren't pressurized to sea level... so as they went up, the air inside the tank vented outward into the airplane... and a pressure vessel can easily withstand that... but the problem is that on the way back down to land, the pressure inside the airplane went up far quicker than it could get back inside the tank, so the tank had a much lower pressure inside than that outside the tank. You've obviously never seen what happens to a pressure vessel when the pressure gradient goes the wrong way... Here's an idea of what can happen: [VIDEO] Imagine being on a plane when a very large object inside of it does that. It could have caused the airplane to implode as well, or have catastrophic damage."},{"id":8170849,"author":"dj_gitmo","month":"2014-08","story":"Vacuum airship","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a rail tank car imploding under vacuum.","quote":"","text":"This rail-bound tank was probably not designed with weight savings as a prime considerations. [VIDEO] Still, this is a fascinating idea and I would love to see someone make a serious attempt at it."},{"id":8171640,"author":"beloch","month":"2014-08","story":"Vacuum airship","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"At atmospheric pressure, a given volume of hydrogen weighs approximately 7% (there is some variation with temperature) of an equivalent volume of air. All that is needed to contain hydrogen at atmospheric pressure is a membrane it can't pass through. The inwards force exerted on the membrane by the atmosphere is perfectly balanced by the outwards force exerted by the hydrogen gas. The only structural material required is for the purpose of harnessing the lifting power of the enclosed gas, providing an aerodynamic shape, etc.. Conversely, a vessel containing a vacuum must be built to withstand the 101,325 N/m^2 net inward force exerted upon it by the atmosphere that is not balanced by the outward pressure of the contents of the vessel. Consider, for a moment, what you would need to build a 1 m^2 coffee table out of if 20 elephants were to be able to stand on top of it at once. For the sake of argument, let's say that we had a magical material that would let us build a replica of the Hindenburg that is of equal weight to the original, only with vacuum vessels replacing the hydrogen bags. The Hindenberg contained 200,000 m^3 of hydrogen lift gas, weighing 1,798 kg and displacing enough atmosphere to provide a total lift of 25,850 kg, 10,000 kg of which were considered it's cargo capacity beyond the passengers it carried. This new version of the Hindenberg, despite being built with materials and techniques that are probably well beyond our current level of technology, would carry"}]},"_BgJEXQkjNQ":{"wy":"HN people link it as the reference footage of the earlier SpaceX pad explosion when discussing later launch failures.","q":{"x":"is a video of SpaceX's failure.","i":48319041,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48319041,"author":"mandevil","month":"2026-05","story":"The most spectacular rocket explosion since N1 just happened in Florida","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the earlier SpaceX static-fire failure.","quote":"is a video of SpaceX's failure.","text":"In September 2016 almost exactly the same thing happened to a Falcon 9 at the Cape, also on a static fire. New Glenn is bigger, so bigger bang, but pretty much exactly the same thing. Off the top of my head, I recall in SpaceX's case it was a helium tank failure- a helium tank weld failed and the helium tank itself shot through the cryogenic oxygen, hit the far wall, and gave off a spark. But that sort of failure is only apparent when everything is pressurized correctly, which means tanks have to be full. The goal of the test is that you detect that sort of failure before it goes boom and then can fix it. [VIDEO] is a video of SpaceX's failure."},{"id":35575951,"author":"mhandley","month":"2023-04","story":"The FAA has granted SpaceX permission to launch its Starship rocket","stance":"reference","why":"Shows earlier destruction of a Falcon 9 pad.","quote":"they also have previous painful experience of destroying a Falcon 9 pad.","text":"And they also have previous painful experience of destroying a Falcon 9 pad. [VIDEO] Really can't afford to repeat that if they are to make the timeline for using Starship as NASA's next lunar lander."},{"id":25547991,"author":"someperson","month":"2020-12","story":"Starship / Super Heavy","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a pad-destroying explosion.","quote":"","text":"An interesting thing here is SpaceX are building TWO launchpads. Which will definitely help prevent delays if an when there is an AMOS-6 [1] or Orb-3 [2] type explosion that destroys the launchpad. It may also help for rapidly testing orbital refueling in 2022, though the plan is to do orbital refueling with 1 launch pad, 1 Super Heavy and 2 Starships (1 standard, 1 refueler) with 1 hour turnaround times between launches (and 5-6 launches) before Starship is ready to depart for the journey to Mars. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":17008524,"author":"wolf550e","month":"2018-05","story":"NASA advisers say SpaceX rocket technology could put lives at risk","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of fairing falling after explosion.","quote":"","text":"> they do not know if a launch escape system would save astronauts in that situation. 1. In the video of the Amos 6 explosion[2], you see the payload fairing falling to the ground, with the satellite inside. You see it happened slowly, the payload did not explore, it burned down after falling. The abort system would have saved the crew in that situation. We were also told[3] the Dragon 1 would have been just fine if it was programmed to open its chutes after the second stage disintegrated under it during ascent on CRS-7. NASA participated in analyzing both failures, they know this. 2. SpaceX have done a pad abort test in 2015[1] that demonstrated the capsule reacting to \"something bad happened to the rocket\" on the launch pad and saving a test dummy's life. 1 - [LINK] 2 - [VIDEO] 3 - [LINK]"},{"id":12407737,"author":"jychang","month":"2016-09","story":"A SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket has exploded at Cape Canaveral","stance":"reference","why":"Used to estimate camera focal length from the footage.","quote":"","text":"Ok, the Falcon 9 1.2 [1] is 70m tall [2], and the rocket fills up about 80% of the vertical frame [3]. Using the \"Image = FL * Obj / distance\" equation, we get: .8 * 24mm = x * 70m/4080m Where x is the focal length of the lens assuming a full frame camera. This comes out to a x = 1119mm equivalent focal length. If you have a crop sensor, this isn't too hard to obtain. I'd be amused if the video was taken with a Canon EF 1200mm lens or something, but the probability of that is 0. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}]},"_XneTBhRPYk":{"wy":"Cited in threads on Apple repair and battery policies as evidence of questionable repair practices, rather than recommended as a must-watch.","q":{"x":"There was also a CBC documentary two years ago that investigated similar complaints about Apple repair practices","i":24476305,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24476305,"author":"jasoneckert","month":"2020-09","story":"Apple Tried Charging the Price of a New MacBook Pro for a Screen Replacement","stance":"reference","why":"CBC documentary investigating Apple repair complaints.","quote":"There was also a CBC documentary two years ago that investigated similar complaints about Apple repair practices","text":"There was also a CBC documentary two years ago that investigated similar complaints about Apple repair practices: [VIDEO] Essentially, this blog post reinforces the findings from the CBC and indicates that little has changed over the past few years regarding Apple's repair practices."},{"id":20652429,"author":"chmod775","month":"2019-08","story":"Apple Is Locking iPhone Batteries to Discourage Repair","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple got caught on hidden camera.","text":"> make up that anecdote Apple got caught on hidden camera. Multiple times. You can find plenty of these kinds of videos, but I think Rossman was referring to this one, featuring the man himself: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19600339,"author":"lloydde","month":"2019-04","story":"How to Improve MacBook Pro Performance and Thermals","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple was caught misdiagnosing and pushing purchasing new products.","text":"Very unlikely. If you can convince them of a repair, they’ll want to use their own supplies. This reminds of Canadian CBC National investigation into how instead of identifying problems, Apple was caught misdiagnosing and pushing purchasing new products. [VIDEO] I don’t know if you’d have to pay, I suspect you would, but an independent repair shop is likely going to care more about you and your nuanced issue."}]},"eXYUNrgqWUU":{"wy":"Cited as an impressive example of real-time digital humans, especially the facial and mouth animation.","q":{"x":"The mouth movement[0] sold it for me.","i":30770913,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31794790,"author":"ricardobeat","month":"2022-06","story":"Realistic one-shot mesh-based head avatars","stance":"reference","why":"Highly detailed face rendering with huge data requirements.","quote":"the saliva 'sticking' to the corners of the mouth","text":"I had the same reaction (note: I actually got things mixed up, the one in question is the Unity \"Enemies\" demo [1], not Unreal), but if you consider the amount of detail - the saliva 'sticking' to the corners of the mouth, the 40+ muscles of the face, lips, eyelids, the iris, etc - it makes sense that it would be a ton of data. IIRC they are working on some kind of ML-powered compression so that this becomes actually possible to ship (the demo is not available to download, presumably it's too big and would barely run on a 3090TI). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30924982,"author":"f0e4c2f7","month":"2022-04","story":"Unreal Engine 5 is now available","stance":"reference","why":"Unity demo with impressive facial animation.","quote":"Recently Unity released a video with quite impressive facial animations.","text":"Recently Unity released a video with quite impressive facial animations. Unlike unreal, this version is not yet ready to actually try yourself yet unfortunately. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30770913,"author":"daenz","month":"2022-03","story":"Hyper-realistic digital humans in Unity","stance":"reference","why":"Realistic mouth animation in real-time digital human demo.","quote":"The mouth movement[0] sold it for me.","text":"The mouth movement[0] sold it for me. Mouths are incredibly hard to do in CG, because you have to have realistic interactions between lips, teeth, and tongue. Not sure how they scanned this so well (maybe it was hand animated?) but it looks excellent. 0. [VIDEO]"}]},"iPkJH6BT7dM":{"wy":"Used as a classic analogy for fractional reserve banking and bank runs.","m":[{"id":35103305,"author":"costco","month":"2023-03","story":"$Roku filed a Form 8-K revealing that $487M stuck at SVB (25% of cash)","stance":"reference","why":"Classic bank run scene as analogy.","quote":"","text":"Where are the people offering to buy SVB claims for 60-70 cents on the dollar? This happened with FTX and traditional bank runs like in [It's a Wonderful Life]( [VIDEO] ). Maybe people are waiting to see how large the FDIC advances will be."},{"id":25985182,"author":"haberman","month":"2021-02","story":"Naked shorting: The curious incident of the shares that didn't exist (2005)","stance":"reference","why":"Scene explaining why deposits can't all be withdrawn at once.","quote":"","text":"Call me crazy but I thought the idea is that banks would use deposits to fund loans and kick some of the profit back to the account holder in the form of reasonable interest rates. That seems capital efficient to me, but it's apparently a quaint relic of the past now that eternal 0% interest rates are the norm. I'm reminded of the scene from It's a Wonderful Life where George Bailey explains to all his account holders why they can't all withdraw their money at once, because it's being put to productive use by their fellow townspeople: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20276421,"author":"holy_city","month":"2019-06","story":"Facebook, Libra, and the Long Game","stance":"reference","why":"Classic scene used to teach how banking works.","quote":"Someone once told me that U of Chicago","text":"Someone once told me that U of Chicago's business school used this classic to describe banking: [VIDEO]"}]},"iqL1BLzn3qc":{"wy":"Cited nostalgically as one of the first videos many people ever played from a CD on a PC, part of Windows 95 lore.","q":{"x":"Edie Brickell and the New Bohemians, \"Good Times\".","i":46975085,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46975085,"author":"lproven","month":"2026-02","story":"How did Windows 95 get permission to put the Weezer video Buddy Holly on the CD?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Edie Brickell and the New Bohemians, \"Good Times\".","text":"> Good times. That, of course, being the other track on some editions of Win95's CD. Edie Brickell and the New Bohemians, \"Good Times\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":47027582,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2026-02","story":"LT6502: A 6502-based homebrew laptop","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I picture this music video that was included on the Windows 95 cd bonus folder","text":"This is probably me experiencing a simulacra but with that slow loading getting up to go get a drink workflow, each page load was more special. It was magical discovering new websites just like trying out new software by picking something up from those \"pegboards\" at computer stores. It also was a simpler time, the technology was in peoples lives but as a small side quest to their main lives. It took the form of a bulky desktop in the den or something like that. When you walked away from that beige box, it didn't follow or know about the rest of your life. A life where a Big Mac meal was only $2.99, a toyota corolla was $9-15k, houses were ~100k, and when average dev salaries were ~50k. That was a different life. I don't know why but I picture this music video that was included on the Windows 95 cd bonus folder when I think of this simulacra: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47029937,"author":"vardump","month":"2026-02","story":"LT6502: A 6502-based homebrew laptop","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"When I saw that video in 1995, I understood something we now know as Youtube would be inevitable","text":"> music video that was included on the Windows 95 cd bonus folder when I think of this simulacra: [VIDEO] When I saw that video in 1995, I understood something we now know as Youtube would be inevitable as the connection speeds improve. Although I thought it'd be like MTV, a way to watch the newest music videos."},{"id":43582645,"author":"nelblu","month":"2025-04","story":"Microsoft employees recall their early years","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It felt absolutely futuristic watching a video from a CD at that time.","text":"One of my favourite memories of Windows 95 was - [VIDEO] . It felt absolutely futuristic watching a video from a CD at that time."},{"id":37313409,"author":"marcodiego","month":"2023-08","story":"Popularity of DOS/4GW made Win95 game compat easier, but with higher stakes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"win95 came with an 'extras' cd-rom with a few videos.","text":"For those who don't know: win95 came with an 'extras' cd-rom with a few videos. Among them there a Weezer clip [1] and \"Good Times\"[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23676082,"author":"netsharc","month":"2020-06","story":"Windows98 Running in the Browser","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"My Pentium 100 could play this 320x240 video fullscreen with no choppiness.","text":"My Pentium 100 could play this 320x240 video [VIDEO] fullscreen with no choppiness. I had forgotten about this video until I saw it last year... But Windows had skeumorphisms, even nowadays the screen with everything minimised is called \"the desk[ ]top\". But I guess briefcases and recycle bins made things relatable..."},{"id":46967402,"author":"netsharc","month":"2026-02","story":"How did Windows 95 get permission to put the Weezer video Buddy Holly on the CD?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This video was also on the CD","text":"Decades later Apple put U2 on everyone's iPhone and people got mad... (/s, yeah the album was a gift on people's account, ready to download to the phone but not taking space otherwise, but I would've found it obnoxious too). This video was also on the CD: [VIDEO] .. holy smokes, let's rewind time 30 years, where the presidential sex scandal was singular, consensual, and was actually a scandal!"},{"id":14034539,"author":"scholia","month":"2017-04","story":"The Mac Pro Lives","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"What about the pernicious inclusion of Edie Brickell's Good Times in Windows 95? ;-)","text":"You never asked for Solitaire or Freecell either. And you never asked for Notepad or Calculator or whatever. Did you have mini-hysterics about Solitaire being bundled, and did you waste hours worrying about it or removing it? What about the pernicious inclusion of Edie Brickell's Good Times in Windows 95? ;-) [VIDEO] Candy Crush Saga was actually one of the benefits of Windows 10 that Microsoft promoted, for two reasons: (1) Candy Crush was enormously popular, and (2) it provides practice in touch operations, just as Solitaire got people used to using a mouse."},{"id":8188286,"author":"lubos","month":"2014-08","story":"Why was Pinball removed from Windows Vista? (2012)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The second song included on Windows 95 CD was \"Good times\" by Edie Brickell -","text":"The second song included on Windows 95 CD was \"Good times\" by Edie Brickell - [VIDEO] Indeed good times back in 1995, can't believe it's been already 20 years."}]},"j9tlJAqMV7U":{"wy":"Cited as a deep dive on coroutines for cache misses and on running huge numbers of coroutines without heap allocation.","q":{"x":"also see this video that goes in depth how to have billions of coroutines with C++","i":20743490,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":26230043,"author":"BenFrantzDale","month":"2021-02","story":"My tutorial and take on C++20 coroutines","stance":"reference","why":"Example of coroutines issuing prefetches around memory latency.","quote":"The example given","text":"It's a big deal because, while it has some downsides, being stalkless means they can have next to no overhead, meaning it can be performant to use coroutines to write asynchronous code for even very fast operations. The example given [VIDEO] is that you can launch multiple coroutines to issue prefetch instructions and process the fetched data, so you can have clean code that issues multiple prefetches and process the results. Whereas in Python (don't get me wrong, I love Python) you might use a generator to \"asynchronize\" slow operations like requesting and processing data from remote servers, C++ coroutines can be fast enough to asynchronously process \"slow\" operations like requesting data from main memory."},{"id":20743490,"author":"je42","month":"2019-08","story":"How Rust optimizes async/await","stance":"reference","why":"Deep dive on running billions of C++ coroutines without heap allocation.","quote":"also see this video that goes in depth how to have billions of coroutines with C++","text":"> In C++, technically every initialization of a sub-coroutine starts defaults to being a new heap allocation. I don't want to spread FUD: my understanding is that the vast majority of these are optimized out by the compiler. But one downside of this approach is that you could change your code and accidentally disable one of these optimizations. I don't think this is correct. C++ 20 allows a lot of choices to implement it without forcing a heap allocation. see [LINK] also see this video that goes in depth how to have billions of coroutines with C++: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18425237,"author":"akubera","month":"2018-11","story":"Exploiting Coroutines to Attack the “Killer Nanoseconds” [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Coroutines with prefetch to hide cache misses.","quote":"jump to ~12:33 to skip background","text":"Gor Nishanov, one of the authors, talks about this in his CppCon2018 talk [VIDEO] (jump to ~12:33 to skip background). It amounts to using CPU prefetch instructions with C++ coroutines to simulate hyperthreading in software by scheduling instructions around cache misses (but is potentially better than hyperthreads because it's not limited to 2/core)"}]},"koqNm_TgOZk":{"wy":"Cited in recycling and plastic-waste threads as an entertaining take on corporate blame-shifting.","m":[{"id":21429455,"author":"vincnetas","month":"2019-11","story":"Coca-Cola, Nestlé, and PepsiCo are the Top Plastic Polluters","stance":"reference","why":"More entertaining media on litter blame shifting","quote":"also more entertaining media","text":"\"The Keep America Beautiful narrow focus on litter, and indeed construction of the modern concept of litter, is seen as an attempt to divert responsibility from industries that manufacture and sell disposable products to the consumer that improperly disposes of the related non-returnable wrappers, filters, and beverage containers.[2]\" [LINK] also more entertaining media [VIDEO]"},{"id":19357023,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2019-03","story":"India Imposes Complete Ban on Solid Plastic Waste Imports","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among depressing recycling videos","quote":"Pick one, they're all just as depressing as the next.","text":"I thought these were just depressing... but yeah, likely more truthful. Pick one, they're all just as depressing as the next. * Adam Ruins Everything - The Corporate Conspiracy to Blame You for Their Trash - YouTube || [VIDEO] * Penn & Teller - Recycling - YouTube || [LINK] * Debunking recycling paper (P&T segment) - YouTube || [LINK] * The shocking scale of our waste - and the myth of recycling | Irene Rompa | TEDxMidAtlantic - YouTube || [LINK]"},{"id":17412061,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2018-06","story":"Plastic recycling is a problem consumers can't solve","stance":"reference","why":"Crying Indian campaign funded by packaging industry","quote":"It was part of a propaganda campaign (a.k.a public relations) by packaging company","text":"You know that famous Indian crying from litter on the highway campaign? It was funded by the packaging industry. It was part of a propaganda campaign (a.k.a public relations) by packaging company to place the blame on litter and non-reusable packaging on the consumer instead of the industry. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15889369,"author":"foxhop","month":"2017-12","story":"Recycling Chaos in U.S. As China Bans 'Foreign Waste'","stance":"reference","why":"Blames corporations for waste, not consumers","quote":"Adam Ruins Everything - The Corporate Conspiracy to Blame You for Their Trash","text":"Agreed but like maybe companies shouldn't produce waste packaging, etc. This sounds an awful lot like \"blame the litter bugs\". Adam Ruins Everything - The Corporate Conspiracy to Blame You for Their Trash: [VIDEO]"}]},"ll3CMgiUPuU":{"wy":"Mentioned as an example of spiritual, non-romantic love in music and as a demanding jazz work that is hard for the uninitiated.","q":{"x":"What about Love Supreme expressed by John Coltrane","i":29158273,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29158273,"author":"galaxyLogic","month":"2021-11","story":"Love seems like a high priority","stance":"reference","why":"Coltrane's A Love Supreme offered as example of non-romantic love.","quote":"What about Love Supreme expressed by John Coltrane","text":"I don't get it. You guys seem to have sex in your mind all the time. What about brotherly love? What about motherly love? What about fatherly love? What about love of one's country? What about love of humanity? What about Love Supreme expressed by John Coltrane [VIDEO]"},{"id":19330421,"author":"InitialLastName","month":"2019-03","story":"The $2M Urinal: Why Hard Work Doesn’t Cut It (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Free jazz example of an art form hard for the uninitiated.","quote":"","text":"Modernism in the arts has a bit of a path-dependency problem that tends to intentionally obscure the \"artistic value\" of the bleeding edge from the uninitiated, but which can make the art more meaningful to those who are familiar with the context. I'll use jazz as an example, since I'm most familiar with that history. You can start with something as approachable, if disorganized as \"Jelly Roll Blues\"[0], and polish the genre into \"Take the A Train\" [1] as it became more generally popular. After that, though, the bleeding edge of the genre self-protects itself away from the common ear by growing increasingly obscure, with bop [2], modal jazz [3], free jazz [4??] becoming progressively less approachable to the uninitiated ear, to the point that the the unfamiliar ear can hear the latter and find itself unable to recognize even as \"music\", while someone familiar with the genre can see the path and appreciate the work in context. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18549293,"author":"ghotli","month":"2018-11","story":"Dropping Acid","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Neither of which sound like A Love Supreme [5] [6] by John Coltrane","text":"For awhile when I was younger I played the game of listen to an album then look up who influenced it, rinse and repeat. Found real gems this way, but along these lines I never found the track in this article. It's really good and it reminds me of E2-E4 [1] [2] by Manuel Göttsching. Which I found because the guy from LCD Soundsystem is quoted in the wikipedia article that when he did 45:33 that he wanted to make something like \"E2-E4\". [3] [4] Neither of which sound like A Love Supreme [5] [6] by John Coltrane but I always associated them in my mind as a kind of their own kinda thing in music's past. Posting for no other reason than that maybe you'll like these too. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [VIDEO] [6] [LINK]"}]},"oRiQz7mOY6A":{"wy":"Cited as video evidence of the detention in threads about surveillance, civil liberties and government overreach.","m":[{"id":47279136,"author":"doom2","month":"2026-03","story":"Pentagon Formally Labels Anthropic Supply-Chain Risk","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of arrest that resembled a kidnapping.","quote":"","text":"The arrest of a Turkish graduate student in Boston looked a lot like a kidnapping.[1] More recently, ICE responded to a judge's order to release a detained refugee by threatening to detain her family and send them to Texas if they came to pick her up and then forced the minor child to stay in a hotel room with three agents. [2, p.8] These may not be cases where people are secretly being taken by the state, but it's not hard to see why people might call the government organization detaining people and moving them around so as not to be found \"secret police\" or \"disappearings\". [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":46690876,"author":"estearum","month":"2026-01","story":"Letter from a Birmingham Jail [King, Jr.] (1963)","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of a student swept into a van by masked agents.","quote":"","text":"The claim was that ICE is going after people for protected speech. The link is a determination by a federal judge to that effect, with the allegation going all the way to the top of the food chain. If you specifically want a video, then here you go: [VIDEO] This is a person being hunted and swept into a van by masked federal agents on the basis of her protected speech. There's no ambiguity here. The administration has said that's why they did it, and a judge has affirmed that as well (see prior link) I didn't claim arrest requires a warrant. I claimed it requires probable cause which is a far higher standard than DHS's claim to make arrests based on reasonable suspicion. The claim that arrests can be made upon reasonable suspicion is indeed flatly refuted by the text of the 4th Amendment."},{"id":44864717,"author":"troyvit","month":"2025-08","story":"Flock Now Using AI to Report to Police If Our Movement Patterns Are \"Suspicious\"","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of student detained by officials.","quote":"","text":"> People are still free to criticize Trump all they want. The First Amendment is pretty clear on this. I guess it depends on how you define \"person.\" [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"pWgyy_rlmag":{"wy":"Invoked as shorthand for hiring and investing by gut feel and appearances instead of what actually predicts results.","q":{"x":"that scene in Moneyball[1] where the old timers are trying to pick baseball players by who \"looks good,","i":36331005,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36331005,"author":"ryandrake","month":"2023-06","story":"Always the Same Warning Signs","stance":"reference","why":"Scene of scouts picking players on looks, compared to investors.","quote":"that scene in Moneyball[1] where the old timers are trying to pick baseball players by who \"looks good,","text":"OP here. I guess rather than \"smarter\" I meant to convey: less gullible, less vulnerable to being influenced by silly signals like someone's mannerisms and confidence, less reliant on unexplainable gut feels. I guess when I think of professional investors picking some of these dogs, my mind imagines that scene in Moneyball[1] where the old timers are trying to pick baseball players by who \"looks good,\" \"has a good jaw,\" \"how the ball sounds when it pops off his bat, and \"what the player's girlfriend looks like.\" 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12599719,"author":"pchiusano","month":"2016-09","story":"I Am Sam Altman, President of YC Group. AMA","stance":"reference","why":"Moneyball scene illustrating focus on irrelevant factors versus performance.","quote":"there's this scene from Moneyball, [1] that I love.","text":"People with families can certainly be successful entrepreneurs and create companies with \"explosive growth that maybe change the world\". (It kinda came across like you were implying that founders with families were mostly only interested in \"lifestyle businesses\".) There are so many factors that contribute to success, and they are never all equal, so any sort of up-front filtering based on some factor you think is correlated with success is going to miss out on people. And besides, I would strongly dispute any arguments that having a family is like some sort of handicap that must be overcome. In a lot of ways, I think having a family is a BETTER situation for starting a company! My impression from reading YC's stuff is more that folks feel the value of the program comes to a large degree from the in-person interactions people have, so it makes sense to require people to be there in person. Related, there's this scene from Moneyball, [1] that I love. They're all sitting around, discussing all this stuff that is at best tangentially related to _the actual things that matter_ for success of the team. It's very tempting and natural bias to latch on to this or that factor which you've become convinced makes a difference \"all else being equal\". But all else is never equal, and the question that is actually important is more the one Billy Beane keeps asking: \"Yeah, but can he hit?\" [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32025923,"author":"blitzar","month":"2022-07","story":"Twitter lays off 30% of its talent acquisition team","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Is it like the Scouts in Moneyball?","text":"What is a talent acquisition team and why did twitter need one? Is it like the Scouts in Moneyball? [VIDEO] \" Jane has a good looking typing motion, has a git checkin rate of .324, and scored a 465 on their leetcode \" \" She has an ugly husband though, suggests a lack of confidence \" \" I like Jim, good coders body, lean cut, good face. Good looking keystroke, the code just explodes off his keyboard with a real pop. You can hear it all the way over at the kombucha tap. \" \" If he codes so good, where are the git checkins? He's checking in at a .154 \""}]},"rGfaJZAdfNE":{"wy":"HN uses it as a primary source on Holmes's responses and, later, as evidence of her manner and voice.","q":{"x":"In this video she is being interviewed on Mad Money, using her (assumed) actual voice.","i":29791089,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29791089,"author":"reidjs","month":"2022-01","story":"Elizabeth Holmes found guilty","stance":"reference","why":"Holmes interviewed using her assumed actual voice","quote":"In this video she is being interviewed on Mad Money, using her (assumed) actual voice.","text":"One interesting find from that search, here’s two videos of Holmes, in the first she fakes a creepy deep voice for the entirety of the interview [LINK] In this video she is being interviewed on Mad Money, using her (assumed) actual voice. [VIDEO] What a strange person."},{"id":10770749,"author":"gamesbrainiac","month":"2015-12","story":"U.S. Probes Theranos Complaints","stance":"reference","why":"Holmes' response to the WSJ article, as alternative take","quote":"This is EH's response to the WSJ article.","text":"Thought I'd put this in here: [VIDEO] This is EH's response to the WSJ article. Very positive defence of EH. Just wanted to add this for an alternative take."},{"id":17379563,"author":"jaytaylor","month":"2018-06","story":"[Review] Bad Blood: Secrets and Idiots in Elizabeth Holmes Fake Valley Startup","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the fake deep voice thing she does- so suspicious; how were people genuinely fooled by it?","text":"Just finished this book, it had me hooked the entire time. I'm left shocked that the sham went on for 12 years. So much negligence on behalf of the VCs, and so many high profile and well-respected people bought into it, even up to the level of famous Four-star General James Mattis. How was it not a huge red flag that there were no domain experts on the Theranos board? The reality distortion field projected by Elizabeth Holmes was obviously incredibly effective and powerful. Tragic that she used this capability for deceptive, selfish reasons rather than a greater good or higher purpose. Finally, the fake deep voice thing she does- so suspicious; how were people genuinely fooled by it? [VIDEO]"},{"id":17106760,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2018-05","story":"Theranos Inc.’s Partners in Blood","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"after watching a few videos it is kind of unsettling","text":"> Holmes' weirdly low voice was an act! She's been caught using her normal register. Was going to say that's kind of a silly thing say, but after watching a few videos it is kind of unsettling: [LINK] [VIDEO] Had the other signals not been there I'd say, whatever, they want to appear more powerful by using \"top 10 tips and tricks of persuasion\". However given the other controversy and shadiness it just adds to the pile."},{"id":17124005,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2018-05","story":"A New Look Inside Theranos’ Dysfunctional Corporate Culture","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here she forgets to fake it for a while","text":"> Like many people who met her for the first time, Beam was taken aback by her deep voice. It was unlike anything he’d heard before. Someone pointed it out the other day here, and I thought well that's just silly. Then went and listen to some videos and it is kind of freaky. It's like she deliberately changes her voice, but it sounds worse and obviously fake. [LINK] Here she forgets to fake it for a while: [VIDEO] I'd think someone should have mentioned it to her. But given the culture in the company I can see what was going on. She could have showed up with a lampshade on her head and nobody would have said anything either. After a while there is nothing to say to someone who does stuff like this: > Still visibly angry, Holmes told the gathered employees that she was building a religion. If there were any among them who didn’t believe, they should leave. Balwani put it more bluntly: Anyone not prepared to show complete devotion and unmitigated loyalty to the company should “get the fuck out.”"}]},"y3RIHnK0_NE":{"wy":"Used as a Skynet joke and cited as a parody that fooled some viewers into thinking it was real.","m":[{"id":41246294,"author":"tiagod","month":"2024-08","story":"Captain Disillusion debunks David Beckham beach kicks [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Defends the video against a claim that it glorifies killing dogs.","quote":"The CGI robot is given the task of killing a CGI robotic dog, and it refuses the task and runs away with it.","text":"> They made a video glorifying killing dogs and I was absolutely done with them. I suppose you're referring to this video? [VIDEO] I don't see how it's glorifying killing dogs. The CGI robot is given the task of killing a CGI robotic dog, and it refuses the task and runs away with it."},{"id":34015360,"author":"bnralt","month":"2022-12","story":"JFK Assassination Records – 2022 Additional Documents Release","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a joke video that fooled Joe Rogan into thinking it was real.","quote":"Joe Rogan thought this joke video[1], showing a Boston Dynamics robot being trained for the military which goes rogue","text":"Some time ago Joe Rogan thought this joke video[1], showing a Boston Dynamics robot being trained for the military which goes rogue and attacks his human handlers when they try to have it shoot a robotic dog, was real. He was talking to Lex Friedman about how the video tricked him, but that the scary thing was how close the video was to reality. Friedman tried to explain to him how things that seem human like, such as the movement that the actual Boston Dynamics robots make, doesn't mean they're anywhere close to actually thinking like a human. The thing is, when someone is outside of a field, it's easy to misunderstand things and assume a capability that's not there. If you've never dealt with the CIA or FBI, it's easy to imagine that they're some all powerful organization that's able to kill off any politician they like at a moments notice with no scrutiny and silence anyone who even raises any questions. If you are familiar with the organizations, this point of view is as naive as someone who says, \"Oh, you program? I came up with the idea for a game where the player can do anything they want and AIs will just generate the content for them. You think you can program that?\" And the issue with conspiracy theories is that any fact that gets presented just gets funneled into the preffered hypothesis. For instance, you mention \"Oswald was killed before he could speak.\" But that's not true, he was answering questions from reporters before he was shot[2]. And the guy who killed h"},{"id":29218984,"author":"not1ofU","month":"2021-11","story":"The bullet effects in Terminator 2 weren’t CGI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"you want skynet, because this is how we get skynet. :-)","text":"Reminded me of this (not sure if the channel is related or not) [VIDEO] ^ you want skynet, because this is how we get skynet. :-)"},{"id":25672257,"author":"cptnapalm","month":"2021-01","story":"Roblox raises $520M at $29.5B valuation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the adorable parody of a robot and its robodog","text":"And, for me, the major impact of those YouTube videos has been the adorable parody of a robot and its robodog: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24489874,"author":"irjustin","month":"2020-09","story":"Boston Dynamics CEO talks profitability and the company’s next robots","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I love watching the documentaries (parodies) of how we get to Skynet","text":"Agreed when you think of them as a normal business. it's bonkers, but if you're looking at it as a R&D shop - closer to pharma or biotech, it's less crazy (still a little crazy tho). That's why it can really on be funded by guys like Alphabet who can take these R&D hits. The bet really looks something iRobot - Every household has a robot servant/maid that's made by Boston Dynamics. Some of the more recent videos make me want to believe it's within my lifetime. I love watching the documentaries (parodies) of how we get to Skynet: [VIDEO]"}]}});
